rganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e44fe6a4-8690-5fbb-8366-3b5cdcddbe76', 'word_count': 787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e44fe6a4-8690-5fbb-8366-3b5cdcddbe76'}, {'abstract': 'Top magazine and long-form articles this week.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/newswallah-long-reads-edition-14/', 'snippet': 'Top magazine and long-form articles this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joe Sacco, the well-known American cartoonist and journalist, uses his “comic journalism” lens on a rural district of Uttar Pradesh, India’s largest and one of its poorest states. Caravan magazine publishes a long piece by him in its latest issue, which also includes a long profile of Imran Khan, the Pakistani cricketer turned politician. Mr. Sacco and an Indian journalist who assisted him, Piyush Srivastava, travel to the district Kushinagar near the border between Uttar Pradesh and Bihar to meet with poor and lower caste families.\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: Long Reads Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sacco, Joe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Economist, The', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Yorker', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Uttar Pradesh State (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-01T07:51:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vikas Bajaj', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vikas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bajaj', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15b4b858-87ba-5bad-ae32-066057b728e0', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15b4b858-87ba-5bad-ae32-066057b728e0'}, {'abstract': 'One of Spain’s most popular holiday events, the colorful Procession of the Three Kings, will proceed down the Paseo de la Castellana from Nuevos Ministerios to Plaza de Cibeles on Friday between 6:30 p.m. and 9 p.m.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/madrid-to-celebrate-three-kings-and-the-gift-of-innocence/', 'snippet': 'One of Spain’s most popular holiday events, the colorful Procession of the Three Kings, will proceed down the Paseo de la Castellana from Nuevos Ministerios to Plaza de Cibeles on Friday between 6:30 p.m. and 9 p.m.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of Spain’s most popular holiday events, the colorful Procession of the Three Kings, will proceed down the Paseo de la Castellana from Nuevos Ministerios to Plaza de Cibeles on Friday between 6:30 p.m. and 9 p.m. It features the elaborately dressed kings, their pages and helpers, who ride on floats distributing sweets to the children lining the route. There are at least 30 carriages, theatrical re-enactments, dancers, musicians, jugglers, street performers, royal pages, giant puppets and many other entertaining characters.According to the Bible, at this time of year, three wise and powerful kings brought gifts of gold, frankincense and myrrh to the infant Jesus. To celebrate the occasion, on Jan. 5, children in Spain go to bed early, awaiting the morning, when they hope to find the presents that they requested of the three kings in letters written on Boxing Day, Dec. 26. They leave shoes on windowsills or balconies or under the Christmas tree to be filled with toys.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Madrid to Celebrate Three Kings, and the Gift of Innocence', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-01T11:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad2ec18c-89a5-5ce3-a54f-338cd68f608d', 'word_count': 232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad2ec18c-89a5-5ce3-a54f-338cd68f608d'}, {'abstract': 'In no other sport  does it seem necessary to embellish a perfectly good word like “ball.”', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/the-year-in-football-language/', 'snippet': 'In no other sport  does it seem necessary to embellish a perfectly good word like “ball.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a running joke in our office. One of our editors starts imitating a coach — “To be a good football team, you have to protect the football, and then make some football plays” — and others sometimes join in.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year in Football Language', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-01T11:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e3df0fe-a048-532c-8106-488bb91026f6', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e3df0fe-a048-532c-8106-488bb91026f6'}, {'abstract': 'I.B.M. is a corporate pioneer in globalization, but the company’s success has not meant more jobs in the United States. Its experience holds lessons for the American economy, corporations and workers. “You can’t be in the commodity space, says Sam Palmisano, the departing I.B.M. chief executive. “You need to have the advanced skills that the future requires.”', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/unboxed-extra-ibm-and-americas-job-challenge/', 'snippet': 'I.B.M. is a corporate pioneer in globalization, but the company’s success has not meant more jobs in the United States. Its experience holds lessons for the American economy, corporations and workers. “You can’t be in the commodity space, says Sam...', 'lead_paragraph': 'I.B.M.’s success in recent years is clear from the numbers — an upward ascent in profits, profit margins, stock-market value and even research and development spending.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/blogs/bitpix-ibm-palmisano2/bitpix-ibm-palmisano2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/blogs/bitpix-ibm-palmisano2/bitpix-ibm-palmisano2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/10/blogs/bitpix-ibm-palmisano2/bitpix-ibm-palmisano2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unboxed Extra: I.B.M. and America’s Job Challenge', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Enterprise Computing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {