-review.html', 'snippet': 'Kurt Weill’s fable “Magic Night” is revived onstage in London, for the first time since the 1920s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — A small girl and boy play with their toys before falling asleep, and when they have done so, the toys come to life. Adventures ensue as Evil is battled, with Good duly prevailing and the toys returning to their former inert state. This is not, as you might think, “Toy Story 17,” but a new children’s show at the Royal Opera House’s Linbury Studio Theater that boasts an almost-forgotten score by none other than Kurt Weill.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/02/arts/magic-1/magic-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 749, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/02/arts/magic-1/magic-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/02/arts/magic-1/magic-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reviving a Long-Lost Tale From Kurt Weill’s Archive', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reviving a Long-Lost Tale From Kurt Weill’s Archive', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Opera House', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weill, Kurt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-01T22:12:24+0000', 'document_type': 'a