}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T01:41:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nelson D. Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nelson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1efafd1b-5c10-58a8-8754-58560cc24544', 'word_count': 1237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1efafd1b-5c10-58a8-8754-58560cc24544'}, {'abstract': 'In 2011, Osama bin Laden was the most popular global topic on Facebook, while the resignation of President Hosni Mubarak of Egypt was the top news issue on Twitter.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/top-social-topics-in-11-bin-laden-and-mubarak/', 'snippet': 'In 2011, Osama bin Laden was the most popular global topic on Facebook, while the resignation of President Hosni Mubarak of Egypt was the top news issue on Twitter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost two-thirds of adults who used the Internet in 2011 also belonged to social networks, like Facebook and Twitter, according to the Pew Internet and American Life Project.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Top Social Topics in ’11: Bin Laden and Mubarak', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T01:49:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/212f32aa-9afc-58ad-95a2-087747ae8942', 'word_count': 593, 'uri': 'nyt://article/212f32aa-9afc-58ad-95a2-087747ae8942'}, {'abstract': 'The news that Oklahoma is now ready to accept and celebrate Woody Guthrie as a native son with an exhibition and study center for his archives is long overdue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/02/opinion/homeward-goes-the-dust-bowl-balladeer.html', 'snippet': 'The news that Oklahoma is now ready to accept and celebrate Woody Guthrie as a native son with an exhibition and study center for his archives is long overdue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The news that Oklahoma is now ready to accept and celebrate Woody Guthrie, the seminal American folk singer who died in 1967, as a native son with an exhibition and a study center for his archives is welcome if long overdue. The state resisted honoring him for decades, because of his leftist politics. “I ain’t a Communist necessarily,” Guthrie said, “but I been in the red all my life.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Homeward Goes the Dust Bowl Balladeer', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Homeward Goes the Dust Bowl Balladeer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guthrie, Woody', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Great Depression (1930's)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T01:54:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/96e44e15-70e5-5af8-8c97-4e4d3eb6ba54', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96e44e15-70e5-5af8-8c97-4e4d3eb6ba54'}, {'abstract': 'As the Jets desperately tried to salvage the season late in their game against the Dolphins, Santonio Holmes, a team captain, was on the bench.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/02/sports/football/santonio-holmes-is-center-of-discord-in-jets-loss.html', 'snippet': 'As the Jets desperately tried to salvage the season late in their game against the Dolphins, Santonio Holmes, a team captain, was on the bench.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIAMI GARDENS, Fla. — A simmering season of discontent for wide receiver Santonio Holmes boiled to the surface Sunday, sending the Jets into the off-season as a splintered squad.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Holmes Ends His Season on Sideline', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Holmes Ends His Season On Sideline', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, Santonio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Dolphins', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T01:58:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Kerasotis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kerasotis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27be24c3-a0d0-53e5-becf-949a0016a8d5', 'word_count': 768, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27be24c3-a0d0-53e5-becf-949a0016a8d5'}, {'abstract': 'The Bloomberg administration should drop the requirement that all applicants for food stamps be electronically fingerprinted, which leads too many New Yorkers to forgo help.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/02/opinion/stigmatizing-food-stamps.html', 'snippet': 'The Bloomberg administration should drop the requirement that all applicants for food stamps be electronically fingerprinted, which leads too many New Yorkers to forgo help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five years ago, New York State stopped requiring that all applicants for food stamps be electronically fingerprinted. Recently, California and Texas ended their requirement, leaving New York City and Arizona the only jurisdictions to continue the stigmatizing requirement. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stigmatizing Food Stamps', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stigmatizing Food Stamps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Stamps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Identification Devices', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fingerprinting', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T01:59:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dcbd773-7ae5-51b5-bf1b-70d9edb2b5ad', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dcbd773-7ae5-51b5-bf1b-70d9edb2b5ad'}, {'abstract': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/02/business/treasury-auctions-set-for-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Treasury’s schedule of financing this week includes a regular weekly auction of new three- and six-month bills on Tuesday and an auction of four-week bills on Wednesday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T01:59:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe695deb-d1f0-5dc5-909e-98d82f8cb515', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe695deb-d1f0-5dc5-909e-98d82f8cb515'}, {'abstract': 'Readers are invited to send in questions to Melinda Gates (and to me) about maternal and child health, and women’s empowerment, while she’s on a trip to Bangladesh.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/send-in-questions-for-melinda-gates/', 'snippet': 'Readers are invited to send in questions to Melinda Gates (and to me) about maternal and child health, and women’s empowerment, while she’s on a trip to Bangladesh.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Melinda Gates will be traveling shortly to Bangladesh to look at maternal health, child health and women’s issues (including family planning and reproductive health) — and she has agreed to answer your questions about these topics while she’s there. So please post your questions for Melinda and me about these issues in the comments below, and we’ll choose some and give you our best answers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Send in Questions for Melinda Gates', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T02:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1aa2fec-888b-5143-b297-85de4efa4aeb', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1aa2fec-888b-5143-b297-85de4efa4aeb'}, {'abstract': 'Living in Washington changes the way politicians see the problems they are supposed to help solve.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/01/the-distorted-view-from-capitol-hill/', 'snippet': 'Living in Washington changes the way politicians see the problems they are supposed to help solve.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, both The Washington Post and The Times published illuminating stories on the growing affluence of members of Congress. Both stories — Peter Whoriskey’s in the Post and Eric Lichtblau’s in the Times — demonstrate how the economic fortunes of those elected to Congress have diverged radically from those of the men and women they represent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Distorted View from Capitol Hill', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank