The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran Tests Naval Cruise Missile', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran Tests Naval Cruise Missile', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Persian Gulf', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missiles and Missile Defense Systems', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T11:19:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1144e6e7-f7b9-56dd-99ad-961ab2003335', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1144e6e7-f7b9-56dd-99ad-961ab2003335'}, {'abstract': 'A new multibillion-dollar effort to clean up the world’s most populous river basin must undo the damage of years of epic incompetence.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/02/grunge-in-the-ganges/', 'snippet': 'A new multibillion-dollar effort to clean up the world’s most populous river basin must undo the damage of years of epic incompetence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VARANASI, India — I needed to do a double and then a triple take to believe my own eyes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grunge in the Ganges', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ganges River', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-02T11:59:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Morrison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morrison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a461cb87-93e3-5103-b20b-6eee53d709af', 'word_count': 512, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a461cb87-93e3-5103-b20b-6eee53d709af'}, {'abstract': 'The Jets’ problems pertain to player personnel. They’re constructed to run the ball and win with defense. That’s why they felt comfortable building around a quarterback like Mark Sanchez.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/02/in-defense-of-brian-schottenheimer/', 'snippet': 'The Jets’ problems pertain to player personnel. They’re constructed to run the ball and win with defense. That’s why they felt comfortable building around a quarterback like Mark Sanchez.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s talk of the Jets reshuffling their offensive coaching staff. They’d be fools to let Brian Schottenheimer, one of the best young offensive coordinators in the game, get away. The Jets’ problems pertain to player personnel. They’re constructed to run the ball and win with defense. That’s why they felt comfortable building around a quarterback that has limitations like Mark Sanchez.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Defense of Brian Schottenheimer', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schottenheimer, Brian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub