r': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6dc8a8be-be36-57ac-b0d9-adcf8329dc50', 'word_count': 1611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6dc8a8be-be36-57ac-b0d9-adcf8329dc50'}, {'abstract': 'Hardy and reliable in the winter months, turnips are a logical foundation for comforting stews and gratins, but they’re also at home in egg or noodle dishes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/02/health/nutrition/turnips-versatile-and-nutritious-in-any-season-turnips-versatile-and-nutritious-in-any-season.html', 'snippet': 'Hardy and reliable in the winter months, turnips are a logical foundation for comforting stews and gratins, but they’re also at home in egg or noodle dishes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Turnips are one vegetable you can count on during the winter months; like carrots, they store well. The root vegetables are members of the cruciferae family, the same family that brings you nutrient-rich vegetables like cabbage, kale and broccoli (genus Brassica). Turnips are rich in sulfuric compounds, particularly glucosinolates, that are believed to have antioxidant properties. They’re also a very good source of potassium. When you can get them with the greens attached, they’re a two-in-one crop, like beets, as their greens bring you a whole new set of nutrients – lots of calcium, vitamin K, vitamin A and beta carotene – and culinary possibilities. Turnip greens are similar in flavor to kale, perhaps a little more bitter, and with a more delicate texture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/03/science/02recipehealth/02recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/03/science/02recipehealth/02recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheigh