,” will be responding to reader questions.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/03/simon-doonan-is-taking-reader-questions/', 'snippet': 'Simon Doonan, the creative ambassador at large for Barneys and the author of “Gay Men Don’t Get Fat,” will be responding to reader questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Doonan, the creative ambassador at large for Barneys New York and the author of the new book “Gay Men Don’t Get Fat,” will be responding to reader questions about porking out, slimming down, the perils of guacamole and chips, and whether or not certain foods can truly be categorized as “straight” or “gay.” Submit your question as a comment below or tweet us at @nytimesdining using the hashtag #asksimon, and he’ll answer on Diner’s Journal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Simon Doonan Is Taking Reader Questions', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T19:49:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bbec8f9-5711-56a2-b068-a7e292bdfe16', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bbec8f9-5711-56a2-b068-a7e292bdfe16'}, {'abstract': 'Peruvian cuisine’s traditional ingredients of potatoes, chili peppers and seafood have been transformed by culinary techniques around the world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/dining/peruvian-cuisine-nyc-review.html', 'snippet': 'Peruvian cuisine’s traditional ingredients of potatoes, chili peppers and seafood have been transformed by culinary techniques around the world.', 'lead_paragraph': '“LET’S go out for Peruvian!” doesn’t quite have the ring of a dinner invitation for Chinese or Italian. But as is increasingly clear, Peruvian cuisine — whose indigenous potatoes, chili peppers and seafood have been supplemented by ingredients and transformed by culinary techniques from many countries, China and Italy included — has much to offer beyond rotisserie chicken.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04UNDER_SPAN/04UNDER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04UNDER_SPAN/04UNDER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04UNDER_SPAN/04UNDER_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04UNDER_SPAN/04UNDER_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04UNDER_SPAN/04UNDER_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Flavors of Peru, Recast', 'kicker': '$25 and Under', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flavors Of Peru, Recast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peru', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T19:49:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Cook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reporte