ff4-5523-b726-e72ab9f7ef5d'}, {'abstract': 'Will Georgia’s hard-hitting ads push families with obesity problems toward help, or toward anger and denial?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/03/georgias-tough-anti-childhood-obesity-campaign/', 'snippet': 'Will Georgia’s hard-hitting ads push families with obesity problems toward help, or toward anger and denial?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The blogosphere hates Georgia’s tough new Strong4Life campaign against childhood obesity. Short ads or billboards, each featuring an obese child (the youngest looks to be about 9, the oldest a teenager), tell Georgia to “stop sugarcoating” the problem. “Fat kids become fat adults,” reads the slogan under what looks to be the youngest child, a little girl who can be seen in a brief video saying she hates to go to school, where kids make fun of her. Another ad, under a young boy, reads: “Big bones didn’t make me this way. Big meals did.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Georgia’s Tough Campaign Against Childhood Obesity', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T21:33:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d87d10cb-c742-5e0e-ae4f-b10de21473cf', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d87d10cb-c742-5e0e-ae4f-b10