rk Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfff3d23-deca-5101-936f-a40c9f3ac09b', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfff3d23-deca-5101-936f-a40c9f3ac09b'}, {'abstract': 'Well known in its raw state, hamachi, or yellowtail, is now available in retail markets, thanks to Local Ocean, a fish farm in Hudson, N.Y.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/dining/hamachi-rarely-sold-for-retail-now-available-to-home-cooks.html', 'snippet': 'Well known in its raw state, hamachi, or yellowtail, is now available in retail markets, thanks to Local Ocean, a fish farm in Hudson, N.Y.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hamachi, or yellowtail, a fish in the jack family, is so very familiar and yet so rare: well known in its raw state, as sushi, sashimi and tartare in restaurants, yet seldom sold in retail markets for the home cook. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM/04STUFF2_FISH_SPAM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hamachi Now Available to Home Cooks', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hamachi Now Available To Home Cooks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T22:47:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0196f0dc-c250-587f-834b-942023615a0f', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0196f0dc-c250-587f-834b-942023615a0f'}, {'abstract': '“Lysistrata Jones” will have played 34 preview performances and 30 regular performances when it closes on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/03/lysistrata-jones-to-close/', 'snippet': '“Lysistrata Jones” will have played 34 preview performances and 30 regular performances when it closes on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Lysistrata Jones,” the new Broadway musical about a college basketball team that brought dribbling, passing, and hoop shots to the Walter Kerr Theater, will close after the Jan. 8 matinee performance, the producers announced on Tuesday, following two months of weak ticket sales. The show opened on Dec. 14 to a mix of positive and negative reviews from critics, and it failed to develop a following despite the relative lack of new original musicals on Broadway last fall, with some ticket sales agents saying that the title – a modern-day twist on the classic Greek play – was off-putting to some potential theatergoers and tourist groups. (The only other original musical to open on Broadway during the fall season, “Bonnie & Clyde,” received worse reviews and closed on Friday.) ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Lysistrata Jones’ to Close', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T22:49:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/faf1ced0-8bf2-52d7-87cd-ae991d8a854b', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/faf1ced0-8bf2-52d7-87cd-ae991d8a854b'}, {'abstract': 'There is some reason to think that FiveThirtyEight’s polling-based forecasts give Rick Santorum too little credit going in to Tuesday night’s Iowa caucuses.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/03/why-id-bet-on-santorum-and-against-my-model/', 'snippet': 'There is some reason to think that FiveThirtyEight’s polling-based forecasts give Rick Santorum too little credit going in to Tuesday night’s Iowa caucuses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The official version of FiveThirtyEight’s polling-based forecasts project Rick Santorum to receive about 19 percent of the vote in the Iowa caucuses on Tuesday night. It translates this to an 18 percent chance of Mr. Santorum’s overcoming his slight deficit with Mitt Romney and Ron Paul and winning the caucuses outright.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why I’d Bet on Santorum (and Against My Model)', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T22:49:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ceed7fa-099d-5d86-82aa-44418c3af89a', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ceed7fa-099d-5d86-82aa-44418c3af89a'}, {'abstract': 'Cooked beef, lamb, pork, veal and ham, chefs say, can be used in almost any dish that calls for ground meat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/dining/meatballs-lucky-to-be-a-leftover.html', 'snippet': 'Cooked beef, lamb, pork, veal and ham, chefs say, can be used in almost any dish that calls for ground meat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOLIDAY cooking is finally over, but many refrigerators are still in hangover mode: stuffed with the remains of Christmas beef, the country ham from New Year’s Day, the rest of that disappointing crown roast of pork or spectacular leg of lamb. The holidays generate a special genre of leftovers: too boring to eat, too expensive to toss. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04MEATBALL_SPAN/04MEATBALL_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 332, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04MEATBALL_SPAN/04MEATBALL_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 332}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04MEATBALL_SPAN/04MEATBALL_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04MEATBALL_SPAN/04MEATBALL_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04MEATBALL_SPAN/04MEATBALL_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lucky to Be a Leftover', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lucky to Be a Leftover', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T22:49:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Moskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84f3d7e3-7b8b-5a9e-af03-77af692485c7', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84f3d7e3-7b8b-5a9e-af03-77af692485c7'}, {'abstract': 'The premiere of “Oprah’s Next Chapter,” the once-a-week successor to her five-day-a-week “Oprah Winfrey Show,” drew far fewer viewers than had watched her previous talk show on local TV stations each weekday.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/03/oprahs-next-chapter-draws-1-1-million-viewers/', 'snippet': 'The premiere of “Oprah’s Next Chapter,” the once-a-week successor to her five-day-a-week “Oprah Winfrey Show,” drew far fewer viewers than had watched her previous talk show on local TV stations each weekday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oprah Winfrey is back on television with a new talk show. But some of her fans don’t seem to know.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oprah’s ‘Next Chapter’ Draws 1.1 Million Viewers', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winfrey, Oprah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen Media Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oprah Winfrey Network', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T22:50:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b753cebf-5e08-5325-a624-30102f3b431f', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b753cebf-5e08-5325-a624-30102f3b431f'}, {'abstract': 'Olive-leaf tea, a gently mellow herbal brew, is now sold at Garden of Eden stores and some online sites.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/dining/olive-leaf-tea-enriches-the-kitchen.html', 'snippet': 'Olive-leaf tea, a gently mellow herbal brew, is now sold at Garden of Eden stores and some online sites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unlike the oil, fruit and wood of the olive tree, which have enriched the kitchen since ancient times, the leaves have been more symbolic, as tokens of peace. But the greenery also has a less familiar culinary application. Dried olive-leaf tea is a gently mellow herbal brew with a scintilla of bitterness that evokes a green olive. Some lemon or honey enhances the flavor; it’s excellent iced, and can be added to a marinade, especially for fish, or — released from its bag — used in a spice rub or to season a salad dressing. Few tea stores sell it, but Garden of Eden now carries Mysia brand olive-leaf tea bags from Turkey. The individual packets are labeled Zeytin Yapragi Cayi, which simply means olive leaf tea. The tea is also available online.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04STUFF3_TEA/04STUFF3_TEA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04STUFF3_TEA/04STUFF3_TEA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/04/dining/04STUFF3_TEA/04STUFF3_TEA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Token of Peace as a Soothing Brew', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Token of Peace As a Soothing Brew', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T22:51:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7175d5d-2ec4-5b58-9cfe-819bf5604c3a', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7175d5d-2ec4-5b58-9cfe-819bf5604c3a'}, {'abstract': 'Adblock, Mozilla and Wikipedia are some of the big-name free software projects looking to raise money in innovative ways.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/03/adblock-and-wikipedia-look-to-raise-money-for-free-software/', 'snippet': 'Adblock, Mozilla and Wikipedia are some of the big-name free software projects looking to raise money in innovative ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Monday, I wrote about how the lead developer of an open-source project – Adblock Plus – had created a start-up to manage the project. As part of a new business plan, the software, which has long been known for blocking ads, would no longer block what it calls “acceptable ads.” (These ads will not be flashy or slow down the browsing experience.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Adblock and Wikipedia Look to Raise Money for ‘Free’ Software', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Open-Source Software', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mozilla Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wikimedia Foundation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wikipedia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T22:57:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/476510e3-59a0-53cb-9d13-414fbcc0b036', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/476510e3-59a0-53cb-9d13-414fbcc0b036'}, {'abstract': "Now that the state has suspended payments for improvements to the city's struggling schools, principals are wondering: What happens to the SmartBoards, teachers and programs they have purchased, with the expectation the money to pay for them was forthcoming?", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/01/03/city-tells-struggling-schools-to-carry-on-while-funds-are-in-flux/', 'snippet': "Now that the state has suspended payments for improvements to the city's struggling schools, principals are wondering: What happens to the SmartBoards, teachers and programs they have purchased, with the expectation the money to pay for them was f...", 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Tells Struggling Schools to Carry On While Funds Are in Flux', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-03T23:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By ANNA M. PHILLIPS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71c1c8cb-d454-5009-9c39-b5a5e4cad28e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71c1c8cb-d454-5009-9c39-b5a5e4cad28e'}, {'abstract': 'The real action will not get underway until 8 p.m. New York time, when caucuses convene around the state. We will be entertaining you with various statistically driven observations before then.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/03/live-blogging-the-iowa-caucuses/', 'snippet': 'The real action will not get underway until 8 p.m. New York time, when caucuses convene around the state. We will be entertaining you with various statis