{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'GigU', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T01:55:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas L. Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0feb355a-ffc8-5a18-ad71-6ecf0b55521f', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0feb355a-ffc8-5a18-ad71-6ecf0b55521f'}, {'abstract': 'Teachers in Idaho and elsewhere have been in revolt over policy makers thrusting technology into classrooms and shifting money to unproven teaching methods.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/technology/idaho-teachers-fight-a-reliance-on-computers.html', 'snippet': 'Teachers in Idaho and elsewhere have been in revolt over policy makers thrusting technology into classrooms and shifting money to unproven teaching methods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'POST FALLS, Idaho — Ann Rosenbaum, a former military police officer in the Marines, does not shrink from a fight, having even survived a close encounter with a car bomb in Iraq. Her latest conflict is quite different: she is now a high school teacher, and she and many of her peers in Idaho are resisting a statewide plan that dictates how computers should be used in classrooms.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/business/JP-TEACHER1/JP-TEACHER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/04/business/JP-TEACHER1/JP-TEACHER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/business/JP-TEACHER1/JP-TEACHER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/business/JP-TEACHER1/JP-TEACHER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/04/business/JP-TEACHER1/JP-TEACHER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Teachers Resist High-Tech Push in Idaho Schools', 'kicker': 'Grading the Digital School', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Teachers Resist High-Tech Push In Idaho Schools', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Series', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Learning', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Idaho', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T01:59:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6d0326e-1c88-59fc-ba2f-e93b9822fd67', 'word_count': 1894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6d0326e-1c88-59fc-ba2f-e93b9822fd67'}, {'abstract': 'The presidential campaign shouldn’t eclipse important protest movements already under way.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/03/beyond-elections-people-power/', 'snippet': 'The presidential campaign shouldn’t eclipse important protest movements already under way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The presidential election may be grabbing headlines, but the true rallying cry for 2012 is to struggle and organize around those issues that a president might take seriously, to stake out positions that would benefit what used to be called the working class (and now goes by “the 99 percent”) and to garner enough political will and power to pressure the president and Congress to move resolutely on the issues that matter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyond Elections: People Power', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T02:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/071bb32f-6881-5220-b96f-38d77ab74051', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/071bb32f-6881-5220-b96f-38d77ab74051'}, {'abstract': 'The $20 billion fund created by BP to pay damages from the 2010 spill in the Gulf of Mexico has halted payments until the judge overseeing the federal court litigation clarifies a recent order.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/business/energy-environment/louisiana-bp-cleanup-payments-on-hold.html', 'snippet': 'The $20 billion fund created by BP to pay damages from the 2010 spill in the Gulf of Mexico has halted payments until the judge overseeing the federal court litigation clarifies a recent order.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The $20 billion fund created by BP to pay damages from the 2010 spill in the Gulf of Mexico has stopped making payments until the judge overseeing the sprawling federal court litigation clarifies a recent order. Judge Carl J. Barbier in New Orleans ruled on Dec. 29 that settlements reached since Nov. 7 must provide 6 percent of the “gross settlement amount” to an escrow account for certain litigation expenses of plaintiffs’ lawyers. In a letter to Judge Barbier, David Pitofsky, the lead counsel for the fund, asked the judge whether, among other questions, his ruling means that people who have already received money should be forced to give some of it back. “We feel compelled by the court’s order to freeze payments to eligible claimants until we get clarification from the court,” said Kenneth R. Feinberg, administrator of the fund. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Louisiana: BP Cleanup Payments on Hold', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Louisiana: Bp Cleanup Payments Halted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BP Plc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinberg, Kenneth R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico Oil Spill (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barbier, Carl J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T02:01:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/70d03e98-fe19-5564-9e3e-0c43fb231a90', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70d03e98-fe19-5564-9e3e-0c43fb231a90'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankees were not interested in courting starter C. J. Wilson this off-season, but they could be more aggressive next winter, when the free-agent starters should be much more appealing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/sports/baseball/yankees-take-conservative-approach-to-2012-setting-sights-on-next-free-agent-class.html', 'snippet': 'The Yankees were not interested in courting starter C. J. Wilson this off-season, but they could be more aggressive next winter, when the free-agent starters should be much more appealing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most telling moment of the Yankees’ off-season was when they simply said no to C. J. Wilson. Here was the No. 1 starter for the Texas Rangers, the two-time defending American League champions. He wanted to come to New York for a visit, and the Yankees would not even extend an invitation.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/sports/KEPNER1/KEPNER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Yankees Are Saving Now for a Potential Splash Next Winter', 'kicker': 'On Baseball', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Yankees Are Saving Now for a Potential Splash Next Winter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Free Agents (Sports)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, C J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T02:04:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/074d821b-b478-57cd-8951-08842a1c41af', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/074d821b-b478-57cd-8951-08842a1c41af'}, {'abstract': 'A new ad campaign for Jackson Hewitt Tax Service wants to get customers to focus more on refund checks than on the actual filing of their taxes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/business/media/jackson-hewitt-turning-tax-time-into-party-time.html', 'snippet': 'A new ad campaign for Jackson Hewitt Tax Service wants to get customers to focus more on refund checks than on the actual filing of their taxes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS the holiday season ends, the tax season begins, giving some revelers little reason to celebrate. A new ad campaign for Jackson Hewitt Tax Service wants to change that by getting customers to focus more on refund checks than on the actual filing of their taxes.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/business/ADCO/ADCO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/04/business/ADCO/ADCO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/04/business/ADCO/ADCO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 568, '