 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Introducing the Cuddly Michael Shannon', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Horowitz, Josh', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shannon, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T15:59:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a740f05-0f66-537b-973e-61e1537f94e0', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a740f05-0f66-537b-973e-61e1537f94e0'}, {'abstract': 'Black nail polish is no longer just for angsty teenagers making their cosmetics purchases on late-night trips to Duane Reade.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/the-new-goth-nail/', 'snippet': 'Black nail polish is no longer just for angsty teenagers making their cosmetics purchases on late-night trips to Duane Reade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Black nail polish is no longer just for angsty teenagers making their cosmetics purchases on late-night trips to Duane Reade. There has been a resurgence of the dark nail on the runways, including those of Cushnie et Ochs, Chanel and Rag & Bone, to name a few. And to disprove the idea that there can’t be more than one shade of black comes the Dickensian Volume, a set of 10 deceivingly dark and metallic shades by the indie nail polish line StrangeBeautiful. Its creator, the designer-turned-beauty-entrepreneur Jane Schub, drew inspiration from Charles Dickens and the Brothers Grimm. Wear a different polish on each nail for the full effect.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New Goth Nail', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T16:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5c6e68d-0c4e-58e2-8e38-64449e95b734', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5c6e68d-0c4e-58e2-8e38-64449e95b734'}, {'abstract': 'Rick Santorum is the Mike Huckabee of 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/the-iowa-is-so-important-phase-ends-the-iowa-is-so-over-phase-begins/', 'snippet': 'Rick Santorum is the Mike Huckabee of 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DES MOINES—In 2008, the very conservative Evangelical Christians in Iowa muddled the Republican presidential nominating contest, giving their support to an unelectable candidate, Mike Huckabee, who won here over the person that Republicans were most likely to settle for in the end, John McCain. That meant the G.O.P. had to spend millions more dollars, run more nasty ads, and toss around more radical right-wing ideas before turning their attention to the general election.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Iowa-Is-So-Important Phase Ends, the Iowa-Is-So-Over Phase Begins', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations