ng lesbian living in Brooklyn, discusses her film in this video segment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While “Pariah” tells the coming-out story of a young African-American lesbian in Brooklyn, its director, Dee Rees, sought to make a film with broader themes. Ms. Rees, who received a Gotham Award this season for breakthrough director and a best director prize from the Black Film Critics Circle, sees her film as a story about identity and self-discovery. “Pariah,” which stars Adepero Oduye, was a breakout hit at Sundance last year and finally made it to theaters last month. Reviewing the film for The Times, Stephen Holden called it “an acutely observed examination of strait-laced parents trying to deny a child’s homosexuality while all the time knowing better.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Identity, Discovery and ‘Pariah’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Pariah (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oduye, Adepero', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rees, Dee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T17:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76290ac6-3028-5493-bce7-36d16bbccf6c', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76290ac6-3028-5493-bce7-36d16bbccf6c'}, {'abstract': 'Marshmallows are having a moment.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/samurai-shopper-pillow-talk/', 'snippet': 'Marshmallows are having a moment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Winter white is always chic. With my off-white faux fur from Portobello Road, my ivory crepe de chine shirt from Gucci and Ann Demeulemeester’s rabbity-white leggings, the Michelin Man’s got nothing on me.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Samurai Shopper | Pillow Talk', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T17:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By S.S. Fair', 'person': [{'firstname': 'S.', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Fair', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bc82884-0bc2-5f33-849e-afebd70158d3', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bc82884-0bc2-5f33-849e-afebd70158d3'}, {'abstract': 'Often care is better at nonprofit nursing facilities. A new study suggests an explanation.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/happier-staffers-at-nonprofit-nursing-homes/', 'snippet': 'Often care is better at nonprofit nursing facilities. A new study suggests an explanation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading between the lines of a study published in The Gerontologist recently, I noticed another vote for nonprofit nursing homes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Happier Staffers at Nonprofit Nursing Homes', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T17:01:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad44e135-4b4a-5d69-9e1d-c357597d0ea1', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad44e135-4b4a-5d69-9e1d-c357597d0ea1'}, {'abstract': 'Barbara Lynch grew up in a tough environment and started cooking for pay at age 13. She now runs nine culinary businesses in Boston that produce $19 million in annual revenue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/business/smallbusiness/after-south-boston-opening-a-restaurant-didnt-seem-so-risky.html', 'snippet': 'Barbara Lynch grew up in a tough environment and started cooking for pay at age 13. She now runs nine culinary businesses in Boston that produce $19 million in annual revenue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Emerging from the housing projects of South Boston where James Bulger, known as Whitey, once ruled, Barbara Lynch has become one of Boston’s most successful and celebrated restaurateurs.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/05/business/05sbiz/05sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/05/business/05sbiz/05sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/05/business/05sbiz/05sbiz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/05/business/05sbiz/05sbiz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/05/business/05sbiz/05sbiz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After South Boston, a Restaurant Was Easy', 'kicker': 'Conversations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After South Boston, A Restaurant Was Easy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynch, Barbara', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T17:02:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Rifkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rifkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/d60386cf-1f28-5b38-8a41-e9ec813a29ad', 'word_count': 1246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d60386cf-1f28-5b38-8a41-e9ec813a29ad'}, {'abstract': 'These days one of the most frequently asked questions in our house — and often the toughest to answer — is the “Can I go …” question.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/negotiating-my-13-year-olds-social-life/', 'snippet': 'These days one of the most frequently asked questions in our house — and often the toughest to answer — is the “Can I go …” question.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week my almost-14-year-old son, Nathaniel, asked me if I would let him go to a New Year’s Eve party. Even though he had yet to be invited to one — plans were still taking shape — he was certain of one thing: He was too old and too cool to hang out at home with his mom, dad and grandma for dinner and a movie to ring in the new year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Negotiating My 13-Year-Old’s Social Life', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T17:06:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randye Hoder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randye', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6d8bd6b-52b7-5f62-94a5-24cb38c45652', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6d8bd6b-52b7-5f62-94a5-24cb38c45652'}, {'abstract': 'The arrangement to sell copies The Wall Street Journal Europe through bulk sales at seminars was “complex,” Audit Bureau of Circulations in Britain found, but also permissible. The newspaper has since stopped the practice.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/04/audit-group-accepts-circulation-practice-of-the-journal-in-europe/', 'snippet': 'The arrangement to sell copies The Wall Street Journal Europe through bulk sales at seminars was “complex,” Audit Bureau of Circulations in Britain found, but also permissible. The newspaper has since stopped the practice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The British auditing group investigating a complex circulation practice at The Wall Street Journal Europe, which led to the resignation of the paper’s publisher in October, said Wednesday that the practice complied with its official newspaper circulation reporting standards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Audit Group Accepts Circulation Practice of The Journal in Europe', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Langhoff, Andrew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Audit Bureau of Circulations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal Europe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T17:08:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f94651a4-2f3b-542f-8624-b74852a627e0', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f94651a4-2f3b-542f-8624-b74852a627e0'}, {'abstract': 'The elements of makeup organization: home base, what you take with you and how you take it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/fashion/makeup-essentials-portable-and-neatly-packed.html', 'snippet': 'The elements of makeup organization: home base, what you take with you and how you take it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR many of us, the new year brings with it a frenzy of resolutions to streamline and organize one’s life. Let’s not overlook the pocketbook. More specifically: the makeup in one’s pocketbook.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption':