like the Gossip, Peaches and CSS, and making their mark at MoMA PS1, Deitch Projects and the Hole. Now Critcheloe has planted his feet firmly in New York City to, we assume, turn the art of the music video forever on its head.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lowdown | Cody Critcheloe', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-04T19:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/984326c7-95e0-54dd-bad3-91af93030002', 'word_count': 672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/984326c7-95e0-54dd-bad3-91af93030002'}, {'abstract': 'The online retailer Warby Parker tries its hand at a pop-up shop in SoHo, inside a yurt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/fashion/warby-parkers-pop-up-store-offers-eyeglass-frames.html', 'snippet': 'The online retailer Warby Parker tries its hand at a pop-up shop in SoHo, inside a yurt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON New Year’s Eve, Andrew Peters, a public relations associate from Washington, was on the prowl for new eyeglasses, something to mix up his conservative day-to-day look. So a friend took him to Warby Parker, a pop-up store in SoHo where prescription glasses are priced mostly at $95, instead of several hundred dollars.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/05/fashion/05WARBY_SPAN/05WARBY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/