s a potential ticket buyer say, “I’ve got to see that”? Television commercials are a mainstay, but expensive. Trailers — which can run longer than 15 or 30 seconds and be widely distributed — are increasingly being used to promote theater, but producing them is still a challenge in terms of style, access and cost.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trailers to Tempt the Theatergoer', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ghost the Musical (Play)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coil Festival', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hodges, Drew', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Naked Angels', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SpotCo', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Steppenwolf Theatre Co', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Temporary Distortion', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vineyard Theater', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T16:20:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Hedli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hedli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7753ff4d-0ad3-5537-abc3-0f9489b06a9c', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7753ff4d-0ad3-5537-abc3-0f9489b06a9c'}, {'abstract': 'The sorriest tears from Wednesday night’s Orange Bowl came from anyone who remembers a college bowl system when these games were grand spectacles and not complete shams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/sports/at-the-orange-bowl-a-crying-game.html', 'snippet': 'The sorriest tears from Wednesday night’s Orange Bowl came from anyone who remembers a college bowl system when these games were grand spectacles and not complete shams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be interesting to calculate when exactly in West Virginia’s absurd 70-33 victory over Clemson in the Orange Bowl Wednesday night that most of the weeping began. No, not by Clemson’s supporters, who were probably too numb to weep. And not by the poor bowl mascot Obie, because we know exactly when the walking orange got turned into a tackling dummy after one Mountaineer touchdown. And not by the Big East, whose sobbing has been going on all through the conference realignment fiasco that is about to turn it into the Big Whatever.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/sports/06leading1/06leading1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/06/sports/06leading1/06leading1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 383}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/sports/06leading1/06leading1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/sports/06leading1/06leading1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/06/sports/06leading1/06leading1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Orange Bowl, a Crying Game', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At the Orange Bowl, a Crying Game', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bowl Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T16:20:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3f56b28-a4c6-502a-81c0-71cc3ec0b60d', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3f56b28-a4c6-502a-81c0-71cc3ec0b60d'}, {'abstract': 'A new plan for American armed forces, driven by $450 billion in cuts, adds attention to threats in Asia but continues a strong presence in the Middle East.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/us/obama-at-pentagon-to-outline-cuts-and-strategic-shifts.html', 'snippet': 'A new plan for American armed forces, driven by $450 billion in cuts, adds attention to threats in Asia but continues a strong presence in the Middle East.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama has for the first time put his own stamp on an all-encompassing American military policy by turning from the grinding ground wars that he inherited from the Bush administration and refocusing on what he described as a smaller, more agile force across Asia, the Pacific and the Middle East.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/us/06pentagon/06pentagon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/06/us/06pentagon/06pentagon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/us/06pentagon/06obama-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/us/06pentagon/06pentagon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/06/us/06pentagon/06pentagon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Puts His Stamp on Strategy for a Leaner Military', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Strategy for a Leaner Military, With Obama Taking the Lead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T16:24:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Bumiller and Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bumiller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d43c2340-0b21-517a-b0df-7639b9699af8', 'word_count': 927, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d43c2340-0b21-517a-b0df-7639b9699af8'}, {'abstract': 'There are many tears when you drop your child off at college. But parents do not cry when their children go back to school in January after a month at home. We are relieved.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/clashing-with-college-children-over-holiday-break/', 'snippet': 'There are many tears when you drop your child off at college. But parents do not cry when their children go back to school in January after a month at home. We are relieved.', 'lead_paragraph': '“What’s the definition of mixed emotions?” goes the joke I heard as a young man. “Your mother-in-law driving off a cliff in your new Cadillac.” LOL.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Clashing With College Children Over Holiday Break', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T16:30:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Schembari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schembari', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64a6bd0d-fd9f-510d-be80-cfd3caef1ea8', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64a6bd0d-fd9f-510d-be80-cfd3caef1ea8'}, {'abstract': 'Sori Yanagi, who died on Dec. 25 in Tokyo at the age of 96, designed a vast array of products, from flatware and furniture to cars, subway stations and even the torch for the 1972 Sapporo Winter Olympics.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/seeing-things-sori-yanagis-everyday-beauty/', 'snippet': 'Sori Yanagi, who died on Dec. 25 in Tokyo at the age of 96, designed a vast array of products, from flatware and furniture to cars, subway stations and even the torch for the 1972 Sapporo Winter Olympics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Japanese designer Sori Yanagi, who died on Dec. 25 in Tokyo at the age of 96, is best known for his 1956 Butterfly Stool. It is both elegant and utterly simple: two curved pieces of molded plywood are held together through compression and tension by a single brass rod. The stool’s graceful shape recalls a butterfly’s wings, and has also been compared to the form of torii, the traditional Shinto shrine gates. According to Matilda McQuaid, the deputy curatorial director at the Cooper-Hewitt National Design Museum, the stool “epitomizes Yanagi’s approach to design. He loved traditional Japanese crafts and was dedicated to the modernist principles of simplicity, practicality and tactility that are associated with Alvar Aalto, Charles and Ray Eames, and Le Corbusier.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Things | Sori Yanagi’s Everyday Beauty', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T16:30:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b6d54b9-ab5c-5849-a74d-8e006b4187af', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b6d54b9-ab5c-5849-a74d-8e006b4187af'}, {'abstract': 'Could invasive Burmese pythons hitch a ride to the Florida Keys?', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/saltwater-may-not-deter-floridas-pythons/', 'snippet': 'Could invasive Burmese pythons hitch a ride to the Florida Keys?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The python, a staple creature in the tales of Rudyard Kipling’s India, is poised to move to Ernest Hemingway’s Florida Keys, and a new study by scientists at the United States Geological Survey indicates that it may happen sooner than later.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saltwater May Not Deter Florida’s Pythons', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snakes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Park Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Geological Survey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Everglades (Fla)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida Keys', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Naples (Fla)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T16:40:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5300c31f-7821-58f1-814f-1b7cd2b59bd6', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5300c31f-7821-58f1-814f-1b7cd2b59bd6'}, {'abstract': 'Questions about bringing plus-ones and who pays for hair and makeup.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/fashion/weddings/the-well-mannered-wedding.html', 'snippet': 'Questions about bringing plus-ones and who pays for hair and makeup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paying for the Salon', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/11/fashion/weddings/peggypost/peggyp