esign the Pines Pavilion', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Renfro, Charles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Diller Scofidio & Renfro', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fire Island (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T22:59:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a630320-9dfe-5ea3-9fc8-ec11f4dfc84f', 'word_count': 382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a630320-9dfe-5ea3-9fc8-ec11f4dfc84f'}, {'abstract': 'The Success Academy charter school network is promoting its new school in Cobble Hill with a bus shelter advertisement, and by hanging leaflets on doors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/01/05/success-academy-blitzes-cobble-hill/', 'snippet': 'The Success Academy charter school network is promoting its new school in Cobble Hill with a bus shelter advertisement, and by hanging leaflets on doors.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Success Academy Blitzes Cobble Hill', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T23:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By BETH FERTIG', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'FERTIG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a200d77-d037-5d8f-9425-fd699a8d3e43', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a200d77-d037-5d8f-9425-fd699a8d3e43'}, {'abstract': 'A former dancer goes on a quest to restore her battered heels before slipping into this season’s must-have mules.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/vain-glorious-the-happy-heel/', 'snippet': 'A former dancer goes on a quest to restore her battered heels before slipping into this season’s must-have mules.', 'lead_paragraph': '“These are not aristocratic feet. I’m here to tell you these are working feet,” says Park Avenue’s go-to podiatrist, Suzanne Levine, after a quick examination of my not-so-pretty pieds. In all fairness, my childhood and adolescence were spent studying ballet. Anyone who’s worn a pair of pointe shoes will confirm that dancing in them is a kind of quiet torture that ceases to bother only after you’ve grown numb to the pain. Your feet never really recover from the trauma. I sought out Levine and a few other foot gurus after determining spring’s must-have shoes: the bejeweled slip-ons from Oscar de la Renta, the velvet clogs from Miu Miu and the heel-less metallic numbers with super-pointy toes from Louis Vuitton, among others. The problem? All of these options are of the 18th-century mule variety. In other words, they leave your heels fully exposed, and mine, still callused and battered to a point seemingly beyond return, were in no position to be naked.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | The Happy Heel', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T23:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4202de44-2e70-50cb-a33e-a83308ca7f2d', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4202de44-2e70-50cb-a33e-a83308ca7f2d'}, {'abstract': 'The world’s top 40 bull riders are bracing for a showdown at Madison Square Garden, and Urban Oysters offers a tour of the Brooklyn Navy Yard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/nyregion/bull-riders-at-madison-square-garden-the-brooklyn-navy-yard.html', 'snippet': 'The world’s top 40 bull riders are bracing for a showdown at Madison Square Garden, and Urban Oysters offers a tour of the Brooklyn Navy Yard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a new year. Who knows where it will take you? For this week at least, the Miser does, with a hodgepodge of cheap fun to guide you from the Brooklyn Navy Yard of the mid-19th century to the future of bull riding in the 21st. Ah, the possibilities. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ARTSMISER_SPAN/06ARTSMISER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ARTSMISER_SPAN/06ARTSMISER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ARTSMISER_SPAN/06ARTSMISER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ARTSMISER_SPAN/06ARTSMISER_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ARTSMISER_SPAN/06ARTSMISER_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bull Riders at Madison Square Garden; the Brooklyn Navy Yard', 'kicker': 'Weekend Miser', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bull Riding', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T23:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Lee Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e5f499d-e515-5893-8029-9d7be8ab679f', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e5f499d-e515-5893-8029-9d7be8ab679f'}, {'abstract': 'Bin-It and Jugglebox Moving both recently entered the New York moving market, delivering stacks of reusable plastic moving boxes and picking them up after the move is complete.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/realestate/rent-a-box-services-make-moving-easier-for-some.html', 'snippet': 'Bin-It and Jugglebox Moving both recently entered the New York moving market, delivering stacks of reusable plastic moving boxes and picking them up after the move is complete.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR Sara Schmidt, an actress, moving to a new apartment used to involve a tiresome quest for cardboard boxes. She picked up castoffs from the street and canvassed her local liquor stores for others — and even then, she still found herself buying expensive new boxes and supplies like packing tape. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/bin-it/bin-it-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 600, 'subType': 