{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T23:22:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb251dcb-defb-56ce-83f4-c12dd19fea9c', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb251dcb-defb-56ce-83f4-c12dd19fea9c'}, {'abstract': 'In riffing on the multiple reasons that I’m enjoying the Republican nomination process as a supremely entertaining piece of transmedia storytelling, I made passing mention of Rick Perry’s Unpopular Opinions, a one-topic Tumblr inspired by the Texas governor’s ad titled “Strong.”', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/presidential-primary-lulz/', 'snippet': 'In riffing on the multiple reasons that I’m enjoying the Republican nomination process as a supremely entertaining piece of transmedia storytelling, I made passing mention of Rick Perry’s Unpopular Opinions, a one-topic Tumblr inspired by the Texa...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In riffing on the multiple reasons that I’m enjoying the Republican nomination process as a supremely entertaining piece of transmedia storytelling, I made passing mention of Rick Perry’s Unpopular Opinions, a one-topic Tumblr inspired by the Texas governor’s ad titled “Strong.” In that spot, Perry asserted that “you know there’s something wrong in this country when gays can serve openly in the military, but our kids can’t openly celebrate Christmas.” The Internet found that argument laughably suspect, and in addition to making the YouTube version of the commercial one of the most “disliked” videos ever, responded with a barrage of interactive/remix mockery. The aforementioned Tumblr is a conspicuous example, and is made of up GIFs of the same self-assured, Carhartt-clad Perry from the commercial, captioned with phrases like “ ‘The Wire’ was a dumb show” and “Kim Jong the Second was a humane leader.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Presidential Primary Lulz', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T23:23:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Walker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4830b35a-26de-5848-a07a-27c260f118d4', 'word_count': 569, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4830b35a-26de-5848-a07a-27c260f118d4'}, {'abstract': 'In “Roadie,” a film by Michael Cuesta, a middle-aged rock ’n’ roll Sherpa goes home to Forest Hills.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/movies/roadie-directed-by-michael-cuesta-review.html', 'snippet': 'In “Roadie,” a film by Michael Cuesta, a middle-aged rock ’n’ roll Sherpa goes home to Forest Hills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About an hour into “Roadie,” a film about a middle-aged rock ’n’ roll Sherpa who’s lost his job and moorings, there’s a jumpy, viscerally uncomfortable scene in a motel room — a tempest fueled by booze and coke, old grudges and new hurts — that’s so strong and beautifully played that it lifts the movie out of its doldrums. A roadie, Jimmy Testagross (a gruffly tender Ron Eldard), has crawled back to his childhood home in Forest Hills, Queens, after being cut free by his longtime employer, Blue Oyster Cult. From his wounded look it seems as if he weren’t only fired, but also stripped of his identity. Refusing to admit the truth, even to himself, he tells everyone he’s the band’s manager.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ROADIE1_SPAN/ROADIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ROADIE1_SPAN/ROADIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ROADIE1_SPAN/ROADIE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ROADIE1_SPAN/ROADIE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/06ROADIE1_SPAN/ROADIE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ejected From a Job With a Band, Landing Back With Mom in Queens', 'kicker': "Movie Review | 'Roadie'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ejected From a Job With a Band, Landing Back With Mom in Queens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eldard, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cannavale, Bobby', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuesta, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hennessy, Jill', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T23:25:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6790d177-8e67-5186-afe0-49fde00cbb08', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6790d177-8e67-5186-afe0-49fde00cbb08'}, {'abstract': 'The Top 100 tours in North America last year sold $2.34 billion in tickets, an increase of 6.3 percent. U2 led the way with $156 million in gross ticket sales, followed by Taylor Swift and Kenny Chesney.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/05/concert-revenue-was-up-in-2011-led-by-u2/', 'snippet': 'The Top 100 tours in North America last year sold $2.34 billion in tickets, an increase of 6.3 percent. U2 led the way with $156 million in gross ticket sales, followed by Taylor Swift and Kenny Chesney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After one of its worst years in recent history, the concert industry rebounded slightly in 2011. But with consumers buying fewer tickets, the industry was still down from its peak in 2009.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Concert Revenue Was Up in 2011, Led by U2', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pollstar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'U2', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-05T23:38:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisa