ne': {'main': 'Jan. 6, 1919 | Theodore Roosevelt Dies', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T09:32:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71280df8-b656-53b6-8089-4229c1552eeb', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71280df8-b656-53b6-8089-4229c1552eeb'}, {'abstract': 'Who are the Me N Ma Girls, and what challenges do they face?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/pop-pioneers/', 'snippet': 'Who are the Me N Ma Girls, and what challenges do they face?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO is Nicole May, and WHO are the Me N Ma Girls? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pop Pioneers', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T09:43:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/731500f2-cb81-543d-8288-6e776252e444', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/731500f2-cb81-543d-8288-6e776252e444'}, {'abstract': 'What prompted Joshua Pusey, a lawyer and the inventor of the modern toboggan, to patent a folded piece of cardboard carrying matches and a striker?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/magazine/who-made-that-matchbook.html', 'snippet': 'What prompted Joshua Pusey, a lawyer and the inventor of the modern toboggan, to patent a folded piece of cardboard carrying matches and a striker?', 'lead_paragraph': 'No one knows what exactly prompted Joshua Pusey, a lawyer and the inventor of the modern toboggan, to patent a folded piece of cardboard carrying matches and a striker in 1892. Though legend suggests it had more to do with vanity than with safety. “I heard that he was a patent attorney and always wearing suits and vests,” explains Mark Bean, president of the match division of New Hampshire’s D. D. Bean & Sons Company, which has been in the trade since 1938. “And that the box of wooden matches was bulky and awkward to carry around.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08wmt_span/08wmt_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 248, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08wmt_span/08wmt_span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 248}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08wmt_span/08wmt_span-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 424, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08wmt_span/08wmt_span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 424, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08wmt_span/mag-08WMT-t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08wmt_span/mag-08WMT-t_CA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08wmt_span/08wmt_span-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Striking on the Modern Matchbook', 'kicker': 'Who Made That?', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '(Matchbook)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum and Dana Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3238b09-1b2a-563b-81e3-66c62cc06e07', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3238b09-1b2a-563b-81e3-66c62cc06e07'}, {'abstract': 'Paired with a photograph, something as insignificant as a Twitter post becomes almost poetic.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/anonymous-tributes-to-anonymous-people/', 'snippet': 'Paired with a photograph, something as insignificant as a Twitter post becomes almost poetic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Around noon yesterday, the Twitter user @Twiiitooo took to a keyboard — most likely on a smartphone — and, as he’d done 11 times already that morning, shared a thought with his more than 400 followers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Anonymous Tributes to Anonymous People’', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Maryland Institute College of Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltimore (Md)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rochester (NY)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T10:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerri MacDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f7157cc-8f71-53a8-9afd-7addb557422f', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f7157cc-8f71-53a8-9afd-7addb557422f'}, {'abstract': 'Would your personal symbol reflect you as you are? Specific interests, talents or skills? Your ideal self? Your personal “brand”? Or your hopes and dreams?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/what-would-your-personal-mascot-be/', 'snippet': 'Would your personal symbol reflect you as you are? Specific interests, talents or skills? Your ideal self? Your personal “brand”? Or your hopes and dreams?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mr. Met, the mascot for the New York Mets baseball team, is a popular team mascot and symbol. If a mascot were designed for you, what would it be, and why? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Would Your Personal Mascot Be?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T10:22:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dd7abd5-092f-58cd-b0d6-6ead7ae1dd82', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dd7abd5-092f-58cd-b0d6-6ead7ae1dd82'}, {'abstract': 'American politicians don’t take much prodding to feel a special love for Israel. And listening to voters in Iowa, the love just got stronger.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/an-israeli-in-iowa/', 'snippet': 'American politicians don’t take much prodding to feel a special love for Israel. And listening to voters in Iowa, the love just got stronger.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DES MOINES, Iowa — On Tuesday, at 6:55 p.m. sharp, I come to the school gym that serves as Precinct 025 to meet Adam (“Just call me Adam” Adam), torchbearer of the Rick Perry for President campaign. “Where are you from?” he asks. “Israel,” I say. “You mean the nation of Israel?” “Both the nation and the land but mostly the State of Israel.” His candidate, Texas Governor Perry, keeps talking about how important it is to defend Israel, to stand with Israel. Adam will make his contribution to this important cause by defending the visiting Israeli journalist from whatever menace caucus night might hold.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Israeli in Iowa', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bachmann, Michele M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T10:30:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shmuel Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shmuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d28a5e43-93f9-5c8b-a131-5da730a4c5da', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d28a5e43-93f9-5c8b-a131-5da730a4c5da'}, {'abstract': 'After upgrading to the latest version of Skype, check your computer to make sure your system’s drivers are all up to date.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/qa-improving-call-quality-after-a-skype-update/', 'snippet': 'After upgrading to the latest version of Skype, check your computer to make sure your system’s drivers are all up to date.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Improving Call Quality After a Skype Update', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internet Telephony', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Skype Technologies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T10:49:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d30af60-1f24-52d6-ace3-932b93a05d46', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d30af60-1f24-52d6-ace3-932b93a05d46'}, {'abstract': 'Knedliky Laznicka (Dukelskych hrdinu 15), the city’s first shop dedicated to dumplings, opened last summer, quickly developing a loyal following that lines up for blocks to buy them.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/in-prague-traditional-dumplings-take-over-a-menu/', 'snippet': 'Knedliky Laznicka (Dukelskych hrdinu 15), the city’s first shop dedicated to dumplings, opened last summer, quickly developing a loyal following that lines up for blocks to buy them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most Czechs can’t live without their dumplings, or knedliky. The dish, which is akin to sliced bread,\xa0 usually accompanies a pork or another meat entrée and is a staple on almost all restaurant menus, or at least all the ones offering “original Czech cuisine” in Prague.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Prague, Traditional Dumplings Take Over a Menu', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Prague (Czech Republic)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T11:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': "By Julie O'Shea", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Shea", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed1391ef-0efe-57e0-98de-26776ddc77bb', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed1391ef-0efe-57e0-98de-26776ddc77bb'}, {'abstract': 'The lack of uniformity and the variety of options in American health-insurance plans adds significantly to costs, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/what-price-pluralism-in-health-insurance/', 'snippet': 'The lack of uniformity and the variety of options in American health-insurance plans adds significantly to costs, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Uwe E. Reinhardt is an economics professor at Princeton. He has some financial interests in the health care field.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Price Pluralism in Health Insurance?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T11:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Uwe E. Reinhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Uwe', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Reinhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/517a1dd2-c595-5f09-b7bd-89f6cdcc6ee0', 'word_count': 1105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/517a1dd2-c595-5f09-b7bd-89f6cdcc6ee0'}, {'abstract': 'The tablet is getting upgrades, production will be open to more manufacturers.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/aakash-the-view-from-the-ministry-of-human-resources/', 'snippet': 'The tablet is getting upgrades, production will be open to more manufacturers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'India Ink, like many other publications, has been following the low-cost Aakash tablet with great interest. We asked the Ministry of Human Resource Development, led by Kapil Sibal, who has spearheaded the project, for his view of how it is going. The Ministry’s full reply is below: ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aakash: The View from the Ministry of Human Resource Development', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T11:18:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Timmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Timmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/548e3979-0917-59f1-afdb-d1eba9fbff9f', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/548e3979-0917-59f1-afdb-d1eba9fbff9f'}, {'abstract': 'The Nook is selling well, although Amazon’s Kindle products still dominate the e-reader market. Many people are skeptical that the plan will help the retailer in the long run as it tries to defend itself the Kindle and Apple’s iPad.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/daily-report-barnes-noble-considers-spinning-off-its-nook-unit/', 'snippet': 'The Nook is selling well, although Amazon’s Kindle products still dominate the e-reader market. Many people are skeptical that the plan will help the retailer in the long run as it tries to defend itself the Kindle and Apple’s iPad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barnes & Noble, the nation’s largest book chain, said on Thursday that it was considering spinning off its Nook e-reader division in an effort to help the nascent — and expensive — digital business grow, report Julie Bosman and Michael J. de La Merced in The New York Times.They write that while the company has made quick work of capturing almost 30 percent of the e-book market in two years, the rise of digital reading has increased the pressure on it to devise a winning long-term strategy against Amazon, which still dominates e-book sales.Sales of Nook devices for the nine-week holiday period ending Dec. 31 were up 70 percent over the same period a year earlier, Barnes & Noble said Thursday, while all Nook-related sales, including devices, accessories and digital content, were up 43 percent.Brian X. Chen, technology reporter for The Times, writes in an accompanying article that the Apple iPad has yet to wipe out the e-readers like the Nook, though that was the popular prediction. He also reports that I.H.S. iSuppli, a research company, estimates that Barnes & Noble has 13 percent of the e-reader market after two years in the business, versus 67 percent for Amazon.The Epicenter blog noted:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption'