a': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Worst Field Since …', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T15:38:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53158af1-2697-5501-acea-213a2e213252', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53158af1-2697-5501-acea-213a2e213252'}, {'abstract': 'The chief of Gawker Media, and king of traffic-trawling online journalism, sent a memo to the staff extolling the virtues of well-turned narratives and “authentic opinion.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/nick-denton-relentless-seeker-of-online-traffic-turns-sentimental-about-writing/', 'snippet': 'The chief of Gawker Media, and king of traffic-trawling online journalism, sent a memo to the staff extolling the virtues of well-turned narratives and “authentic opinion.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nick Denton, chief of Gawker Media, has been both praised and vilified for his relentless focus on user traffic in running Gawker, Deadspin, Jezebel, Lifehacker and other sites he owns. But in a year-in-review/preview message to his staff, Mr. Denton spends a fair amount of pixels on the pure virtues of well-turned narratives, traffic be damned. (He also opens with a quote from Steve Jobs, which seems surprisingly corporate and conventional, but Jobs-mania apparently has no bounds.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nick Denton, Relentless Seeker of Online Traffic, Turns Sentimental About Writing', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Denton, Nick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gawker Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gawker.com', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T15:41:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e43a3e6b-f274-5fb2-9772-83af08d217f8', 'word_count': 1331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e43a3e6b-f274-5fb2-9772-83af08d217f8'}, {'abstract': '“The Artist” may have an advantage at the SAG Awards thanks to its large cast of what are known as day players.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/maybe-it-should-be-called-the-artists/', 'snippet': '“The Artist” may have an advantage at the SAG Awards thanks to its large cast of what are known as day players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — For all the bridesmaids in “Bridesmaids,” and all the housemaids in “The Help,” the most actor-packed film in the race for the ensemble cast award from the Screen Actors Guild on Jan. 29 might be the one about a supposedly solitary silent movie star — that is, “The Artist.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maybe It Should Be Called ‘The Artists’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T15:46:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifie