ir of bracelets passed from mother to daughter tells of a deeper link — and loss.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/magazine/caitlin-flanagan-girl-land.html', 'snippet': 'A pair of bracelets passed from mother to daughter tells of a deeper link — and loss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My father was one of those charismatic professors who forever have a coterie of graduate students pressed around them. My mother was a nurse who left work to raise children. It was along these broad strokes of character that the general story of their marriage was understood: the absent-minded intellectual who never learned to drive and his capable wife who managed the practicalities of their lives. He was respected; she was loved. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08lives/mag-08Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 864, 'width': 864, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08lives/mag-08Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08lives/mag-08Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of Girl Land', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The End of Girl Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T15:52:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlin Flanagan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flanagan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cee3990f-f64f-5696-aab1-4d6f65017de2', 'word_count': 774, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cee3990f-f64f-5696-aab1-4d6f65017de2'}, {'abstract': 'Not after Michael Jackson, her father and electroconvulsive therapy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/magazine/carrie-fisher.html', 'snippet': 'Not after Michael Jackson, her father and electroconvulsive therapy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In your book “Shockaholic,” you write that you’ve been receiving electroconvulsive shock therapy to treat your chronic depression. I gather you had a treatment this morning. How do you feel?  Great. There’s no convulsions anymore. They shouldn’t even really call it electroshock. You’re put to sleep, they give you a medication like the one Harrison Ford used in “What Lies Beneath” when he put his wife in the tub and she can’t move a muscle.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08talk/08talk-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 507, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08talk/08talk-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 507, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08talk/08talk-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/magazine/08talk/08talk-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 's