you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE five boroughs of New York have more than 23,000 restaurants. The restaurants themselves draw the obsessive attention of critics, foodies, diners and health inspectors. But what of the tenants who live above them, the unsung army of city residents who associate home with the smell of sautéed clams and garlic, baking bread, lamb vindaloo? ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08COVER_SPAN/SUB-COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08COVER_SPAN/SUB-COVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08COVER_SPAN/SUB-COVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08COVER_SPAN/SUB-COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08COVER_SPAN/SUB-COVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Living Above the Stove', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Living Above the Stove', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:08:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af442c55-fab5-5fc4-aae0-e0cec7a21620', 'word_count': 1801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af442c55-fab5-5fc4-aae0-e0cec7a21620'}, {'abstract': 'Gritty stories set in Missouri’s Ozarks delve into death, love, family and neighborly disputes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/the-outlaw-album-stories-by-daniel-woodrell-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Gritty stories set in Missouri’s Ozarks delve into death, love, family and neighborly disputes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After eight novels centered mainly on the poor and criminal in his native Missouri Ozarks, including the much lauded “Winter’s Bone,” Daniel Woodrell has published his first story collection, “The Outlaw Album,” and for readers of both crime and literary fiction, there is much to celebrate. Woodrell writes about violence and dark deeds better than almost anyone in America today, in compact, musical prose that doesn’t dwell on visceral detail. An unerring craftsman, he can fully describe a murder in one rich sentence: “The first time he killed the man, Jepperson, an opinionated foreigner from Minnesota, he kept to simple Ozark tradition and used a squirrel rifle, bullet to the heart, classic and effective, though there were spasms of the limbs and even a lunge of big old Jepperson’s body that seemed like he was about to take a step, flee, but he died in stride and collapsed against a fence post.” Every story is loaded with gems like that.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/08POLLUCK/08POLLUCK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/08POLLUCK/08POLLUCK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/08POLLUCK/08POLLUCK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/08POLLUCK/08POLLUCK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/08POLLUCK/08POLLUCK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ozark Justice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ozark Justice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woodrell, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:08:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald Ray Pollock', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'Ray', 'lastname': 'Pollock', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9edceec-919f-5973-bc8b-873f3bcb9bcd', 'word_count': 566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9edceec-919f-5973-bc8b-873f3bcb9bcd'}, {'abstract': 'What finally led Copernicus to publish his sun-centered theory?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/a-more-perfect-heaven-how-copernicus-revolutionized-the-cosmos-by-dava-sobel-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'What finally led Copernicus to publish his sun-centered theory?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few scientists die secure in the knowledge that their greatest discoveries will outlive them, but some of those who missed out make you wince. Gregor Mendel and Johann Friedrich Mie\xadscher discovered, respectively, genes and DNA in the 1860s, yet both died obscure and unappreciated. Alfred Wegener’s fundamental theory of plate tectonics drew scorn until the 1950s — two decades after he died. Last year, the biologist Ralph M. Steinman died just three days before winning a Nobel Prize. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/books/review/Kean/Kean-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 804, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/23/books/review/Kean/Kean-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/23/books/review/Kean/Kean-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Copernicus’s Last Act', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Set the Earth Spinning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Copernicus, Nicolaus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sobel, Dava', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:08:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Kean', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kean', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b27059e-610d-5ebb-89de-b640923ef4a2', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b27059e-610d-5ebb-89de-b640923ef4a2'}, {'abstract': 'In Sara Levine’s first novel, a woman decides to adopt the “core values” from Robert Louis Stevenson’s classic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/treasure-island-by-sara-levine-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Sara Levine’s first novel, a woman decides to adopt the “core values” from Robert Louis Stevenson’s classic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m partial, I confess, to a book with exclamation points in its title. It’s the excitement, the urgency, the exuberance they bring to a page. Imagine if other people had used them: “War and Peace!!!” “The Breast!!!” You’d expect a completely different book.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Robert Louis Stevenson Can Change Your Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pirate Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levine, Sara', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevenson, Robert Louis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:10:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d4c7eea-0301-57e4-b499-6f839799b7f3', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d4c7eea-0301-57e4-b499-6f839799b7f3'}, {'abstract': 'Sara Paretsky’s latest V. I. Warshawski novel involves fans of vampire books, a murder and a right-wing news operation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/politics-as-blood-sport.html', 'snippet': 'Sara Paretsky’s latest V. I. Warshawski novel involves fans of vampire books, a murder and a right-wing news operation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What really matters in a Sara Paretsky mystery are the crimes behind the crimes — the corrupt politics, the class divisions, the economic inequities, the dirty business practices and all the other injustices that incite the wicked deeds we love to read about. BREAKDOWN (Putnam, $26.95) takes its first crack at a soft target: a cult series of lurid vampire novels that sends a group of impressionable preteenage girls into a graveyard to perform a giddy initiation ritual for their book club. But once V. I. Warshawski, the intrepid private eye who sees herself as “a street fighter, a product of the mills and ethnic wars of Chicago’s Steel City,” has rescued the girls from the trauma of discovering a murder victim, the true villains come into sharper focus.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/crime/crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 632, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/crime/crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/crime/crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Politics as Blood Sport', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Politics as Blood Sport', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Todd, C