ppet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Mark Ribowsky’s “Howard Cosell.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyond the Game', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beyond The Game', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:11:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/228a151b-7846-5e29-9994-48c9e6bdeea4', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/228a151b-7846-5e29-9994-48c9e6bdeea4'}, {'abstract': 'Independence Hall is a Unesco site, but not the White House. The Grand Canyon, yes. Niagara Falls, no. Inside the odd politics and big business of World Heritage sites.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/travel/whats-up-with-all-the-unesco-sites.html', 'snippet': 'Independence Hall is a Unesco site, but not the White House. The Grand Canyon, yes. Niagara Falls, no. Inside the odd politics and big business of World Heritage sites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WORLD HERITAGE is big business, bringing hordes of tourists to poor countries that can use the jobs and the cash. It can also overwhelm the very sites it is designed to protect with all the less-savory aspects of mass travel, from chain hotels and restaurants to the impact of thousands of sport-shoed feet treading on fragile ground.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/travel/08UNESCO5/08UNESCO5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/08/travel/08UNESCO5/08UNESCO5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/travel/08UNESCO5/0108UNESCO5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/travel/08UNESCO5/08UNESCO5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/travel/08UNESCO5/08UNESCO5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Does Unesco Recognition Mean, Exactly?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Does Unesco Recognition Mean, Exactly?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:11:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e814ede-1578-5e88-bb68-81957955bb50', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e814ede-1578-5e88-bb68-81957955bb50'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Will Hermes’s “Love Goes to Buildings on Fire.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/on-bass-tina-weymouth.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Will Hermes’s “Love Goes to Buildings on Fire.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Bass, Tina Weymouth', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Bass, Tina Weymouth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:12:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb00aa3a-093a-5631-8c3e-28bf0cc64296', 'word_count': 90, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb00aa3a-093a-5631-8c3e-28bf0cc64296'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SARGE: The Life and Times of Sargent Shriver, by Scott Stossel (Other Press, $19.95.) Idealistic and indefatigable, R. Sargent Shriver (1915-2011) created, among other organizations, the Peace Corps, Head Start and, with his wife, Eunice Kennedy Shriver, the Special Olympics. And he did so while steering a difficult course between independence and loyalty to the Kennedys. Stossel’s authorized biography marches through the 20th century — the Depression, World War II, the assassination of John F. Kennedy — tracing Shriver’s maturation from a young naval officer to a political and social figure who touched the lives of millions.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:13:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb4b233b-a39c-5b26-adfb-03ff40a74b93', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb4b233b-a39c-5b26-adfb-03ff40a74b93'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HAITI: The Aftershocks of History, by Laurent Dubois (Metropolitan/Holt, $32.) Foreign meddling, the lack of a democratic tradition, a humiliating American occupation and cold-war support of a brutal dictator all figure in a scholar’s well-written analysis.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice: Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:13:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b626a8bd-3ff0-5231-97c5-6d2a9f351708', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b626a8bd-3ff0-5231-97c5-6d2a9f351708'}, {'abstract': 'Disagreements over solutions to America’s problems have grown, or deteriorated, into profound differences over just what the problems are.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Disagreements over solutions to America’s problems have grown, or deteriorated, into profound differences over just what the problems are.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four years after a presidential election that looked, briefly, as if it might put an end to America’s partisan warfare, we find ourselves further embroiled in it, as reports from the run-up to the Iowa caucuses reminded us almost daily. Only now what once seemed disagreements over solutions have grown, or deteriorated, into profound differences over just what the problems are.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 619, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:14:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41d9866d-c2f8-5ad5-887d-c2bb8330cc9c', 'word_count': 261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41d9866d-c2f8-5ad5-887d-c2bb8330cc9c'}, {'abstract': 'Tami Hoag’s new mystery, which enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 11 this week, features a cameo from her real-life horse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Tami Hoag’s new mystery, which enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 11 this week, features a cameo from her real-life horse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GOD OF WINE: “She could always go to Bacchus and bury her face against his big, thick neck and cry, and he would nuzzle her hair and breathe his warm, velvety breath on her neck, and she would feel comforted.” Reading that sentence from Tami Hoag’s new suspense novel, “Down the Darkest Road,” which enters the hardcover list at No. 11 this week, it’s easy to recall that Hoag got her start as a romance writer before turning to the grisly mysteries that made her famous. It’s not what you might think, though. Bacchus is a horse, not a bare-chested hunk, and the 15-year-old girl crying into his neck is about to risk her life facing down the villain who abducted her sister. (Spoiler alert: the climax involves a hoof pick as a weapon.) What’s more, Bacchus isn’t even fictional. In her nonwriting life, Hoag is a championship dressage rider — she’s set novels in the equestrian world before — and Bacchus is the horse she’s competed with since a 2006 accident broke her back and led to the death of her previous riding partner, Coco Chanel. “Schooling my horses is like meditation to me,” Hoag writes on her official Web site. “This is the one time of day my mind is not occupied by my fictional characters.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/05/books/review/insidelist15/insidelist15-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 289, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/05/books/review/insidelist15/insidelist15-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/05/books/review/insidelist15/insidelist15-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside The List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:14:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9f2032e-bf9b-5e82-b4a0-e9431690b6f3', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9f2032e-bf9b-5e82-b4a0-e9431690b6f3'}, {'abstract': 'Conservationists try to help sustain the population by rehabilitating former captives and then releasing them into the wild in Indonesian Borneo.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/in-a-mini-eden-for-threatened-orangutans/', 'snippet': 'Conservationists try to help sustain the population by rehabilitating former captives and then releasing them into the wild in Indonesian Borneo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All life is precious, but the demise of the orangutan hits especially close to home. One of our closest relatives — human and orangutan genomes are 97 percent identical, a study published last year in Nature found — their population has dwindled to somewhere around 50,000. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mini-Eden for Endangered Orangutans', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nature Conservancy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Borneo (Indonesia)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indonesia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sumatra (Indonesia)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:15:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Isaacson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaacson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cf2a3f1-a23c-5dc6-b1f6-4e20c4667fca', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cf2a3f1-a23c-5dc6-b1f6-4e20c4667fca'}, {'abstract': 'A complex known as Gotham West will take up nearly all of the block from 44th to 45th Street, and from 10th to 11th Avenue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/realestate/on-the-far-west-side-mega-is-the-new-norm.html', 'snippet': 'A complex known as Gotham West will take up nearly all of the block from 44th to 45th Street, and from 10th to 11th Avenue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE far west side of Midtown, once notable mostly for being home to the Javits Center and the city’s few remaining stables, is quickly becoming known for huge residential projects like Silver Towers, with 1,360 units; MiMA, with more than 800 units as well as a hotel; and Mercedes House, with 850 units.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08RESWESTSIDE2/08RESWESTSIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/r