, Culdesac, on the Web, which allowed them to combine their skills and, as well, to significantly lower their learning curve.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/a-childrens-story/', 'snippet': 'Two months ago Hayley Parker-Barker and her husband introduced their boys’ clothing line, Culdesac, on the Web, which allowed them to combine their skills and, as well, to significantly lower their learning curve.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I first met Hayley Parker-Barker, in 2007, she was just Hayley Parker, a sweet, level-headed senior at F.I.T. and an intern at Narciso Rodriguez. She acquired Barker when she married her husband, Cameron, whom she has known since they were toddlers. And now they have a child of their own, Ender, and live near Salt Lake City, where Cameron is studying computer programming.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Children’s Story', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sneakers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Field, Patricia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodriguez, Narciso', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Moschino', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:21:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec13fe91-ceb7-50eb-8fe8-22c37fbfa471', 'word_count': 643, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec13fe91-ceb7-50eb-8fe8-22c37fbfa471'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas Frank argues that conservative politicians and President Obama are governing to please the plutocrats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/pity-the-billionaire-by-thomas-frank-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Thomas Frank argues that conservative politicians and President Obama are governing to please the plutocrats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas Frank is the thinking person’s Michael Moore. If Moore, the left-wing filmmaker, had Frank’s Ph.D. (in history from the University of Chicago), he might produce books like this one and Frank’s previous best seller, “What’s the Matter With Kansas?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/Kinsley-Noah/Kinsley-Noah-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/Kinsley-Noah/Kinsley-Noah-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/Kinsley-Noah/Kinsley-Noah-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/Kinsley-Noah/Kinsley-Noah-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/Kinsley-Noah/Kinsley-Noah-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rise of the American Oligarchy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bipolar America', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frank, Thomas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:22:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kinsley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kinsley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3e23766-4f04-55e4-a5e1-c3e12ae10531', 'word_count': 1555, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3e23766-4f04-55e4-a5e1-c3e12ae10531'}, {'abstract': 'A new book charts the decline of moderate Republicans, while another explores the Tea Party’s core beliefs about government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/books/review/books-about-conservatism-and-the-tea-party.html', 'snippet': 'A new book charts the decline of moderate Republicans, while another explores the Tea Party’s core beliefs about government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Half a century ago Republicans were a respectable but slightly boring presence on the political scene. Wary of excessive government, they were nonetheless reconciled to its expansion under Franklin D. Roosevelt and were mainly concerned with keeping it lean and solvent. Their beau idéal was Dwight D. Eisenhower, who in 1952 became the first Republican in 24 years to be elected president. His principal opponent for the nomination, Senator Robert Taft of Ohio, had opposed the New Deal and was a staunch isolationist who opposed supporting Britain in the first years of World War II. Eisenhower represented a more pragmatic strain of conservatism, internationalist when it came to foreign policy and willing to accept a larger government role at home. He called it “modern Republicanism.” With Eisenhower’s landslide re-election in 1956, his gospel looked like the future, at least for the G.O.P.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/books/review/08NOAH_RIGHT/08NOAH_RIGHT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 944, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Strange Death of the Republican Moderate', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bipolar America', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skocpol, Theda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldwater, Barry M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:24:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Timothy Noah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Timothy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Noah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/779404cf-ddc3-57a6-a536-ea264c3b906e', 'word_count': 1335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/779404cf-ddc3-57a6-a536-ea264c3b906e'}, {'abstract': 'James Nares’s unseen video work — “about things in motion and motion in things” — finally gets its due with a new show at the Paul Kasmin gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/artifacts-the-pendulum-swings/', 'snippet': 'James Nares’s unseen video work — “about things in motion and motion in things” — finally gets its due with a new show at the Paul Kasmin gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever wonder how it might feel to be a wrecking ball? The heavy, concrete sphere freely swinging through an empty TriBeCa alley in “Pendulum,” a Super 8 film short by the artist James Nares, could tell you: kind of blissful. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifacts | The Pendulum Swings', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:30:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Yablonsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yablonsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4ddb73c-985e-5d29-955b-c5df807610f1', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4ddb73c-985e-5d29-955b-c5df807610f1'}, {'abstract': 'The “Your Money” producer Nadia Taha shares her financial resolution for the coming year.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/06/resolution-resign-as-family-chief-financial-officer/', 'snippet': 'The “Your Money” producer Nadia Taha shares her financial resolution for the coming year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I got married in 2010 to a law student. We entered the marriage with different financial circumstances: I’d been working for a few years, paying taxes, saving for retirement and building credit. He was in school, focused primarily on covering his tuition. So when we merged our finances, it made sense based on our differing positions (and my fascination with personal finance) that I would continue to manage the money for us both.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Resolution: Resign as Family Chief Financial Officer', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Savings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:32:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nadia Taha', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nadia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taha', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/888c5ebc-15fd-50eb-901a-d0b1d9baaf8c', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/888c5ebc-15fd-50eb-901a-d0b1d9baaf8c'}, {'abstract': '“Downton Abbey,” created by Julian Fellowes, begins its second season on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/07/arts/television/downton-abbey-stokes-flames-for-season-2-review.html', 'snippet': '“Downton Abbey,” created by Julian Fellowes, begins its second season on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That second, irresistible slice of chocolate cake is almost always too much. It tastes good, of course, but the pleasurable surprise has dissipated. A sated palate turns discerning — the frosting seems a little too sweet, the base too rich. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/06/arts/downton/downton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleL