bold-vote-of-confidence.html', 'snippet': 'After the 9/11 attacks, Nancy Sheppard felt like staying put in Lower Manhattan and being a part of its recovery. She moved to an apartment in Battery Park City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN the first tower was hit on the blue-sky morning, Nancy Sheppard had just dropped off her 8-year-old son, Benjamin, at first grade and was hurrying down Hudson Street on her way to the offices of Viking Press, where she is vice president for marketing.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08HABI2_SPAN/habi-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08HABI2_SPAN/habi-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08HABI2_SPAN/habi-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08HABI2_SPAN/habi-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/realestate/08HABI2_SPAN/habi-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bold Vote of Confidence', 'kicker': 'Habitats | Lower Manhattan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bold Vote Of Confidence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'BATTERY PARK CITY (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-06T17:34:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Constance Rosenblum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Constance', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenblum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08cb3b80-55ee-5ac6-950c-026415703046', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08cb3b80-55ee-5ac6-950c-026415703046'}, {'abstract': 'The total return, after inflation, of stocks over 15-year periods seems to be repeating the pattern it mapped out decades ago. The compound annual total real return of the Standard & Poor’s 500-stock index peaked at more than 15 percent in 1999, and has since fallen to just 3 percent. That peak was similar to the earlier peak, reached in 1964. After that 1964 peak, the stock market lost momentum and then entered a bear market. By 1979, the market had failed to keep up with inflation over the previous 15 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/01/06/business/Similar-Cycles-for-Stocks.html', 'snippet': 'The total return, after inflation, of stocks over 15-year periods seems to be repeating the pattern it mapped out decades ago. The compound annual total real return of the Standard & Poor’s 500-stock index peaked at more than 15 percent in 1999, a...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The total return, after inflation, of stocks over 15-year periods seems to be repeating the pattern it mapped out decades ago. The compound annual total real return of the Standard & Poor’s 500-stock index peaked at more than 15 percent in 1999, and has since fallen to just 3 percent. That peak was similar to the earlier peak, reached in 1964. After that 1964 peak, the stock market lost momentum and then entered a bear market. By 1979, the market had failed to keep up with inflation over the previo