name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Werschkul and None None', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'None', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'None', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b1e1db2c-e0dc-5a3b-8be2-989c83b01e7b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b1e1db2c-e0dc-5a3b-8be2-989c83b01e7b'}, {'abstract': 'The yoga wear retailer said on Friday that Dennis J. Wilson, its founder and former chief executive, would leave his remaining executive role as chief innovation and branding officer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/07/business/lululemon-athleticas-founder-takes-a-step-back.html', 'snippet': 'The yoga wear retailer said on Friday that Dennis J. Wilson, its founder and former chief executive, would leave his remaining executive role as chief innovation and branding officer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lululemon Athletica, the yoga wear retailer, said on Friday that Dennis J. Wilson, its founder and former chief executive, would leave his remaining executive role as chief innovation and branding officer at the end of January. The company, which is based in Vancouver, British Columbia, offered no explanation about the move. He will remain as chairman. Christine Day, a former Starbucks executive, has been the company’s chief executive since 2008. Mr. Wilson, who is known as Chip, has said that he was greatly influenced by Ayn Rand and has used her books, which promote the idea of people living for their self-interest, to guide his business. Last November, Lululemon provoked an outcry from some customers after it used the opening line from Rand’s “Atlas Shrugged” on its shopping bags. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lululemon Athletica’s Founder Takes a Step Back', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | Company News', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lululemon Athletica’s Founder Takes a Step Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lululemon Athletica Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rand, Ayn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T01:50:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cce652aa-69e2-51f7-b2d9-1c7fff51f7b3', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cce652aa-69e2-51f7-b2d9-1c7fff51f7b3'}, {'abstract': 'Morris Claiborne, the soft-spoken player who won the Jim Thorpe Award for the country’s best defensive back, has found a way to star opposite Tyrann Mathieu, a Heisman finalist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/07/sports/ncaafootball/lsu-cornerback-is-overshadowed-but-not-overlooked.html', 'snippet': 'Morris Claiborne, the soft-spoken player who won the Jim Thorpe Award for the country’s best defensive back, has found a way to star opposite Tyrann Mathieu, a Heisman finalist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW ORLEANS — There is no Vince Young, Cam Newton or Tim Tebow in the Bowl Championship Series title game this year. The most recognizable star in Monday’s game between Louisiana State and Alabama is the flamboyant L.S.U. defensive back Tyrann Mathieu, who said Thursday that he researched the names of his opponents’ mothers in order to talk trash to them. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/07/sports/lsu-2-cty/lsu-2-cty-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 842, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/07/sports/lsu-2-cty/lsu-2-cty-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/07/sports/lsu-2-cty/lsu-2-cty-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'L.S.U. Cornerback Is Overshadowed, but Not Overlooked', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Overshadowed, but Not Overlooked', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mathieu, Tyrann', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louisiana State University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T01:51:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Thamel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thamel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc048660-8063-5398-81cd-7ddb5372c7ea', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc048660-8063-5398-81cd-7ddb5372c7ea'}, {'abstract': 'Due process in college athletics? Surely you jest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/07/opinion/nocera-ncaas-justice-syste