'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T03:24:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles M. Blow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7badd141-3432-5dd2-a78d-7aac7b9d3231', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7badd141-3432-5dd2-a78d-7aac7b9d3231'}, {'abstract': 'As touchdown celebrations have continued to evolve, many football players now engage in a not-so-new midair celebration move that so far has no name.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/07/sports/football/nfls-latest-end-zone-dance-lacks-a-name.html', 'snippet': 'As touchdown celebrations have continued to evolve, many football players now engage in a not-so-new midair celebration move that so far has no name.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the past four-plus decades of football, since a Giants receiver slammed the ball to the turf and invented the end-zone spike, the touchdown celebration has been subject to countless fads and elaborate variations. When viewers tune in to the first round of the N.F.L. playoffs on Saturday, they will most likely see the latest trend, which, however simple, is lacking in one critical way: it does not have a name.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/07/sports/TOUCHDOWN1/TOUCHDOWN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/