': 'Sugar Bowl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beamer, Frank', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoke, Brady', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Michigan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Orleans (La)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T04:06:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Rohan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc7250da-9729-5e2d-a95b-b4be1af73658', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc7250da-9729-5e2d-a95b-b4be1af73658'}, {'abstract': 'Lori Berenson, who served more than 15 years in prison in Peru for aiding a rebel group, returned to Lima after a court-sanctioned holiday visit to see her family in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/07/world/americas/peru-american-lori-berenson-returns-for-parole.html', 'snippet': 'Lori Berenson, who served more than 15 years in prison in Peru for aiding a rebel group, returned to Lima after a court-sanctioned holiday visit to see her family in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Lori Berenson, the New Yorker who served more than 15 years in prison in Peru for aiding a rebel group, returned to Lima on Thursday night after a court-sanctioned holiday visit to see her family in Manhattan. Ms. Berenson, who traveled with her young son, Salvador, was required to return to continue serving her parole, which calls for her to remain in the country until the end of her 20-year sentence in 2015. Many in Peru, where she is seen as a symbol of a bloody period of violence and terror, had questioned whether she would come back from the trip and were surprised when she returned well ahead of a Jan. 11 deadline. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/07/world/briefs-bereson/briefs-bereson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 637, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Peru: American Returns for Parole', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peru: American Returns for Parole', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peru', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berenson, Lori Helene', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T04:11:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Neuman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Neuman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/185fc2e7-bce3-5d40-95ad-d5fbe63039f8', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/185fc2e7-bce3-5d40-95ad-d5fbe63039f8'}, {'abstract': 'The Exxon Mobil Corporation and Kenneth Salazar, the interior secretary, agreed to settle a lawsuit in which Exxon challenged the government’s decision to cancel offshore leases.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/07/business/energy-environment/exxon-and-salazar-in-settlement-on-offshore-leases.html', 'snippet': 'The Exxon Mobil Corporation and Kenneth Salazar, the interior secretary, agreed to settle a lawsuit in which Exxon challenged the government’s decision to cancel offshore leases.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Exxon Mobil Corporation and Kenneth Salazar, the interior secretary, agreed to settle a lawsuit in which Exxon, the world’s largest publicly traded oil company, challenged the government’s decision to cancel offshore leases that may yield “billions of barrels of oil,” according to a court filing. The accord “will allow Exxon Mobil to develop this very large, but technically challenging, resource as quickly as possible using a phased approach,” Patrick McGinn, a spokesman for Exxon, said in an e-mail. Exxon sued on Aug. 12 over a ruling by the department that canceled Gulf of Mexico leases for the so-called Julia unit. The company and the government entered into a settlement agreement on Dec. 30, according to a filing Friday in United States District Court in Lake Charles, Louisiana. Exxon said in its complaint that it sought a suspension for its Julia leases in 2008 because of drilling complexity. It cited federal regulations that allow oil oil producers to suspend production in their fields, partly “to facilitate proper development of a lease.” The Interior Department denied the request in 2009, stating that the company“had not demonstrated a commitment to production” according to court papers. Unsuccessful appeals followed. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exxon and Salazar in Settlement on Offshore Leases', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | Company News', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exxon And Salazar In Settlement On Offshore Leases', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Exxon Mobil Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salazar, Ken', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Offshore Drilling and Exploration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gulf of Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T04:11:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Energy & Environment ', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb128722-8572-5796-8ca7-00d7f7e6da73', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb128722-8572-5796-8ca7-00d7f7e6da73'}, {'abstract': 'Councilman Eric Ulrich’s selection as Mitt Romney’s campaign chairman in the city seems intended to convince New Yorkers that not all the Republican presidential candidates are fringe-position lunatics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/nyregion/eric-ulrich-romneys-young-republican-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'Councilman Eric Ulrich’s selection as Mitt Romney’s campaign chairman in the city seems intended to convince New Yorkers that not all the Republican presidential candidates are fringe-position lunatics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Republican presidential candidate choosing a campaign chairman in New York City is essentially looking for the least-enfeebled arthritic to climb Everest. New Yorkers may elect Republican mayors, and even members of Congress, but we do not contribute to the victories of Republican presidents. Failure here is preordained.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/nyregion/08BIGCITY2_SPAN/08SUB-jpBIGCITY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/08/nyregion/08BIGCITY2_SPAN/08SUB-jpBIGCITY2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/nyregion/08BIGCITY2_SPAN/08SUB-jpBIGCITY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/nyregion/08BIGCITY2_SPAN/08SUB-jpBIGCITY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/nyregion/08BIGCITY2_SPAN/08SUB-jpBIGCITY2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mitt’s Young Republican, Up From the Streets', 'kicker': 'Big City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mitt’s Young Republican, Up From the Streets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ulrich, Eric A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T04:21:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd47d963-c180-57ed-9af2-ac146f6c76bf', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd47d963-c180-57ed-9af2-ac146f6c76bf'}, {'abstract': 'Restrictions on the broadcasting of television historical dramas in China that recently went into effect may soon also extend to Web sites, according to a report released by a business analysis company.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/07/world/asia/china-tv-limits-may-hit-the-web.html', 'snippet': 'Restrictions on the broadcasting of television historical dramas in China that recently went into effect may soon also extend to Web sites, according to a report released by a business analysis company.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Restrictions on the broadcasting of television historical dramas in China that went into effect on Jan. 1 may soon also extend to Web sites, according to a report released this week by Marbridge Consulting, a business analysis company. The report said that the agency regulating film and television, commonly known as Sarft, had said that as of Jan. 1, at least half of any satellite television network’s dramas must be set in the modern day. Last year, the agency expressed disapproval of time-travel shows, which often involve a modern-day protagonist’s being propelled back into a dynastic era. The Marbridge report said that of more than 50 dramas in production at Hengdian World Studios in Zhejiang Province, more than 30 historical costume dramas had halted because of the new restrictions. Sarft’s clampdown on historical dramas is occurring at the same time that it is limiting reality television programs, which it considers “vulgar.” The actions are part of a much wider push to better control culture that was announced last October by Communist Party leaders. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: TV Limits May Hit the Web', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: TV Limits May Hit the Web', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T04:28:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/56d09d51-da3e-5c05-94e2-2b3956274002', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56d09d51-da3e-5c05-94e2-2b3956274002'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in the Hudson Valley.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/08/nyregion/events-in-westchester.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in the Hudson Valley.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A guide to cultural and recreational events in the Hudson Valley. Items for the calendar should be sent at least three weeks in advance to westweek@nytimes.com.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Events in Westchester', 'kicker': 'Calendar', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Westchester', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-07T04:29:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of