ial': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/37457b03-cfec-59e1-b460-f8daab2e76a6', 'word_count': 1363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37457b03-cfec-59e1-b460-f8daab2e76a6'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t be a hazard. Master the etiquette of texting.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/opinion/texting-while-walking.html', 'snippet': 'Don’t be a hazard. Master the etiquette of texting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Navigating the sidewalks of New York City can be as challenging as any rushing sport, like football or rugby. \xa0But when your opponents are walking while text messaging, their field of view is impaired, and this can render a three-block walk to Starbucks somewhere between infuriating and life-threatening.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/opinion/09opdoc-sf-image/09opdoc-sf-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/08/opinion/09opdoc-sf-image/09opdoc-sf-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/opinion/09opdoc-sf-image/08opdoc-sf-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/08/opinion/09opdoc-sf-image/09opdoc-sf-image-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/08/opinion/09opdoc-sf-image/09opdoc-sf-image-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Texting While Walking', 'kicker': 'Op-Docs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T02:53:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey Neistat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Neistat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/51353bd7-a91b-5ccb-8c68-a1e38a3521d9', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51353bd7-a91b-5ccb-8c68-a1e38a3521d9'}, {'abstract': 'So where is that evidence that Mitt Romney or his party cares at all about equality of opportunity?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/opinion/krugman-americas-unlevel-field.html', 'snippet': 'So where is that evidence that Mitt Romney or his party cares at all about equality of opportunity?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month President Obama gave a speech invoking the spirit of Teddy Roosevelt on behalf of progressive ideals — and Republicans were not happy. Mitt Romney, in particular, insisted that where Roosevelt believed that “government should level the playing field to create equal opportunities,” Mr. Obama believes that “government should create equal outcomes,” that we should have a society where “everyone receives the same or similar rewards, regardless of education, effort and willingness to take risk.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarg