al Cushion Months Ahead of Deadline', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T12:21:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Landon Thomas Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Landon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/351e664b-7a76-5f97-a697-faf02d4d9269', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/351e664b-7a76-5f97-a697-faf02d4d9269'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers hope the discovery of a new species of mouse lemur will bring more attention to the struggle to conserve the island’s forests.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/meet-madagascars-new-lemur/', 'snippet': 'Researchers hope the discovery of a new species of mouse lemur will bring more attention to the struggle to conserve the island’s forests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Finding a new species may look easy on paper, the conservationist Jonah Ratsimbazafy says, but it takes a long, long time. He and his colleagues spent many a night in the jungle chasing after camouflaged hamster-size creatures hopping around the branches and brush. Their work paid off, though: after snagging 14 mouse lemurs, they were surprised to find they had a new species. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Meet Madagascar’s New Lemur', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Hered