n Sunset Park. Cabby, bartender and carpenter, to name a few. Turns out that last one – a 10-year stretch working with some sculptors turned contractors – gave him the insight for what would become a photography career, when he was hired in 1989 as staff photographer for the city’s Department of Housing Preservation and Development. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Documenting City at Its Worst, and Its Rebirth', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Racioppo, Larry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Housing Preservation and Development (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T20:09:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0cd010d-2aae-5935-b563-edd1ce8f94c7', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0cd010d-2aae-5935-b563-edd1ce8f94c7'}, {'abstract': 'The arts, and the benefits to the public they provide, sometimes gets lost, barely noticed by government. Fractured Atlas, a New York City-based multi-disciplinary arts service organization, is finding that by creating information and data services for its members and for city arts communities, it can also provide more effective advocacy for the arts to government. Fractured Atlas uses the New York Times’ District API to create and run these services and to link them to its arts advocacy mission.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/nyt-districts-api-helps-fractured-atlas-help-artists/', 'snippet': 'The arts, and the benefits to the public they provide, sometimes gets lost, barely noticed by government. Fractured Atlas, a New York City-based multi-disciplinary arts service organization, is finding that by creating information and data service...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The arts, and the benefits to the public they provide, sometimes gets lost, barely noticed by government. Fractured Atlas, a New York City-based multi-disciplinary arts service organization, is finding that by creating information and data services for its members among the city’s arts communities, it can also provide more effective advocacy for the arts to government. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'NYT Districts API helps Fractured Atlas help artists', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T20:10:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By BRAD STENGER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'STENGER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac13b1f6-83e4-58ab-98fb-efbb3e3efaaf', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac13b1f6-83e4-58ab-98fb-efbb3e3efaaf'}, {'abstract': 'At C.E.S., the world’s biggest computer chip maker, Intel, made it clear it plans to pour a lot of money and effort into turning ultrabooks into the next big computing phenomenon.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/intels-push-for-macbook-air-look-alikes-ultrabooks/', 'snippet': 'At C.E.S., the world’s biggest computer chip maker, Intel, made it clear it plans to pour a lot of money and effort into turning ultrabooks into the next big computing phenomenon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAS VEGAS — Last year, Intel began talking about a new category of superthin notebook computers called the Ultrabook. At this year’s International Consumer Electronics Show, the company, the world’s biggest maker of computer chips, made it clear that it planned to pour a 