ng fugues, which was everything there is to know about the matter, into “The Art of Fugue,” as he titled his collection of 14 fugues and 4 canons. Though Bach worked for years on the project, he never completed it: the final four-voice fugue was left unfinished at his death at 65 in 1750.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Primer on the Many-Layered Fugue', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Primer on the Many-Layered Fugue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orion String Quartet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Woodwind Quintet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Orion Quartet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T22:11:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5236dda1-0eca-583d-a908-76a16f57cd36', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5236dda1-0eca-583d-a908-76a16f57cd36'}, {'abstract': 'The New Jersey Symphony Orchestra, led by Jacques Lacombe, opened it second winter festival, called Fire, with a work by Alexander Scriabin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/arts/music/new-jersey-symphony-orchestra-in-newark-review.html', 'snippet': 'The New Jersey Symphony Orchestra, led by Jacques Lacombe, opened it second winter festival, called Fire, with a work by Alexander Scriabin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWARK — No hard, fast rule dictates that an orchestra has to engage in elaborate sleight of hand when presenting an unfamiliar work; still, there is something to be said for creating a useful context. A uniting theme, however basic, can provide a starting point for appraising a new or uncommon piece. Surrounding it with familiar music that is in some way related allows the audience to discover and apply its own connections.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/arts/symphony/symphony-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 974, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/arts/symphony/symphony-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/10/arts/symphony/symphony-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'All the Colors of a Scriabin Rarity That’s Meant to Be Seen as Well as Heard', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All the Colors of a Scriabin Rarity That’s Meant to Be Seen as Well as Heard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lacombe, Jacques', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T22:15:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dbe28ae-d174-5811-b708-034ab28dbeb0', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dbe28ae-d174-5811-b708-034ab28dbeb0'}, {'abstract': 'The tenor Roberto Alagna will take over the the title role in “Faust” on Monday night, which means he will be singing two major roles in two nights: on Tuesday he is scheduled to appear as Cavaradossi in the season’s opening performance of Puccini’s “Tosca.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/a-last-minute-substitution-for-faust/', 'snippet': 'The tenor Roberto Alagna will take over the the title role in “Faust” on Monday night, which means he will be singing two major roles in two nights: on Tuesday he is scheduled to appear as Cavaradossi in the season’s opening performance of Puccini...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The tenor Roberto Alagna is stepping in for an ailing Joseph Calleja in the title role of “Faust,” by Gounod, at the Metropolitan Opera on Monday evening, the Met said Monday afternoon. The last-minute substitution means that Mr. Alagna will be singing two major roles in two nights: on Tuesday he is scheduled to appear as Cavaradossi in the season’s opening performance of Puccini’s “Tosca.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Last-Minute Substitution for ‘Faust’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALAGNA, ROBERTO', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Calleja, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gounod, Charles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T22:15:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organi