: 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bayerische Motorenwerke AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T23:12:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Schembari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schembari', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/078d0952-08c9-57f2-b4ea-ab2843f2742e', 'word_count': 338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/078d0952-08c9-57f2-b4ea-ab2843f2742e'}, {'abstract': 'The 200-horsepower two-plus-two coupe was developed in tandem with the Subaru BRZ coupe.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/after-long-gestation-scion-fr-s-makes-auto-show-debut/', 'snippet': 'The 200-horsepower two-plus-two coupe was developed in tandem with the Subaru BRZ coupe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is it? The name says it all, according to Toyota: FR-S stands for “front-engine, rear-wheel-drive, sport.” The 2-plus-2 coupe measures nearly a foot longer than a Corolla and carries decidedly sportier pretensions. Its Toyota-branded equivalent, the FT, was displayed at the Tokyo auto show in December, but Toyota clearly wants Scion to get a dose of performance cred, which was previously available only from the tC. The FR-S was previewed as a concept at the 2011 New York auto show.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Long Gestation, Scion FR-S Makes Auto Show Debut', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T23:17:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb774931-5edf-5d3c-899e-21e1cd13aeb0', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb774931-5edf-5d3c-899e-21e1cd13aeb0'}, {'abstract': 'Nicotine replacements have no lasting benefit in helping smokers quit and may backfire, according to the most rigorous long-term study to date.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/health/study-finds-nicotine-gum-and-patches-dont-help-smokers-quit.html', 'snippet': 'Nicotine replacements have no lasting benefit in helping smokers quit and may backfire, according to the most rigorous long-term study to date.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The nicotine gum and patches that millions of smokers use to help kick their habit have no lasting benefit and may backfire in some cases, according to the most rigorous long-term study to date of so-called nicotine replacement therapy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/us/SMOKING/SMOKING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/us/SMOKING/SMOKING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/10/us/SMOKING/SMOKING-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nicotine Gum and Skin Patch Face New Doubt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nicotine Gum And Skin Patch Face New Doubt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nicotine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T23:18:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benedict Carey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benedict', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10bc5bff-db1b-5c48-a739-43d660d1ee1a', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10bc5bff-db1b-5c48-a739-43d660d1ee1a'}, {'abstract': 'Twelve acts from five continents, from the Cape Verdean singer Mayra Andrade to the Chinese jew’s-harp master Wang Li, performed at Webster Hall on Sunday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/arts/music/globalfest-at-webster-hall-review.html', 'snippet': 'Twelve acts from five continents, from the Cape Verdean singer Mayra Andrade to the Chinese jew’s-harp master Wang Li, performed at Webster Hall on Sunday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world was fused at Globalfest on Sunday night. The sold-out event at Webster Hall was the ninth-annual world-music showcase, timed to coincide with the Association of Performing Arts Presenters’ annual convention: 12 acts from 5 continents. At Globalfest the old image of world music as ethnomusicological finds — pure local styles directly imported from out-of-the-way places — has given way to a more worldly music, played by artists who self-consciously decide what to mix. They select from their own heritage and countless other possibilities; after all, they’ve got Internet connections themselves. Globalfest was recorded for NPR. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/arts/SUB-GLOBALFEST-2/SUB-GLOBALFEST-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 956, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/arts/SUB-GLOBALFEST-2/SUB-GLOBALFEST-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/10/arts/SUB-GLOBALFEST-2/SUB-GLOBALFEST-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Night When All the World’s Onstage', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Night When All the World’s Onstage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Assn of Performing Arts Presenters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-09T23:18:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0a1da6c-3bbf-5350-8682-a3fc9f566095', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0a1da6c-3bbf-5350-8682-a3fc9f566095'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands of companies come to Las Vegas each year for the Consumer Electronics Show. But it is becoming interesting to see who skips it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/t