rdplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/relations/', 'snippet': 'Gary Cee reports in from a centrally located dwelling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUESDAY’S PUZZLE — I already know what you’re thinking. You’re thinking that TOILET PAPER would have made a spiffy theme entry. I agree. Unfortunately, the long theme entries here are 10s, not 11s, so I’m guessing the bathroom tissue got flushed (sorry).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday: Cordial Relations', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T03:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26c44774-6142-518f-ac31-dd03473ab91f', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26c44774-6142-518f-ac31-dd03473ab91f'}, {'abstract': 'Since 1984, when primary polling became widespread, the candidates with the three highest volatility scores have been Newt Gingrich, Rick Perry, and Herman Cain, all of whom ran this year.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/ground-game-determines-candidates-strength/', 'snippet': 'Since 1984, when primary polling became widespread, the candidates with the three highest volatility scores have been Newt Gingrich, Rick Perry, and Herman Cain, all of whom ran this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, N.H. — The 2012 primary campaign has been by far the most volatile on record, with one candidate after another surging to the front of the pack and then falling back again.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ground Game Determines Candidates’ Strength', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Herman', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T03:03:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver and Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40220da5-3002-571d-be80-60a23587e9c2', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40220da5-3002-571d-be80-60a23587e9c2'}, {'abstract': 'As both a concept and a continent, the area known as Europe has shifted over time.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/09/where-is-europe/', 'snippet': 'As both a concept and a continent, the area known as Europe has shifted over time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Where is Europe? You might as well ask: What is Europe? For it is a concept as well as a continent, and the borders of both oscillate wildly. For the ancient Persians, it was that small stepping stone separating them from Greece. In the Middle Ages, it became virtually synonymous with Christendom. A relatively recent and generally unaccepted theory sees Europe spanning half the globe, from Iceland to the Bering Strait, nearly touching Alaska.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Is Europe?', 'kicker': 'Borderlines', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T03:03:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/759a7c84-b369-5584-b55e-00d437c6a39f', 'word_count': 2378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/759a7c84-b369-5584-b55e-00d437c6a39f'}, {'abstract': 'The Republicans’ double-debate weekend offered a vivid illustration of why Americans are so cynical about politicians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/opinion/bruni-at-the-republican-debates-please-hold-the-cheese.html', 'snippet': 'The Republicans’ double-debate weekend offered a vivid illustration of why Americans are so cynical about politicians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A charge of “pious baloney” from the king of sanctimonious salami: that was the taste of Debate Weekend, during which Newt Gingrich of all people called out Mitt Romney for larded self-aggrandizement. Takes one lunchmeat to know another. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni