//article/50866ad6-5999-53e0-b593-180beed52901'}, {'abstract': 'Myanmar’s opposition leader, Daw Aung San Suu Kyi, confirmed on Tuesday that she will run for a seat in the country’s new Parliament in an April by-election.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/world/asia/aung-san-suu-kyi-will-run-for-myanmar-parliament.html', 'snippet': 'Myanmar’s opposition leader, Daw Aung San Suu Kyi, confirmed on Tuesday that she will run for a seat in the country’s new Parliament in an April by-election.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANGKOK — Myanmar’s opposition leader, Daw Aung San Suu Kyi, confirmed on Tuesday that she would run for a seat in the country’s new Parliament in a by-election scheduled for April in which her party, the National League for Democracy, will enter the new political structure for the first time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/09/world/brief-aung-san-suu-kyi/brief-aung-san-suu-kyi-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/09/world/brief-aung-san-suu-kyi/brief-aung-san-suu-kyi-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/09/world/brief-aung-san-suu-kyi/brief-aung-san-suu-kyi-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Aung San Suu Kyi Confirms She Will Run for Parliament', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Myanmar Opposition Leader to Run for Parliament', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aung San Suu Kyi, Daw', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National League for Democracy (Myanmar)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T09:38:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth Mydans', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mydans', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f708e7a1-2a47-5674-be4d-933c30557238', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f708e7a1-2a47-5674-be4d-933c30557238'}, {'abstract': 'North Korea said on Tuesday that it will release prisoners in its first special pardon since 2005, as the country escalated efforts to build support for its new leader, Kim Jong-un.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/world/asia/north-korea-announces-prisoner-release.html', 'snippet': 'North Korea said on Tuesday that it will release prisoners in its first special pardon since 2005, as the country escalated efforts to build support for its new leader, Kim Jong-un.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — North Korea said on Tuesday that it would release prisoners in its first special amnesty in seven years, a day after soldiers paraded in the capital, Pyongyang, vowing to become “rifles and bombs” to defend the country’s new leader, Kim Jong-un. The military rally in Pyongyang on Monday and the special pardon, effective from Feb. 1, came as North Korea escalated a campaign to consolidate support for Mr. Kim.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Korea Announces It Will Release Prisoners', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korea Announces It Will Release Prisoners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Un', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amnesties, Commutations and Pardons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T09:40:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e41cc5d-3f4c-59b4-a7c2-e7822ed8a093', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e41cc5d-3f4c-59b4-a7c2-e7822ed8a093'}, {'abstract': 'Jim Wilson covered his first presidential primary in 1976, shooting for The Charlotte Observer. Covering politics over the decades, he has seen tremendous changes – both in the style of the campaigns and the technology used to photograph them.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/photographing-politics-in-changing-times/', 'snippet': 'Jim Wilson covered his first presidential primary in 1976, shooting for The Charlotte Observer. Covering politics over the decades, he has seen tremendous changes – both in the style of the campaigns and the technology used to photograph them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jim Wilson started at The New York Times in 1980, working primarily as a staff photographer and a photo editor. Covering politics over the decades, he has seen tremendous changes – both in the style of the campaigns and the technology he has used to photograph them. In recent weeks, he’s been covering the Republican presidential campaign in Iowa and New Hampshire, focusing almost entirely on Mitt Romney. He spoke with James Estrin from Manchester, N.H. Their conversation had been edited.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photographing Politics in Changing Times', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Jim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Des Moines (IA)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T10:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8acdeb1-84fe-5f45-84bb-1cf48bebfd19', 'word_count': 1226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8acdeb1-84fe-5f45-84bb-1cf48bebfd19'}, {'abstract': 'Jim Estrin asks Jim Wilson about his experience so far in Iowa and New Hampshire, where he has spent much of his time covering Romney.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/on-lens-times-photographer-jim-wilson-in-new-hampshire/', 'snippet': 'Jim Estrin asks Jim Wilson about his experience so far in Iowa and New Hampshire, where he has spent much of his time covering Romney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jim Wilson covered his first presidential primary in 1976, shooting for The Charlotte Observer. Covering politics over the decades, he has seen tremendous changes \xad both in the style of the campaigns and the technology used to photograph them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Lens: Times Photographer Jim Wilson in New Hampshire', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Jim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T10:01:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01742170-3775-53d3-8e03-68a3a98b407a', 'word_count': 71, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01742170-3775-53d3-8e03-68a3a98b407a'}, {'abstract': 'A panel of the Supreme Court threatened to dismiss Prime Minister Yousaf Raza Gilani for not complying with orders to reopen corruption cases against President Asif Ali Zardari.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/world/asia/car-bomb-kills-dozens-in-pakistan.html', 'snippet': 'A panel of the Supreme Court threatened to dismiss Prime Minister Yousaf Raza Gilani for not complying with orders to reopen corruption cases against President Asif Ali Zardari.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — The political and legal crisis in Pakistan took a new turn on Tuesday when the Supreme Court threatened to dismiss Prime Minister Yousaf Raza Gilani for failing to comply with court orders to reopen corruption cases against his political boss: President Asif Ali Zardari. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/world/PAKISTAN/PAKISTAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/11/world/PAKISTAN/PAKISTAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/world/PAKISTAN/PAKISTAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistani Judges Press Premier to Defy President', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistani Judges Press Premier to Defy President', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilani, Yousaf Raza', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tehrik-e-Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zardari, Asif Ali', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T10:09:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood and Ismail Khan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ismail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89533843-f9d1-5ecd-a21c-6f5ecc1d929c', 'word_count': 843, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89533843-f9d1-5ecd-a21c-6f5ecc1d929c'}, {'abstract': 'Would you participate in the campaign if one of your parents were seeking election to the White House?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/what-if-your-parent-ran-for-president/', 'snippet': 'Would you participate in the campaign if one of your parents were seeking election to the White House?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four of Mitt Romney’s five sons are actively engaged in helping their father’s campaign for the Republican presidential nomination, as are the adult daughters of Jon M. Huntsman Jr. If one of your parents were to run for president, would you work on his or her campaign? Would you want your role to be highly public and visible, or would you prefer to stay behind the scenes?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What if Your Parent Ran for President?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T10:24:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/557ea35d-19a9-5440-bead-da21d4e08a06', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/557ea35d-19a9-5440-bead-da21d4e08a06'}, {'abstract': 'The Japanese camera maker said that it was seeking almost $50 million from 19 current and former executives, including its current president.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/business/global/olympus-sues-executives-over-cover-up.html', 'snippet': 'The Japanese camera maker said that it was seeking almost $50 million from 19 current and former executives, including its current president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Olympus said on Tuesday that it was suing 19 current and former executives and board members, including its current president, for around $50 million over an accounting scheme to hide losses going back more than a decade. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/business/OLYMPUS/OLYMPUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/business/OLYMPUS/OLYMPUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/11/business/OLYMPUS/OLYMPUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Olympus Sues Executives Over Cover-Up, but Does Not Dismiss Them', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Olympus Sues Executives Over Cover-Up, but Does Not Dismiss Them', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woodford, Michael C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Olympus Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Takayama, Shuichi', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T10:44:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/716f472c-c874-5f57-8e1c-fc08b2c54c0c', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/716f472c-c874-5f57-8e1c-fc08b2c54c0c'}, {'abstract': 'The African National Congress’s accomplishments as a liberation movement are extraordinary. Too bad its record as a party-in-government is a mixed bag.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/african-national-congress-path-from-rebels-to-rulers/', 'snippet': 'The African National Congress’s accomplishments as a liberation movement are extraordinary. Too bad its record as a party-in-government is a mixed bag.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JOHANNESBURG — Africa’s oldest liberation movement, the African National Congress (A.N.C.), held a lavish birthday bash last weekend to celebrate its founding 100 years ago. More than 40 heads of state gathered in the small church in the small city of Bloemfontein, the A.N.C.’s birthplace, to celebrate the party’s role in defeating white-minority rule and apartheid. And deservedly: the A.N.C.’s accomplishments as a liberation movement are extraordinary. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Rebels to Rulers', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN NATIONAL CONGRESS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T10:57:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_