rt-there-is-snow-if-you-know-where-to-look.html', 'snippet': 'Warm temperatures and weird jet streams mean that skiers need to do some weather research before heading to the slopes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A LACK of snow across much of the United States has ski resorts from Lake Tahoe to Vermont scrambling to make enough on their own. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/travel/15PRAC/15PRAC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 790, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/travel/15PRAC/15PRAC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/travel/15PRAC/15PRAC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Skiers, Take Heart! There Is Snow if You Know Where to Look', 'kicker': 'Practical Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oh, for Just a Little Snow', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skiing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snow and Snowstorms', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:07:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Higgins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Higgins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd5cc6ba-e235-57c8-bb73-8988324c4615', 'word_count': 1315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd5cc6ba-e235-57c8-bb73-8988324c4615'}, {'abstract': 'A Times sportswriter examines how the Academic Index, a measurement of an athlete’s academic performance used by the Ivy League, is typically less relevant to a non-athlete applying to one of the eight Ivies.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/ivy-academic-index/', 'snippet': 'A Times sportswriter examines how the Academic Index, a measurement of an athlete’s academic performance used by the Ivy League, is typically less relevant to a non-athlete applying to one of the eight Ivies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an article published on Christmas Day in The New York Times, I described specific elements of the mysterious Academic Index, a tool used by Ivy League institutions – and increasingly other colleges and universities – to measure the basic academic qualifications of recruited athletes. The Academic Index, created in the 1980s and never disclosed publicly by the institutions themselves, uses a formula that assigns each recruit a number based on his or her standardized tests scores and high school grade point average.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Rare Glimpse Inside the Ivy League’s Academic Index', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:08:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Pennington', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pennington', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a3f16dd-5c34-5a49-96aa-d8502983d7d2', 'word_count': 807, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a3f16dd-5c34-5a49-96aa-d8502983d7d2'}, {'abstract': 'One woman died and several people were injured in a stampede by students desperate to apply for admission to a public university.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/world/africa/stampede-highlights-crisis-at-south-african-universities.html', 'snippet': 'One woman died and several people were injured in a stampede by students desperate to apply for admission to a public university.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JOHANNESBURG — They lined up well before dawn, some driving from the deep countryside with bags of fluffy blankets and neatly packed sandwiches, to wait for the gates to a new life to open. They hoped for a shot at a coveted spot at one of South Africa’s public universities, and with it a chance to escape the indignity of joblessness that afflicts more than a third of the nation. By morning, the line was more than a mile long.\xa0', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/world/africa/10safrica_cnd/10safrica_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/10/world/africa/10safrica_cnd/10safrica_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/world/africa/10safrica_cnd/10safrica_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/10/world/africa/10safrica_cnd/10safrica_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/10/world/africa/10safrica_cnd/10safrica_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fatal Stampede in South Africa Points Up University Crisis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fatal Stampede in South Africa Points Up Crisis at Universities', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN NATIONAL CONGRESS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stampedes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:12:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Polgreen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Polgreen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b63711d2-955f-5d04-81bd-b3a6ee7d8a0c', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b63711d2-955f-5d04-81bd-b3a6ee7d8a0c'}, {'abstract': 'Camilo Pardo, who is credited with the design of the Ford GT, again filled his downtown loft with art cars, neon signage, comely models and the designers who admire them.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/dispatches-from-the-annual-designers-party/', 'snippet': 'Camilo Pardo, who is credited with the design of the Ford GT, again filled his downtown loft with art cars, neon signage, comely models and the designers who admire them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — In addition to the teeming variety of car paintings, neon Porsche signs, race-car parts and other automotive bric-a-brac arranged throughout Camilo Pardo’s loft on Woodward Avenue, there were two notable newcomers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dispatches From the Annual Designers’ Party', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bayerische Motorenwerke AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Porsche AG', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:20:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Patton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b2dffb0-91e4-5595-8948-64ea9f964857', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b2dffb0-91e4-5595-8948-64ea9f964857'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/what-were-reading-348/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Hudson Independent: In Tarrytown, N.Y., an award-winning coffeehouse, Coffee Labs, fights for the right to let dogs hang out. — Jeff Gordinier ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:21:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba07a71e-8878-54d6-b91b-730744333f08', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba07a71e-8878-54d6-b91b-730744333f08'}, {'abstract': 'Alfred Kazin’s “A Walker in the City” is the subject of today’s meeting of the Big City Book Club, with a live chat from 1 to 4 p.m.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/big-city-book-club-a-walker-in-the-city/', 'snippet': 'Alfred Kazin’s “A Walker in the City” is the subject of today’s meeting of the Big City Book Club, with a live chat from 1 to 4 p.m.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am usually suspicious of memoirs produced by writers who have not yet reached middle age, but Kazin, like so many of his provenance and generation, seemed to possess a voice of centenarian authority as a young man. At 26, he had audaciously established himself as a significant literary critic with “On Native Ground,” his magnum opus on American letters of the first half of the 20th century. His writing hardly betrayed a tentative hand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big City Book Club: ‘A Walker in the City’', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Walker in the City, A (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kazin, Alfred', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brownsville (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:22:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ginia Bellafante', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ginia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bellafante', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af0cee69-458e-515f-8ca8-6286b2f91ed0', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af0cee69-458e-515f-8ca8-6286b2f91ed0'}, {'abstract': 'What Romney actually said, in context, was worse.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/who-fires-whom/', 'snippet': 'What Romney actually said, in context, was worse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aaron Carroll has an excellent analysis of Mitt Romney’s faux pas on firing people. No, Romney didn’t actually say that he enjoys firing people — but what he really did say, that competition works in health care because you can fire your insurance company, was actually worse. Carroll:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Fires Whom?', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:26:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/157bdc5b-ac8f-5ad0-8724-221f0c2f9567', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/157bdc5b-ac8f-5ad0-8724-221f0c2f9567'}, {'abstract': 'Lincoln needs more differentiation — not to mention more luxurious models — to justify its existence, but the MKZ concept is only a small first step in advancing that imperative.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/mkz-concept-is-the-next-step-in-lincolns-reinvention/', 'snippet': 'Lincoln needs more differentiation — not to mention more luxurious models — to justify its existence, but the MKZ concept is only a small first step in advancing that imperative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Introduced on Tuesday, Jan. 10: Lincoln MKZ Concept.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'MKZ Concept Is the Next Step in Lincoln’s Reinvention', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'North American International Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolff, Max', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Division of Ford Motor Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:28:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42e2a09b-ea94-518c-983e-be7ad0a18f15', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42e2a09b-ea94-518c-983e-be7ad0a18f15'}, {'abstract': 'Jean Dujardin does his best De Niro impression, in front of Robert De Niro, at the New York Film Critics Circle dinner.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/doing-his-best-de-niro-impression-in-front-of-de-niro/', 'snippet': 'Jean Dujardin does his best De Niro impression, in front of Robert De Niro, at the New York Film Critics Circle dinner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Moments before Robert De Niro sat next to him, Jean Dujardin was trying out his De Niro impression. Mr. Dujardin, the handsome, elastic-faced French star of “The Artist,” was at the New York Film Critics Circle awards dinner on Monday night, where “The Artist” won best director and best film. The event capped off a whirlwind day that included a lunch with the publicist Peggy Siegal and the taping of an appearance on “Charlie Rose.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Doing His Best De Niro Impression, in Front of the Real De Niro', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'De Niro, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dujardin, Jean', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weinstein, Harvey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Film Critics Circle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:29:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50e57d76-9384-5728-bd46-a4737f08d8ee', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50e57d76-9384-5728-bd46-a4737f08d8ee'}, {'abstract': 'A writer who heals injured birds blogs on a controversial incident in Utah.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/other-voices-the-night-it-snowed-grebes-in-utah/', 'snippet': 'A writer who heals injured birds blogs on a controversial incident in Utah.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Suzie Gilbert, a friend and neighbor, has for many years been a hard-working bird rehabilitator, a career described beautifully in her recent book “Flyaway.” She also recently launched a blog, The Crooked Wing, and alerted me to a post on a remarkable occurrence last month in Utah, when thousands of migrating eared grebes, evidently disoriented by a storm, began crash landing on roads, parking lots and the like — surfaces they apparently mistook for water. They can only take flight from water, making such landings a death sentence for those that survived the impact.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Other Voices: The Night it Snowed Grebes in Utah', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biology and Biochemistry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Utah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80ee9dad-99b5-56b9-a779-76708aee8f53', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80ee9dad-99b5-56b9-a779-76708aee8f53'}, {'abstract': 'The house designer Ricardo Tisci delivered a look that is all tough and refined and, literally, ready to wear now and for seasons to come.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/just-one-look-givenchy-2/', 'snippet': 'The house designer Ricardo Tisci delivered a look that is all tough and refined and, literally, ready to wear now and for seasons to come.', 'lead_paragraph': '“This prefall collection is all about the best of Givenchy,” the house designer Ricardo Tisci said at yesterday’s presentation. Inserting a mix of what Tisci named “American 1950s housewife” and “French ’70s style” into “the best of our men’s, women’s and couture,” he delivered a look that is all tough and refined and, literally, ready to wear now and for seasons to come. Hits include pieces featuring a subtle paillette print, a herringbone and cashmere suit, wool jackets stiffened with men’s fabrications, then wrapped in places with the softest organza, and this expertly tailored croc-trimmed tuxedo. Worth noting also are the collection’s elongating boots, furry baseball caps and sharp talon ear studs (see: editors swooning).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Givenchy', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Givenchy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:30:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73321479-a584-50c1-84bd-9aa890264bbc', 'word_count': 115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73321479-a584-50c1-84bd-9aa890264bbc'}, {'abstract': 'Not a good match.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/businessmen-and-economics/', 'snippet': 'Not a good match.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A brief thought on something I’ll try to expand on later. Leaving aside all the questions about what Mitt Romney did or didn’t do at Bain — and about his self-aggrandizing double standard — there’s an even broader question: why does anyone believe that success in business qualified someone to make economic policy?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Businessmen and Economics', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T15:37:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middl