retti, Alain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-10T22:04:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Wells', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wells', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/690e9506-6471-527f-8691-e09dd1e318c4', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/690e9506-6471-527f-8691-e09dd1e318c4'}, {'abstract': 'Decay and disappointment, retreat and regret, are the main themes in the three novellas that make up Anita Desai’s new book.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/books/the-artist-of-disappearance-by-anita-desai-review.html', 'snippet': 'Decay and disappointment, retreat and regret, are the main themes in the three novellas that make up Anita Desai’s new book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The crumbling, all but abandoned manor house as symbol of a social order in distress: the English may have invented that notion, but their former colonial subjects in India have also proved adept at employing it as a literary device. In the three novellas that make up “The Artist of Disappearance,” Anita Desai uses it twice, in differing circumstances and locations, but to the same convincing and plaintive effect.\xa0 ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/arts/book/book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 807, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/11/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thu