_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Names of the Dead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Names of the Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cf6a4bf-54ea-5535-8086-0c7a76d1fed9', 'word_count': 46, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cf6a4bf-54ea-5535-8086-0c7a76d1fed9'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times and other news organizations declared Mitt Romney the winner just moments after the polls closed, based on exit polls and early returns.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/romney-wins-new-hampshire-primary/', 'snippet': 'The New York Times and other news organizations declared Mitt Romney the winner just moments after the polls closed, based on exit polls and early returns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, N.H. — Mitt Romney has won the New Hampshire Republican primary, achieving a sweep of the first two critical contests in the 2012 presidential race and boosting his chances at becoming his party’s nominee this fall.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Wins New Hampshire Primary', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:01:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/180a4b60-465c-5435-aa39-9347edd9d3cf', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/180a4b60-465c-5435-aa39-9347edd9d3cf'}, {'abstract': 'The Scottish government said Tuesday that it would hold a referendum on independence from Britain in 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/world/europe/scotland-plans-independence-vote.html', 'snippet': 'The Scottish government said Tuesday that it would hold a referendum on independence from Britain in 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Scottish government said Tuesday that it would hold a referendum on independence from Britain in 2014, a decision that Alex Salmond, below, who leads the government in Edinburgh, said would be Scotland’s “most important decision for 300 years.” But the path to the referendum promises to be tortuous. Prime Minister David Cameron’s government in London told Parliament that it had the sole constitutional right to approve a referendum, but that he was prepared to devolve the power to the Scottish authorities on terms that would make the referendum result “legal, decisive and fair.” Mr. Salmond said he would hold a referendum on his terms. The Cameron government has said it wants an earlier date for the vote, to end uncertainty that it says is deterring investment in Scotland. The British government also said the question should be a straightforward yes or no on independence, without the third option Mr. Salmond wants on the ballot, which would give Scotland full autonomy as part of Britain.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 669, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/11/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scotland Plans Independence Vote', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scotland Plans Independence Vote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salmond, Alex', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:05:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/493be1c7-97b7-585c-a843-d9a789ce8e60', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/493be1c7-97b7-585c-a843-d9a789ce8e60'}, {'abstract': 'The government approved on Tuesday a controversial $51 billion high-speed rail line linking London to Birmingham, Manchester and Leeds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/world/europe/britain-plan-set-for-fast-trains.html', 'snippet': 'The government approved on Tuesday a controversial $51 billion high-speed rail line linking London to Birmingham, Manchester and Leeds.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The government approved on Tuesday a controversial $51 billion high-speed rail line linking London to Birmingham, Manchester and Leeds with trains capable of running at 250 miles an hour. Transport Minister Justine Greening said the move would shift millions of passengers from commuter airlines and cars to trains. The project would be a major step toward matching Britain’s outdated rail network with those in France, Germany and Japan, leaders in high-speed rail travel. But it faces strong opposition from environmental groups, who say the trains would benefit mainly wealthy commuters and scar stretches of the countryside. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain: Plan Set for Fast Trains', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Plan Set for Fast Trains', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High-Speed Rail Projects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Leeds (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manchester (England)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:08:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/227c3740-3bc1-58da-9edd-34209573319b', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/227c3740-3bc1-58da-9edd-34209573319b'}, {'abstract': 'Seagate’s GoFlex Thunderbolt Adapter is a small station in which one can mount a hard drive and connect it to a computer with a fast Thunderbolt cable.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/seagate-adapter-hits-hard-drives-with-a-thunderbolt/', 'snippet': 'Seagate’s GoFlex Thunderbolt Adapter is a small station in which one can mount a hard drive and connect it to a computer with a fast Thunderbolt cable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAS VEGAS — Seagate on Tuesday introduced an accessory that will give some hard drives a major performance boost.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seagate Adapter Hits Hard Drives With a Thunderbolt', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:08:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/829304dc-21bd-5aca-af6b-36f9b428cdb4', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/829304dc-21bd-5aca-af6b-36f9b428cdb4'}, {'abstract': 'Those plans to teach history will be put on hold: Bud Selig is expected to accept an offer to remain as baseball commissioner.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/sports/baseball/bud-selig-likely-to-remain-as-baseball-commissioner.html', 'snippet': 'Those plans to teach history will be put on hold: Bud Selig is expected to accept an offer to remain as baseball commissioner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sitting at his desk last April, overlooking Lake Michigan 30 floors above downtown Milwaukee, Bud Selig seemed at peace. He had a few initiatives left to accomplish as baseball’s commissioner, but he said that as far as he was concerned, he would retire after the 2012 season.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Selig Is Likely to Stay in Office at Least 2 Years', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Selig Is Likely To Stay in Office At Least 2 Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Selig, Bud', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Milwaukee Brewers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Baseball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:09:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59a0c72e-7846-5520-b98f-943b57083d7e', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59a0c72e-7846-5520-b98f-943b57083d7e'}, {'abstract': 'The “super PAC,” which supports Mitt Romney, purchases $3.6 million in additional in advertising in Florida.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/10/restore-our-future-makes-new-ad-buy/', 'snippet': 'The “super PAC,” which supports Mitt Romney, purchases $3.6 million in additional in advertising in Florida.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A group backing Mitt Romney has purchased $3.6 million in additional advertising in Florida, a person with knowledge of the decision said Tuesday, the building blocks of a television firewall intended to guarantee that no other Republican makes it past the state’s critical primary at the end of the month.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Restore Our Future Makes New Ad Buy', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:11:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Confessore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Confessore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76016126-d379-5b98-86a9-feb016cbe17a', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76016126-d379-5b98-86a9-feb016cbe17a'}, {'abstract': 'An under secretary to Prime Minister Mario Monti resigned after a report that he had vacationed in 2007 and 2008 as the guest of two businessmen later implicated in a corruption investigation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/world/europe/italy-government-official-quits.html', 'snippet': 'An under secretary to Prime Minister Mario Monti resigned after a report that he had vacationed in 2007 and 2008 as the guest of two businessmen later implicated in a corruption investigation.', 'lead_paragraph': ' An under secretary to Prime Minister Mario Monti, Carlo Malinconico, resigned Tuesday after the Italian news media reported that he had vacationed at a five-star Tuscan hotel in 2007 and 2008 as the guest of two businessmen later implicated in a corruption investigation. In a statement issued by the prime minister’s office, Mr. Malinconico denied any wrongdoing and said he was resigning to better defend himself and to protect the credibility of the government. When Mr. Monti took office eight weeks ago, he said his cabinet ministers would act with transparency and be free of conflicts of interest. Mr. Malinconico was a high-ranking member of the staff of Romano Prodi, who was prime minister at the time referenced in the allegations. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Italy: Government Official Quits', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Italy: Government Official Quits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Monti, Mario', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:14:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabetta Povoledo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabetta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Povoledo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/906092e7-cc46-53b4-92a7-cf39c360bc86', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/906092e7-cc46-53b4-92a7-cf39c360bc86'}, {'abstract': 'The West 69th Street Block Association has opposed renaming a portion of that street after Matthew P. Sapolin, who led the mayor’s office for the disabled until his death in late November.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/nyregion/west-69th-street-group-opposes-street-renaming.html', 'snippet': 'The West 69th Street Block Association has opposed renaming a portion of that street after Matthew P. Sapolin, who led the mayor’s office for the disabled until his death in late November.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although he had been blind since age 5 and reliant on a guide dog, Matthew P. Sapolin was the city’s first commissioner of the Mayor’s Office for People With Disabilities, pressing to make the city’s buildings, taxis and sidewalks more accommodating to people who use wheelchairs or cannot see. He was an accomplished wrestler and musician as well.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/nyregion/NAME2/NAME2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 680, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/nyregion/NAME2/NAME2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/11/nyregion/NAME2/NAME2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Too Many Notable People to Name Street for Just One', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Too Many Notable People To Name Street for Just One', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Mayor's Office for People With Disabilities (NYC)", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sapolin, Matthew P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Geographical', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:16:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa0e20f2-2e40-58df-ae42-840963744da6', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa0e20f2-2e40-58df-ae42-840963744da6'}, {'abstract': 'Judges in the trial of former President Hosni Mubarak on Tuesday scheduled a full month for his defense team to make its case.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/world/middleeast/egypt-mubaraks-trial-extended.html', 'snippet': 'Judges in the trial of former President Hosni Mubarak on Tuesday scheduled a full month for his defense team to make its case.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Judges in the trial of former President Hosni Mubarak on Tuesday scheduled a full month for his defense team to make its case, pushing any potential verdict past the day of demonstrations expected on Jan. 25, the anniversary of the uprising that ended his rule. A recent acceleration in the pace of the trial fueled speculation that Egyptian authorities had hoped for a verdict before Jan. 25 that might help mollify anger at the current military-led government. Mr. Mubarak is on trial on charges of corruption and directing the killing of protesters. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: Mubarak’s Trial Extended', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt: Mubarak’s Trial Extended', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T01:17:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mayy El Sheikh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'M