h': 'NoBrow, a Shoreditch-based bookshop and indie publishing company that has two imprints, is known for its graphic novels and other projects created in collaboration with illustrators all over the world. Their output ranges from hardcover books to wrapping paper to handmade books, produced with particular attention to color, printing and paper quality. The result is items that are as much works of art as recreational distractions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In London, a Pop-Up That’s Not a Pop-Up', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T11:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rooksana Hossenally', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rooksana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hossenally', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8539f91a-9921-5755-b132-3708b282928f', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8539f91a-9921-5755-b132-3708b282928f'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Kantrowitz, an expert on paying for college and the founder of FinAid.org, is answering questions from readers of The Choice about financial aid applications.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/kantrowitz-part-three/', 'snippet': 'Mark Kantrowitz, an expert on paying for college and the founder of FinAid.org, is answering questions from readers of The Choice about financial aid applications.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:28 a.m. | Updated We are no longer accepting questions for this feature.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Part 3: Answers on the Fafsa, the Free Application for Federal Student Aid', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None