of charter schools worthwhile. Why should students and parents suffer when adults don’t do their job? That sounds reasonable, he says, but is actually very problematic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/01/11/why-failing-charters-must-be-closed/', 'snippet': 'In an opinion article, the C.E.O. of the New York City Charter School Center says it is reasonable to ask whether disrupting the education of more than 1,000 students makes the closing of charter schools worthwhile. Why should students and parents...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Failing Charters Must Be Closed', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T13:40:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By JAMES MERRIMAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MERRIMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbf4d2d9-e595-5d90-b86b-b111c872cd40', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbf4d2d9-e595-5d90-b86b-b111c872cd40'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/morning-take-out-400/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from the DealBook newsletter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lehman Still Doing Deals in a Second Life on Wall Street \xa0|\xa0 Things that refuse to die include Dracula, fungus, Jason from the “Friday the 13th” movies, zombies — and Lehman Brothers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Take-Out', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T13:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6eecf4f1-fbc4-56ce-a218-71e452a8907e', 'word_count': 2189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6eecf4f1-fbc4-56ce-a218-71e452a8907e'}, {'abstract': 'MittRomney’s early win leaves anchors scrambling and a dispute between Google and Twitter gets personal.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/the-breakfast-meeting-the-spielberg-exception-and-whos-illin/', 'snippet': 'MittRomney’s early win leaves anchors scrambling and a dispute between Google and Twitter gets personal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That Mitt Romney’s victory in the New Hampshire primary was considered all but assured even before the voting began on Tuesday didn’t stop the news coverage from going into overdrive, Brian Stelter reports. But the operation appeared to be running on fumes. Once a winner was crowned — at 8 p.m., precisely when all polls were closed — the topic turned to second place. That, too, was over early, with Ron Paul declared the runner-up in that same hour. The focus then turned to South Carolina, and how Mr. Romney would fare in a state that would test his conservative credentials: CNN had already assembled a group of undecided Republicans there, Mr. Stelter writes, while Fox News promoted its debate there on Monday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: The Spielberg Exception and Who’s Illin’?', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value':