Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40ba5c09-ead2-5944-b868-fdf657efe19b', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40ba5c09-ead2-5944-b868-fdf657efe19b'}, {'abstract': 'Australia takes first place, and the United States ties for 13th places with Belgium. Last place went to North Korea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/12/science/study-ranks-countries-on-nuclear-security.html', 'snippet': 'Australia takes first place, and the United States ties for 13th places with Belgium. Last place went to North Korea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 32 nations with materials that can fuel atom bombs are typically mum on security, which looks to the public like a closed world of barbed wire and armed guards. Behind the scenes, atomic insiders have long told horror stories of risky practices and security flaws that might let the crucial ingredients for nuclear weapons fall into the wrong hands.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Study Ranks Countries on Security of Materials That Fuel Nuclear Arms', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Study Ranks Countries on Security of Materials That Fuel Nuclear Arms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nuclear Threat Initiative', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By William J. Broad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Broad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c581f20-7954-5fc6-9d36-29b607f0d991', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c581f20-7954-5fc6-9d36-29b607f0d991'}, {'abstract': 'There are reasons to believe that college has become easier over the last few decades. And yet in that time, the market value of a college degree has been rising. How can this disconnect be explained?', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/getting-a-degree-less-rigor-more-value/', 'snippet': 'There are reasons to believe that college has become easier over the last few decades. And yet in that time, the market value of a college degree has been rising. How can this disconnect be explained?', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are reasons to believe that college has become easier over the last few decades. And yet during the same time period, the market value of a college degree has risen. Why?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Getting a Degree: Less Rigor, More Value', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9da2c9a9-e1f1-564b-84a5-f300c1f8b4d1', 'word_count': 810, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9da2c9a9-e1f1-564b-84a5-f300c1f8b4d1'}, {'abs