nication of the basics of greenhouse warming in a variable climate.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/can-better-communication-of-climate-science-cut-climate-risks/', 'snippet': 'A Nobelist in physics seeks effective communication of the basics of greenhouse warming in a variable climate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the weekend I noticed a clever new effort to visualize how natural climate variability interacts with the heating effect from accumulating greenhouse gases – made by Teddy TV and the animator Ole Christoffer Haga for the\xa010-part math series Siffer\xa0on Norway’s NRK channel*:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Better Communication of Climate Science Cut Climate Risks?', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Secondary)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crichton, Michael', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gell-Mann, Murray', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:09:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cd7aba8-e533-51fd-8a42-9976f09b89d3', 'word_count': 816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cd7aba8-e533-51fd-8a42-9976f09b89d3'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/what-were-reading-349/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NPR: No weapons, no liquids and now, no cupcakes through airport security. — Susan Edgerley', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:13:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8f7514a-22eb-56c6-bc43-fdc4229df105', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8f7514a-22eb-56c6-bc43-fdc4229df105'}, {'abstract': 'Michelle Obama said a new book’s portrayal of her exasperation with the White House staff was an imaginary portrait of “some angry black woman.”', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/first-lady-brushes-off-books-portrayal/', 'snippet': 'Michelle Obama said a new book’s portrayal of her exasperation with the White House staff was an imaginary portrait of “some angry black woman.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michelle Obama dismissed a new book’s portrayal of her role as first lady, saying it played off an image of her as “some angry black woman” and insisting that she had played little role, at least directly, in the West Wing of the White House.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'First Lady Brushes Off Book’s Portrayal', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:13:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John H. Cushman Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Cushman', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1d7e700-25a9-5ed2-bb91-c6449045328f', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1d7e700-25a9-5ed2-bb91-c6449045328f'}, {'abstract': 'The “Star Trek” actor’s one-man show, called “Shatner’s World: We Just Live in It,” will begin performances next month and will be his first Broadway production in more than 50 years.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/set-faces-to-stunned-william-shatner-is-coming-back-to-broadway/', 'snippet': 'The “Star Trek” actor’s one-man show, called “Shatner’s World: We Just Live in It,” will begin performances next month and will be his first Broadway production in more than 50 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last time William Shatner was featured on a Broadway stage, the original productions of “The Sound of Music” and “How to Succeed in Business Without Really Trying” were still playing nearby and a star trek was not much more than a gleam in the eye of President John F. Kennedy (or maybe Gene Roddenberry). Now, 50 years after he played Paul Sevigne in the 1961 staging of “A Shot in the Dark” and went on to a fairly prolific acting career, Mr. Shatner, 80, is making a return voyage to Broadway in a one-man show that will begin performances next month.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Set Faces to Stunned: William Shatner Is Coming Back to Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shatner, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:15:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51b366ca-9780-55b6-a29d-1a9ced591854', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51b366ca-9780-55b6-a29d-1a9ced591854'}, {'abstract': '“It’s easy, but not helpful or fair, to criticize CME Group’s diligent and appropriate oversight based on what is now known about how MF Global violated exchange and C.F.T.C. rules,”  writes the executive chairman of the CME Group.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/cme-chief-speaks-out-on-c-f-t-c-inquiry/', 'snippet': '“It’s easy, but not helpful or fair, to criticize CME Group’s diligent and appropriate oversight based on what is now known about how MF Global violated exchange and C.F.T.C. rules,”  writes the executive chairman of the CME Group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Terrence Duffy is the executive chairman of the CME Group.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CME  Chief Speaks Out on C.F.T.C. Inquiry', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Futures and Options Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duffy, Terrence A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CME Group Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MF Global Ltd', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:26:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terrence Duffy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duffy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa891adb-1d80-56f9-b51a-14ca72031d37', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa891adb-1d80-56f9-b51a-14ca72031d37'}, {'abstract': 'Norman Mailer, Hunter S. Thompson, Garry Wills and Michael Lewis are some of the authors who have provided peeks behind the curtain of American presidential campaigns.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/page-turners-for-the-presidential-campaign/', 'snippet': 'Norman Mailer, Hunter S. Thompson, Garry Wills and Michael Lewis are some of the authors who have provided peeks behind the curtain of American presidential campaigns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Lewis’s book about the 1996 presidential election, “Trail Fever” (since republished as “Losers”), includes this description of the attendees at a banquet for Republicans in New Hampshire: “Every ounce of being they have left over after pledging allegiance to the United States and singing the national anthem goes into despising the United States government.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Old Page Turners for a New Presidential Campaign', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cramer, Richard Ben', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Halperin, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heilemann, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mailer, Norman', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nixon, Richard Milhous', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Hunter S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wills, Garry', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:40:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62563ca1-b67d-5263-ae6c-690bae3055b5', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62563ca1-b67d-5263-ae6c-690bae3055b5'}, {'abstract': 'Reader responses to our last Complaint Box, about mail addressed to the dead.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/jury-duty-for-the-dead-and-more-from-the-complaint-box-mailbox/', 'snippet': 'Reader responses to our last Complaint Box, about mail addressed to the dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Monday’s Complaint Box reader submission from Bill Stoller, of Pleasantville, N.Y., touched on the most delicate of subjects — death. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jury Duty for the Dead and More From the Complaint Box Mailbox', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:44:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56b25b77-db7a-59bd-ba6c-7457640894ec', 'word_count': 1103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56b25b77-db7a-59bd-ba6c-7457640894ec'}, {'abstract': 'As it enters South Carolina, the Republican presidential race is coming for the first time to a state that is struggling mightily through the downturn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/12/us/politics/south-carolina-voters-weigh-priorities.html', 'snippet': 'As it enters South Carolina, the Republican presidential race is coming for the first time to a state that is struggling mightily through the downturn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NORTH, S.C. — The grim just gets grimmer here at the Edisto Grocery, where all day long people with not enough work come to eat $2.25 fried bologna sandwiches, pick up some horse feed and complain about the price of diesel.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/12/us/12carolina-cnd/12carolina-cnd-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/12/us/12carolina-cnd/12carolina-cnd-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/12/us/12carolina-cnd/12carolina-cnd-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/12/us/12carolina-cnd/12carolina-cnd-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/12/us/12carolina-cnd/12carolina-cnd-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Economic Woes Loom Larger as G.O.P. Heads South', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Economic Woes Loom Larger as G.O.P. Candidates Head South', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T15:48:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83051d3a-72f1-5000-97dc-efec1528d59d', 'word_count': 1227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83051d3a-72f1-5000-97dc-efec1528d59d'}, {'abstract': 'Three new picture books feature prickly animals seeking love, comfort and family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/books/thorny-creatures.html', 'snippet': 'Three new picture books feature prickly animals seeking love, comfort and family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tomi Ungerer has long advocated elevating representatives of the horrid-creature class to the esteemed role of picture-book hero. “Every human being has something the others don’t have, that makes him an individual,” he explained. “This is why I often use animals that everyone hates – a snake, a vulture, a bat – in my books: all those animals are redeemed by the fact that they had appendages or qualities the others didn’t have. In the end, they become the heroes.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/11/books/11childrens/11childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 347, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/11/books/11childrens/11childrens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 347}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cred