ippet': 'With a number of stars returning, and a new quarterback in transfer Zach Mettenberger, expectations are again high for L.S.U. in 2012', 'lead_paragraph': 'Monday’s B.C.S. title game could not have gone worse for Les Miles’ vaunted Tigers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The View from L.S.U.: Next Season Starts Now', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miles, Les', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saban, Nick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Butler Community College', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louisiana State University', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T17:50:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e420f48-3659-584f-871c-81007e72a394', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e420f48-3659-584f-871c-81007e72a394'}, {'abstract': 'Why the Republican primary campaign is like the old joke about the food in the Catskills.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/more-dullness-please/', 'snippet': 'Why the Republican primary campaign is like the old joke about the food in the Catskills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gail Collins: Wow, David, big night in New Hampshire. Mitt Romney broke the critical one-third barrier!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Dullness, Please', 'kicker': 'The Conversation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntsman, Jon M Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T17:57:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks and Gail Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ced65bf-47bc-597e-8890-0c3ff1911052', 'word_count': 728, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ced65bf-47bc-597e-8890-0c3ff1911052'}, {'abstract': 'A new study suggests that blogging makes new moms into happier moms — unlike other social media.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/does-blogging-make-new-moms-happier/', 'snippet': 'A new study suggests that blogging makes new moms into happier moms — unlike other social media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Blogging may make new mothers feel happier. Fellow mothers, do you remember that feeling of isolation that came with staying at home, alone, with that tiny, needy, crying, confusing baby they handed you at the hospital? Fathers, if you took on the primary caregiving role solo, for months or years, you can’t possibly have forgotten that sense of being stranded on an island of Cheerios and Raffi music. If your experience was within the last decade, you know the Internet helps. A small research study, conducted by a new father from Brigham Young University, suggests that blogging — creating a space of your own within the online space — helps even more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Blogging Makes New Moms Happier', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T18:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cd5e293-7739-5cd3-aebe-f05b19c77b27', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cd5e293-7739-5cd3-aebe-f05b19c77b27'}, {'abstract': '“God’s Lake Narrows” is a multilayered interactive project from Canada’s National Film Board that uses a personal approach to tell the story of a remote native reserve.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/an-interactive-project-with-no-reservations/', 'snippet': '“God’s Lake Narrows” is a multilayered interactive project from Canada’s National Film Board that uses a personal approach to tell the story of a remote native reserve.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before reading further, please visit the interactive Web site for Canada’s National Film Board, which hosts “God’s Lake Narrows.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Interactive Project With No Reservations', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T18:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerri MacDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f69278c-ce37-5289-9e06-f492b3175dd5', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f69278c-ce37-5289-9e06-f492b3175dd5'}, {'abstract': 'A newly discovered hormone produced in response to exercise may be lessening susceptibility to obesity, diabetes and other health problems, a new study finds.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/exercise-hormone-helps-keep-us-healthy/', 'snippet': 'A newly discovered hormone produced in response to exercise may be lessening susceptibility to obesity, diabetes and other health problems, a new study finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A newly discovered hormone produced in response to exercise may be turning people’s white fat brown, a groundbreaking new study suggests, and in the process lessening their susceptibility to obesity, diabetes and other health problems. The study, published on Wednesday in Nature and led by researchers at the Dana-Farber Cancer Institute and Harvard Medical School, provides remarkable new insights into how exercise affects the body at a cellular level.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exercise Hormone May Fight Obesity and Diabetes', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hormones', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muscles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T18:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Reynolds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reynolds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f85d1805-1c24-5c88-8b5f-3ab0c5563411', 'word_count': 936, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f85d1805-1c24-5c88-8b5f-3ab0c5563411'}, {'abstract': 'Her  album, “21,” continues to rack up significant sales at a time of year that has been embarrassingly slow in recent years.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/adele-tops-charts-for-15th-time/', 'snippet': 'Her  album, “21,” continues to rack up significant sales at a time of year that has been embarrassingly slow in recent years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adele’s “21” (XL/Columbia) is officially the fastest-selling record in seven years, so it’s not a surprise that this week the album tops Billboard’s chart for the 15th time. What is notable — and a relief for the music industry — is that it continues to rack up significant sales at a time of year that has been embarrassingly slow in recent years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Adele Tops Charts for 15th Time', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adele (Singer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mraz, Jason', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rihanna', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen SoundScan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-11T18:04:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb98328f-812a-5616-9313-613d9ea66e84', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb98328f-812a-5616-9313-613d9ea66e84'}, {'abstract': 'The hypervigilance many veterans bring back from war can cause problems when they drive — but not always.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/when-road-hazards-go-from-i-e-d-s-to-sunday-drivers/', 'snippet': 'The hypervigilance many veterans bring back from war can cause problems when they drive — but not always.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do combat veterans experience distinctive driving problems as a result of being exposed to improvised explosive devices and highway ambushes in Iraq and Afghanistan? A number of researchers believe they do, as my article in today’s Times points out. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Road Hazards Go From I.E.D.’s to Sunday Drivers', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker