nd Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Coups D'Etat and Attempted Coups D'Etat", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilani, Yousaf Raza', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-12T01:40:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/a844c465-85dd-560e-a3c0-e7aaeeb2a995', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a844c465-85dd-560e-a3c0-e7aaeeb2a995'}, {'abstract': 'Polaroid is showing off the SC1630 Smart Camera, a device that tries to blur the lines between a point-and-shoot camera and a smartphone.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/a-camera-phone-sans-phone/', 'snippet': 'Polaroid is showing off the SC1630 Smart Camera, a device that tries to blur the lines between a point-and-shoot camera and a smartphone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAS VEGAS — Every year the camera manufacturers set up huge booths at C.E.S., where they display an impressive array of tiny, powerful cameras. And every year this exercise seems a bit more quaint. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Camera Phone, Sans Phone', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cameras', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Consumer Electronics Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Polaroid Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-12T01:43:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Brustein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '