on her lap, Maria Ortiz was the picture of professionalism. Wearing a black suit, her hair pulled back, she was smiling confidently. It was the end of another long day, one that started at 6 in the morning, before the rest of her family had even hit the snooze button. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/12/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/12/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/12/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/12/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/12/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Credit Nightmare, but Coming Out Better in the End', 'kicker': 'The Neediest Cases', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Credit Nightmare, but Coming Out Better in the End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'East River Development Alliance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Scores', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Community Service Society of New York', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-12T03:21:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca White', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'White', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/276856fc-3f6e-5534-a27a-0ea2de39e83f', 'word_count': 864, 'uri': 'nyt://article/276856fc-3f6e-5534-a27a-0ea2de39e83f'}, {'abstract': 'We need to know who is funding super PACs before we cast our votes, not after.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/11/where-did-they-get-the-money-for-that/', 'snippet': 'We need to know who is funding super PACs before we cast our votes, not after.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past few weeks, voters in early primary and caucus states have been deluged by political advertising. Some of the ads are pure hagiography, while others are slashing. Disclaimers tell viewers which candidate or group with a soothing name is responsible in each case. But even as they choose from among the Republican presidential candidates, voters haven’t been able to find out who is really behind the spots – who has been putting up the big money it takes to make and air these messages.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Did They Get the Money For That?', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizens United v Federal Election Commission (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject