 None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a9ff468-a237-53c1-8896-240e28da3d38', 'word_count': 76, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a9ff468-a237-53c1-8896-240e28da3d38'}, {'abstract': 'Why three decades of exemplary behavior behind bars have not earned the Brink’s robbery accomplice her freedom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/magazine/judith-clarks-radical-transformation.html', 'snippet': 'Why three decades of exemplary behavior behind bars have not earned the Brink’s robbery accomplice her freedom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Oct. 20, 1981, a band of militant zealots armed with automatic weapons tried to rob a Brink’s truck in a shopping mall in Nanuet in Rockland County, N.Y. Before it was over, two armored-car guards were shot and two police officers — one black and one white — were gunned down at a roadblock. The crime was one of the last spasms of ’60s-style, left-wing violence. To the militants, it was an “expropriation” for something they called the Republic of New Afrika, a place that existed mainly in their fevered dreams.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15clark1_span/mag-15clark-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15clark1_span/mag-15clark-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15clark1_span/mag-15clark-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15clark1_span/mag-15clark-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15clark1_span/mag-15clark-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Judith Clark’s Radical Transformation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Judith Clark’s Radical Transformation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bedford Hills Correctional Facility', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weathermen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Judith', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nanuet (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boudin, Kathy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nyack (NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Brink's Inc", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-12T10:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Robbins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e56b940-e726-505c-9c6e-8c8a2ccf0c75', 'word_count': 6188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e56b940-e726-505c-9c6e-8c8a2ccf0c75'}, {'abstract': 'In South Carolina, they know who their enemy is. They’re just too busy attacking one another to fight him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/magazine/tea-party-south-carolina.html', 'snippet': 'In South Carolina, they know who their enemy is. They’re just too busy attacking one another to fight him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I met Karen Martin, a few days before New Year’s, at a cafe in Greenville, the hub of conservative politics in South Carolina. A 54-year-old refugee from the North Shore of Massachusetts, Martin is the lead organizer of the nearby Spartanburg Tea Party. Another Tea Party leader described her to me as a grown-up, and in fact, Martin turned out to be the kind of activist — ideology notwithstanding — who makes you feel hopeful about the new age of political uprising. She recounted how she burst into tears at the moment she realized, watching the news in 2008, that children growing up today wouldn’t have the economic opportunities that she did. She talked about how the Tea Party would need to mature and become more politically sophisticated in the years ahead. “I think the movement is just too young and too emotional,” she said.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15primary3/15primary3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15primary3/15primary3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15primary3/15primary3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15primary3/15primary3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15primary3/15primary3-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight':