': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Letterman, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pasadena (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-12T21:51:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcf9dfda-c7d7-5b98-bfdf-c5094c25d918', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcf9dfda-c7d7-5b98-bfdf-c5094c25d918'}, {'abstract': 'Jay Mandelbaum, head of strategy and business development, is leaving JPMorgan. Barry Zubrow, who was a crucial player for the bank during the financial crisis, will head corporate regulatory affairs.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/12/jpmorgan-shuffles-management-ranks/', 'snippet': 'Jay Mandelbaum, head of strategy and business development, is leaving JPMorgan. Barry Zubrow, who was a crucial player for the bank during the financial crisis, will head corporate regulatory affairs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jamie Dimon has again shuffled his senior management team.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'JPMorgan Shuffles Management Ranks', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dimon, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mandelbaum, Jay', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-12T21:54:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ab1c4f6-e47a-55ae-9425-d2a5cecf7ac1', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ab1c4f6-e47a-55ae-9425-d2a5cecf7ac1'}, {'abstract': 'Two Guggenheim shows draw on 1960s paintings and art objects from the museum’s collections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/arts/design/pop-objects-and-1960s-monochrome-at-guggenheim-review.html', 'snippet': 'Two Guggenheim shows draw on 1960s paintings and art objects from the museum’s collections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a 1965 essay on postwar science-fiction films Susan Sontag noted a common ingredient: a “depersonalization” of the characters, a kind of emotionless zombie state that occurred when their minds were taken over by aliens or robots. Some of this, she wrote, reflected the basic conditions of modern life. But it also pointed toward something new in the nuclear era. Now humans had to contend not only with the fear of death but also the “psychologically insupportable” possibility of a “collective incineration and extinction which could come at any time, virtually without warning.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/arts/13POP1/13POP1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 793, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s the ’60s; Detach Yourself', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None,