s. When the British government tried to ban Strauss’s “Salome” in 1910, the conductor Thomas Beecham appealed successfully and led a London run that was the hottest ticket of the season. Time was when the label “banned in Boston” helped guarantee Broadway ticket sales. And after religious figures inveighed against the 1979 Monty Python film, “Life of Brian,” John Cleese said in a television interview that “these people have made me a very rich man.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/arts/13SUN_SPAN/13SUN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/13/arts/13SUN_SPAN/13SUN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/arts/13SUN_SPAN/SUN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/arts/13SUN_SPAN/13SUN_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/13/arts/13SUN_SPAN/13SUN_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Irresistible Allure Of ‘Banned in Beijing’', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Irresistible Allure Of ‘Banned in Beijing’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Le Poisson Rouge', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huang Ruo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Opera', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-12T23:44:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/71ec383e-14aa-51cd-8851-7f3a706d7bd7', 'word_count': 586, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71ec383e-14aa-51cd-8851-7f3a706d7bd7'}, {'abstract': '“On a Clear Day You Can See Forever” is closing after two and a half months, making way for “Leap of Faith” to open at the St. James Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/12/as-clear-day-clears-out-leap-of-faith-will-leap-onto-broadway/', 'snippet': '“On a Clear Day You Can See Forever” is closing after two and a half months, making way for “Leap of Faith” to open at the St. James Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a move unusual by Broadway standards, the producers of the new musical “Leap of Faith” said on Thursday that the show would begin performances on April 3 at the St. James Theater, now home to the Harry Connick, Jr., musical “On a Clear Day You Can See Forever.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As ‘Clear Day’ Clears Out, ‘Leap of Faith’ Will Leap Onto Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Leap of Faith (Play)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'On a Clear Day You Can See Forever (Play)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Esparza, Raul', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leight, Warren', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': '