'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Woman Struck by a School Bus in Queens', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Woman Struck by a School Bus in Queens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jamaica (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T03:28:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b34da9e-dc84-5a80-bf9c-c924a20b8835', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b34da9e-dc84-5a80-bf9c-c924a20b8835'}, {'abstract': 'The Japanese cabinet resigned en masse on Friday, a move that is common to push a new policy initiative. Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda apparently hopes to convince the opposition to join his push to raise the national sales tax.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/world/asia/japan-prime-minister-shuffles-cabinet.html', 'snippet': 'The Japanese cabinet resigned en masse on Friday, a move that is common to push a new policy initiative. Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda apparently hopes to convince the opposition to join his push to raise the national sales tax.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Japanese cabinet resigned en masse on Friday, in a routine step that allows Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda to announce a new lineup later in the day. Such reshufflings are common in Japan when a leader wants to push a new policy initiative, and Mr. Noda apparently hopes to convince the opposition to join his push to raise the national sales tax. Mr. Noda, a fiscal conservative, has pledged to submit a bill to the next session of Parliament to raise the national sales tax, currently 5 percent, in an effort to rein in Japan’s ballooning national debt, one of the largest among developed nations. It is unclear how many of his 17 ministers he will actually replace, but local news reports said he was expected to leave about a dozen in place. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan: Prime Minister Shuffles Cabinet', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan: Prime Minister Shuffles Cabinet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales and Excise Taxes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Noda, Yoshihiko', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T03:29:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dda9f2c-fc6e-5b5c-9a00-ac5a9c757ae6', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dda9f2c-fc6e-5b5c-9a00-ac5a9c757ae6'}, {'abstract': 'A court ruling confirmed that the Redneck Shop, run by a white supremacist, is in a building that belongs to a black Baptist pastor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/us/in-laurens-sc-the-redneck-shop-and-its-neighbor.html', 'snippet': 'A court ruling confirmed that the Redneck Shop, run by a white supremacist, is in a building that belongs to a black Baptist pastor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAURENS, S.C. — The Redneck Shop has been selling Confederate bikinis and white satin robes on the historic courthouse square in this former mill town for so long that most people have learned to ignore it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/us/KLAN-1/KLAN-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/13/us/KLAN-1/KLAN-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/us/KLAN-1/KLAN-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/us/KLAN-1/KLAN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/13/us/KLAN-1/KLAN-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Uneasy Neighbors in a Southern Gothic Tale', 'kicker': 'Laurens Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Uneasy Neighbors in a Southern Gothic Tale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ku Klux Klan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T03:31:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson and Robbie Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robbie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f419d95-f332-5511-9f5a-26eec4818480', 'word_count': 1283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f419d95-f332-5511-9f5a-26eec4818480'}, {'abstract': 'The withdrawal of another Army combat brigade comes as the United States military shifts its focus on the Asia-Pacific region while sustaining a strong presence in the Middle East, Defense Secretary Leon E. Panetta said Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/world/europe/germany-us-will-withdraw-combat-brigade.html', 'snippet': 'The withdrawal of another Army combat brigade comes as the United States military shifts its focus on the Asia-Pacific region while sustaining a strong presence in the Middle East, Defense Secretary Leon E. Panetta said Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The United States will withdraw another Army combat brigade from Germany, leaving just two brigades in Europe as the military shifts to a new strategy that focuses on the Asia-Pacific region while sustaining a strong presence in the Middle East, Defense Secretary Leon E. Panetta said Thursday. He said the United States was not abandoning Europe, and would sustain sufficient forces there to maintain ties with allies. He described the changes in an interview on Thursday with American Forces Press Service, the Pentagon’s media branch. In 2004, the Bush administration ordered that the number of Army combat brigades in Europe be cut to two from four. Last year, the Obama administration decided to retain a third brigade in Europe, but now has decided to leave only one brigade in Germany and one in Italy. A brigade has about 4,000 soldiers. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Germany: U.S. Will Withdraw Combat Brigade', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Germany: U.S. Will Withdraw Combat Brigade', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T03:34:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fabd9526-5934-56f6-9f0f-93638559ac3b', 'word_count': 145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fabd9526-5934-56f6-9f0f-93638559ac3b'}, {'abstract': 'Neville Bardos, a 12-year-old horse who competes in eventing, has managed a comeback from severe injuries sustained in a fire.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/01/13/sports/13horse_ss.html', 'snippet': 'Neville Bardos, a 12-year-old horse who competes in eventing, has managed a comeback from severe injuries sustained in a fire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Neville Bardos, a 12-year-old horse who competes in eventing, has managed a comeback from severe injuries sustained in a fire.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/sports/13horse_ss-slide-45JC/13horse_ss-slide-45JC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'c