y', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T13:41:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler and Annie Lowrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/791c755a-a16f-5212-aa67-0eabc2460de2', 'word_count': 1099, 'uri': 'nyt://article/791c755a-a16f-5212-aa67-0eabc2460de2'}, {'abstract': 'None so blind as they that will not see.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/bubble-memories-2/', 'snippet': 'None so blind as they that will not see.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hahahaheehee! The Fed transcripts from 2006 show a lot of laughter and an incredible amount of complacency, with people mainly worried about inflation rather than the coming recession.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bubble Memories', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T13:46:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e82dbe9-eef4-5481-906d-3eb8fb5fd161', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e82dbe9-eef4-5481-906d-3eb8fb5fd161'}, {'abstract': 'Also, some hard-edged tweets attacking Ron Paul from Rick Perry’s son, and Michelle Obama joins Twitter.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/the-breakfast-meeting-colbert-for-president-and-iphone-riots-in-china/', 'snippet': 'Also, some hard-edged tweets attacking Ron Paul from Rick Perry’s son, and Michelle Obama joins Twitter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The political performance art known as the Stephen Colbert campaign for president came a step closer to reality last night on “The Colbert Report” on Comedy Central, as Mr. Colbert transferred control of his “super PAC” — Americans for a Better Tomorrow, Tomorrow – to his friend and late-night lead-in Jon Stewart, Brian Stelter reported. Mr. Colbert’s lawyer explained: “You cannot be a candidate and run a super PAC. That would be coordinating with yourself.” Having a good friend run it, however, would be a different story, he said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Colbert for President? and iPhone Riots in China', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colbert, Stephen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Ron', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, Jon', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Comedy Central', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T13:53:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f3d8620-7ba2-5d0a-b25d-c8dfb306f554', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f3d8620-7ba2-5d0a-b25d-c8dfb306f554'}, {'abstract': 'Melinda Gates, just back from a trip to Bangladesh, along with Nicholas Kristof, answer readers’ questions about the developing world.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/melinda-gates-answers-questions-part-iii/', 'snippet': 'Melinda Gates, just back from a trip to Bangladesh, along with Nicholas Kristof, answer readers’ questions about the developing world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ms. Gates, of the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation, recently returned from a three-day trip to Bangladesh. She, along with Nick, agreed to answer readers’ questions about development issues focusing on that part of the world. Here is the third of three installments.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Melinda Gates Answers Questions, Part III', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T13:54:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e338c28-098d-56fb-b2ec-5969cf5dbe18', 'word_count': 1494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e338c28-098d-56fb-b2ec-5969cf5dbe18'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers in the Netherlands found risk was highest among people from Suriname, the Netherlands Antilles, Turkey and Morocco who immigrated before age 4.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/health/research/mental-health-study-links-immigrating-at-young-age-and-higher-risk-of-psychosis.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers in the Netherlands found risk was highest among people from Suriname, the Netherlands Antilles, Turkey and Morocco who immigrated before age 4.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new study has found that among immigrants, younger age at the time of migration predicts a higher incidence of psychotic disorders.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mental Health: Study Links Immigrating at Young Age and Higher Risk of Psychosis', 'kicker': 'Vital Signs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mental Health: Psychosis Is Linked to Early Immigration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Journal of Psychiatry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T13:58:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Bakalar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakalar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1792f14-dafe-5959-a793-35e939b745b7', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1792f14-dafe-5959-a793-35e939b745b7'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Kantrowitz, an expert on paying for college and the founder of FinAid.org, is answering questions from readers of The Choice about financial aid applications this week.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/kantrowitz-answers-part-5/', 'snippet': 'Mark Kantrowitz, an expert on paying for college and the founder of FinAid.org, is answering questions from readers of The Choice about financial aid applications this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this fifth round of responses to reader questions about financial aid, Mark Kantrowitz, founder of the Web sites finaid.org and fastweb.com, addresses privacy issues, when to file and the rules concerning tuition assistance from grandparents and other relatives.Mr. Kantrowitz is no longer accepting questions. His answers will conclude Saturday. (Questions may have been edited).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Part 5: Answers on the Fafsa, the Free Application for Federal Student Aid', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Kantrowitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kantrowitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12e05cf1-ebcf-58a5-bdc8-85570d1adc4b', 'word_count': 2246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12e05cf1-ebcf-58a5-bdc8-85570d1adc4b'}, {'abstract': 'Romney’s effort to make Bush look honest.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/untruths-wholly-untrue-and-nothing-but-untruths/', 'snippet': 'Romney’s effort to make Bush look honest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was deeply radicalized by the 2000 election. At first I couldn’t believe that then-candidate George W. Bush was saying so many clearly, provably false things; then I couldn’t believe that nobody in the news media was willing to point out the lies. (At the time, the Times actually told me that I couldn’t use the l-word either). That was when I formulated my “views differ on shape of planet” motto.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Untruths, Wholly Untrue, And Nothing But Untruths', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1826df55-c0db-54a5-bc50-b625af978cac', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1826df55-c0db-54a5-bc50-b625af978cac'}, {'abstract': 'A Paris-based swimwear line collaborates with A.P.C. to create an 11-piece capsule collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/the-get-tooshie-a-p-c-bathing-suit/', 'snippet': 'A Paris-based swimwear line collaborates with A.P.C. to create an 11-piece capsule collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tooshie, the Paris-based swimwear line devoted to making bums look beautiful, has now partnered with A.P.C., the Francophile favorite, to create an 11-piece capsule collection. Included in the offering are powder blue trunks for men and this psychedelic one-piece for women. “I like the idea of an eccentric print on a very simple bathing suit,” says A.P.C.’s designer Jean Touitou. “Often you see prints like this on terrible shiny materials. The idea was to find a nice matte one.” As for the fit, Tooshie’s designer Natascha Sayn Wittgenstein took care of that: “It’s not about focusing on shapes — cover this, reveal that — but keeping it elegant with these small pieces of fabric,” she says. Jump in!A.P.C + Tooshie swimsuit, $280. Go to apc.fr.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Get | Tooshie + A.P.C. Bathing Suit', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathing Suits', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85fe2bb8-4a9b-5467-b41a-ca19082c2aa1', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85fe2bb8-4a9b-5467-b41a-ca19082c2aa1'}, {'abstract': 'Kristen Wiig and Paul Feig talk about “Bridesmaids” and how to make a comedy about a character who’s actually sad and lost.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/kristen-wiig-on-the-sad-truth-about-bridesmaids/', 'snippet': 'Kristen Wiig and Paul Feig talk about “Bridesmaids” and how to make a comedy about a character who’s actually sad and lost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s deadline day for Oscar nomination ballots and the Bagger hopes there are still a few stray undecideds out there who might be swayed to vote in the “Bridesmaids” direction. The film picked up best comedy at the Critics’ Choice Awards on Thursday night and its producer, Judd Apatow, had an unprintable message for people who think that women can’t be funny.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kristen Wiig on the Sad Truth About ‘Bridesmaids’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bridesmaids (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiig, Kristen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:00:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f00688e8-8a35-5558-b408-211c3ab6292f', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f00688e8-8a35-5558-b408-211c3ab6292f'}, {'abstract': 'Conceptual artist Yoko Ono’s first show in India asks viewers to participate.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/at-yokos-show-funeral-pyres-and-tributes-to-mommy/', 'snippet': 'Conceptual artist Yoko Ono’s first show in India asks viewers to participate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yoko Ono’s first exhibition in India, called “Our Beautiful Daughters,” at the Vadehra Art Gallery in New Delhi, could also be called “Art Under Construction.”\xa0 All seven exhibits in the show, which opened Thursday night, ask the spectators to participate in them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Yoko’s Show, Funeral Pyres and Tributes to Mommy', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ono, Yoko', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:06:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malavika Vyawahare', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malavika', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vyawahare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2d07b78-962b-53aa-b23e-121100f1942f', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2d07b78-962b-53aa-b23e-121100f1942f'}, {'abstract': 'Nelson Seda aspires to be a genuine Bronx B-boy even if he was born a quarter of a century too late.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/living-his-passion-for-hip-hop/', 'snippet': 'Nelson Seda aspires to be a genuine Bronx B-boy even if he was born a quarter of a century too late.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A paint-flecked floor is Nelson Seda’s stage. When the weather is too cold or wet, he clears the chairs from his bedroom, turns up the music and dances, his arms and legs a blur of impossibly graceful angles. An hour later, he may pick up his markers and draw – on canvases, old posters, shoeboxes, anything. And for good measure, he might end the day freestyle rapping.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Living His Passion for Hip-Hop', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rock Steady Crew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:21:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a410aae4-bed3-5a59-8873-44740a0351bf', 'word_count': 732, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a410aae4-bed3-5a59-8873-44740a0351bf'}, {'abstract': 'Leading Off: The N.F.L. gets a classic good vs. evil matchup when the Broncos bring Tebowmania to New England on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/leading-off-tebows-moment-of-truth/', 'snippet': 'Leading Off: The N.F.L. gets a classic good vs. evil matchup when the Broncos bring Tebowmania to New England on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One week into the N.F.L. playoffs, the most entertaining story of the season travels to the place where fun goes to die: the Tim Tebow phenomenon rolls into New England on Saturday. It’s the hero versus the hoodie, an N.F.L. morality tale in four quarters. You thought last week’s Broncos victory over the Steelers was big? That was just a warm-up. It is possible, if the N.F.L. maneuvers the levers it has implanted in everyone’s heads just right, the entire universe will be watching the Broncos-Patriots game.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: Tebow’s Moment of Truth', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANTHONY, CARMELO', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Belichick, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cammalleri, Mike', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elway, John', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fisher, Jeff', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Horford, Al', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Namath, Joe', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roy, Patrick', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Bob', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Denver Broncos', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Dolphins', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Montreal Canadiens', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 20, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 21, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 22, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Steelers', 'rank': 23, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Louis Rams', 'rank': 24, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:41:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10cd2c0c-a70b-5aa8-8b49-2a088c10f619', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10cd2c0c-a70b-5aa8-8b49-2a088c10f619'}, {'abstract': 'The undead seem to be everywhere in popular culture — in television series, video games and, it turns out, poetry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/books/zombie-poetry-takes-on-a-life-of-its-own.html', 'snippet': 'The undead seem to be everywhere in popular culture — in television series, video games and, it turns out, poetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Times may be tough, but if you happen to be a zombie, the future looks bright. Never have the undead seemed more alive. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/14/arts/14zombie-span/14zombie-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/14/arts/14zombie-span/14zombie-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/14/arts/14zombie-span/14zombie-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/14/arts/14zombie-span/14zombie-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/14/arts/14zombie-span/14zombie-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Rhymes With ‘Undead’? Some Poets Know', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Rhymes With ‘Undead’? Some Poets Know', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romero, George A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckett, Tom', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Max', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:47:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae99c3e8-5903-56e0-ab4c-c4b64c404469', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae99c3e8-5903-56e0-ab4c-c4b64c404469'}, {'abstract': 'The Soho House Group announced on Friday that it had sold a majority stake to Mr. Burkle’s Yucaipa Companies and named the billionaire investor as its new chairman.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/burkle-buys-control-of-soho-house/', 'snippet': 'The Soho House Group announced on Friday that it had sold a majority stake to Mr. Burkle’s Yucaipa Companies and named the billionaire investor as its new chairman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It appears that Ronald W. Burkle isn’t content to simply buy a membership for the Soho House line of luxury clubs. Instead, he’s buying control of the company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Burkle Buys Control of Soho House', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burkle, Ronald W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Soho House', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yucaipa Cos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T14:53:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19e37d77-f373-591d-91a6-b512064b968b', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19e37d77-f373-591d-91a6-b512064b968b'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/what-were-reading-351/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'America’s Test Kitchen: A good explanation of why we heat up the oil in a pan before cooking. — Julia Moskin', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T15:05:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51205154-499f-5810-9e72-d82d3113fa2e', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51205154-499f-5810-9e72-d82d3113fa2e'}, {'abstract': 'A “paper pail” design suited for transporting raw oysters or cheap lo mein.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/magazine/the-chinese-takeout-container-is-uniquely-american.html', 'snippet': 'A “paper pail” design suited for transporting raw oysters or cheap lo mein.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Chinese-takeout container, with its Japanese-influenced origami folds, is a uniquely American invention. On Nov. 13, 1894, in Chicago, the inventor Frederick Weeks Wilcox patented a version of what he called a “paper pail,” which was a single piece of paper, creased into segments and folded into a (more or less) leakproof container secured with a dainty wire handle on top. The supportive folds on the outside, fastened with that same wire, created a flat interior surface over which food could slide smoothly onto a plate.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15wmt1_span/15wmt1_span-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 464, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15wmt1_span/15wmt1_span-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 464}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15wmt1_span/mag-15WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 969, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15wmt1_span/mag-15WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15wmt1_span/mag-15WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Chinese-Takeout Container Is Uniquely American', 'kicker': 'Who Made That?', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Chinese-Takeout Container)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Containers and Packaging', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese Food (Cuisine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T15:16:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum and Dana Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce5d5162-4ab8-564f-b6dc-bf138b2270cf', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce5d5162-4ab8-564f-b6dc-bf138b2270cf'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/magazine/reply-all-paying-college-athletes.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I certainly agree with Joe Nocera that basketball and football players are treated like chattel in the present system. Denying players a basic stipend when coaches are making ridiculous amounts is the height of inequity. But paying football and men’s basketball players isn’t feasible, given Title IX regulations and politics. If you disconnect moneymaking sports, women’s sports will very likely lose financing. Paying basketball and football players will not address the core problem of an unsustainable arms race in college sports. The proposition that the N.C.A.A. is in charge and can control college sports is a canard. If the N.C.A.A. pushes too hard, top conferences could create their own basketball playoff and challenge the N.C.A.A. tournament, which is currently a huge windfall for the N.C.A.A. Then the decentralization of power that began with the Supreme Court decision will only get worse. Your proposal offers an important contribution to the debate by arguing for stipends to players, the right to counsel and a defined scholarship term and the opportunity for lifetime health insurance. But getting there isn’t going to be easy.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Let’s Start Paying College Athletes', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let’s Start Paying College Athletes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T15:16:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b4375a8-c640-5236-822d-cbae57863efa', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b4375a8-c640-5236-822d-cbae57863efa'}, {'abstract': 'Writers of the Franzen Generation have decided that the purpose of fiction is to make us feel less alone. Will that be enough to save the novel?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/magazine/why-write-novels-at-all.html', 'snippet': 'Writers of the Franzen Generation have decided that the purpose of fiction is to make us feel less alone. Will that be enough to save the novel?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, I found myself mildly obsessed with a cache of YouTube clips, featuring the novelists Jeffrey Eugenides, Jonathan Franzen, Zadie Smith, David Foster Wallace and Nathan Englander at a 2006 literary conference in Italy called Le Conversazioni. Part of what interested me, in a gate-crashing kind of way, was the backdrop: midsummer on the Isle of Capri, with flora aflame and a sky the color of Chablis. Another part, inevitably, was watching Wallace with the knowledge that he would kill himself two years later. Mostly what I kept coming back to, though, was how lighthearted, how loose — how young — these writers seemed here. It’s not that they weren’t already an accomplished quintet, with a Pulitzer Prize and a National Book Award to their credit. But in 2006 the gravitational center of Anglo-American letters still lay back on U.S. soil with Don DeLillo and Cormac McCarthy and John Updike and Toni Morrison and Philip Roth — those towering figures who, according to a Times survey released earlier that year, produced the greatest American fiction of the previous quarter-century. By comparison, Le Conversazioni might as well have been “The Breakfast Club” and Capri a weirdly paradisiacal high-school library.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15riff_span/mag-15riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 94, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15riff_span/mag-15riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 94}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15riff_span/mag-15riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 161, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15riff_span/15riff_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15riff_span/15riff_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Why Write Novels at All?’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Why Write Novels at All?’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Zadie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eugenides, Jeffrey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, David Foster', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franzen, Jonathan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T15:16:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Garth Risk Hallberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Garth', 'middlename': 'Risk', 'lastname': 'Hallberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b57c032c-63e2-510e-aef8-e88ad85f5daf', 'word_count': 1853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b57c032c-63e2-510e-aef8-e88ad85f5daf'}, {'abstract': 'A sampling of National Football League face masks', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/01/15/magazine/nine-of-a-kind-football-helmets.html', 'snippet': 'A sampling of National Football League face masks', 'lead_paragraph': 'A sampling of National Football League face masks', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grills', 'kicker': 'Nine of a Kind', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T15:16:48+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a37694df-932c-5e12-83e9-1cab69af9139', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a37694df-932c-5e12-83e9-1cab69af9139'}, {'abstract': 'The Google executive who helped start the Egyptian uprising takes stock.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/magazine/wael-ghonim-has-no-regrets.html', 'snippet': 'The Google executive who helped start the Egyptian uprising takes stock.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a Dubai-based Google executive, you created a Facebook page that helped foment the Egyptian uprising. Your memoir, “Revolution 2.0,” recounts how you spent 11 days imprisoned during some of the most dramatic Tahrir Square protests. Major letdown, I imagine. It’s not a matter of enjoying the process as much as looking forward to the result, which is actually why most of the people went down to the streets. When I was released on Feb. 7, I saw Egyptians had managed to stand up together. I felt like I had been jailed for 11 years, but I did not feel I really missed the whole thing.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15talk/mag-15Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 462, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15talk/mag-15Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/magazine/15talk/mag-15Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wael Ghonim Has No Regrets', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I.T. Guy To The Revolution', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghonim, Wael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T15:16:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/113d92c8-0fa4-5514-9b40-86d8d19e25cb', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/113d92c8-0fa4-5514-9b40-86d8d19e25cb'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/magazine/reply-all-fat-trap.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Finally, some medical research on weight gain and the difficulty in losing it and keeping it off that does not blame, shame and condemn the person. Get this article to Oprah, Dr. Oz and those bullies on TV’s “Biggest Loser.” ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fat Trap', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fat Trap', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T15:16:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a13729d9-72a8-5632-9fd7-2e4c8e56e134', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a13729d9-72a8-5632-9fd7-2e4c8e56e134'}, {'abstract': 'Donor’s remorse; taboo titles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/magazine/ethicist-donor-remorse.html', 'snippet': 'Donor’s remorse; taboo titles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a very broke and self-supporting 20-year-old college senior, I donated my eggs in exchange for money. It was a horrendous experience, and I rarely speak about it. Although I assume that some children resulted, I regret my participation and do not wish to know the results. I am now 35, engaged and planning to start a family. Must I tell my fiancé that there may be teenagers walking around with my genetic material? NAME WITHHELD', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Must I Tell My Fiancé?', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Must I Tell My Fiancee', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'In Vitro Fertilization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infertility', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T15:16:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a92260d5-6b86-5b