6:27:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5517dd2-57d7-50c5-9962-3b021b319328', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5517dd2-57d7-50c5-9962-3b021b319328'}, {'abstract': 'Once their toddler really starts talking, there will be no hiding the fact that this couple is less devout than his parents.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/parental-quandary-lying-to-my-in-laws-about-church/', 'snippet': 'Once their toddler really starts talking, there will be no hiding the fact that this couple is less devout than his parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The truth is, S. and her husband have been lying. Fibbing, anyway. Stretching the truth. When her husband’s father, a minister, calls, they make sure to refer to a recent church service. And when her husbands’ parents visit, suddenly, the topic of “choosing a church home” comes off the to-do list and into the midst of the conversation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parental Quandary: Lying to My In-Laws About Church', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T16:29:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier':