ction_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Alderman and Rachel Donadio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donadio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44ef091d-6116-574c-9c33-3f728fab7855', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44ef091d-6116-574c-9c33-3f728fab7855'}, {'abstract': 'Sixteen-year-old girls with cancer are the heroines of two very different novels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/the-tenacity-of-hope.html', 'snippet': 'Sixteen-year-old girls with cancer are the heroines of two very different novels.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I am not a mathematician, but I know this,” says Hazel Grace Lancaster, the narrator of “The Fault in Our Stars,” the latest novel by John Green, a Michael L. Printz medalist and author of several best-\xadselling novels for young adults. “There are infinite numbers between zero and one. There’s .1 and .12 and .112 and an infinite collection of others.” The trouble, she says, is, “I want more numbers than I’m likely to get.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15STANDIFORD/15STANDIFORD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15STANDIFORD/15STANDIFORD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15STANDIFORD/15STANDIFORD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15STANDIFORD/15STANDIFORD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15STANDIFORD/15STANDIFORD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tenacity of Hope', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Tenacity of Hope', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:44:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalie Standiford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Standiford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/132ce05e-9a5d-5b68-b1c4-84c747a54714', 'word_count': 1124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/132ce05e-9a5d-5b68-b1c4-84c747a54714'}, {'abstract': 'The Oklahoma City bombing echoes throughout Eileen Pollack’s novel of family strife.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/breaking-and-entering-by-eileen-pollack-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Oklahoma City bombing echoes throughout Eileen Pollack’s novel of family strife.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eileen Pollack’s new novel, “Breaking and Entering,” takes place in rural Michigan in 1995 — the epicenter and high point of the militia movement, before increased scrutiny and revulsion at the Oklahoma City bombing put some militia groups out of business and sent others underground. (Though not a militiaman, the bomber Timothy McVeigh attended their meetings and spent time on a Michigan farm with his fellow conspirator Terry Nichols.) The Oklahoma City attack comes about a third of the way through Pollack’s book, a real-world event that informs and shadows the fictional ones.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/Thompson-sub/Thompson-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 927, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/Thompson-sub/Thompson-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/Thompson-sub/Thompson-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Shadows of Oklahoma City', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Our Fellow Americans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pollack, Eileen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:45:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean Thompson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thompson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/59fedef1-e1b3-589b-a6c1-af0baab4ede0', 'word_count': 1104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59fedef1-e1b3-589b-a6c1-af0baab4ede0'}, {'abstract': 'A lawsuit in India demands that Internet companies screen content posted on sites like Facebook, Google or Yahoo, that might offend the religious sentiments of Indians. A related criminal case accuses the companies — 21 in all — of violating an Indian law that applies to books, pamphlets and other material that is deemed to “deprave or corrupt.”', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/india-grapples-with-web-censorship/', 'snippet': 'A lawsuit in India demands that Internet companies screen content posted on sites like Facebook, Google or Yahoo, that might offend the religious sentiments of Indians. A related criminal case accuses the companies — 21 in all — of violating an In...', 'lead_paragraph': 'India has long faced an uneasy tension between allowing free expression to its citizens and staunching sectarian violence among its people. It was one of the first countries to ban “The Satanic Verses” by Salman Rushdie. Lawsuits forced the Indian painter Maqbool Fida Husain to live in exile during the last several years of his life. An academic book about the 17th-century warrior known as Shivaji was banned for fear of offending Shivaji’s modern-day fans, until the Supreme Court lifted the prohibition. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/13/technology/bits/13bits-goddess/13bits-goddess-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 425, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'India Grapples With Web Censorship', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:45:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Somini Sengupta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Somini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sengupta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd2e8246-5f65-58bf-b4e8-d71732bf48fe', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd2e8246-5f65-58bf-b4e8-d71732bf48fe'}, {'abstract': 'Gil Scott-Heron disliked being called “the godfather of rap,” but his spoken-word performances pointed the way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/the-last-holiday-a-memoir-by-gil-scott-heron-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Gil Scott-Heron disliked being called “the godfather of rap,” but his spoken-word performances pointed the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gil Scott-Heron grew up surrounded by heroism. He distrusted empty promises and easy solutions. He also had a very hard time with himself. Whether you expect a memoir to explain how the third sentence relates to the first two will determine how you feel about this posthumous book.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15RATLIF/15RATLIF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 961, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15RATLIF/15RATLIF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15RATLIF/15RATLIF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gil Scott-Heron: The Godfather of Rap', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Godfather of Rap', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott-Heron, Gil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:45:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8e7291e-26cb-570d-b9a0-cc26ce253228', 'word_count': 1291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8e7291e-26cb-570d-b9a0-cc26ce253228'}, {'abstract': 'The scientific and social forces that brought midlife America to its current state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/in-our-prime-the-invention-of-middle-age-by-patricia-cohen-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The scientific and social forces that brought midlife America to its current state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An upbeat look at middle age? Patricia Cohen had her work cut out for her. Maybe there’s a capitalist culture somewhere that doesn’t make a blazing fetish of youthfulness and keep it stoked with huge amounts of commercial and medical hot air. If so, it’s not the one we live in. Yet nothing in the sometimes pathetic struggle of Americans over, say, 50 to claim a respectable place in the public consciousness puts Cohen in the grumpy mood she could so rightly claim after absorbing some 200 years of the history of middle age. Instead, she’s delivered a lively, well-researched chronicle of the social and scientific forces that brought midlife America to its current befuddled state — better off by most measures than any previous generation, but miserably out of fashion.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15shapiro-img/15shapiro-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15shapiro-img/15shapiro-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15shapiro-img/15shapiro-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 638, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15shapiro-img/15shapiro-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15shapiro-img/15shapiro-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What It Means to Be Middle Aged', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Middle of the Journey', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Patrica', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:46:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Shapiro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shapiro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/be21a3c7-6592-553f-87eb-d2ff1f5f3011', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be21a3c7-6592-553f-87eb-d2ff1f5f3011'}, {'abstract': 'Caitlin Flanagan offers observations on the passage to adulthood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/girl-land-by-caitlin-flanagan-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Caitlin Flanagan offers observations on the passage to adulthood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gather around, ladies, Caitlin Flanagan is back. And this time she’s after your daughters.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15KELLER/15KELLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15KELLER/15KELLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15KELLER/15KELLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Among the Adolescents', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Not Yet a Woman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flanagan, Caitlin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2