': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/arts/15SUBMOOD1_SPAN/15SUBMOOD1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/arts/15SUBMOOD1_SPAN/15SUBMOOD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/arts/15SUBMOOD1_SPAN/15SUBMOOD1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Perfectly Happy, Even Without Happy Endings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Perfectly Happy, Even Without Happy Endings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:49:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carrie Rickey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carrie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rickey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1ed41ff-b57a-550c-addd-f0ebc027c846', 'word_count': 1517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1ed41ff-b57a-550c-addd-f0ebc027c846'}, {'abstract': 'How Friedrich Nietzsche inspired and provoked his American readers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/american-nietzsche-by-jennifer-ratner-rosenhagen-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'How Friedrich Nietzsche inspired and provoked his American readers.', 'lead_paragraph': ' In 1889, when Friedrich Nietzsche suffered the mental collapse that ended his career, he was virtually unknown. Yet by the time of his death in 1900 at the age of 55, he had become the philosophical celebrity of his age. From Russia to America, admirers echoed his estimation of himself as a titanic figure who could alter the course of history: “I am by far the most terrible human being that has existed so far; this does not preclude the possibility that I shall be the most beneficial.” His origins were humble for the role. The son of a small-town Lutheran minister, he steeped himself in classical literature while growing up in eastern Germany. When he was 24, he secured a professorship in Basel, Switzerland, and a few years later published his first book, “The Birth of Tragedy.” Against the common view of the ancient Greeks as the epitome of serene equipoise, Nietzsche emphasized the “Dionysian” excess and frenzy that complemented the “Apollonian” virtues of clarity and repose. The book’s success was limited, and its author was mocked by one leading classicist as an atavist run amok who should “gather tigers and panthers about his knees, but not the youth of Germany.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15STAR1/15STAR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 648, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15STAR1/15STAR1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15STAR1/15STAR1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Friedrich Nietzsche Did to America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Overman', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nietzsche, Friedrich', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:50:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander Star', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Star', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6da3d043-62ae-5f84-a131-c778de65bf3a', 'word_count': 2094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6da3d043-62ae-5f84-a131-c778de65bf3a'}, {'abstract': 'Michel Houellebecq’s novel features an uncommonly successful artist and a gruesome murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/michel-houellebecqs-version-of-the-american-thriller.html', 'snippet': 'Michel Houellebecq’s novel features an uncommonly successful artist and a gruesome murder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michel Houellebecq’s new novel is a weird air plant of a book: it feeds off atmosphere, rather than soil. The climate of “The Map and the Territory” is very still and very strange, as if something bad had just been detonated over it. The same bad thing may also have convulsed France, where the story is set, because Houellebecq’s Paris exists in a state of flagrant social breakdown and the countryside has become an emptied theme park of itself. This “was exactly what the world would look like,” a character thinks on entering a picturesque French village that only second-home-owners can afford to buy into, “after the explosion of an intergalactic neutron bomb.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15SHULEVITZ/15SHULEVITZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15SHULEVITZ/15SHULEVITZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15SHULEVITZ/15SHULEVITZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15SHULEVITZ/15SHULEVITZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15SHULEVITZ/15SHULEVITZ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michel Houellebecq’s Version of the American Thriller', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Homage to Globalization', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Houellebecq, Michel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:51:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Shulevitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shulevitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7961cc9-53e3-56cb-a838-f10cad747b92', 'word_count': 1919, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7961cc9-53e3-56cb-a838-f10cad747b92'}, {'abstract': 'The protagonist of Shalom Auslander’s novel moves to upstate New York for peace and quiet, and finds Anne Frank living in his attic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/hope-a-tragedy-by-shalom-auslander-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The protagonist of Shalom Auslander’s novel moves to upstate New York for peace and quiet, and finds Anne Frank living in his attic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shalom Auslander is my kind of Jew — an unapologetically paranoid, guilt-ridden, self-loathing Diaspora kvetch, enraged by a God he can’t live with or without. While others of his generation may mine the tradition for a fond retrieval of forgotten lore, Auslander throws stones at the fiddler on the roof. He’s a black comic who’s alloyed the manic existential shtick of Lenny Bruce with the gallows humor that’s been a staple of the repertoire since the Babylonian Exile. In his two previous books, “Beware of God,” a collection of wickedly funny if not just plain wicked stories, and “Foreskin’s Lament,” a memoir that torched the bridges to his strict Orthodox heritage, he’s been relentlessly indicting the Hebrew God as a poor role model. In the process he’s made himself both celebrated and notorious: he is patently not good for the Jews. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/Stern/Stern-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1010, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/Stern/Stern-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/Stern/Stern-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Haunted by the Holocaust', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It Isn’t Even Past', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Auslander, Shalom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:52:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By STEVE STERN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'STERN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/55cfa68a-0c32-5484-922c-e6addeb7294a', 'word_count': 1028, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55cfa68a-0c32-5484-922c-e6addeb7294a'}, {'abstract': 'On the night of the Iowa caucuses, the average viewer of Current TV was 36 years old. Yes, there were only 47,000 of them at any given time that evening — but they were youthful, which gives executives hope.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/current-tv-finds-a-good-number-within-its-tiny-ratings/', 'snippet': 'On the night of the Iowa caucuses, the average viewer of Current TV was 36 years old. Yes, there were only 47,000 of them at any given time that evening — but they were youthful, which gives executives hope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Current TV’s audience is small — very small. The channel’s executives freely admit that in conversations, and will probably say so again on Friday when they hold a news conference with TV reporters gathered in Los Angeles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Current TV Finds a Good Number Within Its Tiny Ratings', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gore, Al', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Olbermann, Keith', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Uygur, Cenk', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Current TV', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MSNBC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:52:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91f2a4bf-8e2f-556c-bf65-e5e2e2daa04c', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91f2a4bf-8e2f-556c-bf65-e5e2e2daa04c'}, {'abstract': 'In Adam Johnson’s novel, a young man moves up in Kim Jong-il’s power structure and then becomes a rival of the dictator.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/books/review/the-orphan-masters-son-by-adam-johnson-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In Adam Johnson’s novel, a young man moves up in Kim Jong-il’s power structure and then becomes a rival of the dictator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The title of Adam Johnson’s second novel is a bit misleading. Raised in the Long Tomorrows orphanage in Chongjin, North Korea, his protagonist believes himself to be the son of the Orphan Master rather than some kid dropped off by his desperate parents. But the primary evidence for this belief — “the unrelenting way the Orphan Master singled him out for punishment” — invites other interpretations. Like the rest of the boys, he is given a name from the list of the 114 Grand Martyrs of the Revolution that will mark him as an orphan for the rest of his life. Pak Jun Do (the given name Jun Do is a homonym of “John Doe”) is appropriate for a character with such a shifting identity, someone who will become both the perpetrator and the victim of countless crimes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15beah-img/15beah-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15beah-img/15beah-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/books/review/15beah-img/15beah-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kim Jong-il’s Romantic Rival', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korean Noir', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Il', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Adam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T17:53:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher R. Beha', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Beha', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/845e8e9c-e7f1-5866-bae9-bf62fb8c8193', 'word_count': 921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/845e8e9c-e7f1-5866-bae9-bf62fb8c8193'}, {'abstract': 'The new Mexican film “Miss Bala” reflects that county’s mood of foreboding as it enters its sixth year of a frontal assault on drug traffickers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/movies/gerardo-naranjos-miss-bala-reflects-mexican-drug-war.html', 'snippe