ions', 'value': 'Restore Our Future', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T18:48:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc0f8ec3-7c64-55dc-bbdd-fdd8584828fe', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc0f8ec3-7c64-55dc-bbdd-fdd8584828fe'}, {'abstract': 'In Iraq’s restive Diyala Province, where violence continues despite the U.S. withdrawal, the safest place to live is shoulder to shoulder with the interred inside the provincial capital’s largest cemetery.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/in-iraq-cemetery-living-among-the-dead/', 'snippet': 'In Iraq’s restive Diyala Province, where violence continues despite the U.S. withdrawal, the safest place to live is shoulder to shoulder with the interred inside the provincial capital’s largest cemetery.', 'lead_paragraph': ' BAGHDAD — The largest cemetery in the restive province of Diyala has more than 20,000 graves, some date back as far as 1600 A.D., and covers more than a mile and a half of land.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Iraq Cemetery, Living Among the Dead', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cemeteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baquba (Iraq)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Diyala Province (Iraq)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T18:51:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Omar Al-Jawoshy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Omar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Al-Jawoshy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6466609d-3b0f-5a5d-9ad8-51d906600a9b', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6466609d-3b0f-5a5d-9ad8-51d906600a9b'}, {'abstract': 'Collaboration is in. But it may not be conducive to creativity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/opinion/sunday/the-rise-of-the-new-groupthink.html', 'snippet': 'Collaboration is in. But it may not be conducive to creativity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOLITUDE is out of fashion. Our\xa0companies, our schools and our culture are in thrall to an idea I call the New Groupthink, which holds that creativity and achievement come from an oddly gregarious place. Most of us now work in teams, in offices without walls, for managers who prize people skills above all. Lone geniuses are out. Collaboration is in.\xa0', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15CAINCOVER/15CAINCOVER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15CAINCOVER/15CAINCOVER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 500}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15CAINCOVER/15CAINCOVER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15CAINCOVER/15CAINCOVER-thumbStandard-v2.j