ppeal-to-s-carolina-evangelicals/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney tries to overcome suspicions many Christian conservatives have about him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As conservative Christian leaders struggle to coalesce behind a single candidate of the religious right, the Romney campaign has put out a radio ad in South Carolina that in essence asks the evangelical leaders, “Why not me?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Makes Appeal to South Carolina Evangelicals', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evangelical Movement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bopp, James Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeMint, Jim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glendon, Mary Ann', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T19:29:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Corasaniti', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corasaniti', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12a602b1-2178-5c52-a0b5-16707aca0737', 'word_count': 220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12a602b1-2178-5c52-a0b5-16707aca0737'}, {'abstract': 'Wang’s pre-fall collection is heavy on the separates; a study in wearable sportswear, akin to that of the 1990’s.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/just-one-look-alexander-wang/', 'snippet': 'Wang’s pre-fall collection is heavy on the separates; a study in wearable sportswear, akin to that of the 1990’s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to believe that Alexander Wang, just 28 years old, is presenting his 19th collection. Building off of signature concepts — cutouts, cool-girl black and the use of technical fabrics — Wang’s prefall collection is heavy on the separates; a study in wearable sportswear, akin to that of the 1990s. Pops of maroon (on a printed sheer dress) and a greenish mustard (on a ruched skirt dress) emerge among all of the black, white and gray, carrying over into accessories. A soon to be coveted pointy-toe heel with mesh detailing and tight ankle strap demonstrates Wang’s ability to evolve ideas from one season to the next. (Have a look at his spring shoes and tell us there isn’t a resemblance.) Clarity arrives in the form of a skirt suit bearing the collection’s defining trompe l’oeil plaid.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Alexander Wang', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T19:29:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48647c32-3767-5106-b7b4-ae33d0b9c58b', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48647c32-3767-5106-b7b4-ae33d0b9c58b'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Homeland Security paid a contractor in 2009 to monitor social networking sites for public reaction to a proposal to move detainees from Guantánamo to a local prison, according to documents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/us/federal-security-program-monitored-public-opinion.html', 'snippet': 'The Department of Homeland Security paid a contractor in 2009 to monitor social networking sites for public reaction to a proposal to move detainees from Guantánamo to a local prison, according to documents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Department of Homeland Security paid a contractor in 2009 to monitor social networking sites — like Facebook, blogs and reader comments on a news article — to see how the residents of Standish, Mich., were reacting to a proposal to move detainees from Guantánamo Bay, Cuba, to a local prison there, according to newly disclosed documents.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Federal Contractor Monitored Social Network Sites', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Federal Contractor Monitored Social Network Sites', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guantanamo Bay Naval Base (Cuba)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Homeland Security Department', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T19:30:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline