'Duhigg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6884a856-8f03-5a71-8e65-bc5156fe310b', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6884a856-8f03-5a71-8e65-bc5156fe310b'}, {'abstract': 'The French know how to easily embellish a humble pantry staple.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/well-always-have-lentils/', 'snippet': 'The French know how to easily embellish a humble pantry staple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I spent a recent holiday in Paris appreciating some of the simple things the French do so well. No, not foie gras and Champagne, but rather humbler products, like cured pork and lentils. Nearly every butcher shop sells brined pork (called demi-sel or petit salé), ready to take home and simmer. On a shelf nearby is a box of lentils, usually the certified lentilles du Puy, arguably the best lentils in the world. Cooked together, they are a classic combination, not fancy at all but uncommonly good. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'We’ll Always Have Lentils', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lentils', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T19:54:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tanis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7a2570e-a402-59ba-9b22-b5b44ed261f1', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7a2570e-a402-59ba-9b22-b5b44ed261f1'}, {'abstract': 'Visual announcements can incorporate everything from digital collages to mock movie trailers and even touches of film noir.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/fashion/weddings/save-the-date-announcements-get-creative-with-videos-bookmarks-and-even-puzzles.html', 'snippet': 'Visual announcements can incorporate everything from digital collages to mock movie trailers and even touches of film noir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AT first, it looked like any other e-mail. But when friends and relatives of Katharine Wentz and Christopher Mazza clicked on the Vimeo video link inside the message, they were treated to a “save the date” show.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/fashion/15FIELDNOTES/15FIELDNOTES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1014, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/fashion/15FIELDNOTES/15FIELDNOTES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/fashion/15FIELDNOTES/15FIELDNOTES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Save the Date, but Watch the Movie First', 'kicker': 'Field Notes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Save the Date, But Watch the Movie First', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magnets and Magnetism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Mail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Storage', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Postcards', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T19:55:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e849c7d3-c4ae-5354-aa12-5475f8064a64', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e849c7d3-c4ae-5354-aa12-5475f8064a64'}, {'abstract': 'Talking with Will Reiser, screenwriter of “50/50,” about a cancer survivor based on Mr. Reiser himself.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/for-5050s-writer-the-hardest-part-was-the-drama/', 'snippet': 'Talking with Will Reiser, screenwriter of “50/50,” about a cancer survivor based on Mr. Reiser himself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the gang from “Bridesmaids” was dancing around the line between comedy and emotional honesty, the filmmakers behind “50/50” were stomping on it. The film, inspired by the screenwriter Will Reiser’s real-life story of being given a cancer diagnosis in his mid-20s, treats its subject matter as he did in real life: as dreadfully serious and dreadfully funny. It has been earning some nominations and prizes for its screenplay – and some good-natured ribbing from Mr. Reiser’s friend Seth Rogen, who starred as the best friend and served as a producer with his writing partner, Evan Goldberg. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For ’50/50’s’ Writer, the Hardest Part Was the Drama', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T19:56:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/332001ec-e953-56f4-a5c9-f6ac780c21fa', 'word_count': 1015, 'uri': 'nyt://article/332001ec-e953-56f4-a5c9-f6ac780c21fa'}, {'abstract': 'Abe Frajndlich turned the camera on celebrated subjects who were usually behind it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/01/15/opinion/sunday/15EXPOSURES_ABE.html', 'snippet': 'Abe Frajndlich turned the camera on celebrated subjects who were usually behind it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Abe Frajndlich turned the camera on celebrated subjects who were usually behind it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1013, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Photographing Photographers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T19:57:54+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/efb9c50a-59a4-59dd-b010-fd1dad0cd45d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/efb9c50a-59a4-59dd-b010-fd1dad0cd45d'}, {'abstract': 'A photographer turned the camera on celebrated subjects who were usually behind it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/opinion/sunday/photographing-photographers.html', 'snippet': 'A photographer turned the camera on celebrated subjects who were usually behind it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the fall of 1970 I arrived at Minor White’s house for a live-in workshop. My first task was to organize his photography library. I was mesmerized by the unforgettable images. Photography has had a relatively short history, and I was struck by the fact that most of the practitioners were anonymous and unknown except to a minuscule circle. Unconsciously I must have started to dream of changing that fact, and widening that circle. More than four decades later, this work is the fruition of that dream.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1013, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E/15EXPOSURES_ABE-slide-QK3E-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review