'', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e217db1b-f5b9-5c0d-b47f-f903da2c2f14', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e217db1b-f5b9-5c0d-b47f-f903da2c2f14'}, {'abstract': 'Spencer C. Barasch, who was said to have hindered federal investigations of fraud at the Stanford Financial Group and later represented the firm, reached a $50,000 civil settlement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/business/ex-sec-official-settles-conflict-case.html', 'snippet': 'Spencer C. Barasch, who was said to have hindered federal investigations of fraud at the Stanford Financial Group and later represented the firm, reached a $50,000 civil settlement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A former enforcement official for the Securities and Exchange Commission who was accused of blocking or closing at least three investigations into the activities of the Stanford Financial Group, which the authorities claim was a $7 billion Ponzi scheme, has settled civil charges brought by the Justice Department accusing him of violating conflict-of-interest rules by later representing Stanford before the commission.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ex-Official at S.E.C. Settles Case for $50,000', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ex-Official At S.E.C. Settles Case For $50,000', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford Financial Group', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barasch, Spencer C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-13T20:07:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d51cee2e-2109-5024-b1b3-fe48152a1f0b', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d51cee2e-2109-5024-b1b3-fe48152a1f0b'}, {'abstract': 'Among American snacks, Hostess Twinkies have been both hero and villain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/fashion/twinkies-a-history.html', 'snippet': 'Among American snacks, Hostess Twinkies have been both hero and villain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON the list of happy childhood memories, the Hostess Twinkie occupies a lofty position. Since the 1930s, it has represented the summit of the pastry maker’s art for untold millions of American children seduced by its loaf-shaped golden sponge cake, soft yet springy, and voluptuous vanilla cream filling.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/fashion/15CULTURAL_SPAN/15CULTURAL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/fashion/15CULTURAL_SPAN/15CULTURAL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/fashion/15CULTURAL_SPAN/0115CULTURALSTUDIES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/fashion/15CULTURAL_SPAN/15CULTURAL_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', '