ouTube video showing four Marines urinating on the bodies of dead enemy fighters in southern Afghanistan is horrible. Horrible for America’s image around the world. Horrible for its strategy of winning support from the Afghan people. Horrible for a professional military that believes its troops behave with the utmost decorum, even in the heat of battle.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/14/world/14BATTLEFIELD/14BATTLEFIELD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/14/world/14BATTLEFIELD/14BATTLEFIELD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/14/world/14BATTLEFIELD/14BATTLEFIELD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 818, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reprehensible Behavior Is a Risk of Combat, Experts Say', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reprehensible Behavior Is a Risk of Combat, Experts Say', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T01:38:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Dao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27a99035-5c1c-5258-9e17-5531dbb39676', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27a99035-5c1c-5258-9e17-5531dbb39676'}, {'abstract': 'How we choose to remember says a lot about what we once wished to forget. Jessica Ingram has been traveling the South to memorialize scenes of great sacrifice — and great injustice — as part of “A Civil Rights Memorial.”', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/honoring-the-struggles-and-sacrifices/', 'snippet': 'How we choose to remember says a lot about what we once wished to forget. Jessica Ingram has been traveling the South to memorialize scenes of great sacrifice — and great injustice — as part of “A Civil Rights Memorial.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jessica Ingram still recalls the summer day a decade ago when she stood at Court Square Fountain in Montgomery, Ala., taking in the details spelled out in historical markers. There, near the State Capitol, she read that the square was the site of the city’s slave market. That none of the men, women or children had been given last names. How much they fetched.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Honoring the Struggles and Sacrifices', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T01:45:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier'