: 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0e953b9-9e78-5b8f-a2c0-d4de7708e0b8', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0e953b9-9e78-5b8f-a2c0-d4de7708e0b8'}, {'abstract': 'The introduction of Tony Sparano, the Jets’ new offensive coordinator, only temporarily deflected attention from the issues of locker room discord and Mark Sanchez’s psyche.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/sports/football/tony-sparano-introduced-as-jets-offensive-coordinator.html', 'snippet': 'The introduction of Tony Sparano, the Jets’ new offensive coordinator, only temporarily deflected attention from the issues of locker room discord and Mark Sanchez’s psyche.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The philosophy espoused by Tony Sparano is, in his words, “a little different” from that of Brian Schottenheimer, his predecessor as the Jets’ offensive coordinator. When asked Friday to describe that philosophy, Sparano used the word “physical” in seven straight sentences. It was as if he were reading from cue cards held up by Coach Rex Ryan.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sparano Hears Firsthand Where Jets Need to Improve', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sparano Hears Firsthand Where Jets Need to Improve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Rex', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sparano, Tony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanchez, Mark', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T03:03:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85b619bb-19f4-50fc-a93d-c218ef55c951', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85b619bb-19f4-50fc-a93d-c218ef55c951'}, {'abstract': 'After losing her job at a publishing house and finding herself without a roommate, Alayne O’Reilly feared losing the apartment she had lived in for nearly 40 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/nyregion/theres-only-one-place-she-calls-home.html', 'snippet': 'After losing her job at a publishing house and finding herself without a roommate, Alayne O’Reilly feared losing the apartment she had lived in for nearly 40 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Alayne O’Reilly looks out the window of her 17th-floor apartment in Greenwich Village and watches the planes go by, she wonders where they are heading. She imagines she knows their destinations, excited by the thought of going to other countries and hearing the different languages. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/14/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/14/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/14/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/14/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/14/nyregion/NEEDIEST/NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Lover of Traveling and Languages, Only One Place Can Be Called Home', 'kicker': 'The Neediest Cases', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For a Lover of Traveling and Languages, Only One Place Can Be Called Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federation of Protestant Welfare Agencies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T03:03:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mathew R. Warren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mathew', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Warren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09e53397-2706-5df8-861a-4f3f8a338891', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09e53397-2706-5df8-861a-4f3f8a338891'}, {'abstract': 'The divide in conservative South Carolina voters could mean that the candidate they perceive to be more moderate, Mitt Romney, walks away the winner of the state’s primary on Jan. 21.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/13/gingrich-and-santorum-vie-to-be-the-conservative-of-choice/', 'snippet': 'The divide in conservative South Carolina voters could mean that the candidate they perceive to be more moderate, Mitt Romney, walks away the winner of the state’s primary on Jan. 21.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DUNCAN, S.C. — Conventional wisdom dictates that for either Newt Gingrich or Rick Santorum to vault past Mitt Romney and win the South Carolina primary on Jan. 21, they must handily win the vote of upstate social conservatives, like the ones who packed a high school lunchroom here Friday night. But if the reactions of the 500 or so people here were any indication, these voters remain split.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gingrich and Santorum Vie to Be the Conservative of Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T03:08:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard A. Oppel Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Oppel', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b3f7f01-f217-5d0e-a4c2-0749e3f4284a', 'word_count': 550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b3f7f01-f217-5d0e-a4c2-0749e3f4284a'}, {'abstract': 'During his State of the City address, the mayor returned to a recurring theme: the city’s much-maligned schools and his goals to improve them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/0