s country’s consulate in Miami after the expulsion by the State Department of Venezuela’s consul general there.', 'lead_paragraph': ' President Hugo Chávez said Friday that he would close his country’s consulate in Miami after the expulsion by the State Department of Venezuela’s consul general there. Mr. Chávez said that he did not intend to expel any United States diplomats in retaliation. The State Department expelled the consul general, Livia Acosta Noguera, after a televised report accused her of having taking part in discussions, during an earlier posting in Mexico, about possible cyberattacks against the United States. The political opposition immediately criticized the move, saying that thousands of Venezuelans used the Miami consulate to vote in Venezuelan elections and that the émigré population there was overwhelmingly anti-Chávez. Mr. Chávez is running for re-election in a vote scheduled for October. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Venezuela Closes Consulate in Miami', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Venezuela Closes Consulate in Miami', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Acosta Noguera, Livia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chavez, Hugo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venezuela', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami (Fla)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T05:19:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'secti