one, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Banerjee, Mamata', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kumar, Nitish', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rushdie, Salman', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Conference', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Goa (India)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rajasthan (India)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T07:15:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/935060bc-8977-5e2e-beab-9e8923ae7fdf', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/935060bc-8977-5e2e-beab-9e8923ae7fdf'}, {'abstract': 'A weekly roundup of news reported from the country’s Bollywood industry.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/newswallah-bollywood-edition-8/', 'snippet': 'A weekly roundup of news reported from the country’s Bollywood industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Salman Khan: Ormax Media, a leading research and consulting firm for the entertainment business released key findings of “Front Page,” their annual report on movies and television. One of the key features of the report is the list of the top 10 male and female stars in Bollywood. Using a star tracking methodology, data was collected through the year from a sample of more than 30,000 moviegoers. The star rankings threw up a few surprises. Among the heroes, Salman Khan was, of course, the top choice for 2011. This is hardly unexpected since he single-handedly propelled two mediocre films – “Ready” and “Bodyguard” – to blockbuster status. But the surprise was how far ahead he is of the competition. He has a 47 percent share, which almost equals that of Shah Rukh Khan (27 percent) and Aamir Khan (22 percent) combined.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: Bollywood Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T07:21:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anupama Chopra', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anupama', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chopra', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eab21001-0157-57a6-8429-9adee11646fe', 'word_count': 764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eab21001-0157-57a6-8429-9adee11646fe'}, {'abstract': 'An explosion in the southern city of Basra ripped through a group of pilgrims headed to a mosque to commemorate one of the holiest Shiite holidays, killing at least 53.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/world/roadside-bomb-kills-dozens-in-southern-iraq.html', 'snippet': 'An explosion in the southern city of Basra ripped through a group of pilgrims headed to a mosque to commemorate one of the holiest Shiite holidays, killing at least 53.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — Insurgents mounted another attack against Iraq’s Shiites on Saturday, as an explosion in the southern city of Basra ripped through a group of pilgrims headed to a mosque to commemorate one of the holiest Shiite holidays.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/world/15IRAQ1sub/15IRAQ1sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'article