oss-strait reconciliation with a variety of economic and trade pacts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/world/asia/taiwan-presidential-election.html', 'snippet': 'A second term for Ma Ying-jeou will probably suit Beijing, which has matched his enthusiasm for cross-strait reconciliation with a variety of economic and trade pacts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAIPEI, Taiwan — President Ma Ying-jeou was re-elected by a comfortable margin on Saturday, fending off a challenge from his main rival, Tsai Ing-wen, who criticized his handling of the economy but also sought to exploit fears among voters that Mr. Ma’s conciliatory approach toward China was eroding the island’s sovereignty.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/world/15TAIWAN/15TAIWAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 792, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/world/15TAIWAN/15TAIWAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/world/15TAIWAN/15TAIWAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'President of Taiwan Is Re-elected, a Result That Is Likely to Please China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'President of Taiwan Is Re-elected, a Result That Is Likely to Please China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soong, James', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ma Ying-jeou', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsai Ing-wen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiwan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T10:21:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4422f850-034f-5449-9881-47e04487906e', 'word_count': 977, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4422f850-034f-5449-9881-47e04487906e'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Kantrowitz, an expert on paying for college and the founder of FinAid.org, concludes his week-long series answering questions from readers of The Choice about financial aid applications.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/kantrowitz-answers-part-6/', 'snippet': 'Mark Kantrowitz, an expert on paying for college and the founder of FinAid.org, concludes his week-long series answering questions from readers of The Choice about financial aid applications.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the sixth and final round of responses to student questions about financial aid, Mark Kantrowitz, founder of the Web sites FinAid.org and fastweb.com, addresses studying abroad and whether a student paying for college on his own can avoid listing his parents’ assets. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Last of 6 Parts: Answers on the Fafsa, the Free Application for Federal Student Aid', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T11:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_nam