Geoff Macdonald, the women’s tennis coach at Vanderbilt University', 'lead_paragraph': 'Geoff Macdonald, the women’s tennis coach at Vanderbilt University, will provide analysis of the Australian Open throughout the next two weeks on this blog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Players to Watch: Six With Games Suited for Success', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T16:54:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By GEOFF MACDONALD', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MACDONALD', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68c8cb1f-3b11-5182-a784-d822ce50d081', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68c8cb1f-3b11-5182-a784-d822ce50d081'}, {'abstract': 'Of growth and family incomes.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/things-were-supposed-to-be-quiet-about/', 'snippet': 'Of growth and family incomes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apologists for rising inequality often argue that since most Americans’ income has risen despite rising inequality, there’s no reason to complain about inequality other than envy. So it’s worth remembering that we used to expect economic growth to deliver large increases in real income, not just a bit of a rise that’s accomplished in large part through longer working hours; and that a major reason so many have seen such small gains is that a large part of growth has been siphoned off to the very high end.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Things We’re Supposed To Be Quiet About', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T16:56:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3165026-573f-5693-b4dd-389579f8d788', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3165026-573f-5693-b4dd-389579f8d788'}, {'abstract': 'The Hex team wipes the dust off their feet.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/dusty-acrostic/', 'snippet': 'The Hex team wipes the dust off their feet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SECOND SUNDAY PUZZLE — The author of today’s quote was one of the pre-eminent writers during the Harlem Renaissance of the 1920s and ’30s. Her books, short stories, plays and essays heavily influenced many of the heavy hitters in literature, such as Alice Walker, Toni Morrison, Ralph Ellison and more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: A Dusty Acrostic', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acrostic Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T17:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1e00e9c-a825-5e39-868c-743d99d9d96f', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1e00e9c-a825-5e39-868c-743d99d9d96f'}, {'abstract': 'As the Giants and the Packers prepare for their playoff game on Sunday, the remarkable life of Emlen Tunnell is a rarely recalled tale of a landmark player for each franchise.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/sports/football/emlen-tunnell-the-giants-greatest-packer.html', 'snippet': 'As the Giants and the Packers prepare for their playoff game on Sunday, the remarkable life of Emlen Tunnell is a rarely recalled tale of a landmark player for each franchise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GREEN BAY, Wis. — Emlen Tunnell, a star defender for the glittering, magnetic Giants, had been summoned to Green Bay. It was 1959, and the new Packers coach, Vince Lombardi, traded for Tunnell, ending his run of 11 record-setting seasons in New York.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/tunnell1/tunnell1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/tunnell1/tunnell1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/tunnell1/tunnell1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/tunnell1/tunnell1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/tunnell1/tunnell1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Emlen Tunnell: The Giants’ Greatest Packer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Giants’ Greatest Packer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tunnell, Emlen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T17:22:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Pennington', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pennington', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e73c8c66-9b85-5851-81bc-78d2a1c1425d', 'word_count': 3529, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e73c8c66-9b85-5851-81bc-78d2a1c1425d'}, {'abstract': 'For both Mitt Romney and President Obama, ratings of favorability and unfavorability that are about even.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/romney-obama-no-popularity-contest/', 'snippet': 'For both Mitt Romney and President Obama, ratings of favorability and unfavorability that are about even.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Mitt Romney becomes the Republican presidential nominee, he will begin the general-election campaign with middling favorability ratings as compared with other recent standard-bearers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney-Obama: No Popularity Contest', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T17:28:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': '