document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By HENDRIK HARTOG', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hendrik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HARTOG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/eaba5aed-3a77-5e88-b324-25c603ddaab8', 'word_count': 1384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eaba5aed-3a77-5e88-b324-25c603ddaab8'}, {'abstract': 'Civilian and military leaders met for the first time since the eruption of clashes that have threatened to topple the government.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/world/asia/pakistan-army-and-civilian-leaders-meet-amid-bitter-split.html', 'snippet': 'Civilian and military leaders met for the first time since the eruption of clashes that have threatened to topple the government.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — Pakistan’s civilian and military leaders met Saturday for the first time since the eruption of a bitter political and legal controversy that stoked rumors of a military coup and could still topple the precariously weak civilian government.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Pakistan, Talks Aim to Ease Split', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan Talks Try to Ease A Civilian-Military Divide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilani, Yousaf Raza', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pakistan Peoples Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zardari, Asif Ali', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Islamabad (Pakistan)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kayani, Ashfaq Parvez', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T17:35:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Declan Walsh and Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Declan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40f8a2d1-f9ce-5eae-9bc5-fbec3ea51a7a', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40f8a2d1-f9ce-5eae-9bc5-fbec3ea51a7a'}, {'abstract': 'Granting Robert Mugabe’s henchmen immunity from prosecution is the only path to a peaceful democratic transition in Zimbabwe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/opinion/zimbabwes-unsavory-path-to-peace.html', 'snippet': 'Granting Robert Mugabe’s henchmen immunity from prosecution is the only path to a peaceful democratic transition in Zimbabwe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ZIMBABWE is fast approaching a dangerous tipping point. Last month, its ailing octogenarian president, Robert Mugabe, angrily defied his critics, calling for early elections in 2012. If a political settlement with Zimbabwe’s security chiefs is not negotiated before the vote, Mr. Mugabe will no doubt rely on them to once again begin a campaign of intimidation and violence, leading to sham elections that could precipitate a regional crisis. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Zimbabwe’s Unsavory Path to Peace', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Zimbabwe&#8217;s Unsavory Path to Peace', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsvangirai, Morgan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mugabe, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Zimbabwe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immunity from Prosecution', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T17:48:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander Noyes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Noyes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b28c67c5-1cce-5514-abd5-3422fa73ca7f', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b28c67c5-1cce-5514-abd5-3422fa73ca7f'}, {'abstract': 'Celebrity traffic jams involving the likes of Martin Scorsese and Steven Spielberg are to be expected at the annual AFI luncheon.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/14/traffic-jams-with-eastwood-and-pitt-scorsese-and-weinstein/', 'snippet': 'Celebrity traffic jams involving the likes of Martin Scorsese and Steven Spielberg are to be expected at the annual AFI luncheon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Aside from “congratulations,” “this is my favorite event” was probably the phrase heard the most often at Friday’s American Film Institute luncheon on Friday. The annual program announces its winners for TV and film in advance – this year they included “Bridesmaids,” “War Horse,” “The Tree of Life,” “Moneyball,” “The Girl With the Dragon Tattoo,” “The Descendants,” “Hugo,” “The Help,” “J. Edgar,” “The Artist” and “Midnight in Paris” – and presents them all with prizes, no speechifying necessary. Instead, everybody schmoozes, lunches and watches clip reels. And because the honorees come from TV and film, there are double the opportunities for stars to rub shoulders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Traffic Jams With Eastwood and Pitt, Scorsese and Weinstein', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'C K, Louis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eastwood, Clint', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grazer, Brian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Payne, Alexander', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pitt, Brad', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scorsese, Martin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spielberg, Steven', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stringer, Howard', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weinstein, Harvey', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN FILM INSTITUTE', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T17:48:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1233673f-b22f-58bd-9a5e-7d35bb08182d', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1233673f-b22f-58bd-9a5e-7d35bb08182d'}, {'abstract': 'Ryan Hall finished second in the U.S. Olympic marathon trials, and Meb Keflezighi won Saturday’s race in Houston.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/sports/in-olympic-marathon-trials-hall-runs-on-faith-but-runs-out-of-gas.html', 'snippet': 'Ryan Hall finished second in the U.S. Olympic marathon trials, and Meb Keflezighi won Saturday’s race in Houston.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — This time, Meb Keflezighi kept his breathing strip where it belonged — on his nose.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/subYMARATHON/subYMARATHON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/subYMARATHON/subYMARATHON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/subYMARATHON/subYMARATHON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/subYMARATHON/subYMARATHON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/15/sports/subYMARATHON/subYMARATHON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Busy, Pain-Filled Comeback Pays Off as Keflezighi Wins to Make U.S. Team', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Busy, Pain-Filled Comeback Pays Off As Keflezighi Wins to Make U.S. Team', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Ryan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marathon Running', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keflezighi, Mebrahtom', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-14T17:56:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeré Longman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/958f4999-1051-5bbe-91ec-5bc2cfb9bc8b', 'word_count': 1097, 'uri': 'nyt://article/958f4999-1051-5bbe-91ec-5bc2cfb9bc8b'}, {'abstract': 'The Tea Party’s influence is diminishing as conservatives seem to be inching toward nominating Mitt Romney.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/sunday-review/gop-history-vs-the-tea-party.html', 'snippet': 'The Tea Party’s influence is diminishing as conservatives seem to be inching toward nominating Mitt Romney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR more than two years, conservatives have been riding a wave of Tea Party insurgency that has formed the most dynamic force in American politics, a protest movement that promised to slash taxes, close the federal deficit and remake Washington. And yet to judge from the results of the Iowa caucuses and the New Hampshire primary, some of the most engaged Republican voters are inching closer to nominating Mitt Romney, the candidate they like least, a Harvard-educated technocrat who is in many ways a mirror image of the president the insurgents want to dislodge. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15TANENHAUS/15TANENHAUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15TANENHAUS/15TANENHAUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/15/sunday-review/15TANENHAUS/15TANENHAUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'l