media': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of the Moment | Neil Barrett', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-15T21:49:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danilo Scarpati', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danilo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scarpati', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/febceaaa-4dbf-5ecd-a76b-c616d72d4a73', 'word_count': 32, 'uri': 'nyt://article/febceaaa-4dbf-5ecd-a76b-c616d72d4a73'}, {'abstract': 'Data to be reported for December will include the Producer Price Index, industrial production and capacity utilization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/business/economic-reports-for-the-week-of-jan-16.html', 'snippet': 'Data to be reported for December will include the Producer Price Index, industrial production and capacity utilization.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ECONOMIC REPORTS Data will include the Producer Price Index for December, and industrial production and capacity utilization for December (Wednesday); weekly jobless claims, the Consumer Price Index for December, housing starts for December, and the Philadelphia Fed manufacturing index for January (Thursday); and existing home sales for December (Friday).', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/16/business/16Ahead/16Ahead-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/16/business/16Ahead/16Ahead-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/16/business/16Ahead/16Ahead-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Economic Reports for the Week Ahead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking Ahead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-15T21:56:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3cf66ec-3f61-51f1-8d4c-e2dcbfce103b', 'word_count': 207, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3cf66ec-3f61-51f1-8d4c-e2dcbfce103b'}, {'abstract': 'Recent studies indicate that our brains may work like JPGS, conserving “bandwidth” and influencing how we see the world.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/do-thrifty-brains-make-better-minds/', 'snippet': 'Recent studies indicate that our brains may work like JPGS, conserving “bandwidth” and influencing how we see the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Might the miserly use of neural resources be one of the essential keys to understanding how brains make sense of the world? Some recent work in computational and cognitive neuroscience suggests that it is indeed the frugal use of our native neural capacity (the inventive use of restricted “neural bandwidth,” if you will) that explains how brains like ours so elegantly make sense of noisy and ambiguous sensory input. That same story suggests, intriguingly, that perception, understanding and imagination, which we might intuitively consider to be three distinct chunks of our mental machinery, are inextricably tied together as simultaneous results of a single underlying strategy known as “predictive coding.” This strategy saves on bandwidth using (who would have guessed it?) one of the many technical wheezes that enable us to economically store and transmit pictures, sounds and videos using formats such as JPEG and MP3.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Thrifty Brains Make Better Minds?', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-15T22:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3942e61a-47b9-52ca-9b0e-b6cf7cd2aa89', 'word_count': 1931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3942e61a-47b9-52ca-9b0e-b6cf7cd2aa89'}, {'abstract': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/business/treasury-auctions-set-for-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Treasury’s schedule of financing this week includes regular weekly auction of new three- and six-month bills on Tuesday and an auction of four-week bills on Wednesday. The stock and bond markets are closed Monday in observance of the Martin Luther King’s Birthday holiday. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-15T22:05:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/e16db88a-218f-5300-93a6-29a881724458', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e16db88a-218f-5300-93a6-29a881724458'}, {'abstract': 'Baltimore intercepted the rookie quarterback T. J. Yates three times and held Houston scoreless after halftime to advance to the A.F.C. championship game against New England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/sports/football/still-unbeatable-at-home-ravens-outlast-texans.html', 'snippet': 'Baltimore intercepted the rookie quarterback T. J. Yates three times and held Houston scoreless after halftime to advance to the A.F.C. championship game against New England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BALTIMORE — As television cameras and recorders descended on him in the Baltimore Ravens’ locker room late Sunday afternoon, Terrell Suggs tucked in his dress shirt, slipped into a vest and slid on his watch. Turning around to face reporters, Suggs instead found Ray Lewis waiting to fix his collar, which had stood up.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/16/sports/YJPRAVENS/YJPRAVENS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/16/sports/YJPRAVENS/YJPRAVENS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/16/sports/YJPRAVENS/YJPRAVENS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/16/sports/YJPRAVENS/YJPRAVENS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/16/sports/YJPRAVENS/YJPRAVENS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Typical Ravens Success: With Grit and Without Fear', 'kicker': 'Ravens 20, Texans 13', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Typical Ravens Success: With Grit and Without Fear', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Houston Texans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reed, Ed', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flacco, Joe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foster, Arian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yates, T J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Ray', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Ravens', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boldin, Anquan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-15T22:19:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57638783-d969-5085-868b-d91f7308bb5a', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57638783-d969-5085-868b-d91f7308bb5a'}, {'abstract': 'The critically panned cross-dressing comedy “Work It” has been canceled by ABC after only two episodes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/arts/television/abc-kills-work-it.html', 'snippet': 'The critically panned cross-dressing comedy “Work It” has been canceled by ABC after only two episodes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The critically panned cross-dressing comedy “Work It” has been canceled by ABC after only two episodes. Repeats of the Tim Allen comedy “Last Man Standing” will run in its place starting Tuesday. “Work It” featured two unemployed men who tried dressing as women to get jobs. It was criticized ahead of its premiere by the Gay & Lesbian Alliance Against Defamation and the Human Rights Campaign, which said in a joint statement that “by encouraging the audience to laugh at the characters’ attempts at womanhood, the show gives license to similar treatment of transgender women.” The show was poorly received by viewers in early January. Days before the schedule change was made public, Paul Lee, the president of ABC Entertainment, defended the show to TV reporters by saying, “We thought there was room for a very, very, very silly show.” ABC has not said what show will succeed “Work It” in the time slot on Tuesday at 8:30 p.m., but it may go to the returning sitcom “Cougar Town,” which has yet to be assigned a spot this season.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ABC Kills ‘Work It’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'ABC Kills ‘Work It’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-15T22:27:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b2796a0-2156-5443-a970-593ac5a0ff91', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b2796a0-2156-5443-a970-593ac5a0ff91'}, {'abstract': 'Ricky Gervais seemingly offended Hollywood the last time he hosted the Golden Globes, but it turns out the stars aren’t so offended. At least Johnny Depp isn’t.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/ricky-gervais-the-host-with-the-most-friends-in-on-the-joke/', 'snippet': 'Ricky Gervais seemingly offended Hollywood the last time he hosted the Golden Globes, but it turns out the stars aren’t so offended. At least Johnny Depp isn’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than a few eyebrows were raised when the Hollywood Foreign Press Association invited Ricky Gervais back to host the Golden Globes tonight. You may remember the kerfuffle that resulted the last time he emceed ceremonies and offended a few celebrities in the room. Or maybe they weren’t entirely offended, as our colleague Dave Itzkoff writes in his profile of the comedian. He adds that Mr. Gervais “has become the entertainment industry’s favorite irreverent person, because he manages to be irreverent in such a deeply reverent way.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ricky Gervais, the Host With the Most (Friends in on the Joke)', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Depp, Johnny', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gervais, Ricky', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hollywood Foreign Press Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-15T22:29:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'n