/16/opinion/section-5-and-the-right-to-vote.html', 'snippet': 'The facts of three cases help explain why the section is crucial and why the justices must rule in a way that preserves its shield against discrimination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is Section 5 of the Voting Rights Act still necessary? That provision requires nine states and some counties and towns with histories of severe racial discrimination to get approval from the Justice Department or a court before changing their voting procedures. The question hovers over three consolidated cases the Supreme Court heard last week on redistricting for legislative and Congressional seats in Texas, a state governed by Section 5. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Section 5 and the Right to Vote', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Section 5 and the Right to Vote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Redistricting and Reapportionment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voter Registration and Requirements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting Rights Act (1965)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T02:09:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/beb9b971-ff42-5189-96c2-9f105bb80e3f', 'word_count': 431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/beb9b971-ff42-5189-96c2-9f105bb80e3f'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama should form a task force to investigate and pursue potential civil and criminal wrongdoing by institutions and people whose conduct had the greatest economic impact.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/opinion/on-the-trail-of-mortgage-fraud.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama should form a task force to investigate and pursue potential civil and criminal wrongdoing by institutions and people whose conduct had the greatest economic impact.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Queens has been harder hit by foreclosures than any other New York borough, and the Federal Bureau of Investigation believes it has found a culprit. Last July, the F.B.I. accused Edul Ahmad, a local broker, of a $50 million mortgage fraud, saying he lured fellow immigrants into subprime mortgages, inflated the values of their properties and concealed his involvement in deals that were ruinous for scores, if not hundreds, of borrowers. Mr. Ahmad pleaded not guilty, and posted $2.5 million bail. Now, according to court papers, as reported in The Times, he is plea-bargaining with federal prosecutors. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Trail of Mortgage Fraud', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Trail of Mortgage Fraud', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T02:10:49+0000', 'docume