': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T02:28:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdf05b19-1e29-5559-b160-7fa64f16e9c1', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdf05b19-1e29-5559-b160-7fa64f16e9c1'}, {'abstract': 'How a Civil War Congressional committee hounded a good officer out of the Army.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/15/the-scapegoat-of-balls-bluff/', 'snippet': 'How a Civil War Congressional committee hounded a good officer out of the Army.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As 1862 dawned, the Union’s military fortunes appeared decidedly grim. Defeats like those suffered at Bull Run and Ball’s Bluff, as well as the growing realization that Gen. George McClellan was at best a reluctant warrior, gave rise to a growing claque of congressional critics. On Dec. 10, 1861, they had established the Joint Committee on the Conduct of the War, designed to examine every aspect of the war effort and empowered to “send for person and papers” as needed. Far from being a dispassionate check on the Executive Branch, however, the committee was, from the very beginning, a tool for partisan politics, a weapon forged out of, in Senator Charles Sumner’s words, “a strong desire to hold someone responsible” for the Union Army’s recent misfortunes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Scapegoat of Ball’s Bluff', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sumner, Charles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T02:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Beard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58e28df1-fd20-557e-b02d-da37bd428365', 'word_count': 1629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58e28df1-fd20-557e-b02d-da37bd428365'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on January 16, 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/pageoneplus/corrections-january-16.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on January 16, 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: January 16', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T02:31:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/f52e0694-07b9-5907-9a9e-c3b13bffe848', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f52e0694-07b9-5907-9a9e-c3b13bffe848'}, {'abstract': 'An examination of some of the earmarks Rick Santorum got as a senator from Pennsylvania suggests that they paid off in the form of campaign contributions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/us/politics/as-rick-santorum-secured-earmarks-2006-donations-flowed-in.html', 'snippet': 'An examination of some of the earmarks Rick Santorum got as a senator from Pennsylvania suggests that they paid off in the form of campaign contributions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The announcements flowed out of Rick Santorum’s Senate office: a $3.5 million federal grant to Piasecki Aircraft to help it test a new helicopter propeller technology; another $3.5 million to JLG Industries to bolster its bid to build all-terrain forklifts for the military; $1.4 million to Medico Industries to upgrade equipment for its munitions work.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/16/us/JP-SANTORUM-1/JP-SANTORUM-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/16/us/JP-SANTORUM-1/JP-SANTORUM-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/16/us/JP-SANTORUM-1/JP-SANTORUM-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Donors Gave as Santorum Won Earmarks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Donors Gave As Santorum Won Earmarks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earmarks (Finance)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taxpayers for Common Sense', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T02:39:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Luo and Mike McIntire', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Luo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McIntire', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25e9c337-8227-5cd3-9167-45a29425abfb', 'word_count': 1491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25e9c337-8227-5cd3-9167-45a29425abfb'}, {'abstract': 'The outcome of a movement to recall Gov. Scott Walker of Wisconsin could have meaning for other states trying to cut union costs and for the presidential election.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/us/scott-walker-recall-drive-is-closely-watched.html', 'snippet': 'The outcome of a movement to recall Gov. Scott Walker of Wisconsin could have meaning for other states trying to cut union costs and for the presidential election.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MILWAUKEE — Thousands of volunteers have raced to collect signatures near busy intersections and malls all over Wisconsin, at makeshift “drive-through” operations in parking lots, during Green Bay Packers viewing parties and New Year’s Eve pub crawls, and even at a fold-up table inside Milwaukee’s airport just off Concourse C.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/16/us/WISCONSIN/WISCONSIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'captio