nyt://article/6ff48801-7f8d-5bc8-9843-38403ff8994c'}, {'abstract': 'Britain plans to turn London into a major foreign exchange trading center for the Chinese renminbi to benefit from faster growth in Asia while strengthening the city’s position as a financial center.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/london-wants-to-tap-chinese-currency-market/', 'snippet': 'Britain plans to turn London into a major foreign exchange trading center for the Chinese renminbi to benefit from faster growth in Asia while strengthening the city’s position as a financial center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Britain plans to turn London into a major foreign exchange trading center for the Chinese renminbi to benefit from faster growth in Asia while strengthening the city’s position as a financial center in the wake of the banking crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Wants to Tap Chinese Currency Market', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renminbi (Currency)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Osborne, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T13:55:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/beec98b9-d4df-5d43-bfac-6fcf8261f9e7', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/beec98b9-d4df-5d43-bfac-6fcf8261f9e7'}, {'abstract': 'Of a consumer dollar, 85 cents still spent here.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/not-so-global/', 'snippet': 'Of a consumer dollar, 85 cents still spent here.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barry Ritholtz sends us to a San Francisco Fed paper from last summer that makes a point on which many people seem confused: despite globalization and all that, the bulk of a consumer dollar spent in America falls on American-produced goods and services.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not So Global', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T13:58:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66def0a0-021e-563b-9c44-2514bb730e9b', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66def0a0-021e-563b-9c44-2514bb730e9b'}, {'abstract': 'Martin Scorsese, Meryl Streep and George Clooney have a leg up at the Oscars, if  the applause inside the ballroom at the Golden Globes is anything to go by.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/the-golden-globes-applause-meter/', 'snippet': 'Martin Scorsese, Meryl Streep and George Clooney have a leg up at the Oscars, if  the applause inside the ballroom at the Golden Globes is anything to go by.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s worth noting the crowd reaction inside the ballroom at the Golden Globes on Sunday. The Beverly Hilton Hotel auditorium was packed with a lot of Hollywood insiders who also get a vote at the Oscars. Who wins a Globe has nothing to do with who wins an Oscar. But the warmth of a winner’s reception in the room can tell a lot about Hollywood sentiment, and where the Oscar ballots are headed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Golden Globes Applause Meter', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bridges, Jeff', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clooney, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fonda, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scorsese, Martin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Streep, Meryl', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T14:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e991669-7d51-57c1-b744-3abf405e0bb4', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e991669-7d51-57c1-b744-3abf405e0bb4'}, {'abstract': 'A company that sells video calling services finds that people with iPhones are four times more likely to spend money on apps than people with Android phones are.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/why-iphone-shoppers-buy-more-apps/', 'snippet': 'A company that sells video calling services finds that people with iPhones are four times more likely to spend money on apps than people with Android phones are.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do Apple consumers have more money? Possibly, but that’s not why app developers seem to like them more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/blogs/bitpix-apple-iphone1/bitpix-apple-iphone1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/blo