name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dakar Rally', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Robby', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bayerische Motorenwerke AG', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hummer Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South America', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T16:02:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c794648c-35a1-576d-82d2-9b52a928d39f', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c794648c-35a1-576d-82d2-9b52a928d39f'}, {'abstract': 'After losing to Rafael Nadal in straight sets at the Australian Open , Alex Kuznetsov finds the positive.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/for-kuznetsov-a-sign-of-progress/', 'snippet': 'After losing to Rafael Nadal in straight sets at the Australian Open , Alex Kuznetsov finds the positive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia — Though a first-round loss in straight sets is not what any player aspires to at a Grand Slam tournament, the 24-year-old Pennsylvanian Alex Kuznetsov leaves Melbourne content.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Kuznetsov, a Sign of Progress', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T16:05:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By BEN ROTHENBERG', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ROTHENBERG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de89ec69-26bc-59ea-b86a-8ddbad79324b', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de89ec69-26bc-59ea-b86a-8ddbad79324b'}, {'abstract': 'For the Huntsman campaign, there’s plenty of blame to go around.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/exit-huntsman/', 'snippet': 'For the Huntsman campaign, there’s plenty of blame to go around.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jon Huntsman had a more impressive resume, a better policy shop, better odds in November and stronger conservative bona fides than many of the candidates vying for the Republican nomination. But several of them are still running while he’s dropped out, and that almost all of them enjoyed temporary surges in the polls while he always languished at the bottom of the pack. For this, there’s plenty of blame to go around: Huntsman can blame the mainstream media, for constantly depicting him as the “sane Republican” tragically doomed to irrelevance by the folly of his party’s base; he can blame his own advisers, for embracing that self-defeating image and trying to re-run John McCain’s 2000 campaign in a very different climate; he can blame Republican voters for dismissing him because of symbolism and identity politics rather than judging him on his record; he can blame conservative machers and opinion-makers for doing the same. (He can even blame his father, who declined\xa0— or wasn’t asked, or both — to play Sheldon Adelson to his son’s cash-poor campaign.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exit Huntsman', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T16:06:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3de3c5be-7e87-5dc7-b4d9-bcee2dde55b3', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3de3c5be-7e87-5dc7-b4d9-bcee2dde55b3'}, {'abstract': 'The amount of water you drink can play a role in how your body regulates blood sugar, researchers have found.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/really-the-claim-drinking-water-can-help-lower-the-risk-of-diabetes/', 'snippet': 'The amount of water you drink can play a role in how your body regulates blood sugar, researchers have found.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE FACTS', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Really? The Claim: Drinking Water Can Help Lower the Risk of Diabetes.', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diabetes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T16:09:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab07004a-c463-53ed-b0ce-d7ffd0ec57ee', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab07004a-c463-53ed-b0ce-d7ffd0ec57ee'}, {'abstract': 'Through an interschool project, students from different worlds are receiving a lesson that goes beyond the pages of the classic “Of Mice and Men.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/education/studying-steinbeck-new-jersey-students-find-common-ground.html', 'snippet': 'Through an interschool project, students from different worlds are receiving a lesson that goes beyond the pages of the classic “Of Mice and Men.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'WESTFIELD, N.J. — When an eighth-grade class at Roosevelt Intermediate School tackled Chapter 4 of John Steinbeck’s “Of Mice and Men” one morning last week, the conversation focused on the loneliness of a minor white character known as Curley’s wife. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/DIVERSITY1/DIVERSITY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/DIVERSITY1/DIVERSITY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/DIVERSITY1/DIVERSITY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/DIVERSITY1/DIVERSITY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/DIVERSITY1/DIVERSITY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Split by Race and Wealth, but Discovering Similarities as They Study Steinbeck', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Split by Race and Wealth, but Discovering Similarities as They Study Steinbeck', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steinbeck, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T16:12:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ae53402-d40f-5e1c-a755-48a4b9afcfa4', 'word_count': 1137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ae53402-d40f-5e1c-a755-48a4b9afcfa4'}, {'abstract': 'In a surprisingly short space of time, a market for the creatures’ gill rakers has developed, based largely on the notion that they have medicinal value.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/voracious-demand-threatens-mantas-and-mobula-rays/', 'snippet': 'In a surprisingly short space of time, a market for the creatures’ gill rakers has developed, based largely on the notion that they have medicinal value.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago, something surprising began turning up in Asia’s fish markets: the gill rakers of manta and mobula rays.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Voracious Demand Threatens Manta and Mobula Rays', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Complementary Medicine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 5, 'major':