ame': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T17:15:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danilo Scarpati', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danilo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scarpati', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6faf1395-ea5c-5535-a03a-fa71e68736a7', 'word_count': 32, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6faf1395-ea5c-5535-a03a-fa71e68736a7'}, {'abstract': 'It will end its run on Jan. 28.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/chinglish-to-close/', 'snippet': 'It will end its run on Jan. 28.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Henry Hwang’s Broadway comedy “Chinglish,” about the misadventures and cultural misunderstanding of an American businessman as he is trying to close a deal in China, will end its run on Jan. 29, after 128 performances. The bilingual play, which features Gary Wilmes as the businessman and Jennifer Lim as a Chinese official who becomes his unlikely ally and lover, opened at the Longacre Theater in October to generally favorable reviews, and was named by Time magazine as the No. 1 new American play of 2011. Despite those accolades, and the strong reputation of Mr. Hwang, a Tony Award winner whose previous work includes “M. Butterfly,” box office sales remained modest. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Chinglish’ to Close', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hwang, David Henry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lim, Jennifer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T17:15:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/279d4519-4c65-5c54-bbaa-e5b419419f29', 'word_count': 113, 'uri': 'nyt://article/279d4519-4c65-5c54-bbaa-e5b419419f29'}, {'abstract': 'They form a nutritious foundation for flavorful fillings and can give new life to leftovers. If you need a quick, utterly satisfying meal, make an omelet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/health/nutrition/omelets-the-ultimate-fast-food-recipes-for-health.html', 'snippet': 'They form a nutritious foundation for flavorful fillings and can give new life to leftovers. If you need a quick, utterly satisfying meal, make an omelet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For some time now the medical literature has been countering the myth that the cholesterol in eggs goes straight to the arteries and that eggs should be shunned by anybody committed to healthy eating. Studies have shown that only a small amount of dietary cholesterol passes into the blood and that saturated fats and trans fats have much bigger effects on cholesterol levels. In fact, according to the Harvard School of Public Health, the only large study that looked at the effect of egg consumption on heart disease found no correlation between the two, except among people with diabetes, who were a bit more likely to develop heart disease if they ate an egg a day.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/science/16recipehealth/16recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/17/science/16recipehealth/16recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/science/16recipehealth/16recipehealth-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/science/16recipehealth/16recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/science/16recipehealth/16recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/17/science/16recipehealth/16recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/science/16recipehealth/16recipehealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Omelets: The Ultimate Fast Food', 'kicker': 'Recipes for Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-16T17:24:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Fitness & Nutrition', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifi