.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/JP-ROCHESTER1/JP-ROCHESTER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/JP-ROCHESTER1/JP-ROCHESTER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/JP-ROCHESTER1/JP-ROCHESTER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/JP-ROCHESTER1/JP-ROCHESTER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/17/nyregion/JP-ROCHESTER1/JP-ROCHESTER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Despite Long Slide by Kodak, Company Town Avoids Decay', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Despite Long Slide by Kodak, Company Town Avoids Decay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eastman Kodak Company', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rochester (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T02:56:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Applebome', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Applebome', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7825af7d-c19b-5f4f-8f8f-687fd3ef34af', 'word_count': 1251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7825af7d-c19b-5f4f-8f8f-687fd3ef34af'}, {'abstract': 'The London School of Economics said it was investigating allegations that a Nazi-themed drinking game led to a brawl during a student union ski trip in France last month.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/world/europe/britain-nazi-game-led-to-brawl-university-student-says.html', 'snippet': 'The London School of Economics said it was investigating allegations that a Nazi-themed drinking game led to a brawl during a student union ski trip in France last month.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The London School of Economics said Monday that it was investigating allegations that a Nazi-themed drinking game during a student union ski trip to the French resort of Val d’Isère last month led to a brawl in which a Jewish student’s nose was broken. The university, one of Britain’s most renowned academic institutions, said in a statement that it was “prepared to take disciplinary action.” The university’s student newspaper said the drinking game involved arranging playing cards on a table in the shape of a swastika, and that players were required to “salute the Führer.” The student who claimed to have been assaulted was not identified by the university, but The Guardian newspaper, quoting an interview with him, said that he had left the card game after “a mix of personal references and general Jewish insults,” that led him to conclude that “I couldn’t forgive myself if I let it slide.” The university was shaken by controversy last year over its decision to accept a $450,000 donation for research from a foundation run by Saif al-Islam al-Qaddafi, a son of the former Libyan leader Muammar el-Qaddafi. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain: Nazi Game Led to Brawl, University Student Says', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Nazi Game Led to Brawl, University Student Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'London School of Economics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T02:57:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e790eb9c-9c2e-501e-9783-ab152e844070', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e790eb9c-9c2e-501e-9783-ab152e844070'}, {'abstract': "Better teachers lead to better test scores and better lives. But with only a few years' data, can districts tell which teachers are good?", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2012/01/16/can-a-few-years-data-reveal-bad-teachers/', 'snippet': "Better teachers lead to better test scores and better lives. But with only a few years' data, can districts tell which teachers are good?", 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can a Few Years’ Data Reveal Bad Teachers?', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T02:59:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c72af54-3535-54a3-89e9-755c97c169ce', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c72af54-3535-54a3-89e9-755c97c169ce'}, {'abstract': 'A salute to a great artist by Elizabeth A. Long.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/16/impressed/', 'snippet': 'A salute to a great artist by Elizabeth A. Long.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUESDAY’S PUZZLE — Elizabeth A. Long has had a few puzzles published in The New York Times each year since 2007, although today’s grid is her first Tuesday. Now she has a puzzle for every day of the week, except weekends. So, Ms. Long: are you planning to bat for the cycle any time soon?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday: Deeply Impressed', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T03:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec153ca3-620a-5010-81fb-10f9f60a39bc', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec153ca3-620a-5010-81fb-10f9f60a39bc'}, {'abstract': 'As the Australian Open began, Rafael Nadal won his first match, against the American Alex Kuznetsov, and stepped back from an earlier criticism of Roger Federer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/sports/tennis/nadal-mends-fences-with-federer-as-australian-open-begins.html', 'snippet': 'As the Australian Open began, Rafael Nadal won his first match, against the American Alex Kuznetsov, and stepped back from an earlier criticism of Roger Federer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE — Perhaps Roger Federer had a point about the benefits of keeping negative thoughts out of the public domain. Only a day after Rafael Nadal deviated from a long-established pattern and ripped his friendly rival Federer at a news conference, Nadal sheepishly said that he regretted the outburst, still valued their connection and should have made his comments to Federer in private.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/sports/YTENNIS/YTENNIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/17/sports/YTENNIS/YTENNIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/sports/YTENNIS/YTENNIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/sports/YTENNIS/YTENNIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/17/sports/YTENNIS/YTENNIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nadal Mends Fences With Federer and Overcomes Knee Problem to Win', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nadal Mends Fences With Federer and Overcomes Knee Problem to Win', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nadal, Rafael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federer,