name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By MARY ANN GIORDANO', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Ann', 'lastname': 'GIORDANO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c795c637-c94e-5a85-b7d8-86fb3c47f164', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c795c637-c94e-5a85-b7d8-86fb3c47f164'}, {'abstract': 'Five of the city’s trendsetters reveal their own androgynous olfactory obsessions.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/vain-glorious-borrowed-from-the-boys/', 'snippet': 'Five of the city’s trendsetters reveal their own androgynous olfactory obsessions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been wearing men’s cologne since the day I was tall enough to reach for my father’s Van Cleef & Arpels Pour Homme. The striking black lacquered bottle held far more appeal than the delicate, glass-encased floral fragrances on my mother’s vanity, as did the rich, intoxicating aroma. It appears my infatuation with masculine scents is more common than spring’s focus on hyper-feminine fragrances might lead us to believe. Here, five of the city’s trendsetters reveal their own androgynous olfactory obsessions.Santa Maria Novella Acqua di Cuba Cologne“I wear this fragrance because it smells like the Maquis in Corsica, the island where I was born.” — Garance Doré, photographer, illustrator and writer', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | Borrowed From the Boys', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T14:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Nima', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nima', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 