ine': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camps and Camping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T17:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alia Khan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42b85315-9167-5475-9b05-5154c1e26712', 'word_count': 970, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42b85315-9167-5475-9b05-5154c1e26712'}, {'abstract': 'Recognizing a steep drop in donations as its profile has fallen, the movement has imposed a curb on its spending.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/occupy-wall-st-faces-its-own-economic-realities/', 'snippet': 'Recognizing a steep drop in donations as its profile has fallen, the movement has imposed a curb on its spending.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With donations to Occupy Wall Street falling significantly and money starting to run low, the movement has imposed a partial spending freeze\xa0to make sure enough money is available for crucial functions like bailing protesters out of jail.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Money Low, Occupy Wall St. Tightens Budget', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Zuccotti Park (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T17:14:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aidan Gardiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aidan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b14bdf0f-6f64-5f54-936a-28bcf69abb50', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b14bdf0f-6f64-5f54-936a-28bcf69abb50'}, {'abstract': 'In rejecting a proposed settlement with Spencer C. Barasch and requiring admissions in a small number of cases, the agency has taken two small steps toward burnishing its image as Wall Street’s top cop.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/is-the-s-e-c-getting-tougher/', 'snippet': 'In rejecting a proposed settlement with Spencer C. Barasch and requiring admissions in a small number of cases, the agency has taken two small steps toward burnishing its image as Wall Street’s top cop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the criticisms that has been leveled against the Securities and Exchange Commission is that even when it has pursued cases, it has not been tough enough in seeking sanctions for violations of the federal securities laws. Since the new year began, however, it seems that the agency has taken a couple of small steps to burnish its image as Wall Street’s top cop. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In S.E.C.’s Actions, a Hint at a Tougher Stance', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barasch, Spencer C', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rakoff, Jed S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T17:17:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c0cd7ee-5ad6-5df0-9a1f-a2f1b1ed25ff', 'word_count': 1039, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c0cd7ee-5ad6-5df0-9a1f-a2f1b1ed25ff'}, {'abstract': 'The Knicks need a productive point guard. Is Baron Davis that player?', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/knicks-idling-waiting-for-baron/', 'snippet': 'The Knicks need a productive point guard. Is Baron Davis that player?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps more than any other N.B.A. team, the Knicks have fostered a reputation for laying franchise fortune at the feet of superstars either in the midst of – or well past – their primes: Bob McAdoo, Spencer Haywood, Stephon Marbury, Steve Francis, Amar’e Stoudemire, Carmelo Anthony and, most recently, Tyson Chandler. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Knicks Idling, Waiting for Baron Davis', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T17:17:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By JIM CAVAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CAVAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5cdd4b0-d4c0-57b5-b955-e76b1c54d7b6', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5cdd4b0-d4c0-57b5-b955-e76b1c54d7b6'}, {'abstract': 'In his victory over Fernando Verdasco at the Australian Open, Bernard Tomic showcases his mental game.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/tomics-most-important-skill-his-temperament/', 'snippet': 'In his victory over Fernando Verdasco at the Australian Open, Bernard Tomic showcases his mental game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I watched Bernard Tomic come back from two sets down to beat Fernando Verdasco, and marveled at his quirky, inventive game. He reminds me of Andy Murray, with his ability to hold the ball on his strings a little longer to disguise his shots, his superb anticipation, and his use of different spins and heights to confuse and confound more powerful opponents. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tomic’s Most Important Skill? His Temperament', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T17:17:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By GEOFF MACDONALD', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MACDONALD', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2da05ef7-4e7d-5245-921d-38028315fef9', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2da05ef7-4e7d-5245-921d-38028315fef9'}, {'abstract': 'Paula Deen’s disclosure that she has Type 2 diabetes has added impetus to the debate on the national diet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/dining/paula-deen-says-she-has-type-2-diabetes.html', 'snippet': 'Paula Deen’s disclosure that she has Type 2 diabetes has added impetus to the debate on the national diet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR 10 years, wielding slabs of cream cheese and mounds of mayonnaise, Paula Deen has become television’s self-crowned queen of Southern cuisine and one of the country’s most popular chefs, with an empire built on layers of gooey butter cake, fried chicken and sheer force of personality.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/dining/17deen_cnd/17deen_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 784, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/17/dining/17deen_cnd/17deen_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/17/dining/17deen_cnd/17deen_cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chef Has Diabetes, and Some Say ‘I Told You So’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Diabetes Gives Deen Cooking Lessons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deen, Paula', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diabetes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Novo Nordisk A/S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T17:20:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Moskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'ty