enforcement by separating fact from hyped-up fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/theater/reviews/newyorkland-at-coil-festival-shows-police-work-review.html', 'snippet': '“Newyorkland,” a multimedia show in the Coil Festival, examines law enforcement by separating fact from hyped-up fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When our culture represents police officers, it’s usually a good cop-bad cop situation. With notable exceptions, like the characters in “The Wire,” those who enforce the law are typically noble or corrupt, glamorous detectives or dirty dealers. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/arts/sub-18nyland/sub-18nyland-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/18/arts/sub-18nyland/sub-18nyland-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/arts/sub-18nyland/sub-18nyland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/arts/sub-18nyland/sub-18nyland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/18/arts/sub-18nyland/sub-18nyland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Officers’ Workaday, Work-a-Night Lives, Far From Movie Glamour', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Newyorkland'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Officers’ Workaday, Work-a-Night Lives, Far From Movie Glamour', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Temporary Distortion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coil Festival', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T21:30:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Zinoman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinoman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cba7c4f3-3e71-5c38-aab1-62437a7d6f28', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cba7c4f3-3e71-5c38-aab1-62437a7d6f28'}, {'abstract': 'The journals aren’t what they were, and haven’t been for a long time.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/17/open-science-and-the-econoblogosphere/', 'snippet': 'The journals aren’t what they were, and haven’t been for a long time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Interesting article in the Times about the push for “open science”, bypassing the traditional structure of refereed journals in favor of a sort of fluid, self-policing online community. I can’t and won’t weigh in on this issue with regards to hard science, but I think there are some interesting parallels with what has been happening in economics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Open Science And The Econoblogosphere', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academic and Scientific Journals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-17T21:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'o