field in New Zealand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/sports/olympics/rugby-the-royals-and-unruly-behavior.html', 'snippet': 'Mike Tindall married Zara Phillips, a granddaughter of Queen Elizabeth II in July, then took part in the rugby World Cup and tabloid antics off the field in New Zealand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yeah, sure, everybody became upset last week when the police revealed they had smuggled a fake bomb into Olympic Park in a security test announced six months ahead of the London Games.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/sports/YLONGMAN2/YLONGMAN2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 764, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/sports/YLONGMAN2/YLONGMAN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/18/sports/YLONGMAN2/YLONGMAN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rugby, the Royals and Unruly Behavior', 'kicker': 'On Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rugby, the Royals and Unruly Behavior', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rugby (Game)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horsemanship and Equestrian Events', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queenstown (New Zealand)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T00:57:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeré Longman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d2a599c-cd34-5506-a22e-feb86604297c', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d2a599c-cd34-5506-a22e-feb86604297c'}, {'abstract': 'Lawyers for Hosni Mubarak, the ousted president, began laying out their defense against charges that he participated in corruption and directed the killing of demonstrators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/world/middleeast/egypt-mubarak-defense-is-opened.html', 'snippet': 'Lawyers for Hosni Mubarak, the ousted president, began laying out their defense against charges that he participated in corruption and directed the killing of demonstrators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lawyers for Hosni Mubarak, the ousted president, called him “a clean man who could not do wrong” as they began laying out their defense on Tuesday against charges that he participated in corruption and directed the killing of demonstrators. Mr. Mubarak’s chief lawyer, Farid el-Deeb, cited the testimony of the former intelligence chief Omar Suleiman that Mr. Mubarak had instructed the police to use only nonlethal weapons like tear gas, water cannons and nightsticks in battling the protests last year that ultimately ended his rule. Mr. Mubarak, 83, was once again rolled into the court on a stretcher. But for the first time since the trial began in August, he then sat up in a wheelchair, presenting his face to the court as he listened to the defense, state news media reported. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: Mubarak Defense Is Opened', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt: Mubarak Defense Is Opened', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Suleiman, Omar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mubarak, Hosni', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T01:09:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Omniya Al Desoukie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Omniya', 'middlename': 'Al', 'lastname': 'Desoukie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f46db049-3d17-5194-b4dd-f2bed13e690d', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f46db049-3d17-5194-b4dd-f2bed13e690d'}, {'abstract': 'British adventurers played cricket on Tuesday at the South Pole to commemorate the centenary of the arrival there of the ill-fated expedition led by Robert Falcon Scott.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/world/europe/at-south-pole-a-game-of-cricket.html', 'snippet': 'British adventurers played cricket on Tuesday at the South Pole to commemorate the centenary of the arrival there of the ill-fated expedition led by Robert Falcon Scott.', 'lead_paragraph': 'British adventurers braving weather of 31 degrees below zero played cricket on Tuesday at the South Pole to commemorate the centenary of the arrival there on Jan. 17, 1912, of the ill-fated British expedition led by Robert Falcon Scott, which reached the pole only to discover that a rival team led by Roald Amundsen, a Norwegian, had accomplished the feat a month earlier. The British cricketers won a narrow victory over a “rest of the world” team of scientists working at the Amundsen-Scott research center. The captain of the British team, Neil Laughton, a former Royal Marine commando, said he hoped the victory would have pleased Captain Scott and his four companions, all of whom died on the 900-mile return trek.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At South Pole, a Game of Cricket', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Antarctica', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At South Pole, a Game of Cricket', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AMUNDSEN, ROALD', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Robert Falcon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cricket (Game)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T01:16:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/67c432bb-04cb-5dda-ab73-7b8dc07e9af6', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67c432bb-04cb-5dda-ab73-7b8dc07e9af6'}, {'abstract': 'The United States and Sudan traded accusations over the humanitarian situation in the states of Blue Nile and South Kordofan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/world/africa/us-says-sudan-is-blocking-aid.html', 'snippet': 'The United States and Sudan traded accusations over the humanitarian situation in the states of Blue Nile and South Kordofan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States and Sudan traded accusations over the humanitarian situation in the states of Blue Nile and South Kordofan, embattled since the north and south of Sudan split into two nations last summer. About 500,000 displaced people face famine if substantial international aid does not begin to flow by March, said Susan E. Rice, the United States ambassador, calling an aid blockade imposed by Sudan “unconscionable and unacceptable.” Sudan’s United Nations envoy, Daffa-Alla Elhag Ali Osman, said the situation for most civilians in the area was “fantastic,” denying any blockade except in “pockets” where rebels took shelter. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Says Sudan Is Blocking Aid', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | United Nations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Says Sudan Is Blocking Aid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Susan E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Famine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Sudan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T01:20:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a34bccd-a0d9-509e-a1d0-1043bfbc98c2', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a34bccd-a0d9-509e-a1d0-1043bfbc98c2'}, {'abstract': 'Buzzing insects were more of a distraction for Serena Williams than her injured left ankle in a first-rou