sday that features two promising ideas: one to ensure that New York will not lose more than $700 million in federal education funds and a second to save taxpayers money by reducing public pension costs. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gov. Cuomo’s Sound Budget', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gov. Cuomo&#8217;s Sound Budget', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Performance Evaluations (Labor)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T02:53:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2eaff9d8-1ef2-5322-9204-96888b83f8c1', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2eaff9d8-1ef2-5322-9204-96888b83f8c1'}, {'abstract': 'If the campaign includes a fraught discussion about minorities and entitlements, work ethics and what it means to be American, it will have gotten under way in earnest in South Carolina this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/us/politics/risks-for-gop-on-attacks-with-racial-themes-political-memo.html', 'snippet': 'If the campaign includes a fraught discussion about minorities and entitlements, work ethics and what it means to be American, it will have gotten under way in earnest in South Carolina this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLESTON, S.C. — South Carolina has the nation’s first female Indian-American governor (a Republican), the highest-ranking African-American in Congress (on the Democratic side) and a rapidly growing population of Latinos, all evidence, longtime political players here say, that the state is shedding its racially charged past.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/us/politics/MEMO/MEMO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/18/us/politics/MEMO/MEMO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/us/politics/MEMO/MEMO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Risks for G.O.P. in Attacks With Racial Themes', 'kicker': 'Political Memo', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Risks for G.O.P. in Attacks With Racial Themes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Juan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T02:55:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Rutenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rutenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/334cf9e8-3237-5c36-825d-5801479e0243', 'word_count': 974, 'uri': 'nyt://article/334cf9e8-3237-5c36-825d-5801479e0243'}, {'abstract': 'The decision by the governor of Delaware to commute a sentence to life without parole meets the imperative of justice. Imposing the death penalty would have been grossly unjust.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/opinion/a-death-penalty-commutation.html', 'snippet': 'The decision by the governor of Delaware to commute a sentence to life without parole meets the imperative of justice. Imposing the death penalty would have been grossly unjust.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Tuesday, Gov. Jack Markell of Delaware commuted the death sentence of Robert Gattis to life without parole. In doing so, he said he gave “great weight” to the careful decision of the state board of pardons to recommend that the sentence be commuted, the first such recommendation the board has made since the death penalty was reinstated there in 1974. As a condition of the commutation, which is supported by a long list of former judges and prosecutors, Mr. Gattis is expected to waive all legal challenges and live out his life in prison. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Death Penalty Commutation', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Death Penalty Commutation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Markell, Jack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amnesties, Commutations and Pardons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Delaware', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T02:57:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/63795d37-506f-5f1f-87b5-b966f77e0c0c', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63795d37-506f-5f1f-87b5-b966f77e0c0c'}, {'abstract': 'On June 30, there will be one more second in the day to close the gap between earth time and official time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/opinion/how-will-you-use-your-leap-second.html', 'snippet': 'On June 30, there will be one more second in the day to close the gap between earth time and official time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When February comes, we will receive the gift of an extra day because 2012 is a leap year. But that isn’t all the extra time we get this year. On the night of June 30, there will be one more second in the day — a leap second.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Will You Use Your Leap Second?', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Will You Use Your Leap Second?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Time', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Leap Year', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Standards and Standardization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institute of Standards and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T02:59:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'perso