Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T04:27:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Weisman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weisman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06105955-ee40-585f-9370-635f56102a41', 'word_count': 1021, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06105955-ee40-585f-9370-635f56102a41'}, {'abstract': 'A daily walk or jog could alter the risk of developing Alzheimer’s or change the course of the disease if it begins in some people, a new study suggests.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/how-exercise-may-keep-alzheimers-at-bay/', 'snippet': 'A daily walk or jog could alter the risk of developing Alzheimer’s or change the course of the disease if it begins in some people, a new study suggests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alzheimer’s disease, with its inexorable loss of memory and self, understandably alarms most of us. This is especially so since, at the moment, there are no cures for the condition and few promising drug treatments. But a cautiously encouraging new study from The Archives of Neurology suggests that for some people, a daily walk or jog could alter the risk of developing Alzheimer’s or change the course of the disease if it begins.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Exercise May Keep Alzheimer’s at Bay', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exercise May Throw Up an Alzheimeru2019s Roadblock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics a