ver Chief’s Age Rattles Leadership', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yardley, Jim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T05:09:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/457b4140-ba88-514c-be82-05418b99a9b2', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/457b4140-ba88-514c-be82-05418b99a9b2'}, {'abstract': 'NYT Front Page for 01/18/2012', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2012/01/18/multimedia/18frontpage.html', 'snippet': 'NYT Front Page for 01/18/2012', 'lead_paragraph': 'NYT Front Page for 01/18/2012', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'NYT Front Page for 01/18/2012', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T05:10:41+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/e1023c45-23af-52d7-88c0-ffa730a7bd10', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/e1023c45-23af-52d7-88c0-ffa730a7bd10'}, {'abstract': 'The nation’s mayors, both Democrats and Republicans, are exasperated as a study shows many areas have years to go until employment recovers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/us/few-cities-have-regained-lost-jobs-report-finds.html', 'snippet': 'The nation’s mayors, both Democrats and Republicans, are exasperated as a study shows many areas have years to go until employment recovers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Less than a tenth of the nation’s metropolitan areas have regained the jobs they lost in the economic downturn, according to a report being released Wednesday by the nation’s mayors as they gather in Washington to express their exasperation that the federal government seems more intent on cutting aid to cities than on sending more.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Few Cities Have Regained Jobs They Lost, Report Finds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Few Cities Have Regained Jobs They Lost, Report Finds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mayors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T05:26:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60079acb-94a6-5a60-973b-24b81fabc777', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60079acb-94a6-5a60-973b-24b81fabc777'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Jack Markell approved the first sentence commutation in Delaware’s recent history, converting the death sentence of Robert Gattis to one of life in prison without the possibility of parole.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/us/delaware-governor-commutes-death-sentence.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Jack Markell approved the first sentence commutation in Delaware’s recent history, converting the death sentence of Robert Gattis to one of life in prison without the possibility of parole.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Jack Markell approved the first sentence commutation in Delaware’s recent history on Tuesday, converting the death sentence of Robert Gattis to one of life in prison without the possibility of parole. Mr. Gattis, who was convicted of the 1990 murder of his longtime girlfriend, Shirley Slay, was scheduled to be executed by lethal injection on Jan. 20. Advocates for Mr. Gattis petitioned the State Board of Pardons for clemency, arguing that his abusive childhood was not understood by the court at the time of his sentencing. Mr. Markell, a Democrat, followed the advice of the board, making Mr. Gattis’s commutation conditional on his agreement not to ask for an additional commutation or a pardon. Mr. Gattis’s lawyers say he will accept those conditions. “This commutation in no way relieves Mr. Gattis of his moral or legal guilt,” Mr. Markell wrote in a statement. “My decision is among the most difficult I have had to make in all my years in public service.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Delaware: Governor Commutes Death Sentence', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Mid-Atlantic', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Delaware: Governor Commutes Death Sentence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Delaware', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amnesties, Commutations and Pardons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Markell, Jack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T05:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d209429-36f9-51cc-87d0-f62a951fdf54', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d209429-36f9-51cc-87d0-f62a951fdf54'}, {'abstract': 'Advantages for investments have significantly reduced the effective federal income tax rate paid by the wealthiest Americans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/us/politics/for-wealthy-tax-cuts-since-1980s-have-been-gain-gain.html', 'snippet': 'Advantages for investments have significantly reduced the effective federal income tax rate paid by the wealthiest Americans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The effective federal income tax rate paid by the wealthiest Americans has dropped significantly during the last several decades, largely because of tax cuts on investment income. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Since 1980s, the Kindest of Tax Cuts for the Rich', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Since 1980s, The Kindest Of Tax Cuts For the Rich', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Gains Tax', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T05:31:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Kocieniewski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kocieniewski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None