htsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/young-concedes-poor-play-in-four-set-loss/', 'snippet': 'A disappointed Donald Young blamed his own poor play on Wednesday in a four-set loss to Lukas Lacko at the Australian Open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia – It wasn’t Donald Young’s day. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Concedes Poor Play in Four-Set Loss', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T08:42:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By JOHN MARTIN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MARTIN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8068cc5-436d-5ac4-a956-69e7cbcab896', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8068cc5-436d-5ac4-a956-69e7cbcab896'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the work of India’s first female photojournalist.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/homai-vyarawalla-first-lady-of-the-lens/', 'snippet': 'A look at the work of India’s first female photojournalist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Homai Vyarawalla, India’s first female photojournalist, who died on Sunday, chronicled India’s independence with a spirit that was unmatched by the following generations, in part because of changes in India itself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Homai Vyarawalla, ‘First Lady of the Lens’', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T08:57:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Timmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Timmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51a73872-2b4c-5ec3-b533-8801e981b09d', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51a73872-2b4c-5ec3-b533-8801e981b09d'}, {'abstract': 'On Jan. 18, 1912, one day after reaching the South Pole, the English explorer Robert F. Scott and his expedition found a tent erected by Roald Amundsen’s expedition, which had reached the pole five weeks earlier.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/jan-18-1912-robert-falcon-scott-discovers-tent-of-explorer-who-beat-him-to-south-pole/', 'snippet': 'On Jan. 18, 1912, one day after reaching the South Pole, the English explorer Robert F. Scott and his expedition found a tent erected by Roald Amundsen’s expedition, which had reached the pole five weeks earlier.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Jan. 18, 1912, one day after reaching the South Pole, the English explorer Robert F. Scott and his expedition found a tent erected by Roald Amundsen’s expedition, which had reached the pole five weeks earlier. Inside the tent, Scott found a note addressed to him from Amundsen. Should he not return home safely, Amundsen wanted someone to tell King Haakon of Norway that he had indeed reached the pole.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jan. 18, 1912 | Robert Falcon Scott Discovers Tent of Explorer Who Beat Him to South Pole', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T09:01:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7639f704-b01b-5e1b-a9b3-00de2e2c5835', 'word_count': 718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7639f704-b01b-5e1b-a9b3-00de2e2c5835'}, {'abstract': 'Even before the Costa Concordia accident, some might have been tempted to dismiss a cruise as voluntary incarceration aboard a floating skyscraper with only honeymooners and retirees for company. But 15 million vacationers a year think otherwise, a third of them from Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/disaster-cripples-cruiser-not-cruising/', 'snippet': 'Even before the Costa Concordia accident, some might have been tempted to dismiss a cruise as voluntary incarceration aboard a floating skyscraper with only honeymooners and retirees for company. But 15 million vacationers a year think otherwise, ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — As every seafarer knows, it’s an ill wind indeed that blows nobody any good.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Disaster Cripples Cruiser, Not Cruising', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cruises', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnival Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T09:13:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bccd012d-a4d3-59b8-8306-ac1915228aea', 'word_count': 448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bccd012d-a4d3-59b8-8306-ac1915228aea'}, {'abstract': 'Can you find the average yards-per-catch in a football game, given each team’s total passing yards and number of pass attempts?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/test-yourself-math-jan-17-2012/', 'snippet': 'Can you find the average yards-per-catch in a football game, given each team’s total passing yards and number of pass attempts?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with this question created by Pete Diamantis at Math for America from the article “Saints Sweep Past the Lions, Mostly by Going Over Them.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, Jan. 18, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T09:26:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c9fdecb-230a-5941-bb61-d20cc4b6e978', 'word_count': 55, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c9fdecb-230a-5941-bb61-d20cc4b6e978'}, {'abstract': 'Why is Wikipedia “going dark” today?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/sites-go-dark-to-protest-sopa-and-pipa/', 'snippet': 'Why is Wikipedia “going dark” today?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHY is Wikipedia “going dark” today?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sites Go Dark to Protest Web Piracy Bills', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T09:43:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87ed0117-03ed-542d-a4cf-4fe8fc3a3645', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87ed0117-03ed-542d-a4cf-4fe8fc3a3645'}, {'abstract': 'Lucas Papademos, Greece’s prime minister, took aim at hedge funds and other private holders of the country’s debt, saying that he would consider legislation forcing them to take losses if no agreement can be reached in critical negotiations scheduled to resume Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/greek-premier-warns-creditors/', 'snippet': 'Lucas Papademos, Greece’s prime minister, took aim at hedge funds and other private holders of the country’s debt, saying that he would consider legislation forcing them to take losses if no agreement can be reached in critical negotiations schedu...', 'lead_paragraph': 'My colleague Rachel Donadio reports that Lucas Papademos, Greece’s prime minister, warned hedge funds and other private bondholders that he would consider legislation forcing them to take losses on their Greek bond holdings if no agreement can be reached in critical negotiations scheduled to resume Wednesday. It was Mr. Papademos’s first interview with a newspaper since he came to office in November. Read the full article »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greek Premier Warns Hedge Funds', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T09:47:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6747078-7217-5c85-a46a-9a99a331c26a', 'word_count': 67, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6747078-7217-5c85-a46a-9a99a331c26a'}, {'abstract': 'The new divide in America is between a smaller mobile class and one that can’t quite pack up and go.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/magazine/adam-davidson-mobile-class.html', 'snippet': 'The new divide in America is between a smaller mobile class and one that can’t quite pack up and go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Throughout American history there has almost always been at least one central economic narrative that gave the ambitious or unsatisfied reason to pack up and seek their fortune elsewhere. For the first 300 or so years of European settlement, the story was about moving outward: getting immigrants to the continent and then to the frontier to clear the prairies, drain the swamps and build new cities.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22economy_span/22economy_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22economy_span/22economy_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22economy_span/22economy_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22economy_span/22economy_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22economy_span/22economy_span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mess on the Ladder of Success', 'kicker': 'It’s the Economy', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stuck In The Loop', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T10:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Davidson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davidson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/684582c2-73c2-5685-86e5-beacc20fcfb6', 'word_count': 1030, 'uri': 'nyt://article/684582c2-73c2-5685-86e5-beacc20fcfb6'}, {'abstract': 'The “fastest man on no legs” may be Olympic-bound.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/magazine/oscar-pistorius.html', 'snippet': 'The “fastest man on no legs” may be Olympic-bound.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oscar Pistorius trains inside a converted garage at the home of his personal trainer, a former professional rugby player. Iron pull-up bars and a variety of ropes and pulleys are bolted to brick walls. Free weights are lined up on the floor, along with hammered-together wooden boxes that serve as platforms for step-ups and standing jumps. Some of the equipment is clamped to an exterior wall of the garage, opposite an uncovered patio; when it rains, athletes just carry on and get soaked. “It’s old-school,” Pistorius said as we drove up to the place early one morning. “Some of the guys who train here, they bang it so hard, they often get sick in the garden. Nobody judges them.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22pistorius3/mag-22Pistorius-t_CA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 846, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22pistorius3/22pistorius3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22pistorius3/22pistorius3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fast Life of Oscar Pistorius', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fast Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pistorius, Oscar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Running', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Assn of Athletics Federations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostheses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T10:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Sokolove', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sokolove', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0afe4b3a-dade-5f47-82ff-0168a945f7cf', 'word_count': 6168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0afe4b3a-dade-5f47-82ff-0168a945f7cf'}, {'abstract': 'In 1965, Grey Villet photographed an interracial couple then embroiled in legal tangles. Rather than focusing on the controversy, he focused on the heart of the matter: love.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/the-heart-of-the-matter-love/', 'snippet': 'In 1965, Grey Villet photographed an interracial couple then embroiled in legal tangles. Rather than focusing on the controversy, he focused on the heart of the matter: love.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the spring of 1965, my husband, Grey Villet, was given a Life Magazine assignment to photograph the Lovings, an interracial married couple then embroiled in legal tangles after their arrest for miscegenation in Virginia. Grey did not concern himself with those entanglements. He chose, as he did in every essay we ever worked on together, to seek out the literal heart of the matter: a love story.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Heart of the Matter: Love', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T10:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barbara Villet', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barbara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Villet', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d75d2102-c8d2-5091-95e2-845ce3178f81', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d75d2102-c8d2-5091-95e2-845ce3178f81'}, {'abstract': 'Student Opinion | Tell us what in the fields of science or medicine you’d choose to investigate if you had all the resources you needed. The cure for a particular disease? Space exploration? Solutions to a global problem? Feel free to dream big.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/given-unlimited-resources-what-scientific-or-medical-problem-would-you-investigate/', 'snippet': 'Student Opinion | Tell us what in the fields of science or medicine you’d choose to investigate if you had all the resources you needed. The cure for a particular disease? Space exploration? Solutions to a global problem? Feel free to dream big.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Science Times this week has several articles about “citizen scientists” taking research into their own hands, whether through labs open to the public (including non-scientists), or via collaborative, online sites for sharing science research and knowledge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Given Unlimited Resources, What Scientific or Medical Problem Would You Investigate?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T10:06:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddd274f6-90f5-5188-af9f-f602f8715e2f', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddd274f6-90f5-5188-af9f-f602f8715e2f'}, {'abstract': 'A new twist emerged from the horrific accounts of the wreck, with the captain saying he tripped during the passenger panic on deck, fell overboard and ended up in a lifeboat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/world/europe/costa-concordia-italy-cruise-ship-rescue-suspended.html', 'snippet': 'A new twist emerged from the horrific accounts of the wreck, with the captain saying he tripped during the passenger panic on deck, fell overboard and ended up in a lifeboat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GIGLIO, Italy — The saga of the stricken cruise liner Costa Concordia took further twists Wednesday, with a warning from Italy’s environment minister that it could sink and news that the captain claimed he had slipped on deck and tumbled overboard, winding up in a lifeboat during the panicky passenger escape — not abandoned ship like a coward, as accused.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multi