ment_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gaia Pianigiani and Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gaia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pianigiani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d58e344b-5040-57a3-8613-85d209a6a5c6', 'word_count': 1036, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d58e344b-5040-57a3-8613-85d209a6a5c6'}, {'abstract': 'A trial program in China requires users of the country’s microblog services to disclose their identities to the government in order to post comments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/world/asia/china-expands-program-requiring-real-name-registration-online.html', 'snippet': 'A trial program in China requires users of the country’s microblog services to disclose their identities to the government in order to post comments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — China will expand nationwide a trial program that requires users of the country’s wildly popular microblog services to disclose their identities to the government in order to post comments online, the government’s top Internet regulator said Wednesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China Expands Program Requiring Real-Name Registration Online', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China Expands Online Registration Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T10:11:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9c81f28-57b7-5948-81cb-6d08a446c9a8', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9c81f28-57b7-5948-81cb-6d08a446c9a8'}, {'abstract': 'In the third year of the IHT’s groundbreaking and sweeping series on women, we ask what comes next in the developed and developing world. Can young women in the West really have it all? Or do their sisters in the dynamic developing world have some advantages?', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/womenscapes/', 'snippet': 'In the third year of the IHT’s groundbreaking and sweeping series on women, we ask what comes next in the developed and developing world. Can young women in the West really have it all? Or do their sisters in the dynamic developing world have some...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Female Factor is the IHT’s look at the place of women in the early 21st century. Many women have more power over their bodies and their fates than at any time in history. What are they doing with this new-found influence? Where do they want to go? This year, the third of this uniquely broad and thorough series on the world’s women, we will paint ‘’Womenscapes,’’ portraits that illustrate the vast possibilities — and the limitations — in a world that has now largely embraced women’s empowerment as a positive force. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Womenscapes', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Telekom AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North America', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T10:30:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cefd4577-6c6c-5e2f-999b-4ad6db590031', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cefd4577-6c6c-5e2f-999b-4ad6db590031'}, {'abstract': 'Israel’s defense minister moved to calm the increasingly tense discourse over Iran’s nuclear program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/world/middleeast/iran-nuclear-program-sanctions-russia-israel-attack.html', 'snippet': 'Israel’s defense minister moved to calm the increasingly tense discourse over Iran’s nuclear program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Israel moved to calm the increasingly tense discourse over Iran’s nuclear program on Wednesday, with the Israeli defense minister asserting that any decision on a possible pre-emptive military strike on Iranian targets was “very far off.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Decision to Attack Iran Is ‘Far Off,’ Israel Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Decision on Whether to Attack Iran Is ‘Far Off,’ Israeli Defense Minister Says', 'name': None, 'seo': Non