rices, and Mike Mussa (A Bit Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T13:45:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef6a154e-bfbe-55fc-bf1e-4be7a7245703', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef6a154e-bfbe-55fc-bf1e-4be7a7245703'}, {'abstract': 'If Mitt Romney is the Republican presidential nominee, by the general election, his taxes might seem like old news.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/a-case-of-good-timing-perhaps-in-romneys-tax-disclosure/', 'snippet': 'If Mitt Romney is the Republican presidential nominee, by the general election, his taxes might seem like old news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pile-on began just moments after Mitt Romney — a man easily worth more than $200 million — admitted Tuesday morning that he pays federal taxes at a rate of about 15 percent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Case of Good Timing, Perhaps, in Romney’s Tax Disclosure', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T13:50:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89a318b5-e9e6-5eb5-adb5-839adbc92159', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89a318b5-e9e6-5eb5-adb5-839adbc92159'}, {'abstract': 'Also, a new urgency and speed among films at Sundance, and China wants to require all microbloggers to reveal their identities before being allowed to post comments.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/the-breakfast-meeting-wikipedia-turns-off-the-lights/', 'snippet': 'Also, a new urgency and speed among films at Sundance, and China wants to require all microbloggers to reveal their identities before being allowed to post comments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wednesday is a day of Web-wide protest over proposed antipiracy laws. Some sites, like Google and Craigslist are donning black and encouraging visitors to contact their local members of Congress (both are still operating, however). Others, most notably the English-language Wikipedia portal, have closed for the day, while also asking visitors to lobby Congress.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Wikipedia Turns Off the Lights', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stunts and Stuntmen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dodd, Christopher J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Lamar S', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Party (Germany)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Motion Picture Assn of America', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wikimedia Foundation', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wikipedia', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T13:59:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fc15a37-7c11-5353-b727-45381e2d9db0', 'word_count': 456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fc15a37-7c11-5353-b727-45381e2d9db0'}, {'abstract': 'Nahalat Binyamin Street, which crosses lower Rothschild Boulevard — Tel Aviv’s chief axis of white Bauhaus beauties — is emerging as the microhood of the moment.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/street-wise-tel-avivs-hot-new-block/', 'snippet': 'Nahalat Binyamin Street, which crosses lower Rothschild Boulevard — Tel Aviv’s chief axis of white Bauhaus beauties — is emerging as the microhood of the moment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nahalat Binyamin Street, which crosses lower Rothschild Boulevard — Tel Aviv’s chief axis of white Bauhaus beauties — is emerging as the microhood of the moment. By day this thin strip is all garment-district shuffle and cheeky graffiti art, but at night the street takes on a moody but electric air. You might find Bar Refaeli, or at least plenty of women who look like models, at the street’s hottest spot, Mizlala (No. 57). Here the city’s star chef, Meir Adoni, whips up dishes like fish kebabs, garlic-sage linguine with white asparagus and something called Palestinian tartar — ground beef, raw tahini paste, pine nuts, charred eggplant puree, cumin and parsley.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Street Wise | Tel Aviv’s Hot New Block', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T14:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Grant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0b462b6-da21-57ab-aeb8-8b9ab6ebd5eb', 'word_count': 331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0b462b6-da21-57ab-aeb8-8b9ab6ebd5eb'}, {'abstract': "As expected, Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo released his proposed state budget on Tuesday, and over all it contained good news for public education: a 4 percent across-the-state increase in aid for schools. There's a catch: only districts that approve teacher evaluation systems will be eligible for the aid.  “The equation is simple at the end of the day,” Mr. Cuomo said. “No evaluation, no money. Period.”", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/01/18/cuomo-puts-money-where-his-mouth-is/', 'snippet': "As expected, Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo released his proposed state budget on Tuesday, and over all it contained good news for public education: a 4 percent across-the-state increase in aid for schools. There's a catch: only districts that approve teach...", 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuomo Puts Money Where His Mouth Is', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T14:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': '