irers — and a few outspoken critics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/sports/golf/on-the-golf-channel-an-analyst-who-is-not-afraid-to-get-rough.html', 'snippet': 'Brandel Chamblee, an opinionated commentator on the Golf Channel, is a master of statistics who has won many admirers — and a few outspoken critics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KAPALUA, Hawaii — During a telecast on the eve of golf’s season-opening event, Nick Faldo waded into shallow waters by predicting a “fabulous run” in the majors for a fellow Englishman, Luke Donald, the No. 1 player in the world and the reigning money champion on the PGA and European Tours. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/sports/19chamblee1/19chamblee1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/19/sports/19chamblee1/19chamblee1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/sports/19chamblee1/19chamblee1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/sports/19chamblee1/19chamblee1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/19/sports/19chamblee1/19chamblee1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Analyst Who Is Able to Back Up Opinions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mixing Statistics With Straight Talk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chamblee, Brandel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Masters Golf Tournament', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'PGA Tour Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Golf Channel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T16:10:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f8b9036-0f83-5219-8c2b-115dcad776c8', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f8b9036-0f83-5219-8c2b-115dcad776c8'}, {'abstract': 'The New Museum’s Carsten Höller exhibition has consistently drawn more visitors per day than any other show in the museum’s 35-year history.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/new-museums-record-breaking-holler-exhibition-prepares-to-close/', 'snippet': 'The New Museum’s Carsten Höller exhibition has consistently drawn more visitors per day than any other show in the museum’s 35-year history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A group of intrepid museum-goers as large as the population of Myrtle Beach, S.C. – slightly more than 30,000 – have screwed up their courage and plummeted down the two-story tubular slide at the New Museum’s Carsten Höller exhibition, which closes Sunday after an almost-three-month run, the museum announced.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Museum’s Record-Breaking Höller Exhibition Prepares to Close', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holler, Carsten', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Museum of Contemporary Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T16:11:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be34e832-50b1-515b-b809-aae7496b4cb7', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be34e832-50b1-515b-b809-aae7496b4cb7'}, {'abstract': 'The mother of a Marine considers how she might have felt if her son had not made it home from Iraq.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/another-mothers-son-lost/', 'snippet': 'The mother of a Marine considers how she might have felt if her son had not made it home from Iraq.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I knew her name from the newspaper articles, but I did not know if I should speak to her. What would I say to the woman whose son had fallen dead at my son’s feet?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Mother’s Son Lost', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T16:17:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Guerra', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Guerra', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60370303-9fc2-5fce-8cf0-cfbc24b50964', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60370303-9fc2-5fce-8cf0-cfbc24b50964'}, {'abstract': 'In two new picture books, a sheep and a bear cope with the arrival of new siblings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/books/baby-brothers.html', 'snippet': 'In two new picture books, a sheep and a bear cope with the arrival of new siblings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Little brothers crop up all the time, but picture books about pesky little brothers in the shape of anthropomorphized animals who happen to be named Peter? Not so much. This month brings two of them: “Another Brother,” written and illustrated by Matthew Cordell (“Trouble Gum”), and “Peter Is Just a Baby,” written and illustrated by Marisabina Russo. Both titles could easily have rolled right out of a resentful older sibling’s mouth.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/books/18brother/18brother-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/18/books/18brother/18brother-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/books/18brother/18brother-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/books/18brother/18brother-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/18/books/18brother/18brother-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Baby Brothers', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T16:21:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae8503d6-deed-51a1-a455-1cc68d4e86b6', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae8503d6-deed-51a1-a455-1cc68d4e86b6'}, {'abstract': 'The eighth-seeded Mardy Fish, the highest ranked American, had trouble controlling his shots and his temper in a first-round loss to Alejandro Falla of Colombia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/sports/tennis/fish-is-ousted-at-australian-open.html', 'snippet': 'The eighth-seeded Mardy Fish, the highest ranked American, had trouble controlling his shots and his temper in a first-round loss to Alejandro Falla of Colombia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia — No. 8 Mardy Fish, the top-ranked American, became the first top 10 player to fall in the men’s draw when he lost in the second round to the 71st-ranked Alejandro Falla of Colombia, 7-6 (4), 6-3, 7-6 (6), in three hours Wednesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/sports/19aussie1/19aussie1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 763, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/sports/19aussie1/19aussie1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/19/sports/19aussie1/19aussie1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fish Is Ousted at Australian Open', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fish Is Ousted at Australian Open', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fish, Mardy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Australian Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T16:23:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08bd53eb-f3ae-5019-858b-60a08c441003', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08bd53eb-f3ae-5019-858b-60a08c441003'}, {'abstract': 'A loophole in recently announced plans to limit the reactors’ lifespans would allow a single 20-year extension.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/world/asia/japanese-nuclear-reactors-could-operate-beyond-40-year-cap.html', 'snippet': 'A loophole in recently announced plans to limit the reactors’ lifespans would allow a single 20-year extension.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Japan could allow nuclear reactors to operate for up to 60 years if they pass safety checks, the government said Wednesday, already revealing a loophole in recently announced plans to cap their lifespans at 40 years. Chief Cabinet Secretary Osamu Fujimura told reporters on Wednesday that the 40-year cap on reactors, announced earlier this month, will still apply in principle — but operators will likely be allowed a single 20-year extension for each reactor, providing they meet strict safety standards. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japanese Reactors Could Operate Beyond 40-Year Cap', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan: Reactors’ Lives May Be Extended', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T16:28:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7653e87-13df-586b-bef5-8f370fe72dc7', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7653e87-13df-586b-bef5-8f370fe72dc7'}, {'abstract': 'David Nalbandian expresses his anger at officiating after losing in five sets to John Isner at the Australian Open.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/isner-outlasts-nalbandian-in-disputed-5-set-victory/', 'snippet': 'David Nalbandian expresses his anger at officiating after losing in five sets to John Isner at the Australian Open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia – In a stunning point-blank shootout at net, John Isner of the United States slammed a forehand volley past a lunging David Nalbandian of Argentina to claim a grueling victory, 4-6, 6-3, 2-6, 7-6 (5), 10-8.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Isner Outlasts Nalbandian in Disputed 5-Set Victory', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T16:35:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis