': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T19:06:29+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/bfd21424-e167-5ea7-a643-14d0483bd61c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/bfd21424-e167-5ea7-a643-14d0483bd61c'}, {'abstract': 'Guest blogger Josh Ruxin, founder of Rwanda Works, writes about the importance of funding palliative care in the developing world.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/time-to-face-the-pain/', 'snippet': 'Guest blogger Josh Ruxin, founder of Rwanda Works, writes about the importance of funding palliative care in the developing world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year in Mexico, just a couple of days into my vacation, I fractured my kneecap. In the emergency room, through pain so severe I found myself yelling at all helpful parties around me, particularly my wife, the doctors noted that they did not have painkillers to send home with me. “WHAT!?” I screamed. “No oxycodone, hydrocodone, nothing?” They explained that Mexico’s drug laws had grown so strict due to the actions of the narcotraficantes that pain meds were tough to come by (and only provided to patients who had been admitted to the hospital). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Time to Face the Pain', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T19:09:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Ruxin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ruxin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fa0f33b-8591-5202-b043-9ef21acb7597', 'word_count': 801, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fa0f33b-8591-5202-b043-9ef21acb7597'}, {'abstract': 'In a 7-to-2 decision, Ruth Bader Ginsburg wrote for the majority that “no just system” would allow a missed deadline to be held against the inmate, Cory R. Maples.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/us/cory-r-maples-must-be-given-second-chance-after-mailroom-mix-up-justices-rule.html', 'snippet': 'In a 7-to-2 decision, Ruth Bader Ginsburg wrote for the majority that “no just system” would allow a missed deadline to be held against the inmate, Cory R. Maples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Supreme Court on Wednesday ruled that an Alabama death row inmate who missed a filing deadline thanks to a mix-up in the mailroom of a prominent New York law firm must be given another chance.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/us/19scotus-cnd/19scotus-cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 361, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/us/19scotus-cnd/19scotus-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/19/us/19scotus-cnd/19scotus-cnd-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Justices Rule for Inmate After Mailroom Mix-Up', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Justices Rule for Inmate After Mailroom Mix-Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maples, Cory R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appeals Courts (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T19:12:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c515aad8-45a3-588f-8db4-839e52d3211e', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c515aad8-45a3-588f-8db4-839e52d3211e'}, {'abstract': 'In notching its 16th No. 1, “21” equals that 1998 movie soundtrack album.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/adele-ties-titanic-on-billboard-chart/', 'snippet': 'In notching its 16th No. 1, “21” equals that 1998 movie soundtrack album.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At this point, Adele’s run at the top of the Billboard chart is titanic. Literally: This week Adele’s “21” (XL/Columbia) notches its 16th No. 1, the first time since the “Titanic” soundtrack in 1998 that a release has topped the list as many times. As Billboard notes, only three albums in the last 20 years have had more weeks at No. 1: Billy Ray Cyrus’s “Some Gave All,” which had 17 in 1992; Garth Brooks’s “Ropin’ the Wind,” with 18 in 1991 and 1992; and the soundtrack to “The Bodyguard,” with 20 in 1992 and 1993.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Adele Ties ‘Titanic’ on Billboard Chart', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grammy Awards', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adele (Singer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Garth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cyrus, Billy Ray', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mraz, Jason', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen SoundScan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T19:12:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98009faf-e964-5fb7-af3e-fb1a29cc221a', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98009faf-e964-5fb7-af3e-fb1a29cc221a'}, {'abstract': 'The company cleared a major obstacle in its effort to forge ahead in reduced form and without its Lincoln Center home, saying on Wednesday that it had reached agreements with both of its major unions.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/new-york-city-opera-announces-deal-with-unions/', 'snippet': 'The company cleared a major obstacle in its effort to forge ahead in reduced form and without its Lincoln Center home, saying on Wednesday that it had reached agreements with both of its major unions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City Opera cleared a major obstacle in its effort to forge ahead in reduced form and without its Lincoln Center home, saying on Wednesday that it had reached agreements with both of its major unions. Rehearsals were to begin immediately.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York City Opera Announces Deal With Unions', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T19:18:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15342f8c-fab3-567d-8ad1-5dcd04f90a1b', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15342f8c-fab3-567d-8ad1-5dcd04f90a1b'}, {'abstract': 'Current low rates are a Bush-era innovation.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/the-history-of-capital-gains-taxes/', 'snippet': 'Current low rates are a Bush-era innovation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s how Romney’s low taxes will be defended by smarter conservatives (the less smart ones will just shout “class warfare”): they’ll claim that there are compelling reasons to have low taxes on capital gains, and that there is therefore nothing wrong with having very high-income people paying lower tax rates than the middle class. David Frum offers a good example. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The History Of Capital Gains Taxes', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T19:24:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab3d229e-1199-5aec-82b1-af6bab02ae45', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab3d229e-1199-5aec-82b1-af6bab02ae45'}, {'abstract': 'Store openings, sales and other shopping events in New York City for the week of Jan. 19.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/fashion/scouting-report-for-jan-19.ht