ants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T20:04:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/580a948b-0da9-59fb-9459-3d07451e6831', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/580a948b-0da9-59fb-9459-3d07451e6831'}, {'abstract': 'Cormac McCarthy has written his first speculative screenplay, a tale of a respected lawyer in the Southwest who tries his hand, unsuccessfully, at dealings in the drug business.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/instead-of-a-new-novel-cormac-mccarthy-delivers-a-screenplay/', 'snippet': 'Cormac McCarthy has written his first speculative screenplay, a tale of a respected lawyer in the Southwest who tries his hand, unsuccessfully, at dealings in the drug business.', 'lead_paragraph': '“An aged man is but a paltry thing/A tattered coat upon a stick, unless/Soul clap its hands and sing,” Yeats wrote in “Sailing to Byzantium,” the poem whose first line Cormac McCarthy borrowed as the title of his 2005 novel, “No Country for Old Men,” which was made into the Oscar-winning movie by Joel and Ethan Coen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Instead of a New Novel, Cormac McCarthy Delivers a Screenplay', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Cormac', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'va