umbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/sports/baseball/18darvish/18darvish-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 732, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/sports/baseball/18darvish/18darvish-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/18/sports/baseball/18darvish/18darvish-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Yu Darvish Signs With the Rangers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At the Wire, The Rangers Sign Darvish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Darvish, Yu', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas Rangers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T22:06:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/650395c0-ad7d-5947-b732-cd553f2e06dc', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/650395c0-ad7d-5947-b732-cd553f2e06dc'}, {'abstract': 'With characteristic candor, Heather Armstrong of Dooce.com has taken to her blog to announce her separation from her husband.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/heather-armstrong-and-her-husband-announce-their-separation-by-blog/', 'snippet': 'With characteristic candor, Heather Armstrong of Dooce.com has taken to her blog to announce her separation from her husband.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Heather Armstrong, a k a Dooce, a k a Queen of the Mommy Bloggers, has announced, via blog post, that she and her husband, Jon, are at least temporarily separating. Mr. Armstrong echoed her words on his own blog, Blurbomat, and publicly announced that he’s available — for work, that is. The small media empire the couple spun from Ms. Armstrong’s blog, Dooce.com (which famously got Ms. Armstrong fired from her job in 2002), once supported them both, along with two daughters and a dog. But apparently the company Ms. Armstrong runs will no longer support Jon\xa0 (at least not until they’re talking settlement).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Heather Armstrong and Her Husband Announce Their Separation — by Blog', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Armstrong, Heather B', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maynard, Joyce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T22:06:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7682313-b613-5e30-b36c-2445671b7e02', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7682313-b613-5e30-b36c-2445671b7e02'}, {'abstract': 'On Tuesday night, as the clock reached 9 p.m. in San Francisco, the staff changed a single line of code from “false” to “true,” locking all Wikipedia pages so they could not be edited. “It made me think of certain science fiction movies when they power down the core reactor,” a spokesman said.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/wikipedia-blackout/', 'snippet': 'On Tuesday night, as the clock reached 9 p.m. in San Francisco, the staff changed a single line of code from “false” to “true,” locking all Wikipedia pages so they could not be edited. “It made me think of certain science fiction movies when they ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Wednesday, Wikipedia, the online encyclopedia, directed its English-language visitors to a spooky, black-and-white Web page that read, “Imagine a World Without Free Knowledge.” The page, part of a protest against two online piracy bills, was simple and minimalistic. But the path to its publication was more complicated.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/business/wiki-black-decoder/wiki-black-decoder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Wikipedia Turned Off the Lights', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wikipedia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-18T22:08:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b135a82e-ab0b-5d12-bba6-3de6cce32994', 'word_count': 468, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b135a82e-ab0b-5d12-bba6-3de6cce32994'}, {'abstract': 'The writer discusses his new novel, “Norumbega Park,” which features a character who becomes obsessed with a house.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/garden/anthony-giardina-on-how-a-house-speaks-for-its-owner-qa.html', 'snippet': 'The writer discusses his new novel, “Norumbega Park,” which features a character who becomes obsessed with a house.', 'lead_paragraph': '“If you know you’re looking for a beautiful house, you approach it with a certain discernment,” Anthony Giardina said. But when a piece of real estate captures your imagination from out of nowhere, he added, “The desire gets amped up.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/garden/19QNA_SPAN/19QNA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/19/garden/19QNA_SPAN/19QNA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/garden/19QNA_SPAN/19QNA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/19/garden/19QNA_SPAN/19QNA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/19/garden/19QNA_SPAN/19QNA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Anthony Giardina on How a House Speaks for Its Owner', 'kicker': 'Currents | Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anthony Giardina on How a House Speaks for Its Owner', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giardina, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'R