ws', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0e6028d-96aa-5f4c-9bfe-779ce28c3c85', 'word_count': 1496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0e6028d-96aa-5f4c-9bfe-779ce28c3c85'}, {'abstract': 'Page Two columnist Manu Joseph, writing about  the five-day Jaipur Literature Festival that opens on Friday, argues that British and American publishers, have caused “a surge of Indian writers who are trying to sell the great Indian exotica to white people, and they guess that what the foreigners love is tradition, poverty, wedding scenes, burning widows, rebirths and talking monkeys.”', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/the-literary-circus-comes-to-jaipur/', 'snippet': 'Page Two columnist Manu Joseph, writing about  the five-day Jaipur Literature Festival that opens on Friday, argues that British and American publishers, have caused “a surge of Indian writers who are trying to sell the great Indian exotica to whi...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Page Two columnist Manu Joseph writes  that “Delhi’s writers, readers, publishers, critics, television anchors — and politicians who can read and write” will shift to Jaipur on Friday for the five-day Jaipur Literature Festival, a serious salon at its heart and a circus on its fringes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Literary Circus Comes to Jaipur', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joseph, Manu', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jaipur (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T00:34:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rendezvous', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rendezvous', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualif