se arrest.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Yu Jie, a prominent writer of 11 books and a critic of the Chinese Communist Party, said Wednesday in a news conference in Washington that he and his family had left China on Jan. 11 after more than a year of harassment and house arrest. Mr. Yu said his ordeal began Oct. 13, 2010, when he was placed under house arrest after the announcement that his close friend Liu Xiaobo, an imprisoned writer, had won the 2010 Nobel Peace Prize. That December, Mr. Yu said, he was detained for four days and tortured nearly to death. He said security officers criticized him for planning to write a biography of Mr. Liu and for writing “China’s Best Actor: Wen Jiabao,” a scathing critique of the prime minister. Mr. Yu was then kept under house arrest, with a travel ban, until this month. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Dissident Author Flees to U.S.', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Dissident Author Flees to U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yu Jie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T01:01:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a4ac4e7-ed2a-524c-9f1e-a0ee6b7860b9', 'word_count': 144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a4ac4e7-ed2a-524c-9f1e-a0ee6b7860b9'}, {'abstract': 'The ad is a rebuttal of a new television commercial being run by Americans for Prosperity, a conservative advocacy group.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/18/obama-fights-back-against-koch-brothers-in-new-ad/', 'snippet': 'The ad is a rebuttal of a new television commercial being run by Americans for Prosperity, a conservative advocacy group.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:09 p.m. | Updated COLUMBIA, S.C. — Facing attacks on his character from some particularly aggressive and well-financed opponents,President Obama will begin running the first broadcast television ad of his presidential re-election campaign on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Fights Back Against Koch Brothers in New Ad', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, Charles G', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, David H', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Americans for Prosperity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T01:12:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28d95943-9b29-5a4f-960a-5dcdbd99c8c5', 'word_count': 525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28d95943-9b29-5a4f-960a-5dcdbd99c8c5'}, {'abstract': 'Justice Minister Kenneth Clarke said that the inquiry over whether the secret intelligence service, MI6, was complicit in the abuse of terrorism suspects after Sept. 11 could not proceed until all related criminal investigations were completed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/world/europe/britain-mi6-rendition-inquiry-put-on-hold.html', 'snippet': 'Justice Minister Kenneth Clarke said that the inquiry over whether the secret intelligence service, MI6, was complicit in the abuse of terrorism suspects after Sept. 11 could not proceed until all related criminal investigations were completed.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Justice Minister Kenneth Clarke announced Wednesday that the government was scrapping an inquiry into allegations that the secret intelligence service, MI6, was complicit in the abuse of terrorism suspects who were caught up in the American-run process of extraordinary rendition after the Sept. 11 attacks. Mr. Clarke said that a team of lawyers and investigators under a retired judge, Sir Peter Gibson, had reviewed more than 10,000 secret documents, but that the next phase, summoning testimony by MI6 officials and others, could not proceed until all related criminal investigations were completed. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain: Rendition Inquiry Put on Hold', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Rendition Inquiry Put on Hold', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extraordinary Rendition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Secret Intelligence Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Torture', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T01:12:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifi