an is exhuming the bodies of 11 Japanese soldiers killed in Assam during World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Image of the Day: January 19', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T13:26:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f33ab3b-1a5f-5faf-a046-7242ed8e24be', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f33ab3b-1a5f-5faf-a046-7242ed8e24be'}, {'abstract': 'The estimated death toll from a fungal disease in eastern North America has soared from one million in 2009 to as many as 6.7 million today.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/in-bat-deaths-a-catastrophe-in-the-making/', 'snippet': 'The estimated death toll from a fungal disease in eastern North America has soared from one million in 2009 to as many as 6.7 million today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A “biodiversity crisis”: that’s how some conservationists describe new numbers released this week by the federal Fish and Wildlife Service on so-called white-nose syndrome. According to the agency, 5.7 million to 6.7 million bats have died from the fungal ailment in eastern North America since an epidemic first broke out in upstate New York in 2006. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Bat Deaths, a Catastrophe in the Making?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bats', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biodiversity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hibernation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Biological Diversity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North America', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Northeastern States (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T13:27:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna M. Foster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6606533c-8a33-5e1e-ba12-704f20cebcae', 'word_count': 558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6606533c-8a33-5e1e-ba12-704f20cebcae'}, {'abstract': 'Jude Law settles with News Corporation, a reporter is killed in Pakistan and a new film festival from YouTube.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/the-breakfast-meeting-the-web-rages-and-gingrich-pushes-back-at-abc/', 'snippet': 'Jude Law settles with News Corporation, a reporter is killed in Pakistan and a new film festival from YouTube.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The campaign of Newt Gingrich issued an open letter on Wednesday night to “ABC News leadership,’’ as word leaked out – if a siren on Drudge Report can be called a leak — that the network had conducted an interview with one of Mr. Gingrich’s former wives, Marianne Gingrich. The letter, signed by Mr. Gingrich’s two daughters from his first marriage, Kathy Lubbers and Jackie Cushman, faulted the network for engaging in “talk about the past, just days before an important primary election,” Trip Gabriel and Brian Stelter reported. The interview will be broadcast on “Nightline” at 11:35 p.m. Thursday, shortly after a Republican debate sponsored by CNN.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: The Web Rages and Gingrich Pushes Back at ABC', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venice International Film Festival', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Marianne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Ridley', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC News', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Drudge Report', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Voice of America', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wikipedia', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T13:49:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae115788-b2a2-5a03-93e3-bc60067fd59d', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae115788-b2a2-5a03-93e3-bc60067fd59d'}, {'abstract': 'In ridiculing Newt Gingrich, Mitt Romney repeats the bogus claim that the government doesn’t create jobs.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/romneys-new-target-gingrichs-job-creation-claims/', 'snippet': 'In ridiculing Newt Gingrich, Mitt Romney repeats the bogus claim that the government doesn’t create jobs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IRMO, S.C. – Mitt Romney’s stump speeches at rallies like the one here on Wednesday night are largely patriotic goo, but in the last day or two he’s also thrown in a newly sharpened jab at Newt Gingrich.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney’s New Target: Gingrich’s Job Creation Claims', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T13:54:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b723936f-02ff-50e7-a88c-c26c49e38e6e', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b723936f-02ff-50e7-a88c-c26c49e38e6e'}, {'abstract': 'The Campster escapes the Northeastern winter with a trip to the Everglades National Park.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/the-campster-the-everglades/', 'snippet': 'The Campster escapes the Northeastern winter with a trip to the Everglades National Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this column, Jeff Thrope stakes out sublime weekend excursions for the city-dwelling camper.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Campster | The Everglades', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Thrope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thrope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed25a558-77fd-5ecb-92d8-020f2ae6f27b', 'word_count': 733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed25a558-77fd-5ecb-92d8-020f2ae6f27b'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Filling vacancies in elective office created by corruption or job-jumping comes with a price, and it isn’t all for democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/politicians-ambitions-and-indiscretions-on-taxpayers-tab/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Filling vacancies in elective office created by corruption or job-jumping comes with a price, and it isn’t all for democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': ' In the dispute over New York City’s policy of digitally fingerprinting applicants for food stamps, an interesting footnote was provided by Joel Berg, executive director of the New York City Coalition Against Hunger. In 2007, Gov. Eliot Spitzer (remember him?) banned finger-imaging for most of the state beyond the city. Mr. Berg reminded us that one elected official among Mr. Spitzer’s fellow Democrats vigorously attacked the governor for this action.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Politicians’ Ambitions and Indiscretions, on Taxpayers’ Tab', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:09:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb0d49a8-b7b4-53d3-9473-723f4d3b7531', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb0d49a8-b7b4-53d3-9473-723f4d3b7531'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Rick Perry of Texas ended his bid for the Republican presidential nomination and endorsed Newt Gingrich.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/perry-to-end-bid-for-presidency/', 'snippet': 'Gov. Rick Perry of Texas ended his bid for the Republican presidential nomination and endorsed Newt Gingrich.', 'lead_paragraph': '11:45 a.m. | Updated NORTH CHARLESTON, S.C. — Gov. Rick Perry of Texas dropped out of the Republican presidential race here on Thursday and announced his endorsement for the candidacy of Newt Gingrich, a man he called a “conservative visionary.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Perry Ends Bid for Presidency', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endorsements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Rick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:17:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny and Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bd9a3f8-f0d4-5b7f-9899-dc409c98edf3', 'word_count': 1228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bd9a3f8-f0d4-5b7f-9899-dc409c98edf3'}, {'abstract': 'An effort of more than three years to unwind Lehman Brothers assets will recover almost all the $11 billion that the central bank stood to lose.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/e-c-b-stands-to-recover-nearly-all-of-its-lehman-loans/', 'snippet': 'An effort of more than three years to unwind Lehman Brothers assets will recover almost all the $11 billion that the central bank stood to lose.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:31 p.m. | Updated ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'European Central Bank to Recover Most of Its Lehman Loans', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bundesbank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lehman Brothers Holdings Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lone Star Funds', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:18:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bc58278-e8e5-5364-938a-b76e1825a890', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bc58278-e8e5-5364-938a-b76e1825a890'}, {'abstract': 'This time on taxes.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/the-tweeting-dead/', 'snippet': 'This time on taxes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oy. Ari Fleischer is using Twitter to repeat the old zombie lie about how the tax burden on the rich has risen, because they’re paying a higher share of federal taxes than they used to. Mark Thoma handles this fine, no need for me to weigh in. Basically, if the income of the 1 percent has tripled relative to the income of the middle quintile, how are they suffering if their tax burden relative to the middle class rises by a substantially smaller amount?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tweeting Dead', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:27:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66f86d44-499c-54cc-b8b3-ed561173baa0', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66f86d44-499c-54cc-b8b3-ed561173baa0'}, {'abstract': 'Healthy patients can delay bone density retests, nine things you didn’t know about Twitter, headphones to shut out the world and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/thursday-reading-healthy-patients-can-delay-bone-density-retests/', 'snippet': 'Healthy patients can delay bone density retests, nine things you didn’t know about Twitter, headphones to shut out the world and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and online in our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: Healthy Patients Can Delay Bone Density Retests', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:31:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b606c461-0b56-5bc1-9d69-dcc76e643e72', 'word_count': 121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b606c461-0b56-5bc1-9d69-dcc76e643e72'}, {'abstract': 'Government infrastructure = no jobs, private infrastructure =job?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/says-law-for-thee-but-not-for-me/', 'snippet': 'Government infrastructure = no jobs, private infrastructure =job?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In correspondence, Mark Thoma notes a double standard in the conservative outcry over the Keystone XL decision. As he notes, when it comes to the question of whether government spending can create jobs, the usual suspects claim that it’s logically impossible: income has to be spent somewhere, so all the government can do is divert funds from other uses. But when it’s a private investment, somehow that logic no longer applies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Say’s Law For Thee But Not For Me', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:34:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ca69152-9deb-5fc4-87ea-a092451e0e41', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ca69152-9deb-5fc4-87ea-a092451e0e41'}, {'abstract': 'The move comes after a court sided with city authorities, who had called on the protestors to leave the colorful campsite that encircles the entrance to St. Paul’s Cathedral, located in the heart of London’s financial district.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/occupy-london-to-appeal-against-campsite-ruling/', 'snippet': 'The move comes after a court sided with city authorities, who had called on the protestors to leave the colorful campsite that encircles the entrance to St. Paul’s Cathedral, located in the heart of London’s financial district.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Protestors in the Occupy London movement have until Jan. 27 to appeal against a legal ruling to remove their campsite in front of St. Paul’s Cathedral, located in the heart of London’s financial district.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupy London to Appeal Ruling Against Campsite', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "St Paul's Cathedral (London)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:47:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0afb8a2b-da45-5c36-ab7a-f026b1ae95b8', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0afb8a2b-da45-5c36-ab7a-f026b1ae95b8'}, {'abstract': 'This week, for the first time in two decades, the tomb of the Scipios has been re-opened to the public.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/long-awaited-scipio-tombs-reopen-in-rome/', 'snippet': 'This week, for the first time in two decades, the tomb of the Scipios has been re-opened to the public.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The founder of one of ancient Rome’s most illustrious clans, consul Lucius Cornelius Scipio Barbato, began building monumental tombs for his relatives in the third century B.C., and the family continued to use the site as a place of rest until the first century A.D. The site was forgotten, then made into a public park in 1929 and shuttered for restorations in 1992. This week, for the first time in two decades, the tomb of the Scipios has been reopened to the public (Via di Porta Sebastiano 9, www.060608.it).Among the Scipios entombed there were two of the Punic War’s greatest Roman heroes: Scipio Africanus Major, the general who defeated Hannibal and ended the Second Punic War, and Scipio Africanus Minor, who destroyed Carthage and ended the Third Punic War.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Long-Awaited Scipio Tombs Reopen in Rome', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tombs and Tombstones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:48:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Ruggeri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ruggeri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffb5820c-4ea9-5869-9abe-9b3f8e214124', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffb5820c-4ea9-5869-9abe-9b3f8e214124'}, {'abstract': 'A roundup of opinion articles from international media on bravery during a shipwreck, Salman Rushdie’s visit to India, censorship in Turkey and the German presidency.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/the-price-of-leadership/', 'snippet': 'A roundup of opinion articles from international media on bravery during a shipwreck, Salman Rushdie’s visit to India, censorship in Turkey and the German presidency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Duty to Be Brave?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Price of Leadership', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rushdie, Salman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schettino, Francesco', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wulff, Christian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Asia Times', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Costa Crociere SpA', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Der Spiegel', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Haaretz', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hurriyet', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:52:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Politi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Politi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ba64244-de0a-5e7e-8ef2-6be46ee75e80', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ba64244-de0a-5e7e-8ef2-6be46ee75e80'}, {'abstract': 'For the third year in a row the Poe Toaster has failed to make an annual birthday visit to the Baltimore grave of Edgar Allan Poe and the organizers of a vigil say they will no longer stake out this mysterious fan.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/edgar-allan-poe-fans-say-nevermore-to-vigil-for-mystery-admirer/', 'snippet': 'For the third year in a row the Poe Toaster has failed to make an annual birthday visit to the Baltimore grave of Edgar Allan Poe and the organizers of a vigil say they will no longer stake out this mysterious fan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It wouldn’t be an Edgar Allan Poe story without an unhappy ending. For the third year in a row an enigmatic fan who has visited the Baltimore grave of that macabre author has failed to materialize, and the custom of awaiting this visitor’s arrival has come to an end.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Edgar Allan Poe Fans Say ‘Nevermore’ to Vigil for Mystery Admirer', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poe, Edgar Allan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Poe, Edgar Allan, House and Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltimore (Md)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T14:53:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5795d4bb-6bcd-5fdf-9b10-1544c58dc054', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5795d4bb-6bcd-5fdf-9b10-1544c58dc054'}, {'abstract': 'Vibrant Media, a digital advertising company, will announce Thursday that it has purchased Image Space Media, an image advertising company.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/vibrant-media-deal-to-place-ads-on-photographs/', 'snippet': 'Vibrant Media, a digital advertising company, will announce Thursday that it has purchased Image Space Media, an image advertising company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Consumers have gotten used to seeing digital advertisements near articles they read, before videos they watch or next to search results on the Internet. Now they will probably start seeing more ads placed on photographs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Media Deal Brings Ads to Photographs', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T15:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanzina Vega', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanzina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vega', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c413912c-3c35-50c5-924d-b4e488c7d2b6', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c413912c-3c35-50c5-924d-b4e488c7d2b6'}, {'abstract': 'A new concept store in Aoyama takes the company back to its roots.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/seeing-things-found-muji/', 'snippet': 'A new concept store in Aoyama takes the company back to its roots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I make a beeline for Muji whenever I visit Tokyo (or New York, London or Paris). Japan’s chicly simple no-brand brand hasn’t set up shop yet in Los Angeles, and I’m addicted to its basic, everyday products. When I was in Tokyo a few weeks ago, I was excited to discover Found Muji, a new concept store that opened in November. (The Web site is in Japanese only.) Housed in the Aoyama-area retail space where Muji’s first stand-alone store opened in 1983, Found Muji takes the company back to its roots.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Things | Found Muji', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muji', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tokyo (Japan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T15:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab5fdb02-ba8a-54dd-9861-c97129dc98cd', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab5fdb02-ba8a-54dd-9861-c97129dc98cd'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/what-were-reading-354/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Post: So much for that “healthier eating” thing: The night before Paula Deen announced she has diabetes, Page Six reports, she finished off a meal at Ristorante Asellina with the tiramisu. — Patrick Farrell', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T15:03:34+0000', 'document_type'