y', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Anonymous (Internet Group)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T19:54:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a49f5003-cb24-51e9-b284-59e8e03aa5ff', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a49f5003-cb24-51e9-b284-59e8e03aa5ff'}, {'abstract': 'A video offers a rare glimpse of the wave of self-immolations that has spread across North Africa and the Middle East.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/videos-capture-self-immolations-in-morocco/', 'snippet': 'A video offers a rare glimpse of the wave of self-immolations that has spread across North Africa and the Middle East.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Silhouetted against a ball of flame, a Moroccan protester lurched forward through a screaming crowd in the center of Morocco’s capital, Rabat. The scene, captured on video, offers a rare glimpse of the spate of self-immolations across North Africa and the Middle East in emulation of the Tunisian protest that helped set off the Arab Spring.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Self-Immolation Protest in Morocco Captured on Video', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morocco', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:03:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9822a19a-0e5d-57ed-99ae-c8ae131e3963', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9822a19a-0e5d-57ed-99ae-c8ae131e3963'}, {'abstract': 'When your father-in-law is a minister, admitting you don’t go to church is hard — and admitting your family doesn’t fully embrace the faith is even harder.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/parental-quandary-considered-lying-to-the-in-laws-about-church/', 'snippet': 'When your father-in-law is a minister, admitting you don’t go to church is hard — and admitting your family doesn’t fully embrace the faith is even harder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'S. admits it. She and her husband have been lying to her in-laws about going to church. I suggested they were fibbing, but whatever you call it, the truth remains: it’s so much easier to pretend, on the phone to her husband’s minister father and his wife, that the religion S. and her husband both grew up in remains a part of their daily life. But as S. said when she described her quandary to me, the differences between the way she and her husband practice their religion and the way his parents do can’t be tiptoed around for long — not with a chatty toddler in the mix.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parental Quandary Considered: Lying to the In-Laws About Church', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'n