], 'headline': {'main': 'Santorum Claims Victory in Iowa', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:21:16+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Werschkul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c968d400-aab5-5abf-bce7-03812306bee9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c968d400-aab5-5abf-bce7-03812306bee9'}, {'abstract': 'A fresh assessment shows how humans have enriched most ecosystems even as they’ve disrupted them.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/how-humans-spread-both-ecological-disruption-and-diversity/', 'snippet': 'A fresh assessment shows how humans have enriched most ecosystems even as they’ve disrupted them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Erle Ellis of the University of Maryland, Baltimore County, is one of a growing network of biologists charting the fate of living systems in the Anthropocene, this epoch in which that phenomenon once known as “nature” is increasingly shaped, wittingly or not, by human actions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Humans Spread Both Ecological Disruption and Diversity', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biodiversity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Maryland, Baltimore County', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:23:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27ce5546-2dad-5ba9-a3dd-37c92cc6ae2a', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27ce5546-2dad-5ba9-a3dd-37c92cc6ae2a'}, {'abstract': 'Changes to the way autism is diagnosed may make it harder for many people who would no longer meet the criteria to get health, educational and social services, researchers say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/health/research/new-autism-definition-would-exclude-many-study-suggests.html', 'snippet': 'Changes to the way autism is diagnosed may make it harder for many people who would no longer meet the criteria to get health, educational and social services, researchers say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Proposed changes in the definition of autism would sharply reduce the skyrocketing rate at which the disorder is diagnosed and might make it harder for many people who would no longer meet the criteria to get health, educational and social services, a new analysis suggests. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/science/20autism/20autism-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/20/science/20autism/20autism-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/science/20autism/20autism-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/science/20autism/20autism-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/20/science/20autism/20autism-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New Definition of Autism Will Exclude Many, Study Suggests', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Autism Rule Will Trim Many, A Study Suggests', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN PSYCHIATRIC ASSN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Asperger's Syndrome", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:25:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Research', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benedict Carey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benedict', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1db75f8-feec-5dc5-a9e5-ae65cc7816bf', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1db75f8-feec-5dc5-a9e5-ae65cc7816bf'}, {'abstract': 'No other Republican candidate expresses visceral, full-bodied disgust with President Obama quite like Newt Gingrich does.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/newt-gingrich-nutty-radio-talk-show-host/', 'snippet': 'No other Republican candidate expresses visceral, full-bodied disgust with President Obama quite like Newt Gingrich does.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEAUFORT, S.C. – Newt Gingrich is climbing past Rick Santorum in the polls here, and is already proclaiming himself the only conservative alternative to Mitt Romney. The reason for his rising popularity is not really because he’s more conservative than Mr. Santorum – he’s not – or because he will bring about more conservative change, as Rick Perry said in endorsing him today before leaving the race.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newt Gingrich, Nutty Radio Talk Show Host', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:25:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ad7ef48-1003-5ed9-b5cb-f6c1d08b8b13', 'word_count': 606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ad7ef48-1003-5ed9-b5cb-f6c1d08b8b13'}, {'abstract': 'Not telling the story you may want.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/corporate-taxes-and-the-01-percent/', 'snippet': 'Not telling the story you may want.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some readers have asked whether the picture of very low taxes on the very rich changes once you take taxes on corporate profits into account, and impute them to stockholders. The answer is that yes, it does, somewhat — but there are a number of implications of such an imputation, and if I were a conservative, I really wouldn’t want to go there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corporate Taxes And The .01 Percent', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:26:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/459a68c7-b319-5cb8-a6b6-0b71e7ce56ae', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/459a68c7-b319-5cb8-a6b6-0b71e7ce56ae'}, {'abstract': 'Severe weather last July in in the Rockies and Tropical Storm Lee in August and September caused more than $1 billion in damages, bringing the total number of such events in 2011 to 14, NOAA says.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/2-more-billion-dollar-weather-disasters/', 'snippet': 'Severe weather last July in in the Rockies and Tropical Storm Lee in August and September caused more than $1 billion in damages, bringing the total number of such events in 2011 to 14, NOAA says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The count of billion-dollar weather disasters for 2011 has taken another jump. The National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration, still adding up the costs of the extreme weather events that plagued the country last year, announced on Thursday that two more had surpassed the $1 billion mark, for a total of 14. And the agency is still counting.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two More Billion-Dollar Weather Disasters', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Irene (2011)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gulf Coast (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi River', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Northeastern States (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wyoming', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:27:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa0ee841-c080-5eca-8bb3-61c83bd022ec', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa0ee841-c080-5eca-8bb3-61c83bd022ec'}, {'abstract': 'The show notes promised “psychedelic elegance” and “Wilde meets Zappa,” and backstage Dries Van Noten explained that his men’s collections had become “so serious” and he wanted to get a little crazy.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/dries-van-noten-wilde-but-not-crazy/', 'snippet': 'The show notes promised “psychedelic elegance” and “Wilde meets Zappa,” and backstage Dries Van Noten explained that his men’s collections had become “so serious” and he wanted to get a little crazy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The show notes promised “psychedelic elegance” and “Wilde meets Zappa,” and backstage Dries Van Noten explained that his men’s collections had become “so serious” and that he wanted to get a little crazy. Both Oscar Wilde and Frank Zappa are fairly loaded guns, and after seeing the show tonight at the Grand Palais, I can’t really tell you how much of either man was represented in the multicolored prints (mildly psychedelic), the calligraphy-lettered quotations on shirts (definitely Wilde), or the elongated ’70s tailoring (could be Frank). The prints certainly didn’t make a crazy statement. If you’re in the market next fall for a good-looking peacoat or streamlined overcoat with modest military styling, this collection is worth a look. But there was very little madness in Mr. Van Noten’s method. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dries Van Noten: Wilde but Not Crazy', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Noten, Dries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:34:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/141827ae-adca-5ba4-8559-f97cfd66f0b8', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/141827ae-adca-5ba4-8559-f97cfd66f0b8'}, {'abstract': 'Suggestions for fostering a meeting of the minds over classic and contemporary texts, to share ideas and bridge interpersonal or cultural gaps.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/reading-with-strangers-ways-to-study-literature-collaboratively/', 'snippet': 'Suggestions for fostering a meeting of the minds over classic and contemporary texts, to share ideas and bridge interpersonal or cultural gaps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inspired by the example of two New Jersey high school teachers who brought their students together in reading John Steinbeck’s “Of Mice and Men,” we offer you some ideas for reading alongside others in different places, circumstances and stages of life, and perhaps to bridge some divides.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reading With Strangers: Ways to Study Literature Collaboratively', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:36:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9e30e57-7de5-5827-885d-1347c38d8c31', 'word_count': 1182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9e30e57-7de5-5827-885d-1347c38d8c31'}, {'abstract': 'New state figures undermine the mayor’s boasts that New York City’s economy weathered the downturn better than the rest of the nation and bounced back faster.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/citys-jobless-rate-rises-as-nations-falls/', 'snippet': 'New state figures undermine the mayor’s boasts that New York City’s economy weathered the downturn better than the rest of the nation and bounced back faster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Almost since the recession began, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg has boasted that New York City’s economy weathered the downturn better than the rest of the nation and bounced back faster.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City’s Jobless Rate Rises as Nation’s Falls', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T20:40:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGeehan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGeehan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4115f2e3-1ba7-5340-99cd-f171f8351193', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4115f2e3-1ba7-5340-99cd-f171f8351193'}, {'abstract': 'Didier Cuche, an Olympic and world champion who has won four of the last five World Cup downhill titles, will retire at the end of the season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/sports/swiss-downhill-star-cuche-announces-retirement.html', 'snippet': 'Didier Cuche, an Olympic and world champion who has won four of the last five World Cup downhill titles, will retire at the end of the season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KITZBÜHEL, Austria — The skier Didier Cuche, a former butcher from the small Swiss village of Le Paquier, has won an Olympic medal, 4 world championship medals and 18 World Cup victories. His impressive 19-year career was hardly showing signs of a steep decline, as he has won four of the last five World Cup downhill titles. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/sports/YSKI/YSKI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 615, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/sports/YSKI/YSKI-thumbS