ported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/179c767c-f197-562b-854b-bc867384b17a', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/179c767c-f197-562b-854b-bc867384b17a'}, {'abstract': 'As the popular television series “Downton Abbey” proves, stories of Americans mingling with members of the British aristocracy titillate as much as they did when Edith Wharton wrote of them. Jan. 24 is the 150th anniversary of her birth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/books/heiresses-of-whartons-era-in-fashion-on-her-150th-birthday.html', 'snippet': 'As the popular television series “Downton Abbey” proves, stories of Americans mingling with members of the British aristocracy titillate as much as they did when Edith Wharton wrote of them. Jan. 24 is the 150th anniversary of her birth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In dramas about the British aristocracy we Americans await with tingly pleasure the inevitable moment when the family learns\xa0that there is no more money to run the estate, and everyone must retrench or — worse\xa0 — the heir must get a job. Then, like the arrival of the cavalry in a western, all is saved — the footmen, the ancestral portraits, even the Georgian silver — by the\xa0imminent commingling of fortunes with an American kissing cousin who has daughters and dollars. The “Upstairs Downstairs” details long familiar from novels, movies and television shows, and now\xa0from the popular “Downton Abbey,” seem to render us spellbound.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20TALES1_SPAN/20TALES1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20TALES1_SPAN/20TALES1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank':