ashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T23:23:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/dc50d343-671a-5b76-92b1-d9077f507af1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/dc50d343-671a-5b76-92b1-d9077f507af1'}, {'abstract': 'Woody Guthrie wrote of love; Jonatha Brooke assembled the pieces and performed the songs. The result is a little like Virginia Woolf channeling Mark Twain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/arts/music/jonatha-brooke-recalls-woody-guthrie-at-allen-room-review.html', 'snippet': 'Woody Guthrie wrote of love; Jonatha Brooke assembled the pieces and performed the songs. The result is a little like Virginia Woolf channeling Mark Twain.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I never dread the day that I will die/Because my sunset is somebody’s morning sky,” Woody Guthrie wrote in his journal on April 17, 1942. More than six decades later those words became the bridge in “My Battle,” an unpublished Guthrie lyric set to music by the folk-rock singer-songwriter Jonatha Brooke and recorded on her 2008 album, “The Works.” That record was the focus of Ms. Brooke’s 90-minute Lincoln Center American Songbook show, in celebration of Guthrie’s centennial, in the Allen Room on Wednesday night.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/BROOKE1/BROOKE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/BROOKE1/BROOKE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/BROOKE1/BROOKE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyond the Roaming and Rambling, The Sensitive Side of Woody Guthrie', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beyond the Roaming and Rambling, The Sensitive Side of Woody Guthrie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'American Songbook (Music Series)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guthrie, Nora', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guthrie, Woody', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T23:23:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/05cf5c98-3622-5840-83a7-6e561a88deeb', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05cf5c98-3622-5840-83a7-6e561a88deeb'}, {'abstract': 'With shows like “War Horse,” “Rock of Ages” and “Chicago,” Broadway has come to embrace film adaptations as good for business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/theater/war-horse-stage-and-screen-versions-help-each-other.html', 'snippet': 'With shows like “War Horse,” “Rock of Ages” and “Chicago,” Broadway has come to embrace film adaptations as good for business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR decades Broadway producers regarded Hollywood as the agent of death. They fought to delay film adaptations of “West Side Story,” “Hello, Dolly!,” “The Wiz” and other shows for fear that those movies would decimate ticket sales for the Broadway originals and their national tours. But this winter producers of the hit Broadway play “War Horse” have been cheering Steven Spielberg’s new screen version, while those on the musical “Rock of Ages” have been helping rush the movie version (starring Tom Cruise and Alec Baldwin) toward a June release.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20ADAPT_SPAN/20ADAPT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20ADAPT_SPAN/20ADAPT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20ADAPT_SPAN/20ADAPT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20ADAPT_SPAN/20ADAPT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20ADAPT_SPAN/20ADAPT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Symbiotic Bond for Stage and Screen Versions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Symbiotic Bond for Stage and Screen Versions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T23:23:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eff1dbd2-386a-56a3-af0b-6475d2ee374c', 'word_count': 1355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eff1dbd2-386a-56a3-af0b-6475d2ee374c'}, {'abstract': 'At Chamberfest on Wednesday, Juilliard students took on Brahms’s Piano Quartet in C minor (Op. 60) and Messiaen’s “Quartet for the End of Time.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/arts/music/chamberfest-with-juilliard-students-at-paul-hall.html', 'snippet': 'At Chamberfest on Wednesday, Juilliard students took on Brahms’s Piano Quartet in C minor (Op. 60) and Messiaen’s “Quartet for the End of Time.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you think back on a concert in the hours and days after it’s over, it is no longer a unified whole. You have a general impression, sure, but what sticks out most are specific moments, individual players, passages of special eloquence.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20CHAMBER_SPAN/20CHAMBER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20CHAMBER_SPAN/20CHAMBER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20CHAMBER_SPAN/CHAMBER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20CHAMBER_SPAN/20CHAMBER_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20CHAMBER_SPAN/20CHAMBER_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailw