web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/arts/music/the-minneapolis-band-howler-at-pianos-review.html', 'snippet': 'The Minneapolis Band Howler began a four-show tour of the city at Pianos on Wednesday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just how long will it take for Howler to become very, very handsome? It’s the sort of transformation that happens to young and deeply capable bands plucked from relative obscurity — the bustling Minneapolis indie-rock scene in this case, but still — and delivered to the wider world.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20HOWLER1_SPAN/HOWLER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20HOWLER1_SPAN/HOWLER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20HOWLER1_SPAN/HOWLER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20HOWLER1_SPAN/HOWLER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20HOWLER1_SPAN/HOWLER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Some Smoother Edges, Soon to Come', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Some Smoother Edges, Soon to Come', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Howler', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T23:28:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/97860ed3-2845-5ac5-a103-7bb1e0b2e189', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97860ed3-2845-5ac5-a103-7bb1e0b2e189'}, {'abstract': 'The creation of a small number of high-end units from buildings that once housed multitudes may seem incongruous, but developers say the decision is driven by the market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/realestate/institutional-buildings-converted-to-luxury-housing.html', 'snippet': 'The creation of a small number of high-end units from buildings that once housed multitudes may seem incongruous, but developers say the decision is driven by the market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE have been times in New York’s history, in response to economic downturns, when large apartments have been split up into smaller units. This is not one of those times.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/realestate/22CONDOCOVER_SPAN/condo-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/realestate/22CONDOCOVER_SPAN/condo-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/realestate/22CONDOCOVER_SPAN/condo-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/realestate/22CONDOCOVER_SPAN/condo-cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/realestate/22CONDOCOVER_SPAN/condo-cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Buildings Once Institutional, Now Exclusive', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Once Institutional, Now Exclusive', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T23:29:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fred A. Bernstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fred', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37ce2d5b-7ea9-5446-b225-77526706b23a', 'word_count': 967, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37ce2d5b-7ea9-5446-b225-77526706b23a'}, {'abstract': 'PARIS — An intelligence analyst in the United States says Hezbollah is almost certainly implicated in the seizure of bomb-making chemicals in Thailand this week. But contrary to statements by Thai officials, terrorists probably planned to use the ingredients inside the country.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/19/tracking-the-hezbollah-connection-in-thailand/', 'snippet': 'PARIS — An intelligence analyst in the United States says Hezbollah is almost certainly implicated in the seizure of bomb-making chemicals in Thailand this week. But contrary to statements by Thai officials, terrorists probably planned to use the ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — When police in Thailand this week discovered large stockpiles of chemicals often used to make bombs, they quickly said the material was going to be shipped out of Thailand to another (unnamed) country. The chemicals —fertilizer ingredients in bags labelled as kitty litter — were going to be packed into boxes labeled electric fans and exported.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tracking the Hezbollah Connection in Thailand', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chabad-Lubavitch', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangkok (Thailand)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T23:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/143706ff-b75a-5f0f-9924-072515983f73', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/143706ff-b75a-5f0f-9924-072515983f73'}, {'abstract': 'The E. J. Strickland Project and Olatuja Project performed robustly different sets at 92Y Tribeca on Wednesday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/arts/music/e-j-strickland-project-and-olatuja-project-at-92y-tribeca.html', 'snippet': 'The E. J. Strickland Project and Olatuja Project performed robustly different sets at 92Y Tribeca on Wednesday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The E. J. Strickland Project and Olatuja Project, which appeared one after the other at 92Y Tribeca on Wednesday night, have a lot more in common than the last word in their names. But that mundane point of overlap tells you something about each group. One, led by the jazz drummer E. J. Strickland, made its set a bundle of newly rearranged Stevie Wonder songs; its project was discrete, like a home-improvement job. The other, led by the bassist Michael Olatuja and his wife, the singer Alicia Olatuja, simply delivered a well-honed message; its project was a perpetual thing, a way of being. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20TRIBECA1_SPAN/20TRIBECA1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20TRIBECA1_SPAN/20TRIBECA1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20TRIBECA1_SPAN/TRIBECA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20TRIBECA1_SPAN/20TRIBECA1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/20TRIBECA1_SPAN/20TRIBECA1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'West African Rhythms And a Catalog Retooled', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'West African Rhythms And a Catalog Retooled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gospel Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Golden Lady', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strickland, E J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-19T23:32:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b798558a-baa7-5198-9c21-b9a32b2b6c3f', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b798558a-baa7-5198-9c21-b9a32b2b6c3f'}, {'abstract': 'The tabloid crime photographer Weegee is the subject of two exhibitions, at the International Center of Photography and at the Kasher Gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/arts/design/weegee-at-international-center-of-photography-review.html', 'snippet': 'The tabloid crime photographer Weegee is the subject of two exhibitions, at the International Center of Photography and at the Kasher Gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The International Center of Photography has been Weegee Central since the end of 1993. That’s when it was bequeathed 16,000 photographs and 7,000 negatives by Wilma Wilcox, the longtime companion of Arthur Fellig (1899-1968). Fellig, the Austrian-born son of Jewish immigrants, grew up poor on the Lower East Side, fell in love with photography as a darkroom assistant, and in 1935 set out as a freelance news photographer. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/2DSUBWEEGEE5/2DSUBWEEGEE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 847, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/arts/2