om-a-file-sharing-sites-shutdown/', 'snippet': 'The tech world is still digesting the news that federal authorities had shut down Megaupload, a popular file-sharing site that they said was a piracy hub and accounted for 4 percent of global Internet traffic.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The tech world is still digesting Thursday’s news that federal authorities had shut down Megaupload, a popular file-sharing site that they said was a piracy hub and accounted for 4 percent of global Internet traffic. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Fallout From a File-Sharing Site’s Shutdown', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T13:56:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d28e01b-a2b4-5f78-81b7-5e060a45651b', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d28e01b-a2b4-5f78-81b7-5e060a45651b'}, {'abstract': 'How Christopher Dodd was blindsided by SOPA opposition, and Colbert throws his support behind…Herman Cain?', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/the-breakfast-meeting-at-g-o-p-debate-news-media-in-the-crosshairs/', 'snippet': 'How Christopher Dodd was blindsided by SOPA opposition, and Colbert throws his support behind…Herman Cain?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Newt Gingrich seized Thursday night’s Republican presidential debate from the start with a scathing response to a question from John King, of CNN, about the allegations from Mr. Gingrich’s ex-wife that she was asked “to enter into an open marriage.” He met the “question with cold anger,” Jim Rutenberg and Jeff Zeleny reported, denying the account from Marianne Gingrich, which was reported first on Thursday by ABC News, and he put the blame squarely on the news media:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: At G.O.P. Debate, News Media in the Cross Hairs', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Herman', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colbert, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dodd, Christopher J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Marianne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, John', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC News', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Comedy Central', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Motion Picture Assn of America', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Politico, The', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T13:59:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad0cce59-fb09-5cad-b007-6af81bd04258', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad0cce59-fb09-5cad-b007-6af81bd04258'}, {'abstract': 'Artists and writers, take note! A new members-only workspace, complete with a library, a living room and an art studio, has opened in Long Island City.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/just-opened-the-oracle-club/', 'snippet': 'Artists and writers, take note! A new members-only workspace, complete with a library, a living room and an art studio, has opened in Long Island City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone lamenting the dearth of surrealist literary salons in New York has only to stop by The Oracle Club, a new members-only workspace for artists and writers in Long Island City. Open since early December, the club is the result of Jenna Gribbon and Julian Tepper — a couple who live above the space and a painter and novelist, respectively — having asked themselves, one morning in October, “What would be the most amazing scenario we could imagine for ourselves?” The three-room space includes a library, a living room and an art studio, all decorated by Gribbon and Tepper. “It’s Salvador Dali’s house meets Whistler’s Peacock Room meets Coco Chanel’s house meets Cocteau’s Chapel,” Gribbon said. For monthly fees ranging from $50 to $175, members get a key; access to the space from 7 a.m. to 11 p.m., seven days a week; and a standing invitation to any readings or events held at the club. The interior is still evolving. “I’m looking for the perfect small kaleidoscope to put in the bathroom wall as a fake peep hole, so you think you’re going to peep into the bathroom but it turns out to be a kaleidoscope,” Gribbon said. “Everything in time,” Tepper said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just Opened | The Oracle Club', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organizations, Societies and Clubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tepper, Julian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island City (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Aguirre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aguirre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68252c39-6caf-5279-9a88-ee3e51e0072f', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68252c39-6caf-5279-9a88-ee3e51e0072f'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Feig, director of “Bridesmaids,” talks about his contribution to adult cinema and the year in comedy.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/a-shining-moment-for-the-bridesmaids-director/', 'snippet': 'Paul Feig, director of “Bridesmaids,” talks about his contribution to adult cinema and the year in comedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite heavy interest, Kristen Wiig and Annie Mumolo are not currently working on a sequel to “Bridesmaids,” as Ms. Wiig has said several times (and confirmed in our recent TimesTalk). But there is a follow-up of sorts to the hit comedy: “Bridesmaids,” the porn version.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Shining Moment for the ‘Bridesmaids’ Director', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feig, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiig, Kristen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13aabd83-2b0f-5371-8c49-05e7f1726778', 'word_count': 96, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13aabd83-2b0f-5371-8c49-05e7f1726778'}, {'abstract': 'Christopher Dodd, the president of the Motion Picture Association of America, suggested the White House convene a meeting to discuss antipiracy. Read more »', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/dodd-calls-for-hollywood-and-silicon-valley-to-meet/', 'snippet': 'Christopher Dodd, the president of the Motion Picture Association of America, suggested the White House convene a meeting to discuss antipiracy. Read more »', 'lead_paragraph': 'Christopher Dodd, the president of the Motion Picture Association of America, suggested the White House convene a meeting to discuss antipiracy. Read more »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dodd Calls for Hollywood and Silicon Valley to Meet', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:03:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba521310-b0a9-5195-823b-4549d8ab3f9a', 'word_count': 24, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba521310-b0a9-5195-823b-4549d8ab3f9a'}, {'abstract': 'The new ads for the Microsoft search engine focus on young athletes, or as one executive put it, “real people doing inspiring, cool things and inspiring other real people to do inspiring, cool things.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/in-new-ads-for-bing-doing-is-the-thing/', 'snippet': 'The new ads for the Microsoft search engine focus on young athletes, or as one executive put it, “real people doing inspiring, cool things and inspiring other real people to do inspiring, cool things.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bing, the search engine from Microsoft, is changing advertising approaches for the first time since 2009, when it bing-ed its first “bing.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In New Ads for Bing, ‘Doing’ Is the Thing', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search Engines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearce, Kevin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:04:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bcfcd49-77b2-5ae4-b94f-4c1364a5e50a', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bcfcd49-77b2-5ae4-b94f-4c1364a5e50a'}, {'abstract': 'The protest against antipiracy legislation grew out of a grass-roots movement — a collective flexing of Internet muscle that started in some of the less mainstream parts of the Web, like the social news site Reddit and the blogging service Tumblr, and in e-mail chains and countless message boards. Read more »', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/outcry-over-antipiracy-bills-began-as-grass-roots-grumbling/', 'snippet': 'The protest against antipiracy legislation grew out of a grass-roots movement — a collective flexing of Internet muscle that started in some of the less mainstream parts of the Web, like the social news site Reddit and the blogging service Tumblr,...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The protest against antipiracy legislation grew out of a grass-roots movement — a collective flexing of Internet muscle that started in some of the less mainstream parts of the Web, like the social news site Reddit and the blogging service Tumblr, and in e-mail chains and countless message boards. Read more »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Outcry Over Antipiracy Bills Began as Grass-Roots Grumbling', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:06:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90f7d351-5f04-57f9-b1c5-d3fba93af711', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90f7d351-5f04-57f9-b1c5-d3fba93af711'}, {'abstract': 'A discussion with the former Village Voice senior movie critic J. Hoberman about The Voice, film culture and the precarious habit of cinephilia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/movies/j-hoberman-talks-about-village-voice-and-film-culture.html', 'snippet': 'A discussion with the former Village Voice senior movie critic J. Hoberman about The Voice, film culture and the precarious habit of cinephilia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1958 the writer and independent filmmaker Jonas Mekas contacted Jerry Tallmer, an editor and critic at The Village Voice and asked why the paper, which had started three years earlier, didn’t have a regular movie column. Mr. Tallmer invited Mr. Mekas to write one. He did. His first movie column that year inaugurated The Voice’s extraordinary, decades-long commitment to deeply personal, often political, always partisan, sometimes cranky and wonderfully crazed film criticism written by Andrew Sarris, Molly Haskell, Amy Taubin, Georgia Brown and David Edelstein, among many others, a tradition that effectively came to an end this month, when that paper’s longtime senior film critic, J. Hoberman, was laid off and told that his position was being eliminated.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/arts/22CRITICS1_SPAN/22CRITICS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 351, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/arts/22CRITICS1_SPAN/22CRITICS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 351}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/arts/22CRITICS1_SPAN/22CRITICS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/arts/22CRITICS1_SPAN/22CRITICS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/arts/22CRITICS1_SPAN/22CRITICS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Changing Science of Movie-ology', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Changing Science Of Movie-ology', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis and A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01f6b53f-7f03-53fc-948a-ad8f73f63328', 'word_count': 1806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01f6b53f-7f03-53fc-948a-ad8f73f63328'}, {'abstract': 'What Joseph Conrad’s “Lord Jim” can tell us about the Costa Concordia debacle.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/whoops-i-tripped-into-a-lifeboat/', 'snippet': 'What Joseph Conrad’s “Lord Jim” can tell us about the Costa Concordia debacle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Capt. Francesco Schettino would have us believe that he did not abandon his ship, the Costa Concordia, after it struck a reef. He does not deny that he was safely off board before hundreds of others, but he claims his absence was accidental. \xa0He told the Italian newspaper La Repubblica that he was dutifully escorting passengers into lifeboats “in an orderly fashion” when, “[s]uddenly, since the ship was at a 60-to-70 degree angle, I tripped and I ended up in one of the boats. That’s how I found myself there.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whoops, I Tripped Into a Lifeboat', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maritime Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Conrad, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schettino, Francesco', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:15:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18b45f86-1db5-54ec-851c-c3066464438e', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18b45f86-1db5-54ec-851c-c3066464438e'}, {'abstract': 'CliffsNotes Films has created a new animated “edutainment” series based on classic literature. What is your take on these and other products like them?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/seven-minute-shakespeare/', 'snippet': 'CliffsNotes Films has created a new animated “edutainment” series based on classic literature. What is your take on these and other products like them?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over on Education Life, I have a short piece about CliffsNotes Films, a new series in which classic literature becomes animated “edutainment.” The first offerings — six Shakepearean plays — debuted online in October.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seven-Minute Shakespeare', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e954a1f-7a62-5b97-bf82-1e2ab7f70a67', 'word_count': 1476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e954a1f-7a62-5b97-bf82-1e2ab7f70a67'}, {'abstract': 'Why even read the Cliffs Notes? Just watch them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/education/edlife/totes-shakespeare.html', 'snippet': 'Why even read the Cliffs Notes? Just watch them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF reading Cliffs Notes’s “Hamlet” seems to be a pokey, 20th-century way to speed-comprehend Shakespeare, never fear. Now there’s a seven-minute cartoon version, complete with a talking Yorick’s skull. Think of it as Cliffs Notes for Cliffs Notes.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/education/edl-22cliffnotes-t_CA0/edl-22cliffnotes-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 639, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/education/edl-22cliffnotes-t_CA0/edl-22cliffnotes-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/education/edl-22cliffnotes-t_CA0/edl-22cliffnotes-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Totes Shakespeare', 'kicker': 'Reading?', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Totes Shakespeare', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CliffsNotes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b37f04c-49b5-5948-ad61-b0048d9e9c59', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b37f04c-49b5-5948-ad61-b0048d9e9c59'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/what-were-reading-355/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Details: Which nutritional supplements should you take — and avoid? — Jeff Gordinier', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:33:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline