me': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:35:28+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'Thomas Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/2b43c592-880b-5222-92cb-3e82f7b0354d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/2b43c592-880b-5222-92cb-3e82f7b0354d'}, {'abstract': 'Among the assets seized from the founder of the file-sharing Web site were luxury cars worth an estimated $4.8 million.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/megaupload-founders-assets-included-fleet-of-pricey-cars/', 'snippet': 'Among the assets seized from the founder of the file-sharing Web site were luxury cars worth an estimated $4.8 million.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Updated | 11:55 a.m. As our colleague Ben Sisario reports, authorities in the United States has charged the founder of Megaupload, one of the Internet’s most popular file-sharing sites, and six others in charge of its operation with running a criminal piracy enterprise causing $500 million in damages to copyright owners. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fleet of Pricey Cars Seized at Megaupload Founder’s Mansion', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Zealand', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:36:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. David Goodman and Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'David', 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95284fc5-29b1-5fe5-b75a-7e15b674fa8a', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95284fc5-29b1-5fe5-b75a-7e15b674fa8a'}, {'abstract': 'Thursday’s episode of the NBC comedy “30 Rock” spun satire from a real-life incident in which its co-star Tracy Morgan was criticized for a homophobic standup routine.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/30-rock-seeks-to-close-a-controversial-chapter-with-comedy/', 'snippet': 'Thursday’s episode of the NBC comedy “30 Rock” spun satire from a real-life incident in which its co-star Tracy Morgan was criticized for a homophobic standup routine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When real-life events pierce the fictional world of “30 Rock,” the NBC series unhesitatingly turns it into fodder for comedy, whether it’s the death of the North Korean leader Kim Jong-il or a controversy that engulfed its co-star Tracy Morgan while the show was on hiatus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '’30 Rock’ Seeks to Close a Controversial Chapter With Comedy', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hate Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fey, Tina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morgan, Tracy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:38:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95683825-c072-52c1-9b30-e0688d4d7717', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95683825-c072-52c1-9b30-e0688d4d7717'}, {'abstract': 'Who pays them? The answer seems to keep shifting.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/more-about-corporate-taxes-and-the-01-percent/', 'snippet': 'Who pays them? The answer seems to keep shifting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A further thought about the proper calculation of tax burdens on very high income Americans: if you remember past debates, it’s kind of peculiar to see conservatives jumping up and down to say that Mitt Romney does too pay reasonable taxes if you include the profits taxes on the corporations in which he invests. Because if memory serves me, just a few years ago conservatives were denouncing the “flypaper theory” of tax incidence, arguing that much of the burden of corporate taxes really falls on labor, not on stockholders. Is it just my imagination?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More About Corporate Taxes And The .01 Percent', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:41:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c17aa1f8-8bd9-583b-bed9-b2b8e9ec2d98', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c17aa1f8-8bd9-583b-bed9-b2b8e9ec2d98'}, {'abstract': 'The architect Kevin Roche has a long list of notable works to his name, but he was first known for the Ford Foundation Building, which was completed in 1967.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/an-architect-returns-to-his-first-triumph/', 'snippet': 'The architect Kevin Roche has a long list of notable works to his name, but he was first known for the Ford Foundation Building, which was completed in 1967.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 89, the architect Kevin Roche finds himself back in the spotlight with the opening this week of the renovated American Wing at the Metropolitan Museum of Art.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Architect Returns to His First Triumph', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roche, Kevin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zinsser, William', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Foundation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:42:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David W. Dunlap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Dunlap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b4fef69-acd8-5ff9-bc49-b5e521c17bf9', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b4fef69-acd8-5ff9-bc49-b5e521c17bf9'}, {'abstract': 'In celebration of the 100th anniversary of Robert Falcon Scott’s expedition to Antarctica, the Natural History Museum will hold the exhibition “Scott’s Last Expedition” from Friday through Sept. 2.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/marking-an-epic-polar-journey-in-london/', 'snippet': 'In celebration of the 100th anniversary of Robert Falcon Scott’s expedition to Antarctica, the Natural History Museum will hold the exhibition “Scott’s Last Expedition” from Friday through Sept. 2.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few scientific explorations have captured the public’s imagination like Robert Falcon Scott’s expedition to Antarctica from 1910 to 1913. He and a team of scientists withstood incredible hardships\xa0 and perished before reaching home, but not before they had discovered a large number of zoological and geological specimens that greatly furthered scientific knowledge of the vast region. These included 2,109 different species of animals and plants from land and water, more than 400 of them new to science.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Marking an Epic Polar Journey in London', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Robert Falcon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:44:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Valerie Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Valerie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cabe8f4-d031-5149-8934-10229f33ca0d', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cabe8f4-d031-5149-8934-10229f33ca0d'}, {'abstract': 'As a number of sports get riskier, the death of freestyle skier Sarah Burke prompts questions about dangerous competition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/sports/after-burkes-death-a-time-for-contemplationand-questions.html', 'snippet': 'As a number of sports get riskier, the death of freestyle skier Sarah Burke prompts questions about dangerous competition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is when sports gets hard, on days when people like Sarah Burke die, when the awe over spectacular performances in increasingly dangerous sports is replaced by horror that someone could die doing them. Burke’s sport, freestyle skiing, is one of a long line of sports growing more perilous as the athletes get better and\xa0 the physics get riskier as speeds and heights increase. Sure, we cheer as we see skiers and snowboarders flip in the air, gasp as race cars pass inches from one another at top speeds, howl as hockey players and football players pound each other, our knowledge of the dangers safely stashed away. But when the worst happens, then what? How long can the knowledge stay stashed away?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Burke’s Death, a Time for Contemplation—and Questions', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Burke’s Death, a Time for Contemplation—and Questions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burke, Sarah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freestyle Skiing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T14:44:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3731cf58-7832-53cc-a843-8cf2a741b91b', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3731cf58-7832-53cc-a843-8cf2a741b91b'}, {'abstract': 'The horror director Ti West, whose latest film is “The Innkeepers,” likes to build suspense by subverting audience expectations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/movies/building-suspense-with-ti-west-and-the-innkeepers.html', 'snippet': 'The horror director Ti West, whose latest film is “The Innkeepers,” likes to build suspense by subverting audience expectations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EARLY in “The Innkeepers,” a film about two desk clerks at a soon-to-be-shuttered hotel who engage in some amateur ghost hunting, one tells the other to watch closely a video clip that purports to contain evidence of paranormal phenomena. There’s the image of a lone, stationary rocking chair on a dark porch. Tension builds, and the score swells, but what happens next is a surprise, one that challenges audience expectations. The moment reflects the filmmaking goals of its director, Ti West, who has spent his relatively brief career subverting the assumptions of horror fans.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/arts/22WEST3_SPAN/22WEST3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/arts/22WEST3_SPAN/22WEST3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/arts/22WEST3_SPAN/22WEST2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'i