'main': 'Friday Reading: Why Taxes Aren’t as High as They Seem', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:06:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/539527a9-dc6a-5373-a0cf-20d64ff77f57', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/539527a9-dc6a-5373-a0cf-20d64ff77f57'}, {'abstract': 'An increase in killings of allied forces and concerns over peace talks between the United States and the Taliban could create a rocky road for ending the war in Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/friday-roundup-mistrust-in-afghanistan-and-taliban-talks/', 'snippet': 'An increase in killings of allied forces and concerns over peace talks between the United States and the Taliban could create a rocky road for ending the war in Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Friday’s New York Times takes a look at two problems that could complicate ending the war in Afghanistan: the growing mistrust between Afghan soldiers and coalition forces and concerns by Afghan officials over clarity regarding peace talks between the United States and the Taliban.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Roundup: Mistrust in Afghanistan and Taliban Talks', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karzai, Hamid', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'North Atlantic Treaty Organization', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:07:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By At War', 'person': [{'firstname': 'At', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'War', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e2cfd12-ab69-5b7a-8d56-e567dc81e362', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e2cfd12-ab69-5b7a-8d56-e567dc81e362'}, {'abstract': 'A Gatorade shower for Giants Coach Bill Parcells in 1985 was actually a form of revenge. In the last 25 years, though, the dousing of the coach has become a winning tradition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/sports/football/a-splashy-football-tradition-gatorade-style.html', 'snippet': 'A Gatorade shower for Giants Coach Bill Parcells in 1985 was actually a form of revenge. In the last 25 years, though, the dousing of the coach has become a winning tradition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For nearly three decades, the thrill of victory for football coaches everywhere has often come with the unmistakable taste of orange. Or fruit punch. Or lemon-lime.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/21/sports/21gatorade1/21gatorade1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/21/sports/21gatorade1/21gatorade1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':