45cd'}, {'abstract': 'The situation is actually improving.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/debt-and-transfiguration-2/', 'snippet': 'The situation is actually improving.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ryan Avent points out an important fact: if you look at total debt, public and private, the United States is doing better at deleveraging than countries that talk much more about the evils of debt than we do. I thought I would redo the calculation for the US so that I understood it. I focus on nonfinancial debt — that’s because money that banks owe to each other is more a reflection of the structure of the financial system than of the degree of overborrowing more broadly. So here’s nonfinancial debt, public plus private, as a percentage of GDP:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debt And Transfiguration', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:33:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfa6a8c2-01c3-5951-a524-76b43004ffcb', 'word_count': 233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfa6a8c2-01c3-5951-a524-76b43004ffcb'}, {'abstract': 'From earthy frontier expressions to late-20th-century urban slang, Americans have eagerly reshaped language.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/speaking-american-a-history-of-english-in-the-united-states-by-richard-w-bailey-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'From earthy frontier expressions to late-20th-century urban slang, Americans have eagerly reshaped language.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There has always been disagreement on these American shores as to just what the “best” English is. The status of Parisian French or Tuscan Italian has long been unassailable. Yet in the early 1940s, fusty Chicagoans were writing to The Chicago Tribune declaring Midwestern speech America’s “purest,” while New York radio announcers were speaking in plummy Londonesque, complete with rolled r’s. Down in Charleston, S.C., the elite’s sense of the best English involved peculiar archaisms like “cam” for “calm” and “gyardin” for “garden.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/McWhorter-SUB/McWhorter-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 942, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/McWhorter-SUB/McWhorter-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/McWhorter-SUB/McWhorter-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Americans Have Reshaped Language', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Talk This Way', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slang', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John McWhorter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McWhorter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5866ecdf-36b7-5091-89ce-b218317153f3', 'word_count': 1194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5866ecdf-36b7-5091-89ce-b218317153f3'}, {'abstract': 'How are a select few people able to learn a staggering number of languages?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/babel-no-more-the-search-for-the-worlds-most-extraordinary-language-learners-by-michael-erard-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'How are a select few people able to learn a staggering number of languages?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the most surprising qualities of “Babel No More,” Michael Erard’s globe-trekking adventure in search of the world’s virtuosos of language learning, is that a book dealing with language acquisition and polyglot linguistics can be so gripping. But indeed it is — part travelogue, part science lesson, part intellectual investigation, it is an entertaining, informative survey of some of the most fascinating polyglots of our time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/Constantine/Constantine-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 992, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/Constantine/Constantine-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/Constantine/Constantine-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of Mastering Many Tongues', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They’ll Say Anything', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erard, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:37:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Constantine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Constantine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/860d22c8-b221-5e0d-bdfc-89859479f7be', 'word_count': 1172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/860d22c8-b221-5e0d-bdfc-89859479f7be'}, {'abstract': 'A proposed change in the definition of autism could change many lives, and not for the better.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/experts-consider-changing-the-definition-of-autism/', 'snippet': 'A proposed change in the definition of autism could change many lives, and not for the better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Autism, as a psychiatric disorder, is defined by the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, published by the American Psychiatric Association, which covers all mental health disorders for both children and adults. That definition matters — an official diagnosis of autism is what opens up access to services and treatment to many affected individuals, both children and adults.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Experts Consider Changing the Definition of Autism', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Asperger's Syndrome", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carey, Benedict', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN PSYCHIATRIC ASSN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:37:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a0cff59-5f81-52fe-b75e-a2e6b4a689fa', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a0cff59-5f81-52fe-b75e-a2e6b4a689fa'}, {'abstract': 'For anyone researching a “golden age” of vice, there is nothing quite as satisfying as a guide to local harlots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/guidebooks-to-babylon.html', 'snippet': 'For anyone researching a “golden age” of vice, there is nothing quite as satisfying as a guide to local harlots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Librarians may regard me as a highbrow pervert, frowning over their spectacles at my choice of reading matter. In certain archives, I’ve even been directed to sit at a solitary table, where my movements can be carefully watched. But I’ve learned to ignore the suspicious looks. The truth is, for any writer who is researching a “golden age” of vice — whether it be Renaissance Venice, Georgian London, belle époque Paris or fin de siècle New Orleans — there is nothing quite so satisfying as a guide to local harlots.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-PEROTTET/22-PEROTTET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, 