': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE BALFOUR DECLARATION: The Origins of the Arab-Israeli Conflict,  by Jonathan Schneer (Random House, $17.) Schneer’s richly documented study puts the famous declaration — a Nov. 2, 1917, letter by the British foreign secretary Arthur James Balfour supporting the establishment in Palestine of “a national home for the Jewish people” — in the context of Britain’s promises to Arabs, Jews and its European allies in World War I.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:39:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/4628723f-378d-5600-9c32-3ab3b44f57ea', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4628723f-378d-5600-9c32-3ab3b44f57ea'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DISTRUST THAT PARTICULAR FLAVOR,  by William Gibson (Putnam, $26.95.) Instead of predicting the future, Gibson, one of our greatest science-fiction writers, finds the future all around him in this beguiling collection of essays.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice: Recent books of particular interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:40:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/451ab6c4-6fff-57a6-8c0a-5eb5a2745285', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/451ab6c4-6fff-57a6-8c0a-5eb5a2745285'}, {'abstract': 'Charles Isherwood shares his earliest experiences in theater, in the audience and on the stage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Charles Isherwood shares his earliest experiences in theater, in the audience and on the stage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Charles Isherwood, who reviews Ian Donaldson’s biography of Ben Jonson on the cover, has been a theater critic at The New York Times since 2004. “When I grew up theater was what the adults did, mostly,” Isherwood wrote in an e-mail. “But I evinced an early sensitivity to the form. One of my first memories is of being taken to see a stage version of ‘The Wizard of Oz.’ I was so traumatized by the appearance of the Wicked Witch of the West that I had to be taken home in hysterics at the intermission. Not the last time I’ve gotten weepy at the theater.” Isherwood went to Stanford, where he was “a generic English major”: “My mother bemoaned this fact, and remains pleasantly surprised that I am still employed today. I took classes in Shakespeare and Restoration comedy and Japanese theater, but never formally studied theater. For me college was basically an excuse to sit around reading Henry James novels, when I wasn’t racing off to San Francisco in my secondhand Chevy Vega to see bands like Siouxsie and the Banshees and the Gang of Four and dance all night at clubs. Had I known the path my career would take, I would have spent a lot more time on Molière and Stanislavsky.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 633, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:41:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8304d6c6-28f7-5a78-b0b7-0bb3e862ccdc', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8304d6c6-28f7-5a78-b0b7-0bb3e862ccdc'}, {'abstract': 'Walter Mosley’s latest novel starring Leonid McGill ranges from an East River mansion to a shabby house in Coney Island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/walter-mosleys-leonid-mcgill-tries-to-atone.html', 'snippet': 'Walter Mosley’s latest novel starring Leonid McGill ranges from an East River mansion to a shabby house in Coney Island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A big city never looks the same once you’ve walked its streets with a hard-boiled private eye. Preferably someone as perceptive and thoughtful as Leonid McGill, the shady but honorable bruiser-for-hire in an addictive series of New York crime novels by Walter Mosley. A former mob fixer who has gone straight, McGill doesn’t so much walk the city as case it for danger. Keeping pace with him is as much an education as an adventure. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/crime/crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/crime/crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/crime/crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cheaters Never Win', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cheaters Never Win', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Connell, Carol", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MOSLEY, WALTER', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barr, Nevada', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:42:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3dea6559-4b08-55da-8251-df44f480152b', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3dea6559-4b08-55da-8251-df44f480152b'}, {'abstract': 'The plaids and patchwork in Junya Watanabe’s show today looked culled from a farm supply store or a college town like Portland, and so felt a bit in a worn groove.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/paris-mens-lean-and-still-hungry/', 'snippet': 'The plaids and patchwork in Junya Watanabe’s show today looked culled from a farm supply store or a college town like Portland, and so felt a bit in a worn groove.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The plaids and patchwork in Junya Watanabe’s show Friday looked culled from a farm supply store or a college town like Portland, Ore., and so felt a bit in a worn groove. At Yves Saint Laurent, Stefano Pilati stuck to slim dark tailoring with leather insets and flashes of silver on shoes. There was much black leather, which was based on motorcycle gear but given more polish and presented in ensemble. You can imagine people coveting pieces, especially some of the nicely shaped jackets, but the show didn’t offer much direction. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Men’s: Lean and  Still Hungry', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pilati, Stefano', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watanabe, Junya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Saint Laurent, Yves', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:44:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Horyn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horyn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/535bc5aa-5712-59b9-98dc-be7ba6205da4', 'word_count': 92, 'uri': 'nyt://article/535bc5aa-5712-59b9-98dc-be7ba6205da4'}, {'abstract': 'Are you ready for your greatest challenge — learning to throttle back and enjoy skiing the Spanish way? Then stop counting the day’s vertical feet and follow this itinerary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/travel/day-planner-turn-off-the-alarm.html', 'snippet': 'Are you ready for your greatest challenge — learning to throttle back and enjoy skiing the Spanish way? Then stop counting the day’s vertical feet and follow this itinerary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So you’ve skied the chutes of Jackson Hole and you’ve risen at dawn to porpoise through the powder from a helicopter. But are you ready for your greatest challenge — learning to throttle back and enjoy skiing the Spanish way? Then stop counting the day’s vertical feet and follow this itinerary. ', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Day Planner (Turn Off the Alarm)', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Day Planner (Turn Off The Alarm)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Adventure Travel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skiing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T15:45:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36e67da7-3ab6-5931-ba1a-5a946456ee10', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36e67da7-3ab6-5931-ba1a-5a946456ee10'}, {'abstract': 'The ski area is enjoyed as much for its slopes as for its embrace of the good life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/01/22/travel/22SPAIN.html', 'snippet': 'The ski area is enjoyed as much for its slopes as for its embrace of the good life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ski area is enjoyed as much for its slopes as for its embrace of the good life.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subty