e', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/travel/22BRAZIL-slide-8PCX/22BRAZIL-slide-8PCX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/travel/22BRAZIL-slide-8PCX/22BRAZIL-slide-8PCX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On a Brazilian Island, Sophistication With a Barefoot Atmosphere', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beaches', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:17:04+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/db56f9ef-2de0-5a6c-9ad0-26f35d8dfd6f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/db56f9ef-2de0-5a6c-9ad0-26f35d8dfd6f'}, {'abstract': 'Intimidation; intervention.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/magazine/ethicist-there-goes-the-neighborhood.html', 'snippet': 'Intimidation; intervention.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A co-worker of mine saw a van plow into two parked cars. She wrote down the license plate number as the van drove away, but when she pulled up alongside it, she recognized the driver — a known criminal in her neighborhood who is always armed and has a very bad reputation. They made eye contact, and he knows that she saw the accident. Is it her responsibility to get involved with this dangerous man? YAMIT REMZ, NEW YORK CITY', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'There Goes the Neighborhood', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'There Goes The Neighborhood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bulimia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roommates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:18:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d5844b1-c2ab-538d-962b-fdeef777b277', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d5844b1-c2ab-538d-962b-fdeef777b277'}, {'abstract': 'The pencil’s stylish cousin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/magazine/the-hand-held-highlighter.html', 'snippet': 'The pencil’s stylish cousin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once, when readers wanted to remember something, they had to mark important passages with thin, wobbly lines in drab, hard-to-relocate colors. Before the rise of the highlighter, says Dennis Baron, a University of Illinois professor and the author of “A Better Pencil,” attentive readers relied on “a combination of underlining and marginal notes.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22wmt1_span/mag-22WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22wmt1_span/mag-22WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 308}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22wmt1_span/mag-22WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22wmt1_span/mag-22WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22wmt1_span/mag-22WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hand-Held Highlighter', 'kicker': 'Who Made That?', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Highlighter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bible', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Avery Dennison Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'G T Luscombe Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pens and Pencils', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:18:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Greenbaum and Dana Rubinstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87e22d01-c2fd-5345-a268-f8a68750af51', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87e22d01-c2fd-5345-a268-f8a68750af51'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/magazine/reply-all-stephen-colbert.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We all knew that there was Stephen Colbert, the man, and Stephen Colbert, the character on television. Well, Charles McGrath tells us there’s a third Colbert: his televised persona interacting with our reality. For those of us waiting for the G.O.P. primaries to be over so we can just get to November and cast our pre-determined vote, we may actually have to pay attention, because Stephen Colbert (and his multiple personalities) will be providing some quality entertainment. And who knows? Maybe he will accidentally (or purposely?) change the entire process. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Many Stephen Colberts Are There?', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Many Stephen Colberts Are There?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colbert, Stephen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:18:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/b565b9c0-cae6-5ad6-86f9-c8a69a0e3543', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b565b9c0-cae6-5ad6-86f9-c8a69a0e3543'}, {'abstract': 'The Massachusetts Democrat refuses to go quietly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/magazine/the-not-so-retiring-barney-frank.html', 'snippet': 'The Massachusetts Democrat refuses to go quietly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You recently said about Newt Gingrich: “He’s just one of the worst people I know of who didn’t commit violence on somebody.” Did he kill your dog?He transformed American politics from one in which people presume the good will of their opponents, even as they disagreed, into one in which people treated the people with whom they disagreed as bad and immoral. He was a kind of McCarthy-ite who succeeded.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22talk/mag-22Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 623, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22talk/mag-22Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/magazine/22talk/mag-22Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Not-So-Retiring Barney Frank', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Not-So-Retiring Barney Frank', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frank, Barney', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:18:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By <a href="http://query.nytimes.com/search/query?query=&amp;d=&amp;o=&amp;v=&amp;c=&amp;n=10&amp;dp=0&amp;daterange=full&amp;bylquery=andrew%20goldman&amp;sort=newest">ANDREW GOLDMAN</a>', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/e64d7a15-4e07-5026-9f10-f61ae66ff9b3', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e64d7a15-4e07-5026-9f10-f61ae66ff9b3'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/magazine/reply-all-yoga-injuries.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I first heard about William Broad’s debate-sparking article, “How Yoga Can Wreck Your Body,” in yoga class on Saturday afternoon. As the class inhaled in an extended downward dog, the teacher asked if anyone had read the article. As we were guided into pigeon pose, the teacher offered this: “The thing is,” she told us, “if you’re mindful, you won’t get hurt.” I went home and read the article. I had not so much a crisis of confidence, but a feeling that “if you’re mindful, you won’t get hurt” was very possibly an inadequate answer.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'All Bent Out of Shape', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All Bent Out of Shape', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yoga', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sports Injuries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:18:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a862373-b34c-50ad-8511-764a4f65e8d5', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a862373-b34c-50ad-8511-764a4f65e8d5'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/magazine/reply-all-fat-trap.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Fat Trap” (Jan. 1), Tara Parker-Pope implied that obesity is virtually incurable based on the observation that eating calorie-restricted diets (less than 1,200 or 1,500 calories a day) doesn’t cure it.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fat Trap', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fat Trap', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:18:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/70da29ad-73ef-56b3-9d72-c63a9b705640', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70da29ad-73ef-56b3-9d72-c63a9b705640'}, {'abstract': 'Jayne Ann Krentz, whose paranormal romance “Copper Beach” hits the fiction list at No. 8, is an ardent defender of her genre, arguing that romances are by nature feminist and subversive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Jayne Ann Krentz, whose paranormal romance “Copper Beach” hits the fiction list at No. 8, is an ardent defender of her genre, arguing that romances are by nature feminist and subversive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FEMALE POWER: Jayne Ann Krentz’s latest paranormal romance, “Copper Beach,” is new on the hardcover fiction list at No. 8. Krentz is no stranger to this page — she’s had more than 30 New York Times best sellers — and with her success she has become a vocal defender of the romance category as a whole. In interviews, she talks about the genre’s link to the “heroic tradition,” and as far back as 1992 she edited a cultural studies anthology, “Dangerous Men and Adventurous Women,” in which she argued that romances were by nature both feminist and subversive: “Readers understand that the books celebrate female power. In the romance novel . . . the woman always wins. With courage, intelligence and gentleness she brings the most dangerous creature on earth, the human male, to his knees.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/books/review/29insidelist-img/29insidelist-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 126, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/20/books/review/29insidelist-img/29insidelist-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/20/books/review/29insidelist-img/29insidelist-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside The List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:20:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/482bc75b-7823-5670-b62b-eb4c6b1cf590', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/482bc75b-7823-5670-b62b-eb4c6b1cf590'}, {'abstract': 'In this biography, Georges Remi, creator of the Tintin comics, emerges in three dimensions from behind his famous comic books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/herge-son-of-tintin-by-benoit-peeters-translated-by-tina-a-kover-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this biography, Georges Remi, creator of the Tintin comics, emerges in three dimensions from behind his famous comic books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cartoonists are a peculiar breed of artist. For one thing, they’re hesitant about the use of the A-word. Their craft — now that’s better — also comes with great constraints. Because the work appears in newspapers and comic books, it must strike a commercial chord quickly: there isn’t much time for it to “find an audience.” The field doesn’t boast many lonely geniuses whose work is discovered posthumously. The demands of reproduction put limits on complexity, and the panel format — those squares and rectangles — erects graphic walls. For successful cartoonists, producing every day on someone else’s schedule, the grind is relentless. Cartoonists tend not to be boulevardiers; their way of life is sedentary and often solitary, and their days are spent in the company of invented characters in an invented world. Life and work can merge in curious ways. I happen to be the son of a cartoonist — a happy and well-adjusted cartoonist — and collaborated with him on “Prince Valiant,” but it was clear to all of us in the family that our situation was not quite normal. Or, thinking back to the moment when an electrician came into my father’s studio and found him posing — on his back, wearing a wig and a skirt, holding a garbage can lid as a shield and using a plunger on a rubber tube to snap a Polaroid — it may just have been that we had a different sense of what “normal” was.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-MURPHY/22-MURPHY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-MURPHY/22-MURPHY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-MURPHY/22-MURPHY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-MURPHY/22-MURPHY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-MURPHY/22-MURPHY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Georges Remi: Learning His Lines', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Learning His Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Remi, Georges', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:22:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cullen Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cullen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c34b8cba-1e0a-58cf-b18b-067ed8f8c994', 'word_count': 1673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c34b8cba-1e0a-58cf-b18b-067ed8f8c994'}, {'abstract': 'A biography of Margaret Fuller: feminist, writer, editor, traveler.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/the-lives-of-margaret-fuller-a-biography-by-john-matteson-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Margaret Fuller: feminist, writer, editor, traveler.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Margaret Fuller, a woman of great talent and promise, had the misfortune to be born in Massachusetts in 1810, at a time and place in which the characteristics of what historians have termed “true womanhood” were becoming ever more rigidly defined. Well brought-up women like herself were to be cultured, pious, submissive and genteel. Fuller, by contrast, was assertive and freethinking. She was also — and to some extent, still is — a difficult person to like.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-NORTON/22-NORTON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 782, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-NORTON/22-NORTON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-NORTON/22-NORTON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Margaret Fuller: Woman of the World', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Woman of the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fuller, Margaret', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:23:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Beth Norton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Beth', 'lastname': 'Norton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad7840dc-2c38-5732-9854-271a0258decc', 'word_count': 1190, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad7840dc-2c38-5732-9854-271a0258decc'}, {'abstract': 'William H. Gass — novelist, essayist, philosopher — looks back on a lifelong attachment to literature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/life-sentences-literary-judgments-and-accounts-by-william-h-gass-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'William H. Gass — novelist, essayist, philosopher — looks back on a lifelong attachment to literature.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When reviewers take the trouble to compliment a writer on her style,” William H. Gass writes in “Life Sentences,” his new collection of essays, “it is usually because she has made it easy for them to slide from one sentence to another like an otter down a slope.” Gass’s sentence enacts its own dissent from this critical preference for clear and simple prose: as soon as the otter barges in, the reader’s attention is forcibly drawn away from Gass’s meaning and toward his style.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/12/books/review/KIRSCH/KIRSCH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/12/books/review/KIRSCH/KIRSCH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/12/books/review/KIRSCH/KIRSCH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'William Gass’s Lifelong Attachment to Literature', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Praise of Style', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gass, William H', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:24:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Kirsch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirsch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e547338-7ef6-5413-b2bf-a4f16906209f', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e547338-7ef6-5413-b2bf-a4f16906209f'}, {'abstract': 'Long before Guantánamo, military tribunals had a history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/justice-and-the-enemy-nuremberg-9-11-and-the-trial-of-khalid-sheikh-mohammed-by-william-shawcross-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Long before Guantánamo, military tribunals had a history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the campaign trail in June 2008, Barack Obama commented on the Supreme Court’s extension of the constitutional right of habeas corpus to detainees held at the naval facility at Guantánamo Bay, Cuba. “During the Nuremberg trials, part of what made us different was even after these Nazis had performed atrocities that no one had ever seen before, we still gave them a day in court, and that taught the entire world about who we are but also the basic principles of rule of law,” Obama said. “Now the Supreme Court upheld that principle yesterday,” he added. Obama’s statement linked Nuremberg to an idea central to his presidential campaign: The Bush administration in the fight against Islamist terrorists had compromised American values and undermined America’s wartime rule-of-law legacy. The Supreme Court had begun the task of restoring those values, Obama suggested, and he promised to finish the job as president. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-GOLDSMITH/22-GOLDSMITH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-GOLDSMITH/22-GOLDSMITH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-GOLDSMITH/22-GOLDSMITH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-GOLDSMITH/22-GOLDSMITH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-GOLDSMITH/22-GOLDSMITH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Shadow of Nuremberg', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Shadow of Nuremberg', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shawcross, William', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Tribunals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuremberg War Crimes Tribunal (1945-49)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mohammed, Khalid Shaikh', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:25:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By JACK GOLDSMITH', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'GOLDSMITH', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc524743-9bd8-5517-810f-0cf1ba597ea4', 'word_count': 851, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc524743-9bd8-5517-810f-0cf1ba597ea4'}, {'abstract': 'A Brown University psychologist looks into the revolting science of everything that makes us gag.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/books/review/thats-disgusting-unraveling-the-mysteries-of-repulsion-by-rachel-herz-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A Brown University psychologist looks into the revolting science of everything that makes us gag.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Did you hear the one about the Texan at his first Passover Seder? He was mightily impressed with the soup. “These matzo balls sure are delicious, ma’am,” he told his hostess. “What other parts of the matzo do y’all cook with?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-HENIG/22-HENIG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 762, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-HENIG/22-HENIG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/books/review/22-HENIG/22-HENIG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What We Find Gross, and Why', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gross Me Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T16:26:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Marantz Henig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': 'Marantz', 'lastname': 'Henig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9861d900-f83d-5dde-a155-c5dd916b82d0', 'word_count': 1081, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9861d900-f83d-5dde-a155-c5dd916b82d0'}, {'abstract': 'A look at an author’s collectibles, exquisitely curated and displayed, in an Upper West Side home.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/collect-call-tracy-zabars-charm-bracelets-snowglobes-santa-mugs-etc-etc/', 'snippet': 'A look at an author’s collectibles, exquisitely curated and displayed, in an Upper West Side home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Collect Call | Tracy Zabar’s Charm Bracelets, Snowglobes, Santa Mugs, etc., etc.', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '201