ne}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T21:17:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By KEITH SCHLOSSER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SCHLOSSER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f6bb797-96fa-549c-90ec-55d7cc781f9c', 'word_count': 548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f6bb797-96fa-549c-90ec-55d7cc781f9c'}, {'abstract': 'Unlike most civil lawsuits over copyright infringement, the criminal indictment against the site does not come with a fat appendix listing the songs or movies that were reportedly traded. Instead, it includes pages of financial transactions and correspondence.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/looking-for-signs-of-crime-in-megauploads-memos/', 'snippet': 'Unlike most civil lawsuits over copyright infringement, the criminal indictment against the site does not come with a fat appendix listing the songs or movies that were reportedly traded. Instead, it includes pages of financial transactions and co...', 'lead_paragraph': 'For those who follow copyright issues, cases of infringement are usually fought with civil lawsuits. Yet the operators behind the site Megaupload have been charged with criminal offenses, and four of them are being held without bail in New Zealand. What is different about Megaupload, and why have the authorities cracked down so hard on it?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking for Signs of Crime in Megaupload’s Memos', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T21:19:57+0000', 'document_type': 'articl