referred by admissions. If in good standing on completion, they can transition into the larger university.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'General Studies: Nuts and Bolts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nuts and Bolts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-20T22:29:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8627780-2866-5c24-b7e4-1918dc173797', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8627780-2866-5c24-b7e4-1918dc173797'}, {'abstract': 'An interactive graphic tracks the expenditures by independent groups during the campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/interactive-graphic-tracking-independent-spending/', 'snippet': 'An interactive graphic tracks the expenditures by independent groups during the campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since the start of the presidential campaign, independent groups have spent at least $29.5 million asking voters to elect or defeat candidates through television advertisements and other means. The most active groups have been “super PACs,” which can raise unlimited money from individuals, corporations and labor unions. Although these groups cannot legally be tied to a candidate, several of the most-active ones are run or advised by former associates or campaign staff. Track the spending of each of these independent groups here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Interactive Graphic: Tracking Independent Spending', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 