', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/21/world/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/21/world/BRAZIL/BRAZIL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Brazilian Magnate Points to Himself for Inspiration', 'kicker': 'The Saturday Profile', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Brazilian Magnate Points to Himself for Inspiration', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Batista, Eike', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T01:41:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexei Barrionuevo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexei', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrionuevo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Biography', '_id': 'nyt://article/559fc728-1c3c-5901-90f6-63bb85cd41fb', 'word_count': 1389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/559fc728-1c3c-5901-90f6-63bb85cd41fb'}, {'abstract': 'Human rights advocates and American officials criticized the government for limiting political freedom after a dissident who had carried out a hunger strike died.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/world/americas/cuba-is-condemned-after-prisoners-death.html', 'snippet': 'Human rights advocates and American officials criticized the government for limiting political freedom after a dissident who had carried out a hunger strike died.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — Human rights advocates and American officials condemned the Cuban government on Friday for continuing to limit political freedom, reacting to the death of an imprisoned Cuban dissident who had carried out a hunger strike to protest his sentence. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prison Death Brings Outcry Against Cuba', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prison Death Brings Outcry Against Cuba', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T01:42:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damien Cave', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cave', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cb6d864-d5d3-5c95-be74-5d37469ebfac', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cb6d864-d5d3-5c95-be74-5d37469ebfac'}, {'abstract': '“Patti Smith: Camera Solo” at the Wadsworth Atheneum is the first American museum show dedicated primarily to Ms. Smith’s photography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/nyregion/from-patti-smith-images-romantic-and-morbid.html', 'snippet': '“Patti Smith: Camera Solo” at the Wadsworth Atheneum is the first American museum show dedicated primarily to Ms. Smith’s photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Patti Smith entered the world of famous people in 1975 with her album “Horses,” which combined punk and classic ’60s rock with elements of Van Morrison and Bob Dylan’s folk agitation. But Ms. Smith has always been more than a musician. In the late ’60s and ’70s, living in New York, she wrote poetry, music criticism for Rolling Stone and Creem, and a play with her onetime partner Sam Shepard. Her memoir, “Just Kids,” about her relationship with the photographer Robert Mapplethorpe, won the 2010 National Book Award. And last year she marked another celebrity rite of passage with a television acting debut on “Law & Order: Criminal Intent.”', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/nyregion/22ARTSCT1/22ARTSCT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 680, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/nyregion/22ARTSCT1/22ARTSCT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/nyregion/22ARTSCT1/22ARTSCT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Patti Smith, Images Romantic and Morbid', 'kicker': 'Arts | Connecticut', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Patti Smith, Images Romantic and Morbid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hartford (Conn)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Patti', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wadsworth Atheneum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T01:44:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1a1a790-fb67-57e4-b27f-4bc406af35a1', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1a1a790-fb67-57e4-b27f-4bc406af35a1'}, {'abstract': 'The Miami Dolphins abandoned their quest for a big name and chose Joe Philbin as their new coach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/sports/football/dolphins-hire-packers-coordinator-joe-philbin-as-head-coach.html', 'snippet': 'The Miami Dolphins abandoned their quest for a big name and chose Joe Philbin as their new coach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Miami Dolphins, who last year tried to woo Jim Harbaugh while still employing Tony Sparano, and who were rebuffed by Jeff Fisher last week, finally abandoned their quest for a big name and chose Joe Philbin as their new coach on Friday. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dolphins Hire Packers Coordinator as Head Coach', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Miami Hires Philbin as Coach', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Dolphins', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Philbin, Joe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T01:44:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Battista', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Battista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af52e376-a89e-5495-9ea5-fe4fcc2c47f8', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af52e376-a89e-5495-9ea5-fe4fcc2c47f8'}, {'abstract': 'In the prelude to South Carolina’s Republican primary on Saturday, the candidates have trying to engage students of the state’s military college, the Citadel.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/20/the-caucus-republican-candidates-at-the-citadel/', 'snippet': 'In the prelude to South Carolina’s Republican primary on Saturday, the candidates have trying to engage students of the state’s military college, the Citadel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over on The Caucus, Ben Werschkul follows the Republican candidates to the Citadel, the military college of South Carolina. Candidates and presidents alike have long used the Citadel as a backdrop for foreign policy speeches. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Caucus: Republican Candidates at the Citadel', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T01:46:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By At War', 'person': [{'firstname': 'At', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'War', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4e02e43-5d79-5dca-bf50-9583ab3efb3a', 'word_count': 107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4e02e43-5d79-5dca-bf50-9583ab3efb3a'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Sam Brownback created an office to eliminate out-of-date or burdensome laws, but the “criminal sodomy” statute was not selected for repeal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/us/sodomy-law-remains-official-in-kansas.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Sam Brownback created an office to eliminate out-of-date or burdensome laws, but the “criminal sodomy” statute was not selected for repeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KANSAS CITY, Kan. — Gov. Sam Brownback created the Office of the Repealer to recommend the elimination of out-of-date, unreasonable and burdensome state laws that build up in any bureaucracy over time. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/21/us/REPEAL/REPEAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 579, 'width': 378, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/21/us/REPEAL/REPEAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/21/us/REPEAL/REPEAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kansas Law on Sodomy Stays on Books Despite a Cull', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kansas Law On Sodomy Stays on Books Despite a Cull', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brownback, Sam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T01:50:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. G. Sulzberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Sulzberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16b68ac7-b088-5cd3-8622-a3a8445146c6', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16b68ac7-b088-5cd3-8622-a3a8445146c6'}, {'abstract': 'Samuel Mullet Sr. said he would allow an electronic monitor, but prosecutors said the issue in his continued custody was the danger he posed to the community, not his aversion to electricity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/us/amish-man-in-beard-attacks-would-allow-electronic-monitor.html', 'snippet': 'Samuel Mullet Sr. said he would allow an electronic monitor, but prosecutors said the issue in his continued custody was the danger he posed to the community, not his aversion to electricity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Samuel Mullet Sr., the renegade Amish leader being held in Ohio in connection with beard-cutting attacks on Amish rivals, made a new request for bail on Thursday, telling a federal judge that he would allow electricity inside his home to permit electronic monitoring. But federal prosecutors replied on Friday that Mr. Mullet posed a danger to the community and that the Amish aversion to electricity had not figured in the decision to keep him in custody until his trial, which is now scheduled for March. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/21/us/AMISH/AMISH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/21/us/AMISH/AMISH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/21/us/AMISH/AMISH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amish Man Held in Beard Attacks Seeks Bail Deal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Amish Man Held in Beard Attacks Seeks Bail Deal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beards', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hate Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMISH', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T01:54:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Eckholm', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eckholm', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/627bfec2-7cab-5c40-a41e-365e026ed873', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/627bfec2-7cab-5c40-a41e-365e026ed873'}, {'abstract': 'Thanks to Newt Gingrich, sex was very much on the minds of South Carolina voters this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/opinion/collins-opening-newts-marriage.html', 'snippet': 'Thanks to Newt Gingrich, sex was very much on the minds of South Carolina voters this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Right now, you are probably asking yourself whether two divorces, a history of adultery and an ex-wife who says you asked for an open marriage would be enough to disqualify a person from becoming president of the United States.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 's