 Message (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T17:55:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2864d8dc-2e89-54b6-95a4-9f9597257003', 'word_count': 1324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2864d8dc-2e89-54b6-95a4-9f9597257003'}, {'abstract': 'Europe’s powers to nudge Hungary’s prime minister, Viktor Orban, back from authoritarianism are limited. But to its credit, it has begun wielding them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/opinion/sunday/hungarys-lurch-backward.html', 'snippet': 'Europe’s powers to nudge Hungary’s prime minister, Viktor Orban, back from authoritarianism are limited. But to its credit, it has begun wielding them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The soothing words of Hungary’s prime minister, Viktor Orban, do little to counter his government’s assaults on the independence of Hungary’s press, judiciary and central bank. Without strong European pressure, Mr. Orban is unlikely to change the new banking, judiciary and media laws. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hungary’s Lurch Backward', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hungary’s Lurch Backward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hungary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Parliament', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Orban, Viktor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T18:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/3767391e-cbca-58d4-833d-b2c0a382e4ca', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3767391e-cbca-58d4-833d-b2c0a382e4ca'}, {'abstract': 'The push by Republican politicians and conservative justices to eliminate efforts to ensure diversity on campuses is squarely at odds with America’s racial history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/opinion/sunday/the-affirmative-action-war-goes-on.html', 'snippet': 'The push by Republican politicians and conservative justices to eliminate efforts to ensure diversity on campuses is squarely at odds with America’s racial history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month, the Obama administration issued important guidance to colleges and universities on how to increase racial diversity on campuses, explaining ways to navigate the narrow legal channel charted by the Supreme Court. The benefits of diversity, the Department of Education said, contribute to “the educational, economic and civic life of this nation.” The administration’s support for such efforts stands in stark contrast to the policy of the George W. Bush administration to discourage them. That difference has played out between the political parties for decades, as it will in this presidential election.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Affirmative Action War Goes On', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Affirmative Action War Goes On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AFFIRMATIVE ACTION', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Michigan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Texas at Austin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Anthony M', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalia, Antonin', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALITO, SAMUEL A JR', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Clarence', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T18:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/42c412fc-1f7f-5616-81ad-19dafe646f11', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42c412fc-1f7f-5616-81ad-19dafe646f11'}, {'abstract': 'John Huppenthal, the Arizona school superintendent, forced Tucson to dismantle its much-needed Mexican-American studies program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/opinion/sunday/rejected-in-tucson.html', 'snippet': 'John Huppenthal, the Arizona school superintendent, forced Tucson to dismantle its much-needed Mexican-American studies program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Tucson Unified School District has dismantled its Mexican-American studies program, packed away its offending books, shuttled its students into other classes. It was blackmailed into doing so: keeping the program would have meant losing more than $14 million in state funding. It was a blunt-force victory for the Arizona school superintendent, John Huppenthal, who has spent years crusading against ethnic-studies programs he claims are “brainwashing” children into thinking that Latinos have been victims of white oppression.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rejected in Tucson', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rejected in Tucson', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tucson (Ariz)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mexican-Americans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huppenthal, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T18:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/88668f52-6090-5a5f-b386-406f64b95852', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88668f52-6090-5a5f-b386-406f64b95852'}, {'abstract': 'The House Ethics Committee should thoroughly investigate four lawmakers for receiving special mortgage loans from Countrywide Financial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/opinion/sunday/friends-of-angelo-part-2.html', 'snippet': 'The House Ethics Committee should thoroughly investigate four lawmakers for receiving special mortgage loans from Countrywide Financial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four lawmakers have been referred to the House Ethics Committee for receiving special mortgage loans from the notorious “V.I.P.” program of Countrywide Financial. Ethics rules require members to accept loans only “on terms generally available to the public.” It should be of great interest for the public, particularly those mired in mortgage trouble, to see if anything comes out of the Congressional investigation. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friends of Angelo, Part 2', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Friends of Angelo, Part 2', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Towns, Edolphus', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sessions, Pete', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gallegly, Elton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Countrywide Financial Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House Committee on Ethics', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T18:00:13+0000', 'document