n was supposed to be complete. But in the second season after his departure, they are doing something that few expected: winning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When LeBron James left the Cavaliers, the devastation was supposed to be complete. But in the second season after his departure, they are doing something that few expected: winning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quick Rebound in Cleveland', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T18:05:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By BENJAMIN HOFFMAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HOFFMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de80592f-4f83-52a2-9da0-69586f3c944e', 'word_count': 530, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de80592f-4f83-52a2-9da0-69586f3c944e'}, {'abstract': 'Less private, more public — and that’s a good thing.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/the-ongoing-debt-transformation/', 'snippet': 'Less private, more public — and that’s a good thing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A followup on my note about US deleveraging. It turns out that if you measure debt as a percentage of potential GDP (as estimated by the CBO) and use a stacked-area graph, you get a pretty clear picture. Here it is, using nonfinancial debt (for reasons explained in the previous post):', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ongoing Debt Transformation', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T18:05:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0731b31c-d2ad-5258-9579-e58155f0b57e', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0731b31c-d2ad-5258-9579-e58155f0b57e'}, {'abstract': 'An environmental scientist explains the challenges in efforts to gauge global warming through disaster patterns.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/tallying-disasters-and-gauging-global-warming/', 'snippet': 'An environmental scientist explains the challenges in efforts to gauge global warming through disaster patterns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I encourage you to read “Global Warming and Severe Weather: Is There a Link?” This excellent new\xa0Green Grok\xa0post is by\xa0Bill Chameides, the blogging dean of the Nicholas School for the Environment at Duke University. (Hat tip @KeithKloor.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tallying Disasters and Gauging a Greenhouse Impact', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disasters and Emergencies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T18:21:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af2f36cf-2e68-555e-9737-5d7945629e24', 'word_count': 835, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af2f36cf-2e68-555e-9737-5d7945629e24'}, {'abstract': 'In both parties, there is a long tradition of underwhelming nominees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/opinion/sunday/douthat-a-good-candidate-is-hard-to-find.html', 'snippet': 'In both parties, there is a long tradition of underwhelming nominees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE are 300 million people in the United States of America. There are millions of political activists, volunteers, organizers and would-be officeholders. There are hundreds of thousands of elected officials. Yet somehow, out of all this multitude, the Republican Party has been unable to find a candidate for the White House in 2012 who inspires anything but weary resignation from its voters.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag