094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07d62d75-7166-500c-aa88-2e3ff2e75962'}, {'abstract': 'Not everyone agrees that readers complaining to the Haggler have a legitimate beef. So the Haggler is letting them vent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/your-money/restaurant-bill-shock-some-say-au-contraire-haggler.html', 'snippet': 'Not everyone agrees that readers complaining to the Haggler have a legitimate beef. So the Haggler is letting them vent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AFTER every episode of the Haggler, it is a safe bet that a handful of readers will write to say that the Haggler is a patsy and a twit. Or words to that effect. The gist of these e-mails is that the wrong party — an aggrieved consumer, in most instances — has prevailed. The Haggler, it is argued, failed to appreciate the company’s side of a dispute, and the consumer should have known better, or behaved differently, or merely had the decency to shut up.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Restaurant Bill Shock? Some Readers Say ‘Au Contraire’', 'kicker': 'The Haggler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Restaurant Bill Shock? Some Readers Say ‘Au Contraire’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Balan, Nello', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Truffles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nello (NYC Restaurant)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T22:29:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Segal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Segal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62212fed-e3e0-540e-b842-a211d2f5ff2a', 'word_count': 907, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62212fed-e3e0-540e-b842-a211d2f5ff2a'}, {'abstract': 'At times, the debate over antipiracy legislation has looked like a food fight, but maybe it’s a watershed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/business/in-antipiracy-debate-media-worlds-and-generations-clash.html', 'snippet': 'At times, the debate over antipiracy legislation has looked like a food fight, but maybe it’s a watershed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BACK in high school they always told us that brains prevail — that cool gets you only so far.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/business/22clash-pic/22clash-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/business/22clash-pic/22clash-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/business/22clash-pic/22clash-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Clash of Media Worlds (and Generations)', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Clash of Media Worlds (and Generations)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audio Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T22:31:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74e6a89c-31e7-516c-9eae-a2693d09a2b7', 'word_count': 1203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74e6a89c-31e7-516c-9eae-a2693d09a2b7'}, {'abstract': 'World War I still exerts a tenacious hold on the imagination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/opinion/sunday/why-world-war-i-resonates.html', 'snippet': 'World War I still exerts a tenacious hold on the imagination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN France I live near a little village called Sadillac. It’s no more than a cluster of houses, an old chateau, a church and a graveyard surrounded by a few farms and vineyards. The village probably hasn’t changed much since the French Revolution; its population hovers around 100. By the graveyard is a simple obelisk with the names of the 30 or so young men from Sadillac who died in the First World War, 1914-18. It’s almost impossible to imagine the effect on this tiny community of these fatalities over four years. Every year on Nov. 11 at 11 a.m. — the hour and the day of the 1918 armistice — villagers gather to participate in a short memorial service around the obelisk.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/sunday-review/22BOYD/22BOYD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/sunday-review/22BOYD/22BOYD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/sunday-review/22BOYD/22BOYD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/sunday-review/22BOYD/22BOYD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/sunday-review/22BOYD/22BOYD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why World War I Resonates', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why World War I Resonates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T22:33:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Boyd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/19b47534-9deb-58a5-a88a-2ede20a8be82', 'word_count': 1057, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19b47534-9deb-58a5-a88a-2ede20a8be82'}, {'abstract': 'The 60 goats living in the rail yard near Pier 96 at the Port of San Francisco clear brush as fire prevention and offer a green alternative to toxic herbicides.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/us/city-grazing.html', 'snippet': 'The 60 goats living in the rail yard near Pier 96 at the Port of San Francisco clear brush as fire prevention and offer a green alternative to toxic herbicides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 60 goats living in the rail yard near Pier 96 at the Port of San Francisco contribute to the city of San Francisco in their own way, clearing brush as fire prevention and offering a green alternative to toxic herbicides. Perched on the edge of Bayview-Hunters Point, an industrial area, these hard workers avoid the busy roads and — incredibly — return home when called.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27B', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/us/22BCINTEL1/22BCINTEL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/22/us/22BCINTEL1/22BCINTEL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/us/22BCINTEL1/22BCINTEL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/22/us/22BCINTEL1/22BCINTEL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/22/us/22BCINTEL1/22BCINTEL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'City Grazing', 'kicker': 'The Bay Citizen | Local Intelligence', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City Grazing: Port of San Francisco', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Goats', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T22:33:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louise Rafkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rafkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39f81008-6558-5817-be94-e896ba61fc64', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39f81008-6558-5817-be94-e896ba61fc64'}, {'abstract': 'If you’re trying to decide whether to invest in Europe, various market factors may offer better gauges than the lowering of countries’ debt ratings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/your-money/credit-downgrades-can-be-poor-predictors-fundamentally.html', 'snippet': 'If you’re trying to decide whether to invest in Europe, various market factors may offer better gauges than the lowering of countries’ debt ratings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THOUGH Wall Street is worried that the European debt crisis will worsen, the stock market barely budged when Standard & Poor’s downgraded the credit ratings of several euro zone nations — in the process stripping France, Austria and the euro zone’s primary rescue fund of their AAA standing. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Credit Downgrades Can Be Poor Predictors', 'kicker': 'Fundamentally', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Credit Downgrades Can Be Poor Predictors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Moody's Corporation", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Standard & Poor's Corp", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Ratings and Credit Rating Agencies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-21T22:34:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul J. Lim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d68fd6e-dc5d-5050-aaf9-c18539dbfb20', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d68fd6e-dc5d-5050-aaf9-c18539dbfb20'}, {'abstract': 'If liberals care about middle-class salaries, public education and other state-funded services, they need to care about controlling health care costs as much as conservatives do.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/21/what-we-give-up-for-health-care/', 'snippet': 'If liberals care about middle-class salaries, public education and other state-funded services, they need to care about controlling health care costs as much as conservatives do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN it comes to health care, most liberals are committed above all to ensuring that every American has insurance. In their view, the greatest achievement of the health care reform act passed under President Obama is to finally erase the moral stain of the United States’ being the only major developed country without universal coverage. But we also hold the questionable distinction of having the world’s most expensive health care system — what about cost control? For many liberals, that just sounds like a cover for heartless conservatives who care only about cutting benefits and not about helping people in need.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We Give Up for Health Care', 'kicker': 'Ezekiel J. Emanuel', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid'