lip Alder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a6d6c5e-56db-55e0-a738-7a5301218603', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a6d6c5e-56db-55e0-a738-7a5301218603'}, {'abstract': 'City defeated third-place Tottenham Hotspur, 3-2, with a penalty winner in the fourth and final minute of added time by Mario Balotelli, who was lucky not to have received a red card.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/sports/soccer/manchester-city-wins-on-penalty-kick.html', 'snippet': 'City defeated third-place Tottenham Hotspur, 3-2, with a penalty winner in the fourth and final minute of added time by Mario Balotelli, who was lucky not to have received a red card.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Manchester City is to become England’s champion after 44 years of longing for that title, its followers around the world might remember Sunday as the day Lady Luck finally bestowed her full favors on the Blues. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/sports/YONSOCCER/YONSOCCER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/23/sports/YONSOCCER/YONSOCCER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 376}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/sports/YONSOCCER/YONSOCCER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/sports/YONSOCCER/YONSOCCER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/23/sports/YONSOCCER/YONSOCCER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Good Fortune, and Penalty Kick, Help Manchester City', 'kicker': 'On Soccer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Good Fortune, and Penalty Kick, Help Manchester City', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manchester City (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tottenham Hotspur (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-22T22:39:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hughes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2d12030-bc03-5922-9026-1e1236e583a7', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2d12030-bc03-5922-9026-1e1236e583a7'}, {'abstract': 'A cello that belonged to Bernard Greenhouse, a founding member of the Beaux Arts trio, was sold to a woman who declined to be identified and will be on loan to an 18-year-old cellist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/arts/music/stradivari-cello-sells-for-more-than-6-million.html', 'snippet': 'A cello that belonged to Bernard Greenhouse, a founding member of the Beaux Arts trio, was sold to a woman who declined to be identified and will be on loan to an 18-year-old cellist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is considered one of the greatest cellos in the world, a beautifully preserved product of Antonio Stradivari that was once owned by Nicolò Paganini and most recently by Bernard Greenhouse, a founding member of the Beaux Arts Trio. Mr. Greenhouse played the cello for a half century, and after his death last year, his family decided to sell it, hoping it would go to a deserving musician.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/arts/cello/cello-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/arts/cello/cello-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/23/arts/cello/cello-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Beloved Set of Strings Goes to a Good Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Beloved Set of Strings Goes to a Good Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenhouse, Bernard', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Musical Instruments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reuning, Christopher', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stradivari, Antonio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellos', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-22T22:41:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/856a6207-a8a4-5a0b-af57-a574c2e1b4ac', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/856a6207-a8a4-5a0b-af57-a574c2e1b4ac'}, {'abstract': 'All Pigs Must Die brought a range of musical influences to its set at Acheron on Friday night, more than just the two most prominent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/arts/music/all-pigs-must-die-metal-and-hardcore-at-acheron-review.html', 'snippet': 'All Pigs Must Die brought a range of musical influences to its set at Acheron on Friday night, more than just the two most prominent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All Pigs Must Die sits right where hardcore and underground metal intersect. Its Rosetta Stone might be the first LPs by Negative Approach, Discharge and Slayer, melted together. About half of the band’s total stance, titles to lyrics to singing, seems inflexible, nonnegotiable. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/arts/sub-pigs-1/sub-pigs-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/23/arts/sub-pigs-1/sub-pigs-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/arts/sub-pigs-1/sub-pigs-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/arts/sub-pigs-1/sub-pigs-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/23/arts/sub-pigs-1/sub-pigs-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Underground Metal and Hardcore, Set Aflame', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Underground Metal and Hardcore, Set Aflame', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'All Pigs Must Die (Music Group)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-22T22:44:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f7bc32b-be6c-513b-9446-6c9329883505', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f7bc32b-be6c-513b-9446-6c9329883505'}, {'abstract': '“The Artist” picked up the top prize, best feature, from the Producers Guild of America, which announced its winners on Saturday night in Los Angeles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/movies/awardsseason/the-artist-wins-producers-guild-prize.html', 'snippet': '“The Artist” picked up the top prize, best feature, from the Producers Guild of America, which announced its winners on Saturday night in Los Angeles.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Artist” has made its way to pole position in the movie awards race. This black-and-white, largely silent film, starring Jean Dujardin, above, with Uggie, picked up the top prize, best feature, from the Producers Guild of America, which announced its winners on Saturday night in Los Angeles. The guild has followed the lead of the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences in nominating 10 films for best picture in the last two years. It has also accurately predicted the eventual best-picture Oscar winner, even in the expanded field. When it chose “The Hurt Locker” over the much higher-grossing “Avatar” in 2010, awards watchers considered it a bellwether prize. (Though the Academy may nominate anywhere from 5 to 10 films this year, the Producers Guild stuck with 10.) Steven Spielberg’s “Adventures of Tintin” won best animated film, and “Beats, Rhymes & Life: The Travels of a Tribe Called Quest” earned the documentary feature prize. ABC’s hit “Modern Family” took home the first award of the night, for best TV comedy, and “Downton Abbey” (PBS) won for best long-form show. “Boardwalk Empire” (HBO) picked up best drama, beating “Mad Men” (AMC). “The Amazing Race” (CBS) won for television competition, and “The Colbert Report” (Comedy Central) won for talk show.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Artist’ Wins Producers Guild Prize', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘The Artist’ Wins Producers Guild Prize', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Producers Guild of America', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-22T22:45:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'Awards Season', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ceceb9ba-4593-563a-971e-274d80e1c5f6', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ceceb9ba-4593-563a-971e-274d80e1c5f6'}, {'abstract': 'The American Ballet Theater is to announce on Monday that Ethan Stiefel, a principal dancer for the theater since 1997, will retire at the end of the 2012 Metropolitan Opera House season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/arts/dance/footnote.html', 'snippet': 'The American Ballet Theater is to announce on Monday that Ethan Stiefel, a principal dancer for the theater since 1997, will retire at the end of the 2012 Metropolitan Opera House season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The American Ballet Theater is to announce on Monday that Ethan Stiefel, a principal dancer for the theater since 1997, will retire at the end of the 2012 Metropolitan Opera House season. His final performance is scheduled for July 7 as part of the company’s production of “Le Corsaire,” according to a statement from the theater. “Although it is not easy to step away from performing, I am gladdened by the fact that I have other invaluable opportunities to continue to contribute to the art form,” Mr. Stiefel said in the statement. “I am deeply grateful to everyone who has helped to shape my success as an artist and has supported me throughout the years.” He became the artistic director of Royal New Zealand Ballet in September.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnote', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stiefel, Ethan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-22T22:46:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/59e876d7-91cd-513f-845f-ee29d8de493a', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59e876d7-91cd-513f-845f-ee29d8de493a'}, {'abstract': 'Been there, done that.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/harding/', 'snippet': 'Been there, done that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I gather that they’re still trying to claim that the recovery from the 1920-21 recession somehow vindicates austerity policies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Harding', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-22T22:48:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/016e1a46-b5dd-5525-a3f3-77db10662b7d', 'word_count': 63, 'uri': 'nyt://article/016e1a46-b5dd-5525-a3f3-77db10662b7d'}, {'abstract': 'The competition sought scripts for 30-minute situation comedies about modern family life and attracted more than 235 entries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/business/media/marketers-seeking-family-shows-hold-a-script-contest.html', 'snippet': 'The competition sought scripts for 30-minute situation comedies about modern family life and attracted more than 235 entries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR several years, marketers like Frito-Lay and General Motors have sponsored contests asking consumers to create television commercials, with winning spots appearing on shows including the Super Bowl. Now, a group of marketers has expanded the idea by sponsoring a contest asking consumers to create shows.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/business/2