rld', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Declan Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Declan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff1203ee-984e-50bd-a6fa-17f4d13bd3b7', 'word_count': 1558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff1203ee-984e-50bd-a6fa-17f4d13bd3b7'}, {'abstract': 'It is a season of doubt and fear in much of the West, as the relative decline of Europe and the United States seems more and more apparent. But, Chrystia Freeland writes, the current era of global prosperity owes its life to Western policies and technology. How much of China and India’s success is rooted in the West?', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/the-emerging-gilded-age/', 'snippet': 'It is a season of doubt and fear in much of the West, as the relative decline of Europe and the United States seems more and more apparent. But, Chrystia Freeland writes, the current era of global prosperity owes its life to Western policies and t...', 'lead_paragraph': 'As IHT Rendezvous kicks off a week of special coverage of the World Economic Forum in Davos, Chrystia Freeland, an IHT Page Two columnist and Reuters’s global editor at large, writes about the new Gilded Age. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Emerging Gilded Age', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freeland, Chrystia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T02:06:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c715bd55-fa7c-5e6f-b5a4-cd48eb22465a', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c715bd55-fa7c-5e6f-b5a4-cd48eb22465a'}, {'abstract': 'Some Mexican-American students who live in Tijuana wake up at 3 a.m. and lawfully cross the border to get an education in Chula Vista, Calif.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/opinion/striving-in-two-worlds.html', 'snippet': 'Some Mexican-American students who live in Tijuana wake up at 3 a.m. and lawfully cross the border to get an education in Chula Vista, Calif.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is it like to get up at 3 every morning to leave the country to go to school? To walk for miles and wait in long lines at an international crossing, to hustle along by trolley and bus, worrying all the while about missing the 8 a.m. bell? This is the bleary-eyed world of young Mexican-Americans who live in Tijuana and go to school in Chula Vista, Calif., as described by Patricia Leigh Brown in The Times. These striving students, or “transfronterizos,” are not immigrants but American citizens, lawfully crossing the border to get an education. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Young Strivers in Two Worlds', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Young Strivers in Two Worlds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mexican-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T02:08:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a4dfd9d-fbcc-5dc8-8b3b-8bbcd7135ac2', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a4dfd9d-fbcc-5dc8-8b3b-8bbcd7135ac2'}, {'abstract': 'The Brooklyn Historical Society has exhibitions about the history of Brooklyn, the history of the Dodgers and an online show about the Lefferts family’s formative role in the borough.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/opinion/searching-through-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'The Brooklyn Historical Society has exhibitions about the history of Brooklyn, the history of the Dodgers and an online show about the Lefferts family’s formative role in the borough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just as Americans visit the Old World in search of family roots, tourists are showing up at the Brooklyn Historical Society, in hopes of tracking down their grandparents’ neighborhood ties and old houses — brownstones in Park Slope, row houses in Bay Ridge — in the New World. As usual, Walt Whitman anticipated the future: “There will come a time here in Brooklyn and all over America, when nothing will be of more interest than authentic reminiscences of the past.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Searching Through Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'Editorial | The City Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Searching Through Brooklyn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Historical Society', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T02:11:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis X. Clines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Clines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/69e93587-24ba-5e63-9a17-a6b414777827', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69e93587-24ba-5e63-9a17-a6b414777827'}, {'abstract': 'At issue is a rule intended to curb speculative commodities trading, which some consumer advocates have blamed for inflating prices at the gas pump and the grocery store.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/22/after-a-legal-setback-commodity-traders-complaint-is-to-be-heard-by-a-lower-court/', 'snippet': 'At issue is a rule intended to curb speculative commodities trading, which some consumer advocates have blamed for inflating prices at the gas pump and the grocery store.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wall Street’s legal challenge to a regulatory crackdown met a procedural obstacle last week, when a federal appeals court dismissed the case.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After a Legal Setback, Commodity Traders’ Complaint Is to Be Heard by a Lower Court', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Commodities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Futures and Options Trading', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subje