s': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T08:22:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tiffany Frasier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tiffany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frasier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c193031f-0632-532a-b416-c99e35cd9a8d', 'word_count': 20, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c193031f-0632-532a-b416-c99e35cd9a8d'}, {'abstract': 'On Jan. 23, 1973, President Richard Nixon announced an accord had been reached to end the Vietnam War.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/jan-23-1973-nixon-announces-end-of-u-s-involvement-in-vietnam/', 'snippet': 'On Jan. 23, 1973, President Richard Nixon announced an accord had been reached to end the Vietnam War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Jan. 23, 1973, President Richard Nixon announced an accord had been reached to end the Vietnam War. In a televised speech, Nixon said the accord would “end the war and bring peace with honor.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jan. 23, 1973 | Nixon Announces End of U.S. Involvement in Vietnam', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T09:07:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b58a586b-4e72-5d6d-91b6-8d84d8211fbd', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b58a586b-4e72-5d6d-91b6-8d84d8211fbd'}, {'abstract': 'Can you calculate the amount spent to train each employee, if given the total cost of the training program and the number of people who were trained?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/test-yourself-math-jan-23-2012/', 'snippet': 'Can you calculate the amount spent to train each employee, if given the total cost of the training program and the number of people who were trained?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with this question created by Patrick Honner at Math for America from the article “Private Sector Gets Job Skills; Public Gets Bill.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, Jan. 23, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T09:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47749b79-22b1-5043-b4fa-70f96e0c9350', 'word_count': 53, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47749b79-22b1-5043-b4fa-70f96e0c9350'}, {'abstract': 'HONG KONG — Lunar New Year celebrations have begun all across Asia, and in some places the weeklong festivities include hearty meals of dog meat. While the tradition of eating dog persists, notably in northern Vietnam and southern China, it also seems to be in general decline throughout the region.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/an-unsavory-holiday-tradition/', 'snippet': 'HONG KONG — Lunar New Year celebrations have begun all across Asia, and in some places the weeklong festivities include hearty meals of dog meat. While the tradition of eating dog persists, notably in northern Vietnam and southern China, it also s...', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — For weeks now, in the prelude to the Lunar New Year holiday that started Monday in Asia, the ramshackle restaurants along Nhât Tân Street in Hanoi have been jammed with patrons. Happens every New Year. The menus feature meat prepared seven different ways, and the meals, which typically cost about $15, almost always include heroic drinking bouts with rice wine or the local turpentine called ruou. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not to Everyone’s Taste', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lunar New Year', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far East, South and Southeast Asia and Pacific Areas', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hanoi (Vietnam)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seoul (South Korea)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T09:37:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c5966d9-e772-53f0-9e2e-c270908e9967', 'word_count': 690, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c5966d9-e772-53f0-9e2e-c270908e9967'}, {'abstract': '“The hill again?” Paul Octavious has been photographing the same hill in Chicago for about four years. He’s fascinated by the subject — never changing, yet always different.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/more-of-the-same-but-different/', 'snippet': '“The hill again?” Paul Octavious has been photographing the same hill in Chicago for about four years. He’s fascinated by the subject — never changing, yet always different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Octavious never calls the hill from his series “Same Hill, Different Day” by name.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More of the Same — but Different', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T10:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kristen Joy Watts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kristen', 'middlename': 'Joy', 'lastname': 'Watts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01633129-d9a3-5560-8efa-77087d44a630', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01633129-d9a3-5560-8efa-77087d44a630'}, {'abstract': 'What is the significance of Chinese New Year?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/ringing-in-the-year-of-the-dragon/', 'snippet': 'What is the significance of Chinese New Year?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chinese New Year starts today. To mark the beginning of the Year of the Dragon, we pose questions that link to a variety of Times articles from the past and present, as well as to outside resources. Use this collection to teach students about the holiday and its cultural significance and traditions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ringing In the Year of the Dragon', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T10:30:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten and Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e09286e2-f5b5-5f67-8c75-a48b1bdbf5f4', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e09286e2-f5b5-5f67-8c75-a48b1bdbf5f4'}, {'abstract': 'The European Union agreed to impose a phased ban on oil purchases from Iran, and the United States expanded its sanctions to include the country’s third-largest bank.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/world/middleeast/iran-urged-to-negotiate-as-west-readies-new-sanctions.html', 'snippet': 'The European Union agreed to impose a phased ban on oil purchases from Iran, and the United States expanded its sanctions to include the country’s third-largest bank.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — The European Union agreed Monday to impose a phased ban on oil purchases from Iran that officials said was needed to help force a shift in policy and avert the risk of military strikes against Tehran, as the United States expanded its sanctions to include the country’s third-largest bank. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/world/middleeast/24iran_cnd/24iran_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/24/world/middleeast/24iran_cnd/24iran_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/world/middleeast/24iran_cnd/24iran_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/world/middleeast/24iran_cn