nts and the 49ers on Sunday essentially came down to a 25-year-old special-teams player who managed to be in the right position to fall on a uniquely contoured ball best known for taking strange bounces.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/sports/football/24araton-pic/24araton-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/24/sports/football/24araton-pic/24araton-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/sports/football/24araton-pic/24araton-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/sports/football/24araton-pic/24araton-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/24/sports/football/24araton-pic/24araton-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Journeyman Giant Kept Ending Up in the Right Place', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Journeyman Giant Kept Ending Up in the Right Place', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Francisco 49ers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Devin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T16:51:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Araton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Araton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa93f761-7ead-575b-9b12-bd1710bbe60d', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa93f761-7ead-575b-9b12-bd1710bbe60d'}, {'abstract': 'What disgusts humans is proving irresistible to researchers exploring the evolutionary value of revulsion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/science/disgusts-evolutionary-role-is-irresistible-to-researchers.html', 'snippet': 'What disgusts humans is proving irresistible to researchers exploring the evolutionary value of revulsion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Disgust is the Cinderella of emotions. While fear, sadness and anger, its nasty, flashy sisters, have drawn the rapt attention of psychologists, poor disgust has been hidden away in a corner, left to muck around in the ashes. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/science/24DISG/24DISG-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/science/24DISG/24DISG-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/24/science/24DISG/24DISG-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Survival’s Ick Factor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Survival’s Ick Factor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emotions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T16:54:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Gorman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fe8a952-ce54-5940-8aba-baea6c784559', 'word_count': 1538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fe8a952-ce54-5940-8aba-baea6c784559'}, {'abstract': 'Roger Federer defeated Bernard Tomic at the Australian Open by making his opponent move out of his comfort zone.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/breaking-down-federer-vs-tomic/', 'snippet': 'Roger Federer defeated Bernard Tomic at the Australian Open by making his opponent move out of his comfort zone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roger Federer’s straight sets victory against Australian teenager Bernard Tomic in the fourth round of the Australian Open this morning highlighted a major difference in how the two players view and play the sport.Federer, who will play against Juan Martin del Potro in the quarterfinals Tuesday (Federer’s 1,000th tour match), won, 6-4, 6-2, 6-2, displaying a game style vastly different from his inexperienced yet highly touted opponent. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Federer vs.Tomic: Breaking It Down', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T16:56:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': "By CRAIG O'SHANNESSY", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'SHANNESSY", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9b8dcd7-1025-5e40-8a49-d44985a6948a', 'word_count': 1165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9b8dcd7-1025-5e40-8a49-d44985a6948a'}, {'abstract': 'A new study shows that children wear a whole lot less sunscreen once they hit the age when Mom and Dad stop slathering it on.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/a-sunscreen-warning-for-parents-of-teenagers/', 'snippet': 'A new study shows that children wear a whole lot less sunscreen once they hit the age when Mom and Dad stop slathering it on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Half of a group of 360 fifth graders told researchers from Memorial Sloan-Kettering that they used sunscreen regularly when in the sun. Three years later, in 2007, among those same children, at least two things had changed: they were now eighth graders, with all the increased rebellion and decreased supervision that that implies — and now, only about 25 percent still used sunscreen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sunscreen Warning for Parents of Teenagers', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sunscreen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T16:57:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': N