'', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6948019a-494a-5b7b-bd18-6875f2cd28c7', 'word_count': 235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6948019a-494a-5b7b-bd18-6875f2cd28c7'}, {'abstract': 'Massachusetts Senate candidates Scott Brown and Elizabeth Warren on Monday morning agreed to try to limit third-party advertisers from weighing in on their race.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/massachusetts-senate-candidates-look-to-limit-outside-advertising/', 'snippet': 'Massachusetts Senate candidates Scott Brown and Elizabeth Warren on Monday morning agreed to try to limit third-party advertisers from weighing in on their race.', 'lead_paragraph': '9:45 p.m. | Updated  BOSTON – Senator Scott P. Brown and Elizabeth Warren, his likely Democratic opponent, agreed Monday on a plan to try to stop outside groups from running attack ads in their high-profile, high-stakes race. But while they labored over a mutually acceptable agreement, the hardest part – trying to enforce it – comes now. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Massachusetts Senate Candidates Look to Limit Outside Advertising', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Scott P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T17:25:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby Goodnough and Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodnough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/178ea9d3-11e1-52e9-994d-ba34c99b4bde', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/178ea9d3-11e1-52e9-994d-ba34c99b4bde'}, {'abstract': 'A reporter will discuss the challenges posed by increasingly globalized high-tech industries, based on a deep look inside the economics of Apple’s manufacturing.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/charles-duhigg-responds-to-readers-on-apple-and-the-ieconomy/', 'snippet': 'A reporter will discuss the challenges posed by increasingly globalized high-tech industries, based on a deep look inside the economics of Apple’s manufacturing.', 'lead_paragraph': '7:36 p.m. | Updated  Charles Duhigg has provided answers to select reader questions. See the comments, below.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Charles Duhigg Responds to Readers on Apple and the iEconomy', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T17:26:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42787f62-a171-5723-b558-43d23ae3a6e4', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42787f62-a171-5723-b558-43d23ae3a6e4'}, {'abstract': 'Light and simple, enjoyable but not distracting, these portable meals will keep you going through the workday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/health/nutrition/lunches-to-take-to-work-recipes-for-health.html', 'snippet': 'Light and simple, enjoyable but not distracting, these portable meals will keep you going through the workday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many people who have chimed in on the Recipes for Health page on Facebook have requested recipes for lunches they can take to work and eat at a desk. Although I work at home, my lunch requirements are probably similar to those of many of you who work in an office. I don’t like to eat complex dishes with strong flavors like raw garlic or onion at midday, because I don’t want those flavors lingering when I get back to work. I want a lunch that’s light and simple, enjoyable but not distracting. It usually includes protein and a vegetable of some kind, often a complex carbohydrate like whole-grain bread or a grain. I don’t spend much time preparing it because I’ve made it ahead (salad, wraps, something I’ve tested for this column that fits the bill), or because it’s as simple as cottage cheese and avocado with a corn tortilla or two, heated over the gas burner so that it has a nice charred taste.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/science/23recipehealth/23recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/24/science/23recipehealth/23recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/0