s together 24 essays about the love lives of American Muslim women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Zahra Noorbakhsh was 14 when her Iranian immigrant mother discovered that Zahra was defying the family ban on mingling with boys: one was among her four friends heading to the movies together. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/INSHALLAH-JP-1/INSHALLAH-JP-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/INSHALLAH-JP-1/INSHALLAH-JP-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/INSHALLAH-JP-1/INSHALLAH-JP-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/INSHALLAH-JP-1/INSHALLAH-JP-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/INSHALLAH-JP-1/INSHALLAH-JP-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lifting Veil on Love and Islam', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lifting Veil On Love And Islam', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:01:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/127deb58-c993-5b73-98cc-5885b03e018d', 'word_count': 1058, 'uri': 'nyt://article/127deb58-c993-5b73-98cc-5885b03e018d'}, {'abstract': 'In the American Songbook series on Saturday evening Michael Cerveris, of “Assassins” and “Sweeney Todd” fame, affected the look but not the attitude of a rambling musician from way back.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/arts/music/michael-cerveris-in-american-songbook-series-review.html', 'snippet': 'In the American Songbook series on Saturday evening Michael Cerveris, of “Assassins” and “Sweeney Todd” fame, affected the look but not the attitude of a rambling musician from way back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If all you know of the theater singer Michael Cerveris are his fierce portrayals of John Wilkes Booth in the Stephen Sondheim-John Weidman musical “Assassins” and the razor-swinging title character in the 2005 Broadway revival of “Sweeney Todd,” his two Saturday evening concerts as part of Lincoln Center’s American Songbook series demanded a radical readjustment of perception.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/songbook/songbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/songbook/songbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/songbook/songbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Songs of the South, Hold t