rformed shortly before the crash was “a contributing cause, or the cause,” a city official said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maintenance work was the likely culprit in the elevator accident that killed an advertising executive in Midtown last month, a city official said on Monday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Elevator Work Blamed in Death of Suzanne Hart', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elevators and Escalators', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hart, Suzanne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transel Elevator Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Midtown Area (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:27:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley and Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fdb789a-724b-58a2-9909-627350a20d88', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fdb789a-724b-58a2-9909-627350a20d88'}, {'abstract': 'While the old Super Bowl theory of the stock market no longer seems to work, there is an election-year correlation that might be worth noting if the game is not close.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/on-super-bowls-elections-and-stock-prices/', 'snippet': 'While the old Super Bowl theory of the stock market no longer seems to work, there is an election-year correlation that might be worth noting if the game is not close.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If the Super Bowl game in two weeks is not close, Barack Obama may be in trouble when November rolls around. On the other hand, a close game would give him a better-than-even chance of winning the election.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Super Bowls, Elections and Stock Prices', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:27:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26b02b39-070d-530e-8615-3e711bcd409e', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26b02b39-070d-530e-8615-3e711bcd409e'}, {'abstract': 'This Gallup tracking poll was conducted during the final days of the South Carolina primary and immediately afterward.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/gingrich-closes-in-on-romney-in-poll/', 'snippet': 'This Gallup tracking poll was conducted during the final days of the South Carolina primary and immediately afterward.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the latest example of the remarkable volatility of the Republican race, today’s Gallup tracking poll finds Newt Gingrich and Mitt Romney essentially tied among Republicans and Republican-leaning independent voters nationwide. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gingrich Closes In on Romney in Poll', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:27:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dalia Sussman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dalia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sussman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d29f9cb4-c340-52be-b515-8ad373e29777', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d29f9cb4-c340-52be-b515-8ad373e29777'}, {'abstract': '“All Star Dealers,” a new reality show on Discovery, centers on a sports memorabilia authenticator and auction house.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/arts/television/on-all-star-dealers-sports-memorabilia-rules-review.html', 'snippet': '“All Star Dealers,” a new reality show on Discovery, centers on a sports memorabilia authenticator and auction house.', 'lead_paragraph': '“All Star Dealers,” a sports-memorabilia addition to the bloated auction/pawnshop/storage locker subgenre of reality television, should have been a winner, with endless stories to draw on and a built-in fan base. But rather than find its own formula, it was content to borrow from existing shows, and it borrowed all the wrong things.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/DEALERS1/DEALERS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/DEALERS1/DEALERS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/DEALERS1/DEALERS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/DEALERS1/DEALERS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/DEALERS1/DEALERS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Examining the Flotsam, Hoping for the Treasures', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Examining the Flotsam, Hoping for the Treasures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Discovery Channel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:28:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/73d1807a-32e7-54d7-a2c6-bd4408fe540b', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73d1807a-32e7-54d7-a2c6-bd4408fe540b'}, {'abstract': 'On primary nights, while hardworking daily reporters are trying desperately to tally up vote counts and crunch the exit polling, magazine guys like me get to watch TV and think about things like: “Hey, what is that on Newt Gingrich’s campaign signs, and why are the letters so flippin’ small?”', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/squinting-at-gingrichs-slogan/', 'snippet': 'On primary nights, while hardworking daily reporters are trying desperately to tally up vote counts and crunch the exit polling, magazine guys like me get to watch TV and think about things like: “Hey, what is that on Newt Gingrich’s campaign sign...', 'lead_paragraph': 'On primary nights, while hardworking daily reporters are trying desperately to tally votes and crunch the exit polling, magazine guys like me get to watch TV and think about things like: “Hey, what is that on Newt Gingrich’s campaign signs, and why are the letters so flippin’ small?” And so, on the heels of my recent sit-down with Newt and my article on South Carolina for the magazine, here’s a column on Gingrich’s new slogan and what we should make of it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Squinting at Gingrich’s Slogan', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slogans and Mottoes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:28:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Bai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c60ab845-4050-5613-91b6-0b6fc96200f1', 'word_count': 82, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c60ab845-4050-5613-91b6-0b6fc96200f1'}, {'abstract': 'A juice joint takes its healthy ideas to the World Wide Web.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/drink-up-pressed-juicerys-chalkboard-goes-live/', 'snippet': 'A juice joint takes its healthy ideas to the World Wide Web.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pressed Juicery in Los Angeles began as a labor of love. When the co-founder Carly Brien’s mother got ill, she and her friends Hayden Slater and Hedi Gores decided to change career paths (from public relations, production and full-time motherhood, respectively) and create something healthful and positive. At the time, Los Angeles was lacking a juicery that used a hydraulic press, a machine that squelches an entire fruit or vegetable into a pulp, extracting three to four times more than an average juicer. After yoga class one day, Slater found the perfect spot for one: a 22-square-foot broom closet adjacent to the Brentwood Country Mart. Today, Pressed is a thriving business — a second location (in a truck) opened in Malibu last summer, and at the end of last year Slater and friends brought a storefront to West Hollywood. The juicery also takes orders online and delivers nationwide to loyals who can’t live without its Greens 2 (a blend of kale, spinach, romaine, parsley, cucumber, celery, apple and lemon), Detox 2 (pineapple, green apple and mint) and Almond Milk (almonds, dates, vanilla bean, sea salt and filtered water).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Drink Up | Pressed Juicery’s Chalkboard Goes Live', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:29:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Moonves', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moonves', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3073e184-78fa-5e22-825d-6293cc707b5e', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3073e184-78fa-5e22-825d-6293cc707b5e'}, {'abstract': 'Paris', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/01/23/fashion/20120123-VERSACE-copy.html', 'snippet': 'Paris', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paris', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/fashion/runway/20120123-VERSACE-slide-8O5R/20120123-VERSACE-slide-8O5R-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/fashion/runway/20120123-VERSACE-slide-8O5R/20120123-VERSACE-slide-8O5R-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/23/fashion/runway/20120123-VERSACE-slide-8O5R/20120123-VERSACE-slide-8O5R-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Versace Couture', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Couture (Fashion)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:29:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4ff9d7ae-7989-5cb5-ba96-2226e0cd608d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4ff9d7ae-7989-5cb5-ba96-2226e0cd608d'}, {'abstract': 'The Mexican-born guitar duo Rodrigo y Gabriela, after honing skills and an eclectic style abroad, are welcomed by fans in their hometown, Mexico City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/arts/music/rodrigo-y-gabriela-return-to-mexico-city-in-triumph.html', 'snippet': 'The Mexican-born guitar duo Rodrigo y Gabriela, after honing skills and an eclectic style abroad, are welcomed by fans in their hometown, Mexico City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — The two acoustic guitarists onstage here sounded at times as if the Gypsy Kings had taken possession of Jimmy Page. Intricate melodies — and solos challenging the laws of nylon — rode over crashing, furious percussion as they raced from heavy-metal riffs to jazz to something vaguely Turkish.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/rodrigo-y-gabriel/rodrigo-y-gabriel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 764, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/rodrigo-y-gabriel/rodrigo-y-gabriel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/24/arts/rodrigo-y-gabriel/rodrigo-y-gabriel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexican Guitarists Come Home From the Cold', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexican Guitarists Come Home From the Cold', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico City (Mexico)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:29:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2559dcd1-f33d-5d38-b716-9db8a6cc1fa4', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2559dcd1-f33d-5d38-b716-9db8a6cc1fa4'}, {'abstract': '“Follies” closes on Broadway without turning a profit, though the head of the Kennedy Center says it outdid expectations and will help the center in the long run.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/follies-closes-without-turning-a-profit/', 'snippet': '“Follies” closes on Broadway without turning a profit, though the head of the Kennedy Center says it outdid expectations and will help the center in the long run.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than 216,000 people saw the Broadway revival of “Follies” by the time it ended its five-and-a-half month run on Sunday, and most major theater critics raved about the production, which added three weeks of performances in response to ticket demand. Yet all of this was not enough to recoup the show’s capitalization and turn a profit, according to Michael Kaiser of the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts, where the “Follies” revival began last spring and which was one of its lead producers on Broadway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Follies’ Closes Without Turning a Profit', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kennedy, John F, Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-23T22:29:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'bylin