lead_paragraph': 'Not long ago, a veteran Republican strategist told me that a politician could succeed with his zipper down, but not with his words unbridled.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/frank-bruni/frank-bruni-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gusts of Gingrich', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Gusts of Gingrich', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T01:52:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/54a104b4-646e-54f2-8556-0d0e4dc459d9', 'word_count': 824, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54a104b4-646e-54f2-8556-0d0e4dc459d9'}, {'abstract': 'Digital humanists have new tools at their disposal, but does that necessarily make for a more accurate reading of texts?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/mind-your-ps-and-bs-the-digital-humanities-and-interpretation/', 'snippet': 'Digital humanists have new tools at their disposal, but does that necessarily make for a more accurate reading of texts?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question I posed at the conclusion of my last post was how do the technologies wielded by digital humanities practitioners either facilitate the work of the humanities, as it has been traditionally understood, or bring about an entirely new conception of what work in the humanities can and should be? I’m going to sneak up on that question by offering a piece of conventional (i.e., non-digital) literary analysis that deals, as the digital humanities do, with matters of statistical frequency and pattern.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mind Your P’s and B’s: The Digital Humanities and Interpretation', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T02:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fec4412-4445-5aa8-8065-1c9ddf6fae04', 'word_count': 2920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fec4412-4445-5aa8-8065-1c9ddf6fae04'}, {'abstract': 'A man who grew up relying on his grandmother was left to fend for himself when she moved away. He got help from a friend’s mother and a charity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/nyregion/suddenly-left-to-fend-for-himself.html', 'snippet': 'A man who grew up relying on his grandmother was left to fend for himself when she moved away. He got help from a friend’s mother and a charity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Travis Watson remembers the moment when he went from having everything to having nothing. He had spent a night at a friend’s house in fall 2010 and was returning to his grandmother’s home in Brooklyn, where he and his four siblings lived. He was greeted by the sight of a moving van loaded with furniture: his grandmother was moving to North Carolina, and Mr. Watson had not been invited to go with her. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/nyregion/24NEEDIEST/24NEEDIEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/24/nyregion/24NEEDIEST/24NEEDIEST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/nyregion/24NEEDIEST/24NEEDIEST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/nyregion/24NEEDIEST/24NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/24/nyregion/24NEEDIEST/24NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Learning to Fend for Himself and Obey House Rules', 'kicker': 'The Neediest Cases', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Learning to Fend for Himself and Obey House Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Community Services', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T02:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Otis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f8be873-6ffb-51ca-bb83-928cbae9ce4a', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f8be873-6ffb-51ca-bb83-928cbae9ce4a'}, {'abstract': 'The sky-high corporate fees at Davos are waived for heads of state and other high-ranking government officials who are the real deal makers with each other and with industry.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/free-pass-for-matchmaking-at-a-setting-in-the-alps/', 'snippet': 'The sky-high corporate fees at Davos are waived for heads of state and other high-ranking government officials who are the real deal makers with each other and with industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — The stars of the guest list for this year’s annual World Economic Forum, which begins here Wednesday, are a who’s who of government leaders pulling the strings of the global economy. On the list are Angela Merkel, the chancellor of Germany; Timothy F. Geithner, the United States secretary of the Treasury; Mario Draghi, the president of the European Central Bank; and Christine Lagarde, the managing director of the International Monetary Fund.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Free Pass for Matchmaking at a Setting in the Alps', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T02:04:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ross Sorkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Ross', 'lastname': 'Sorkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d5bb138-66c3-51e7-9237-efb7807c7c73', 'word_count': 943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d5bb138-66c3-51e7-9237-efb7807c7c73'}, {'abstract': 'In his State of the Union address, here’s hoping President Obama promotes policies that will help more people realize the American dream.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/opinion/brooks-free-market-socialism-.html', 'snippet': 'In his State of the Union address, here’s hoping President Obama promotes policies that will help more people realize the American dream.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I hope President Obama read about Maddie Parlier as he was working on his State of the Union address. Parlier is the subject 