ne, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreclosures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attorneys General', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T02:25:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nelson D. Schwartz and Shaila Dewan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nelson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ff55233-63b6-5442-9007-62327ddf8a62', 'word_count': 1092, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ff55233-63b6-5442-9007-62327ddf8a62'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands of refugees were left stranded after the United States stopped processing visas in Damascus, saying the situation was too volatile to send officials for required interviews.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/world/middleeast/unrest-strands-iraqis-in-syria-awaiting-american-visas.html', 'snippet': 'Thousands of refugees were left stranded after the United States stopped processing visas in Damascus, saying the situation was too volatile to send officials for required interviews.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — The United States has nearly halted the processing of visas for Iraqi refugees in Syria, leaving thousands of people who fled a war in their homeland marooned in a country in the grip of an increasingly violent insurrection, with little hope of leaving anytime soon.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unrest and American Safety Concerns Strand Iraqis in Syria Awaiting Visas for U.S.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unrest and American Safety Concerns Strand Iraqis in Syria Awaiting Visas for U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Damascus (Syria)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Visas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T02:28:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Arango', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arango', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa6de47e-7191-5070-8abe-ef374eda183a', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa6de47e-7191-5070-8abe-ef374eda183a'}, {'abstract': 'How the repeating rifle could have changed the course of the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/the-unions-newfangled-gimcracks/', 'snippet': 'How the repeating rifle could have changed the course of the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In late December 1861 Abraham Lincoln issued a directive that, had it been vigorously pursued, might have brought the Civil War to a rapid end: An order, via Gen. James Ripley, the Army’s ordnance chief, for 10,000 Spencer repeating rifles. Because Ripley resisted the order for months and did nothing to help put the rifles into volume production, initial deliveries didn’t start until about a year and a half after Lincoln first tested the rifle. Consequently, Union soldiers had to fight with less efficient weapons, handicapping them and greatly lengthening the bloody conflict. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Union’s ‘Newfangled Gimcracks’', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T02:29:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Leigh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leigh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8176ba8a-149e-5bb8-a944-e1cda1605c61', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8176ba8a-149e-5bb8-a944-e1cda1605c61'}, {'abstract': 'Pheasant Island is owned for six months a year by France, six months by Spain. But no one is allowed to set foot on it.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/23/the-worlds-most-exclusive-condominium/', 'snippet': 'Pheasant Island is owned for six months a year by France, six months by Spain. But no one is allowed to set foot on it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You are not allowed to visit Pheasant Island, which lies near the Atlantic Ocean terminus of the French-Spanish border. But “it can easily be seen from the Joncaux bank, on the Bay Path,” the Web site for the local tourist office [1] suggests, without a hint of irony. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The World’s Most Exclusive Condominium', 'kicker': 'Borderlines', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Names, Geographical', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Treaties', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Denmark', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Luxembourg', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T02:30:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea1666ee-9e78-5c95-b03d-cea96ff2cfc7', 'word_count': 1745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea1666ee-9e78-5c95-b03d-cea96ff2cfc7'}, {'abstract': 'The president’s re-election team is enjoying the Republican fight, but knows much can change before Election Day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/us/politics/confident-obama-knows-wild-cards-can-loom-large.html', 'snippet': 'The president’s re-election team is enjoying the Republican fight, but knows much can change before Election Day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — As President Obama prepares to address the state of the union on Tuesday night, the state of his presidency has been improving lately — thanks to encouraging economic news 