ain': 'New Teachers Union Ad Takes Aim at Bloomberg', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T13:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By MARY ANN GIORDANO', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Ann', 'lastname': 'GIORDANO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4be71099-15e6-5c38-93c6-4313366dfb96', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4be71099-15e6-5c38-93c6-4313366dfb96'}, {'abstract': 'Times reporters look into the specifics of the Romney tax returns.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/inside-the-romney-tax-returns/', 'snippet': 'Times reporters look into the specifics of the Romney tax returns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney’s campaign released details of his federal tax returns on Tuesday morning, providing the most detailed view yet of his wealthy family’s finances. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the Romney Tax Returns', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T13:42:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris, Nicholas Confessore and Stephanie Strom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Confessore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/068e5a88-0cb4-5283-b36d-16a0dd9cf80c', 'word_count': 1692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/068e5a88-0cb4-5283-b36d-16a0dd9cf80c'}, {'abstract': 'Some bubbles are much worse than others.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/a-tale-of-two-bubbles/', 'snippet': 'Some bubbles are much worse than others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the educational things about being something of a public figure is that I keep learning new things about how nefarious and dishonest I really am. The latest evilness I’ve learned about in comments and correspondence is that I have a double standard on bubbles — I talk about the Bush housing bubble, but I never said a word about the Clinton stock bubble.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tale Of Two Bubbles', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T13:54:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/914ce970-f25b-5b17-a87c-34b9a8d80e5c', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/914ce970-f25b-5b17-a87c-34b9a8d80e5c'}, {'abstract': 'Casio’s G-Shock GB6900 watch uses a new, low-power Bluetooth chip called Bluetooth Smart (or Bluetooth 4.0) to do amazing things with a PC or smartphone. Butt the watch doesn’t work unless your phone also has Bluetooth 4.0 — and right now, only a couple of them do.', 'web_url': 'https://pogue.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/a-new-g-shock-watch-awaits-a-new-bluetooth/', 'snippet': 'Casio’s G-Shock GB6900 watch uses a new, low-power Bluetooth chip called Bluetooth Smart (or Bluetooth 4.0) to do amazing things with a PC or smartphone. Butt the watch doesn’t work unless your phone also has Bluetooth 4.0 — and right now, only a ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'As one Las Vegas cab driver told me, the International Consumer Electronics Show held earlier this month seems to be in an economic bubble all its own. “The big fashion industry convention show was down, from 80,000 attendees to 40,000,” he said. “But C.E.S. didn’t seem to get the e-mail that we’re in an economic slump.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A New G-Shock Watch Awaits a New Bluetooth', 'kicker': 'Pogue&#039;s Posts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bluetooth Wireless Technology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Consumer Electronics Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Watches and Clocks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Casio Computer Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T13:57:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Pogue', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogue', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05b55610-59a9-5166-93ff-9f781e101742', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05b55610-59a9-5166-93ff-9f781e101742'}, {'abstract': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/opinion-report-newt-gingrich-insider/', 'snippet': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the editorial page', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opinion Report: Newt Gingrich, Insider', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T13:57:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22880b9b-8ba2-5122-80d8-50318f2a0ed5', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22880b9b-8ba2-5122-80d8-50318f2a0ed5'}, {'abstract': 'On Monday, Twitter acquired the Internet security start-up Dasient, its second security acquisition in three months. Twitter has long had issues with trending topic scams and spams,and is a frequent target for high-profile hacks. Dasient’s scanning software helps businesses identify and contain malware on the Web.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/twitter-acquires-second-security-firm-in-three-months/', 'snippet': 'On Monday, Twitter acquired the Internet security start-up Dasient, its second security acquisition in three months. Twitter has long had issues with trending topic scams and spams,and is a frequent target for high-profile hacks. Dasient’s scannin...', 'lead_paragraph': 'If recent acquisitions are any indication, Twitter is beefing up security on its site.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twitter Acquires Second Security Firm in Three Months', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c4cf096-25f4-582e-89b5-e17ab4aec5ae', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c4cf096-25f4-582e-89b5-e17ab4aec5ae'}, {'abstract': 'A British editor’s subversive-cool aesthetic comes to the storied Italian house by way of a capsule collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/de-coding-jefferson-hacks-new-shoes-for-tods/', 'snippet': 'A British editor’s subversive-cool aesthetic comes to the storied Italian house by way of a capsule collection.', 'lead_paragraph': '“My first Tod’s piece was a gommini loafer,” says Jefferson Hack, the Dazed & Confused co-founder and editor of AnOther and AnOther Man magazines. “Diego [Della Valle] invited me to have it made to my personal specifications, as I was not a typical Tod’s customer.” And so it began. One trip to the company’s headquarters in Le Marche, Italy, and Della Valle, the C.E.O., was asking Hack to design a capsule collection that would bring the British editor’s subversive-cool aesthetic to the storied Italian house. Featuring seven styles (four for women, three for men) including a sea-blue suede lace-up ankle shoe and a patent leather slipper flat, the collection “breaks with convention and is untethered to the formal codes of dress or casual attire,” says Della Valle, by way of explaining the capsule’s name: No-Code.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'De-Coding Jefferson Hack’s New Shoes for Tod’s', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Tod's Group", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38253f7a-5aff-576e-8148-9880d1e06a96', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38253f7a-5aff-576e-8148-9880d1e06a96'}, {'abstract': 'The State Department said that a joke during Jay Leno’s monologue, which implied that the Golden Temple in Amritsar was a summer home owned by Mitt Romney, was free speech protected by the Constitution.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/state-department-defends-leno-after-joke-offends-sikhs/', 'snippet': 'The State Department said that a joke during Jay Leno’s monologue, which implied that the Golden Temple in Amritsar was a summer home owned by Mitt Romney, was free speech protected by the Constitution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though most comedians hope that their material stands on its own, some additional support from their country now and then doesn’t hurt either: The State Department stood up for Jay Leno after the “Tonight Show” host offended some Indian Sikhs with a joke that implied that a holy shrine in India was a home owned by Mitt Romney.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'State Department Defends Leno After Joke Offends Sikhs', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indian-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'majo