', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lizette Alvarez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lizette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alvarez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a0685d0-c9e4-5289-97c0-919064e27e2e', 'word_count': 1479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a0685d0-c9e4-5289-97c0-919064e27e2e'}, {'abstract': 'The dangers of post-partisan dreams.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/jared-bernstein-on-the-summers-memo/', 'snippet': 'The dangers of post-partisan dreams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jared makes the best case for arguing that Larry wasn’t really worrying about the invisible bond vigilantes. That’s still not how I read it, but again, it’s water under the bridge.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jared Bernstein On The Summers Memo', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:42:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/08ef6a89-c044-5478-a164-e91d9a2f74d9', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08ef6a89-c044-5478-a164-e91d9a2f74d9'}, {'abstract': 'Nearly 100 galleries from 20 countries converge in Delhi.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/india-art-fair-goes-international/', 'snippet': 'Nearly 100 galleries from 20 countries converge in Delhi.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The India Art Fair, one of the biggest art exhibitions in South Asia,\xa0 opens in New Delhi on Wednesday. The five-day event brings together a host of artists, curators, critics, dealers and collectors from across the globe, and this year will draw ninety-one galleries from twenty countries, representing the work of a thousand contemporary and modern artists.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'India Art Fair Goes International', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kapoor, Anish', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Galleria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:47:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamposh Raina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamposh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Raina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d88bdd8e-f4dd-52a8-8adc-34ca91b107fc', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d88bdd8e-f4dd-52a8-8adc-34ca91b107fc'}, {'abstract': 'Deadly showdowns between Chinese security forces and Tibetans in a restive region of western China spread to a second town on Tuesday, outside advocacy groups reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/world/asia/china-says-tibetan-monks-rioted-provoking-deadly-confrontation.html', 'snippet': 'Deadly showdowns between Chinese security forces and Tibetans in a restive region of western China spread to a second town on Tuesday, outside advocacy groups reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — Deadly showdowns between Chinese security forces and Tibetans in a restive region of western China spread to a second town on Tuesday, outside advocacy groups reported. At least two and perhaps as many as five Tibetans were killed by gunfire and many more wounded, the groups said, in what appeared to be the most violent outbreak in the region in nearly four years.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/world/TIBET/TIBET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 979, 'width': 705, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deadly Confrontation Spreads in Tibetan Region of China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deadly Clashes Spread In Tibetan Area of China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tibet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:48:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher and Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b841efa5-942d-54ca-aa11-087dc77cb0c1', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b841efa5-942d-54ca-aa11-087dc77cb0c1'}, {'abstract': 'Adoptees use DNA testing to find family ties, a dearth of disclosure about expired airline tickets, women appear to suffer more from pain and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/tuesday-reading-women-may-suffer-more-from-pain/', 'snippet': 'Adoptees use DNA testing to find family ties, a dearth of disclosure about expired airline tickets, women appear to suffer more from pain and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times', 'lead_paragraph': 'A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tuesday Reading: Women May Suffer More From Pain', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:48:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96fea9b9-eedd-5d3b-ba09-1d5090378d00', 'word_count': 154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96fea9b9-eedd-5d3b-ba09-1d5090378d00'}, {'abstract': 'The nuclear industry’s policy group joins with utility coalitions to press for centralization of the waste and renewing the hunt for a permanent resting place.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/wanted-parking-space-for-nuclear-waste/', 'snippet': 'The nuclear industry’s policy group joins with utility coalitions to press for centralization of the waste and renewing the hunt for a permanent resting place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Obama administration killed a plan to create a nuclear waste repository at Yucca Mountain in Nevada, the government established a “blue-ribbon commission” to study what to do next. Its final report is due on Sunday, and this week three organizations began a public lobbying campaign for several of the recommendations that they assume the commission will make.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wanted: Parking Space for Nuclear Waste', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Wastes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Department', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn of Regulatory Utility Commissioners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy Institute', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nuclear Regulatory Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maine', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nevada', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Mexico', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Salt Lake City (Utah)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yucca Mountain (Nev)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:49:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f58f519c-df72-544c-b10f-52e08cea0d28', 'word_count': 629, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f58f519c-df72-544c-b10f-52e08cea0d28'}, {'abstract': 'The Butler Bulldogs made it to the national championship game in each of the past two seasons. The year looks to be a challenging one, but the team won two road games over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/for-butler-a-pivotal-road-trip/', 'snippet': 'The Butler Bulldogs made it to the national championship game in each of the past two seasons. The year looks to be a challenging one, but the team won two road games over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2010 and 2011, the Butler Bulldogs were college basketball’s favorite Cinderella story. But this season has been a struggle. Matt Rhinesmith, a sports reporter for The Butler Collegian, will file occasional posts for The Quad about the team’s efforts to return to the N.C.A.A. tournament.The Butler men’s basketball team started off its longest road trip of the season with wins over Illinois-Chicago and Loyola this past weekend. Playing at Loyola’s newly renovated Gentile Center on Saturday, the Bulldogs were able to cope with a hostile environment and earn a 63-57 victory.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Butler, a Pivotal Road Trip', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevens, Brad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Butler University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hinkle Fieldhouse', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Wisconsin-Green Bay', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:51:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Rhinesmith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rhinesmith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4d01b70-33ee-5cbd-9061-5baf1f712754', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4d01b70-33ee-5cbd-9061-5baf1f712754'}, {'abstract': 'Romney slams his rival as “an influence-peddler.”', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/without-a-ravenous-crowd-gingrich-stumbles-in-the-first-florida-debate/', 'snippet': 'Romney slams his rival as “an influence-peddler.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many Republican voters in South Carolina said that much of Newt Gingrich’s appeal stemmed from his masterly debating skills. They believed he deftly made brilliant points, was vastly knowledgeable on his feet, and brutally shut down his opponents, and they dreamed of his doing the same thing to President Obama.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Without a Ravenous Crowd, Gingrich Stumbles in the First Florida Debate', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:57:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02441d73-8574-5a67-b3f2-1fb9a40d5dd9', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02441d73-8574-5a67-b3f2-1fb9a40d5dd9'}, {'abstract': 'The $801 million sale comes as the London-based banking giant is shedding assets to focus on its more profitable operations.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/hsbc-sells-latin-american-businesses/', 'snippet': 'The $801 million sale comes as the London-based banking giant is shedding assets to focus on its more profitable operations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – HSBC, one of Europe’s biggest banks, said on Tuesday that it had agreed to sell its businesses in Costa Rica, El Salvador and Honduras for $801 million as part of its plan to focus on more profitable operations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'HSBC Sells Latin American Businesses', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HSBC Holdings PLC.', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Latin America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:58:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2dd0131-d152-5ad6-9d73-7a270faf74c4', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2dd0131-d152-5ad6-9d73-7a270faf74c4'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/what-were-reading-357/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Daily Beast.com: In the end, it always comes down to food and drink. The “beer test” rule in the presidential election, according to the Democratic Party partisan Paul Begala, rates a politician’s electability by whether voters would like to have a few beers with the candidate. Mitt Romney is challenged, he claims, because “you get the feeling he’d be stiff even over a chocolate shake.” — Glenn Collins ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T14:59:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By THE NEW YORK TIMES', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d17ea58-1f70-5c34-b10a-86ae16b87816', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d17ea58-1f70-5c34-b10a-86ae16b87816'}, {'abstract': 'One of the first things new Kindle users discover is that Amazon is giving away e-books. In fact, there is an entire best-seller list made up of free titles. But the contemporary novels on this list are not as up-to-date as they might be.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/bestsellers-from-a-vanished-world/', 'snippet': 'One of the first things new Kindle users discover is that Amazon is giving away e-books. In fact, there is an entire best-seller list made up of free titles. But the contemporary novels on this list are not as up-to-date as they might be.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the last two months, five million or so people got a Kindle Fire. Some of them were upgrading from earlier Kindles. They know the ropes. But millions of others must be new at this, and are stumbling around with their tablets looking for content. If they are like the rest of us and still paying the holiday bills, they want it free. That would be Angry Birds, of course, but also the Kindle Top 100 Free best-seller list. Some of these books are out of copyright — “Dracula,” Jane Austen — and some seem to have originated with Amazon itself. But at the top and interspersed all the way through are novels written by living if somewhat obscure authors. Romances, mostly, often with a Christian theme.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/business/bits-kindle-fire/bits-kindle-fire-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/23/business/bits-kindle-fire/bits-kindle-fire-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/23/business/bits-kindle-fire/bits-kindle-fire-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Sellers From a Vanished World', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amazon Kindle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vidal, Gore', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T15:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Streitfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Streitfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3b1f40b-77d1-54da-bdef-f4c95ec82308', 'word_count': 755, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3b1f40b-77d1-54da-bdef-f4c95ec82308'}, {'abstract': 'The model Aymeline Valade transforms with makeup by Pat McGrath and hair by Orlando Pita.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/model-morphosis-aymeline-valade-at-christian-dior-couture/', 'snippet': 'The model Aymeline Valade transforms with makeup by Pat McGrath and hair by Orlando Pita.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The photographer Greg Kessler captures the transformations of the Spring 2012 Couture collections in Paris. Here, the model Aymeline Valade before (left) and after (right) transforms, with makeup by Pat McGrath and hair by Orlando Pita. (Drag the orange bar left to reveal the full transformation.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Model-Morphosis | Aymeline Valade at Christian Dior Couture', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Models (Professional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T15:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Kessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e0eb5c6-c9b5-5e26-86c2-94f5ea0514a6', 'word_count': 54, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e0eb5c6-c9b5-5e26-86c2-94f5ea0514a6'}, {'abstract': 'Demian Bichir but not Leonardo DiCaprio, Rooney Mara but not Tilda Swinton and other interesting twists in the race.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/parsing-the-surprises-and-the-snubs/', 'snippet': 'Demian Bichir but not Leonardo DiCaprio, Rooney Mara but not Tilda Swinton and other interesting twists in the race.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:26 a.m. | Updated  As usual, the Bagger and other Oscar watchers were in for some surprises with the nominations on Tuesday morning, starting with the number of nominees. Few predicted that there would be a field of nine best picture contenders, and fewer still – hardly anyone – expected that one of those would be “Extremely Loud & Incredibly Close,” Stephen Daldry’s adaptation of Jonathan Safran Foer’s post-9/11 novel. The film also earned a best supporting actor nod for its 82-year-old star, Max von Sydow. Gasps were heard from the press and others assembled in the announcement room when the poorly reviewed film was called.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Parsing the Surprises and the Snubs', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALLEN, WOODY', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chastain, Jessica', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daldry, Stephen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiCaprio, Leonardo', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foer, Jonathan Safran', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hill, Jonah', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malick, Terrence', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mara, Rooney', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Melissa', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pitt, Brad', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swinton, Tilda', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'von Sydow, Max', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T15:03:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62c6323f-1cd6-5afc-b4c1-8b58f6fe8866', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62c6323f-1cd6-5afc-b4c1-8b58f6fe8866'}, {'abstract': 'Paris', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/01/24/fashion/runway-couture/chanel-spring-2012-couture.html', 'snippet': 'Paris', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paris', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/24/fashion/runway/0124chanelcouture-slide-D0Q3/0124chanelcouture-slide-D0Q3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chanel: Spring 2012 Couture', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paris Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Couture (Fashion)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chanel, Coco', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagerfeld, Karl', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T15:15:30+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Couture Runway', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/e2b30e7f-aca4-5868-a000-50bb3878d1ce', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/e2b30e7f-aca4-5868-a000-50bb3878d1ce'}, {'abstract': 'The real surprise in the foreign-language Oscar race is “Bullhead,” a Belgian crime drama, which beat out Wim Wenders’s “Pina.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/a-belgian-surprise-and-a-snub-for-germany-in-oscar-race/', 'snippet': 'The real surprise in the foreign-language Oscar race is “Bullhead,” a Belgian crime drama, which beat out Wim Wenders’s “Pina.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The foreign-language Oscar category delivered two surprises Tuesday morning: the choice of “Bullhead,” Belgium’s unexpected submission, and the absence of Wim Wenders’s “Pina,” Germany’s submission. The three strongest films in the shortlist of nine made public last week were clearly  Iran’s “Separation,”  Israel’s “Footnote” and Canada’s “Monsieur Lazhar,” and all three, as expected, received nominations, leaving only two spots to be fought over by the six other contenders. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Belgian Surprise and a Snub for Germany in Oscar Race', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T15:15:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68b82116-a70f-5563-aaed-571f58775be2', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68b82116-a70f-5563-aaed-571f58775be2'}, {'abstract': 'Despite an infusion of cash to a “super PAC” supporting him, Newt Gingrich faces an expensive Florida media market, and a well-financed opponent.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/how-far-will-5-million-go-for-gingrich-in-florida/', 'snippet': 'Despite an infusion of cash to a “super PAC” supporting him, Newt Gingrich faces an expensive Florida media market, and a well-financed opponent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It sounds like a lot of money\xa0 — $5 million from a wealthy benefactor to help Newt Gingri