Home Box Office', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABRAMOVIC, MARINA', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:09:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/496714bb-d3c5-5a57-b638-374222872376', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/496714bb-d3c5-5a57-b638-374222872376'}, {'abstract': 'The Korean automaker’s sales in the metro area for an 11-month period in 2011 totaled 17,005, up from 9,304 in the same period in 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/edmunds-says-kia-sales-rose-83-percent-in-new-york/', 'snippet': 'The Korean automaker’s sales in the metro area for an 11-month period in 2011 totaled 17,005, up from 9,304 in the same period in 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kia experienced exceptional growth in the New York metropolitan area in 2011, with sales increasing 83 percent, according to Edmunds.com. Its Web site compared new-car registration data provided by Polk, the automotive forecasting company, from January through November 2010 with the same 11-month period in 2011. Kia’s performance made it the fastest-growing automotive brand in the metropolitan area among the 35 brands included in the Edmunds analysis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Edmunds Says Kia Sales Rose 83 Percent in New York', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kia Motors Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York Metropolitan Area', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:15:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cheryl Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cheryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21b65f59-6faf-5072-b172-e8d2a4f941be', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21b65f59-6faf-5072-b172-e8d2a4f941be'}, {'abstract': 'Why this year’s nominees are a conventional crop: With no all-out comedy or beakout indie, the list isn’t much of a surprise.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/mostly-dramas-from-white-men-why-its-a-conventional-best-picture-list/', 'snippet': 'Why this year’s nominees are a conventional crop: With no all-out comedy or beakout indie, the list isn’t much of a surprise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Stretched all the way to nine entries, the year’s field of best picture nominees says one thing loud and clear about the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences: It hasn’t changed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mostly Dramas From White Men: Why It’s a Conventional Best Picture List', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Academy Awards (Oscars)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Universal Pictures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weinstein Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:19:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/003f15be-fbe9-5e38-905a-9a1cf596d464', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/003f15be-fbe9-5e38-905a-9a1cf596d464'}, {'abstract': '“Rebecca,” one of the Broadway season’s most expensive musicals, has postponed its opening after producers couldn’t raise all the money to capitalize the show.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/without-money-in-place-rebecca-postpones-broadway-opening-until-next-season/', 'snippet': '“Rebecca,” one of the Broadway season’s most expensive musicals, has postponed its opening after producers couldn’t raise all the money to capitalize the show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The producers of the Broadway-bound musical “Rebecca,” which had been scheduled to begin performances on March 27, said on Tuesday that they were postponing the show until the 2012-13 season because they ran out of time to raise the production’s $12 million capitalization. The musical, which had been set to begin rehearsals on Feb. 6, would have been one of the most expensive of the 2011-12 theater season, but the producers were widely known around Broadway to be struggling to raise the final 20 percent of the show’s budget.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Without Money in Place, ‘Rebecca’ Postpones Broadway Opening Until Next Season', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boggess, Sierra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sprecher, Ben', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:19:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/680fb5c3-b34b-5aea-a45e-e9c2db93ab42', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/680fb5c3-b34b-5aea-a45e-e9c2db93ab42'}, {'abstract': 'The Romney campaign released Mitt Romney’s tax returns on Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/01/24/us/politics/2011-romney-tax-returns.html', 'snippet': 'The Romney campaign released Mitt Romney’s tax returns on Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Romney campaign released Mitt Romney’s tax returns on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mitt and Ann Romney’s Tax Returns', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:20:18+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a9590261-fa72-5156-a26d-19abc80b2ca0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a9590261-fa72-5156-a26d-19abc80b2ca0'}, {'abstract': 'Alison Price Becker’s new Flatiron restaurant will have a nice, personal touch: custom wallpaper that depicts all of her favorite things.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/off-the-wall-alison-18s-biographical-toile/', 'snippet': 'Alison Price Becker’s new Flatiron restaurant will have a nice, personal touch: custom wallpaper that depicts all of her favorite things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Wall | Alison Eighteen’s Biographical Toile', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flatiron District (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:21:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Earle-Levine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Earle-Levine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dde665c-3f8b-5020-a55e-fd31918d7ba9', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dde665c-3f8b-5020-a55e-fd31918d7ba9'}, {'abstract': 'The quest to name a “song of the summer” has become both more scientific and more quixotic — in part because we’re all entombed in our earbuds now, listening to our niche, custom-curated music feeds, rather than communally riding down Main Street in low-riders blasting “Steal My Sunshine.”', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/is-it-too-early-for-a-song-of-the-summer/', 'snippet': 'The quest to name a “song of the summer” has become both more scientific and more quixotic — in part because we’re all entombed in our earbuds now, listening to our niche, custom-curated music feeds, rather than communally riding down Main Street ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The quest to name a “song of the summer” has become both more scientific and more quixotic — in part because we’re all entombed in our earbuds now, listening to our niche, custom-curated music feeds, rather than communally riding down Main Street in low-riders blasting “Steal My Sunshine.” And, in part, because most of winter this year, at least in New York, feels weirdly like mid-April.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is It Too Early for a Song of the Summer?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:23:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Sternbergh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sternbergh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fce70bd-1c5a-5a5f-9ff9-b78d9481468a', 'word_count': 193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fce70bd-1c5a-5a5f-9ff9-b78d9481468a'}, {'abstract': 'A study says requiring a 20 percent or 10 percent down payment would price too many creditworthy borrowers out of the housing market.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/big-downpayments-could-bar-creditworthy-borrowers-from-market-study-finds/', 'snippet': 'A study says requiring a 20 percent or 10 percent down payment would price too many creditworthy borrowers out of the housing market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Requiring a minimum down payment of 20 percent, or even 10 percent, on home loans would push many creditworthy borrowers into higher-cost loans or out of the mortgage market entirely, a new study says.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Down Payments Could Bar Creditworthy Borrowers From Market, Study Finds', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of North Carolina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:25:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11621074-03a4-5553-888c-ac45fb766468', 'word_count': 550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11621074-03a4-5553-888c-ac45fb766468'}, {'abstract': 'A decade after the euro’s birth as a full-fledged currency, the anniversary has been more a sickbed vigil than a celebration. Inconvenient fact seldom impedes the euro-skeptic narrative, argues Brian Childs in Another View column.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/fate-of-the-euro-a-contrarian-view/', 'snippet': 'A decade after the euro’s birth as a full-fledged currency, the anniversary has been more a sickbed vigil than a celebration. Inconvenient fact seldom impedes the euro-skeptic narrative, argues Brian Childs in Another View column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — A decade after the euro’s birth as a full-fledged currency, the anniversary has been more a sickbed vigil than a celebration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking Past the Skepticism on Fate of the Euro', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Monetary Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T18:30:20+0000', 'documen