eld its ground in its second week with only a modest drop to just under 9 million viewers from the 10 million who tuned in to last week’s premiere. It also dipped in the 18-to-49-year-old demographic by 10 percent. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘House’ and the Big House Lift Fox', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Laurie, Hugh', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:03:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51edbb52-9c7c-53ba-b9c0-9e8ad6792854', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51edbb52-9c7c-53ba-b9c0-9e8ad6792854'}, {'abstract': 'Dissecting the Italian-American menu.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/five-dishes-at-parm/', 'snippet': 'Dissecting the Italian-American menu.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week I review Parm, where Rich Torrisi and Mario Carbone are serving food more or less taken from the Italian-American deli canon: Sausage and peppers, potato and eggs, garlic bread, pizza knots and all kinds of parms. I interviewed the two chefs earlier this week about five of the dishes I especially enjoyed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Five Dishes at Parm', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:14:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Wells', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wells', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37876ff9-2cfd-5dbb-84df-ce8837b098f4', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37876ff9-2cfd-5dbb-84df-ce8837b098f4'}, {'abstract': 'Of all the Oscar snubs, perhaps the lack of major nominations for “Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows: Part 2″ is the one that most rankles.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/why-harry-potter-deserved-more/', 'snippet': 'Of all the Oscar snubs, perhaps the lack of major nominations for “Harry Potter and the Deathly Hallows: Part 2″ is the one that most rankles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe the idea is that if the Academy nominates as many movies as possible across the various high-profile categories, more moviegoers will have a rooting interest and more people will watch. “Tree of Life” fans, “Midnight in Paris” fans, “A Separation” fans, “Rango” fans — just about everyone is theoretically covered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why ‘Harry Potter’ Deserved More', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fiennes, Ralph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kloves, Steve', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rickman, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yates, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:21:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b606c62a-e7a3-552c-9f3b-76a78c547b15', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b606c62a-e7a3-552c-9f3b-76a78c547b15'}, {'abstract': 'A General Motors plant manager, the mayor of San Antonio and an elementary school teacher are among the guests invited to watch the State of the Union address with Michelle Obama.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/joining-mrs-obama-will-be/', 'snippet': 'A General Motors plant manager, the mayor of San Antonio and an elementary school teacher are among the guests invited to watch the State of the Union address with Michelle Obama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The guests joining Michelle Obama in her box seats overlooking the House chamber for the president’s State of the Union speech on Tuesday night include Jill Biden, the vice president’s wife; Valerie Jarrett, the president’s senior adviser; and a panoply of ordinary and extraordinary people chosen as archetypes of the policies, priorities and accomplishments Mr. Obama will seek to emphasize.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Joining Mrs. Obama Will Be …', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:22:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By John H. Cushman Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Cushman', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 