nk': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kobach, Kris W', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN FEDERATION OF STATE, COUNTY AND MUNICIPAL EMPLOYEES', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Service Employees International Union', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:42:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/218928ae-9785-597f-99fb-b34ac09aba37', 'word_count': 461, 'uri': 'nyt://article/218928ae-9785-597f-99fb-b34ac09aba37'}, {'abstract': 'In search of apps to help a dyslexic second grader learn.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/apps-for-children-with-dyslexia/', 'snippet': 'In search of apps to help a dyslexic second grader learn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The parent of a second grader newly diagnosed with dyslexia wrote me asking if I knew of any apps that might help her son with reading and math. She’d searched and come up with nothing — and so did I, with the same result. There is software out there designed to help children with dyslexia read (expensive software) but nothing yet in the way of an app marketed for that purpose (and there’s obviously no app that will “fix” dyslexia).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apps for Children With Dyslexia', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dyslexia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:45:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75cbaaa5-bde1-587a-8ff0-5bed597814c5', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75cbaaa5-bde1-587a-8ff0-5bed597814c5'}, {'abstract': 'Nearly 1,000 New York restaurant operators have responded to an online survey by the City Council, which will hold hearings on the new food-safety grades.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/hundreds-of-restaurateurs-sound-off-on-sanitary-inspections/', 'snippet': 'Nearly 1,000 New York restaurant operators have responded to an online survey by the City Council, which will hold hearings on the new food-safety grades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York City Council will announce Wednesday that nearly 1,000 restaurant operators have responded, after only two weeks, to a Web survey seeking their views about the city health department’s new letter-grading system for food safety.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hundreds of Restaurateurs Sound Off on Sanitary Inspections', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:45:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28f7b70d-2ebb-5608-b574-c9077715b5f7', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28f7b70d-2ebb-5608-b574-c9077715b5f7'}, {'abstract': 'Things people now said they didn’t say.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/a-bizarre-turn-in-the-stimulus-debate-boring/', 'snippet': 'Things people now said they didn’t say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wow. Just wow. John Cochrane puts up a post that to all appearances amounts to a complete retreat from his previous denunciations of deficit spending in a recession. But he simultaneously denies having ever held the position everyone thought he held, and denounces me and Brad DeLong as big meanies. I’ll outsource the analysis to Noah Smith. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bizarre Turn In The Stimulus Debate (Boring)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:49:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c72eb6c-7d5c-5337-8592-75ee2d36a611', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c72eb6c-7d5c-5337-8592-75ee2d36a611'}, {'abstract': 'After a long, controversial wait, Mitt Romney released details of his federal tax returns on Tuesday, including information on the trusts managed by the investment bank Goldman Sachs.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/goldman-makes-money-for-the-romneys/', 'snippet': 'After a long, controversial wait, Mitt Romney released details of his federal tax returns on Tuesday, including information on the trusts managed by the investment bank Goldman Sachs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a long, controversial wait, Mitt Romney released details of his federal tax returns on Tuesday, inciting a flurry of wide-eyed analysis from those curious to see exactly how Mr. Romney’s personal finances stack up.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goldman Makes Money for the Romneys', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T20:54:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Roose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb3ebcb2-fb98-5e83-a5e9-1941aaae8145', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb3ebcb2-fb98-5e83-a5e9-1941aaae8145'}, {'abstract': 'Alison Price Becker returns with Alison Eighteen; City Bakery alums open their own place in Prospect Heights; Franny’s plans to move.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/dining/alison-eighteen-opens-off-the-menu.html', 'snippet': 'Alison Price Becker returns with Alison Eighteen; City Bakery alums open their own place in Prospect Heights; Franny’s plans to move.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Opening', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/dining/25OFF_SPAN/25OFF_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/25/dining/25OFF_SPAN/25OFF_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/dining/25OFF_SPAN/25OFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/dining/25OFF_SPAN/25OFF_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/25/dining/25OFF_SPAN/25OFF_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Menu', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Off the Menu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a6eb245-4dc7-53f5-8b6e-64e75d6635bf', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a6eb245-4dc7-53f5-8b6e-64e75d6635bf'}, {'abstract': 'Originally born in southern Ukraine, Sergei Polunin joined the Royal Ballet School in 2003 at age 13.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/star-dancer-quits-royal-ballet/', 'snippet': 'Originally born in southern Ukraine, Sergei Polunin joined the Royal Ballet School in 2003 at age 13.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sergei Polunin, the dark-eyed phenom who was the youngest male dancer ever to be made a principal with the Royal Ballet, abruptly quit the company on Tuesday afternoon. Monica Mason, the director of the Royal Ballet, said in a press statement, “This has obviously come as a huge shock. Sergei is a wonderful dancer, and I have enjoyed watching him tremendously, both on stage and in the studio, over the past few years. I wish him every success in the future.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Star Dancer Quits Royal Ballet', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mason, Monica', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:04:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40f7408a-851d-565a-b128-335c3b2fbe05', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40f7408a-851d-565a-b128-335c3b2fbe05'}, {'abstract': 'The Weather Channel Companies named the digital advertising veteran David Kenny as the company’s chairman and chief executive on Tuesday, succeeding Michael J. Kelly, who had been the chief executive since 2009.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/david-kenny-named-chief-of-the-weather-channel/', 'snippet': 'The Weather Channel Companies named the digital advertising veteran David Kenny as the company’s chairman and chief executive on Tuesday, succeeding Michael J. Kelly, who had been the chief executive since 2009.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Weather Channel Companies, which owns the Weather Channel, replaced its leadership on Tuesday, and named the digital advertising veteran David Kenny its chairman and chief executive.Mr. Kenny will start immediately. Michael J. Kelly, who had been the chief executive since 2009, will become a special adviser to the company and an adviser to Bain Capital, the private equity firm that co-owns the company with the Blackstone Group and Comcast’s NBCUniversal.The surprise change signaled a desire on the part of the co-owners to expand the Weather Channel’s digital and mobile businesses more quickly. Through mobile apps and Web sites like Weather.com, the company sees an opportunity to have a much bigger international footprint.“Six and a half billion people need information about weather,” Mr. Kenny said in an interview Tuesday after addressing the company’s staff in Atlanta.Mr. Kenny also said that the company would make more investments in long-form programming — part of a gradual move away from live weather news and toward taped weather-related reality shows.“Now that people can get the information they need for their forecast off the Web, and certainly on their mobile phone, that gives us more license to do more long-form on the cable channel,” he said. The channel remained committed to live news during severe weather, he added.Mr. Kenny, a member of the board of directors of Yahoo, was the president of Akamai until he stepped down last October. He was previously the co-founder of VivaKi, a digital agency that is part of the Publicis Groupe.In a statement, the three co-owners of the Weather Channel said that Mr. Kenny’s experience “gives him a deep understanding of the TV and digital business, and he has the leadership experience and vision to drive global initiatives across all platforms.”Mr. Kelly, similarly, had a digital media background when he was hired two and a half years ago.Mr. Kenny said he was initially recruited to join the board of the company, and was later asked by the board to consider the chief executive position.“What I’ve long stood for is convergence,” he said. “I think you’ll see us find better ways to run the Weather Channel as one company, really organized around our hundreds of millions of users.”NBCUniversal and the private equity firms have owned the Weather Channel since 2008.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'David Kenny Named Chief of the Weather Channel', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Michael J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenny, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weather Channel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:04:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90857dd0-71e9-535f-93ce-b97a29f2595f', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90857dd0-71e9-535f-93ce-b97a29f2595f'}, {'abstract': 'Sometimes, you want to save an industrial ecology.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/chinese-manufacturing-and-the-auto-bailout/', 'snippet': 'Sometimes, you want to save an industrial ecology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jon Cohn, in correspondence, makes an important point with regard to the big Times report on Chinese manufacturing. Since he hasn’t posted about it, I will.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese Manufacturing and the Auto Bailout', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:05:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8110cbd-1274-5855-8ccb-4732a6a21775', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8110cbd-1274-5855-8ccb-4732a6a21775'}, {'abstract': 'Should the definition of depression be expanded to include grieving for a loved one?', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/expanding-the-definition-of-depression/', 'snippet': 'Should the definition of depression be expanded to include grieving for a loved one?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Does grieving over a loved one constitute full-blown depression?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Expanding the Definition of Depression', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emotions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grief (Emotion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:08:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb32f245-13e7-56e2-889a-97d1571febab', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb32f245-13e7-56e2-889a-97d1571febab'}, {'abstract': 'The photographer, known for her candid images, on her new book and upcoming projects.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/the-lowdown-cass-bird/', 'snippet': 'The photographer, known for her candid images, on her new book and upcoming projects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cass Bird’s photographs are instantly recognizable: her subjects always look mischievous, and like they are having just a little too much fun. Whether it’s a teenage model, her own children or the actor Viggo Mortensen (whom she photographed for the cover of T Magazine), Bird consistently manages to get the people she shoots to let their guard down so that the images ultimately reflect an integral part of her own personality: the hyperactive goofball. In her work for the Gap, Wrangler, Urban Outfitters and numerous magazines, Bird’s aesthetic is best described as “young, carefree, loving life and you’re so jealous,” and her influence radiates throughout popular photography.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lowdown | Cass Bird', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:10:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0f9f880-3fe6-5ca9-a69f-9aef539d64b0', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0f9f880-3fe6-5ca9-a69f-9aef539d64b0'}, {'abstract': 'Why the psychological argument against open marriage is a moral argument as well.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/newt-and-marianne-and-ted-and-alice/', 'snippet': 'Why the psychological argument against open marriage is a moral argument as well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since I’m cited repeatedly as the tribune of an unforgiving moral absolutism in Timothy Noah’s piece kinda-sorta defending Newt Gingrich’s request that his second wife tolerate his ongoing affair, it would be unsporting of me not to find something in his brief to dispute. So I’ll take on this point:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newt and Marianne and Ted and Alice', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:11:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/121cd141-0cf7-5f2d-ae40-6888c02df088', 'word_count': 358, 'uri': 'nyt://article/121cd141-0cf7-5f2d-ae40-6888c02df088'}, {'abstract': 'The protesters, who had challenged the rules keeping them from camping in the park, decided to drop the suit after barricades around the park were removed.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/occupy-wall-street-drops-suit-on-zuccotti-park/', 'snippet': 'The protesters, who had challenged the rules keeping them from camping in the park, decided to drop the suit after barricades around the park were removed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Occupy Wall Street protesters have decided to drop a lawsuit challenging the rules that prohibited them from camping inside Zuccotti Park, their lawyers said on Tuesday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupy Wall Street Drops Suit on Zuccotti Park', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stallman, Michael D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Zuccotti Park (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:13:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a82e5b7e-8669-5e62-a1b1-2447aea2b878', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a82e5b7e-8669-5e62-a1b1-2447aea2b878'}, {'abstract': 'A Canadian writer-director knows his film, “Monsieur Lazhar,” faces long odds, but then again, it already had to overcome the challenges of difficult subject matter (suicide) and a cast that include children who had never acted before.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/the-long-odds-for-a-small-canadian-film/', 'snippet': 'A Canadian writer-director knows his film, “Monsieur Lazhar,” faces long odds, but then again, it already had to overcome the challenges of difficult subject matter (suicide) and a cast that include children who had never acted before.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Canadian Philippe Falardeau, who wrote and directed “Monsieur Lazhar,” one of the five nominees for best foreign-language film, is at the Sundance Film Festival this week. Early on Tuesday morning, he was in a rented condo with his producers watching the Oscar nominations telecast, and when they heard the word “Canada,” he said, “we screamed so loud, with a mixture of joy and relief, that I didn’t even hear the title of my film.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Long Odds for a Small Canadian Film', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Falardeau, Philippe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Villeneuve, Denis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALGERIA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montreal (Quebec)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T21:14:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/752e0518-445a-5b37-9fbf-30c10c73ebeb', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/752e0518-445a-5b37-9fbf-30c10c73ebeb'}, {'abstract': 'The Futures Industry Association has set up a committee to explore new safeguards for customer money, including regularly reporting the whereabouts of money.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/after-mf-globals-demise-futures-industry-explores-tougher-rules/', 'snippet': 'The Futures Industry Association has set up a committee to explore new safeguards for customer money, including regularly reporting the whereabouts of money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is not every day that Wall Street volunteers for new regulation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'he