ype_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe062fe3-5774-5097-aefc-bebc589b989a', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe062fe3-5774-5097-aefc-bebc589b989a'}, {'abstract': 'Muslim groups attending the festival threatened violence if his image was shown.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/books/rushdies-video-talk-is-canceled-at-india-literature-festival.html', 'snippet': 'Muslim groups attending the festival threatened violence if his image was shown.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Salman Rushdie’s virtual participation at the Jaipur Literature Festival in India was canceled at the last minute on Tuesday after Muslim groups attending the festival threatened violence if his image was shown. After canceling an in-person appearance, Mr. Rushdie planned to address thousands of festival attendees by video conference on Tuesday. “There are a large number of people adverse to this link in and around this property, and they have threatened violence,” said Thakur Ram Pratap Singh of Diggi, the owner of Diggi Palace, the festival site. “This decision is necessary to protect everyone here,” he said. Mr. Rushdie’s plans to attend the festival, above, stirred controversy before it opened, and he said on the first day that he would not attend, citing a risk to his safety. Mr. Rushdie’s “Satanic Verses” was banned in India in 1988, and he has been the target of criticism and a fatwa from Muslim groups. His life was threatened several times before the festival, officials told the organizers. Mr. Rushdie criticized both the Indian government and Islamic leaders in an interview on Tuesday evening with the television channel NDTV. Mr. Rushdie said he had been cast as an enemy of Islam even though the real enemies were “the various extremist leaders and their followers, who behave like this because what they do is to strengthen the extremely negative image of Islam as an intolerant, repressive and violent culture.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rushdie’s Video Talk Is Canceled at India Literature Festival', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rushdie’s Video Talk Is Canceled at India Literature Festival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rushdie, Salman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-24T23:50:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikhila Gill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikhila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/6638bdf0-0541-5b01-a160-c116fbe4ac60', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6638bdf0-0541-5b01-a160-c116fbe4ac60'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Butler was the only Nashville session leader ever to win a Grammy Award for producer of the year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/arts/music/larry-butler-producer-for-kenny-rogers-dies-at-69.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Butler was the only Nashville session leader ever to win a Grammy Award for producer of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Larry Butler, the producer of some of Kenny Rogers’s biggest hits and the only Nashville session leader ever to win a Grammy Award for producer of the year, died on Friday at his home in Pensacola, Fla. He was 69.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Larry Butler, Producer for Kenny Rogers, Dies at 69', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Larry Butler, 69, Producer for Kenny Rogers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'ran