i this week rather than at the PGA tour stop at Torrey Pines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN DIEGO — Tiger Woods was asked in December why he was bypassing this week’s PGA Tour stop at Torrey Pines despite having won here 6 times in 12 appearances. It is also the site of his last major victory, the 2008 United States Open. Woods instead committed to play in a concurrent European Tour event in Abu Dhabi with less than half the total purse — $2.7 million to $6 million here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Abu Dhabi, Not Torrey Pines, Woods Acknowledges Lure of Appearance Fees', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T00:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d34893e6-7354-5e80-a574-90503ba66777', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d34893e6-7354-5e80-a574-90503ba66777'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankees avoided arbitration with catcher Russell Martin by agreeing to a one-year, $7.5 million deal.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/martin-agree-to-deal-with-yankees/', 'snippet': 'The Yankees avoided arbitration with catcher Russell Martin by agreeing to a one-year, $7.5 million deal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just hours after Jorge Posada, their catcher of the past, announced his retirement, the Yankees said Tuesday that they had agreed on a contract with Russell Martin, their catcher of the present.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Martin Agrees to Deal With Yankees', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Russell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T00:10:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0dfd4ef-7f71-5e99-a834-6cfcb87dd4ce', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0dfd4ef-7f71-5e99-a834-6cfcb87dd4ce'}, {'abstract': 'The Kennedy library on Tuesday released 45 hours of the final secret recordings the president made in the Oval Office in the last three months leading up to his assassination.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/j-f-k-s-final-words-in-office/', 'snippet': 'The Kennedy library on Tuesday released 45 hours of the final secret recordings the president made in the Oval Office in the last three months leading up to his assassination.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before President John F. Kennedy left the White House for his fateful trip to Dallas, he said he expected a “rough day” when he returned to Washington, according to recently released recordings of his final meetings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'J.F.K.’s Final Words in Office', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libraries and Librarians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Sixties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, John Fitzgerald', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T00:10:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lori Moore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2a731ee-289c-5250-9ff5-03186e7e8d81', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2a731ee-289c-5250-9ff5-03186e7e8d81'}, {'abstract': 'Gregory Mankiw, a Harvard professor and adviser to Mitt Romney, argues that Warren Buffett’s tax rate is higher than Mr. Buffett says it is.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/24/buffett-vs-mankiw-on-taxes/', 'snippet': 'Gregory Mankiw, a Harvard professor and adviser to Mitt Romney, argues that Warren Buffett’s tax rate is higher than Mr. Buffett says it is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By inviting Debbie Bosanek, Warren Buffett’s secretary, to sit in the first lady’s box at the State of the Union address, President Obama has signaled that he intends to talk about the tax rate on some investments. Mr. Obama and Mr. Buffett both argue that many investment managers pay too little tax, because the tax code treats their pay as an investment return — and thus taxes it at a much lower rate than ordinary income. Mr. Buffett has famously said that, as a result, his secretary pays a higher tax rate than he does.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Buffett vs. Mankiw on Taxes', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name':