e New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Homelessness to Sudden Science Celebrity', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garvey, Samantha', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intel Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T09:55:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ef60cdd-1d17-5e9a-8618-873e309419e8', 'word_count': 99, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ef60cdd-1d17-5e9a-8618-873e309419e8'}, {'abstract': 'For the first time since the Iranian nuclear threat emerged in the mid-1990s, at least some of Israel’s most powerful leaders believe that the conditions for an Israeli assault have been met.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/magazine/will-israel-attack-iran.html', 'snippet': 'For the first time since the Iranian nuclear threat emerged in the mid-1990s, at least some of Israel’s most powerful leaders believe that the conditions for an Israeli assault have been met.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the Sabbath evening approached on Jan. 13, Ehud Barak paced the wide living-room floor of his home high above a street in north Tel Aviv, its walls lined with thousands of books on subjects ranging from philosophy and poetry to military strategy. Barak, the Israeli defense minister, is the most decorated soldier in the country’s history and one of its most experienced and controversial politicians. He has served as chief of the general staff for the Israel Defense Forces, interior minister, foreign minister and prime minister. He now faces, along with Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and 12 other members of Israel’s inner security cabinet, the most important decision of his life — whether to launch a pre-emptive attack against Iran. We met in the late afternoon, and our conversation — the first of several over the next week — lasted for two and a half hours, long past nightfall. “This is not about some abstract concept,” Barak said as he gazed out at the lights of Tel Aviv, “but a genuine concern. The Iranians are, after all, a nation whose leaders have set themselves a strategic goal of wiping Israel off the map.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29iran_span/mag-29Iran-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29iran_span/mag-29Iran-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29iran_span/mag-29Iran-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29iran_span/mag-29Iran-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29iran_span/mag-29Iran-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will Israel Attack Iran?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Will Israel Attack Iran?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mossad', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arab-Israeli War (1967)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T09:58:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronen Bergman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bergman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7183ba1-d796-5216-9fc3-b3d469fc5538', 'word_count': 7643, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7183ba1-d796-5216-9fc3-b3d469fc5538'}, {'abstract': 'The fastest-growing middle class in history was supposed to benefit everyone. Here’s what went wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/magazine/come-on-china-buy-our-stuff.html', 'snippet': 'The fastest-growing middle class in history was supposed to benefit everyone. Here’s what went wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time I visited China, in 2005, an American businessman living there told me that the country was so huge and was changing so fast that everything you heard about it was true, and so was the opposite. That still seems to be the case. China is the fastest-growing consumer market in the world, and American companies have made billions there. At the same time, Chinese consumers aren’t spending nearly as much as American companies had hoped. China has simultaneously become the greatest boon and the biggest disappointment.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29economy1/29economy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29economy1/29economy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29economy1/29economy1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29economy1/29economy1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29economy1/29economy1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Come On, China, Buy Our Stuff!', 'kicker': 'It’s the Economy', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Come on, China, Buy Our Stuff!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renminbi (Currency)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T10:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Davidson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davidson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fe78e57-cee5-5723-ac44-693baab63661', 'word_count': 1080, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fe78e57-cee5-5723-ac44-693baab63661'}, {'abstract': 'In 2008, Greg Girard returned to an American military base he had visited in Japan in the mid-1970s. Not much, he found, had changed.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/home-away-from-home/', 'snippet': 'In 2008, Greg Girard returned to an American military base he had visited in Japan in the mid-1970s. Not much, he found, had changed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the first night in his Tokyo apartment, Greg Girard turned on the radio and heard an English-speaking D.J. “You’ve been listening to the Far East Network of the American Forces,” the voice said, segueing into a recording of “The Star-Spangled Banner.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Uncovering a Home Away From Home', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T10:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kerri MacDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kerri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f66676de-47d3-5ebe-a625-e7d68eb363fb', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f66676de-47d3-5ebe-a625-e7d68eb363fb'}, {'abstract': 'Scottish authorities set out on Wednesday to prepare the ground for a potentially divisive referendum on their land’s independence after three centuries of union with England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/world/europe/scots-launch-bid-for-vote-on-independence.html', 'snippet': 'Scottish authorities set out on Wednesday to prepare the ground for a potentially divisive referendum on their land’s independence after three centuries of union with England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EDINBURGH — Scotland’s first minister, Alex Salmond, took the first formal step on Wednesday toward an independence referendum that the government in Edinburgh hopes will secure a mandate from Scotland’s five million people for the country’s withdrawal from the United Kingdom within as little as five years.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/world/sub-sub-scotland1/sub-sub-scotland1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scots Begin Bid for Vote on Independence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scottish Leader Sets Timeline for Referendum on Independence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Salmond, Alex', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T10:08:11+0000', 'document_