e', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/business/Volt2/Volt2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/business/Volt2/Volt2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/26/business/Volt2/Volt2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Accusations of Delay in Disclosing Volt Fire', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Accusations of Delay In Disclosing Volt Fire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chevrolet Division of General Motors Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Batteries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54ff5aae-c477-5e19-8053-8da1dfa5aff1', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54ff5aae-c477-5e19-8053-8da1dfa5aff1'}, {'abstract': 'For less than the cost of café con leche and an order of churros, music lovers in Madrid can take in a top-notch concert at the Festival Internacional de Improvisación Hurta Cordel, an annual celebration of experimental and improvised music started in 1996.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/a-festival-of-experimental-music-in-madrid/', 'snippet': 'For less than the cost of café con leche and an order of churros, music lovers in Madrid can take in a top-notch concert at the Festival Internacional de Improvisación Hurta Cordel, an annual celebration of experimental and improvised music starte...', 'lead_paragraph': 'For less than the cost of café con leche and an order of churros, music lovers in Madrid can take in a top-notch concert at the Festival Internacional de Improvisación Hurta Cordel, an annual celebration of experimental and improvised music started in 1996. From Wednesday through Jan. 29, La Casa Encendida will charge four euros admission to see performances by legends like the avant-rock guitarist Fred Frith and free-jazz pianist Alexander von Schlippenbach alongside shows featuring up-and-coming artists from Spain and abroad. (Tickets are also available on www.entradas.com.)Moving across genres like free jazz, contemporary classical and experimental electronica, the musicians push past more mainstream definitions of music, each in his or her own way. The festival lineup includes a collaboration between the established British improvisers Michael Fischer, John Edwards and Mark Sanders, as well as a concert by 2|2code, an Australian-Spanish duo that treads the line between music and conceptual art. 2|2code creates ambient surround-sound mash-ups drawn from sources as varied as classic Hollywood films, amateur news footage, and speeches by George W. Bush.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Festival of Experimental Music in Madrid', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madrid (Spain)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T11:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Grace Duggan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Grace', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Duggan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48f545da-748b-5c8f-96c9-78f5b4de1dbd', 'word_count': 226, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48f545da-748b-5c8f-96c9-78f5b4de1dbd'}, {'abstract': 'The latest political news from today’s Times and a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/the-early-word-compare-and-contrast/', 'snippet': 'The latest political news from today’s Times and a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Compare and Contrast', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T11:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dff8c5e9-085a-51a4-bdfc-31351f60c035', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dff8c5e9-085a-51a4-bdfc-31351f60c035'}, {'abstract': 'The share of Americans receiving benefits from Medicaid, unemployment insurance or food stamps correlates largely with education levels, but participation among all groups increased between 2007 and 2010, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/who-receives-government-assistance/', 'snippet': 'The share of Americans receiving benefits from Medicaid, unemployment insurance or food stamps correlates largely with education levels, but participation among all groups increased between 2007 and 2010, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Casey B. Mulligan is an economics professor at the University of Chicago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Receives Government Assistance?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment Insurance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T11:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey Mulligan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mulligan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15a461bd-ee34-51af-a195-aee57ddf7a69', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15a461bd-ee34-51af-a195-aee57ddf7a69'}, {'abstract': 'Scouts and team observers flocked to the fence to watch Mike Singletary take the North linebacker group through individual drills.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/day-2-of-senior-bowl-defensive-notes/', 'snippet': 'Scouts and team observers flocked to the fence to watch Mike Singletary take the North linebacker group through individual drills.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jene Bramel of Footballguys.com covered the defensive practices at the Senior Bowl from Mobile, Ala, on Tuesday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Day 2 of Senior Bowl, Defensive Notes', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T11:19:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jene Bramel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bramel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c2d9abd-90cc-5f88-96bf-5fb7d4ba01c7', 'word_count': 1421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c2d9abd-90cc-5f88-96bf-5fb7d4ba01c7'}, {'abstract': 'A stream of messages that, like Gibreel Farishta, the author dreamed he had written.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/twitter-feed-of-a-lawbreaker/', 'snippet': 'A stream of messages that, like Gibreel Farishta, the author dreamed he had written.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Writer Amitava Kumar was advised to leave the recently held Jaipur Literature Festival after he had read, along with Hari Kunzru, extracts from Salman Rushdie’s “The Satanic Verses.” The novel has been banned in India since its publication in 1988 because the government held that the book would hurt the sentiments of Muslims. The following is a stream of messages that, like Gibreel Farishta in The Satanic Verses, Mr. Kumar dreamed he had written. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Twitter Feed of A Lawbreaker', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kumar, Amitava', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jaipur (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T11:36:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amitava Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amitava', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6841b19e-812e-5177-a50d-d4c030698640', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6841b19e-812e-5177-a50d-d4c030698640'}, {'abstract': 'Climate scientists get new allies in their fights for fairness in document probes and legal challenges.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/a-legal-defense-fund-for-climate-scientists/', 'snippet': 'Climate scientists get new allies in their fights for fairness in document probes and legal challenges.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For years, climate scientists have been assailed from many sides — through e-mail hacking, death threats, politician’s demands for documents, Freedom of Information requests (many having the strong smell of a fishing expedition). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Legal Defense Fund for Climate Scientists', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Information Act', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whistle-Blowers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuccinelli, Kenneth T II', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mann, Michael E', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruch, Jeff', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Suffolk County Community College', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Union of Concerned Scientists', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T11:46:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a26f14eb-9d40-5f3d-b47a-fe09fb471acb', 'word_count': 235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a26f14eb-9d40-5f3d-b47a-fe09fb471acb'}, {'abstract': 'What was thought to be an unpublished song by Irving Berlin turns out to have been composed in 1926 by the team of Lew Brown and Cliff Friend.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/sometimes-even-the-experts-can-be-fooled/', 'snippet': 'What was thought to be an unpublished song by Irving Berlin turns out to have been composed in 1926 by the team of Lew Brown and Cliff Friend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the realm of musical excavation, even experts can come to the wrong conclusions. In the Feb. 10 episode of “Michael Feinstein’s American Songbook,” the PBS series that follows Mr. Feinstein, the entertainer and musical preservationist, as he rummages through old musical archives, the discovery of an unpublished Irving Berlin song was to be announced. The song, titled “So Will I,” which was recently copyrighted by the Rodgers and Hammerstein Organization, which administers Berlin’s publishing but had no record of the song, has since been found to have been composed in 1926 by the team of Lew Brown and Cliff Friend and published by the Berlin company. A bouncy, wistful breakup song, it was recorded in 2005 by Ian Whitcomb on his album “Sentimentally Yours.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sometimes Even the Experts Can Be Fooled', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'American Songbook (Music Series)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berlin, Irving', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinstein, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T12:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89d5f96a-08d4-5497-abde-fe3cc3d422a2', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89d5f96a-08d4-5497-abde-fe3cc3d422a2'}, {'abstract': 'Increasing our cash balance has lowered my stress level from Code Red to Easy Blue.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/running-the-numbers-why-our-sales-increased/', 'snippet': 'Increasing our cash balance has lowered my stress level from Code Red to Easy Blue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My New Year’s resolution a year ago included a vow to do a better job of recording statistics on a wide variety of performance metrics. As it turned out, 2011 was the best year I’ve had in my 26 years of business life. I actually ended up with what seems like a reasonable amount of money in my pocket.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Running the Numbers: Why Our Sales Increased', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T12:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Downs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2decf41-1545-5bdf-b382-b1b4e8669d55', 'word_count': 1565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2decf41-1545-5bdf-b382-b1b4e8669d55'}, {'abstract': 'There are a handful of impressive receivers and a tight end beginning to turn heads, but quarterback play at Senior Bowl practices has been spotty.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/day-2-of-senior-bowl-offensive-notes/', 'snippet': 'There are a handful of impressive receivers and a tight end beginning to turn heads, but quarterback play at Senior Bowl practices has been spotty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cecil Lammey and Matt Waldman covered the offensive practices at the Senior Bowl on Tuesday from Mobile, Ala. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Day 2 of Senior Bowl, Offensive Notes', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'co