fertilization doesn’t mean more live births — but it does mean more risk.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/using-more-than-two-in-vitro-embryos-adds-to-risks/', 'snippet': 'Transferring more than two embryos during in vitro fertilization doesn’t mean more live births — but it does mean more risk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States is one of the few countries that still routinely transfer more than two embryos during an in vitro fertilization procedure. While that’s a practice that may be in keeping with our national “more is more” philosophy, and with the natural instinct of anxious parents to maximize the chances of success, The Times’s Vital Signs column just reported on a British study showing that transferring three or more embryos does not increase the number of live births. Instead, those larger transfers are associated with “a significantly higher risk of complications, including preterm birth and low birth weight.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Using More Than Two In Vitro Embryos Adds to Risks', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'BIRTHS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'In Vitro Fertilization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infertility', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T19:39:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34dbcecb-cbe1-5815-87b9-77b71c6400f7', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34dbcecb-cbe1-5815-87