victory over the Bobcats, but games against quality teams will be a better indication of where New York stands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before Tuesday night’s game against the Charlotte Bobcats, the Knicks had lost their previous six games. With the Knicks performing below expectations, this streak harkened to Micheal Ray Richardson’s famous quote “the ship be sinking.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Knicks Over Bobcats: That Was Supposed to Happen', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T20:07:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By MIKE KURYLO', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'KURYLO', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/707bf32c-0823-5a6a-a607-55f3bc5dd3cb', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/707bf32c-0823-5a6a-a607-55f3bc5dd3cb'}, {'abstract': 'WNET’s show on the city’s cultural scene, “SundayArts,” gets a new name and an expanded Web site.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/sundayarts-show-on-city-culture-moves-to-prime-time/', 'snippet': 'WNET’s show on the city’s cultural scene, “SundayArts,” gets a new name and an expanded Web site.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City’s arts and culture scene will get a high-profile spotlight and a more vibrant digital presence\xa0when WNET’s afternoon “SundayArts” show takes over a\xa0 prime-time slot, on Thursdays at 8 p.m. Along with the move, the half-hour program will take on a new name, NYC-ARTS, as its news, interviews and video archives are merged with the existing Web site and mobile platforms of NYC-ARTS.org, a\xa0 guide to arts and cultural events in the city. NYC-ARTS, developed with the help of city funds by the advocacy and research organization Alliance for the Arts, moved its operations to WNET last year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘SundayArts’ Show on City Culture Moves to Prime Time', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T20:09:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5fd3879-ada7-5939-b72a-c6fad90b12ac', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5fd3879-ada7-5939-b72a-c6fad90b12ac'}, {'abstract': 'The Pirate Bay, a notorious peer-to-peer file-sharing Web site that is a source of free copyrighted music and movies, said it was creating a new section for 3-D printer files.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/3-d-printing-copyright-issues-enter-peer-to-peet-networks/', 'snippet': 'The Pirate Bay, a notorious peer-to-peer file-sharing Web site that is a source of free copyrighted music and movies, said it was creating a new section for 3-D printer files.', 'lead_paragraph': ' As I wrote in a column in November, people will soon be able to download files of physical objects and print them out at home. Although being able to print out a new mug or toothbrush at home sounds magical, I said that there would surely be copyright problems that occur as a result of this technology’s going mainstream.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Uh-Oh, Peer-to-Peer Networks Discover 3-D Printing', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'na