2012-01-25T20:29:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/752c57b5-9dae-584d-b1c3-e7825205a0c0', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/752c57b5-9dae-584d-b1c3-e7825205a0c0'}, {'abstract': 'It’s gradually becoming clearer that for the very old and frail, hospitals are places to avoid whenever possible, and surgery can become a source of danger in itself.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/avoiding-surgery-in-the-elderly/', 'snippet': 'It’s gradually becoming clearer that for the very old and frail, hospitals are places to avoid whenever possible, and surgery can become a source of danger in itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It may take members of our parents’ generation (and our own) a long time to get over thinking of hospitals as refuges of safety and operating rooms as harbingers of better days ahead. But it’s gradually becoming clearer that for the very old and frail, and for nursing home residents in particular, hospitals are places to avoid whenever possible, and surgery can become a source of danger in itself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Avoiding Surgery in the Elderly', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T20:29:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e4d84de-7274-5e9c-b8c8-9a3918bfe916', 'word_count': 926, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e4d84de-7274-5e9c-b8c8-9a3918bfe916'}, {'abstract': 'Ten suggestions for looking at the campaign season through an arithmetical lens, using New York Times resources.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/the-united-states-of-numeracy-the-math-of-a-presidential-campaign/', 'snippet': 'Ten suggestions for looking at the campaign season through an arithmetical lens, using New York Times resources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A presidential race is a gift to American history and civics classes. But it’s also rife with learning opportunities for math students, too. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The United States of Numeracy: The Math of a Presidential Campaign', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T20:32:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Honner and Holly Epstein Ojalvo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Honner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Holly', 'middlename': 'Epstein', 'lastname': 'Ojalvo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a94b3b6-61d4-5625-b969-f05d58adb113', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a94b3b6-61d4-5625-b969-f05d58adb113'}, {'abstract': 'It’s entirely possible that, just a few weeks ago, you sang the fine old tune “Auld Lang Syne” to welcome the New Year. You might still puzzle over the lyrics, no matter how many times you’ve sung it, and you might not know that the man responsible for the song as we know it is Robert Burns, the Scottish poet who was born on this day in 1759.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/a-toast-to-the-bard-of-ayrshire/', 'snippet': 'It’s entirely possible that, just a few weeks ago, you sang the fine old tune “Auld Lang Syne” to welcome the New Year. You might still puzzle over the lyrics, no matter how many times you’ve sung it, and you might not know that the man responsibl...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s entirely possible that, just a few weeks ago, you sang the fine old tune “Auld Lang Syne” to welcome the New Year. You might still puzzle over the lyrics, no matter how many times you’ve sung it, and you might not know that the man responsible for the song as we know it is Robert Burns, the Scottish poet who was born on this day in 1759. “Robert Burns and ‘Auld Lang Syne,’” a lovely little exhibition at the Morgan Library and Museum, can take care of explaining the lyrics; seeing it would be a great way to kick off your celebration of Burns Night, the Scottish holiday marking the birth of the Scottish poet behind the song.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Toast to the Bard of Ayrshire', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scotch (Whiskey)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whiskey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burns, Robert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'RIVERDALE (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T20:37:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rosie Schaap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rosie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1b0ccba-3759-5d70-87b7-2759e07d480d', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1b0ccba-3759-5d70-87b7-2759e07d480d'}, {'abstract': 'At the World Economic Forum, Chancellor Angela Merkel of Germany said Europe had made more progress on the sovereign debt crisis than foreign investors acknowledged.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/business/global/merkel-pleads-for-patience-to-let-europe-solve-its-problems.html', 'snippet': 'At the World Economic Forum, Chancellor Angela Merkel of Germany said Europe had made more progress on the sovereign debt crisis than foreign investors acknowledged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — Chancellor Angela Merkel of Germany said Wednesday that Europe had made more progress in dealing with the sovereign debt crisis than foreign investors acknowledged, as she toned down her usual calls for budgetary austerity.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/business/DavosMerkel2/DavosMerkel2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/26/business/DavosMerkel2/DavosMerkel2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/business/DavosMerkel