}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T22:50:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0cc4922-bfc9-5a77-a4bb-851bf742f912', 'word_count': 235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0cc4922-bfc9-5a77-a4bb-851bf742f912'}, {'abstract': 'Ingrid Michaelson, who has risen to pop semistardom by licensing music to commercials and television shows, takes a blunt approach in her new album.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/arts/music/human-again-ingrid-michaelson-at-joes-pub.html', 'snippet': 'Ingrid Michaelson, who has risen to pop semistardom by licensing music to commercials and television shows, takes a blunt approach in her new album.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hours before her album-release show at Joe’s Pub on Tuesday night, Ingrid Michaelson was in the back of an ambulance, speeding toward the emergency room. The incident — a false alarm, as she described it onstage — had involved heart palpitations, a mounting panic and some awkward embarrassment. But what if her “near-death experience,” as she wryly put it, had yielded a musical epiphany? “What if I came up here and started doing, like, freestyle jazz?” she asked, before delivering an impromptu-sounding gag song with her guitar. Then, laughing: “\xa0‘Grey’s Anatomy’ will not like that one.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/arts/sub-ingrid/sub-ingrid-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 833, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/arts/sub-ingrid/sub-ingrid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/26/arts/sub-ingrid/sub-ingrid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Catchy Voice of a Hundred TV Moments Shows a Blunter Edge', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Catchy Voice of a Hundred TV Moments Shows a Blunter Edge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michaelson, Ingrid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-25T22:52:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/38bb86b7-5767-5d8f-8c4a-d60ef548f71c', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38bb86b7-5767-5d8f-8c4a-d60ef548f71c'}, {'abstract': 'Kickstarter is a site where creative people solicit start-up money for various projects. And people actually pledge money to help the projects in return for, maybe, a T-shirt or a CD.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/technology/personaltech/financing-the-stuff-of-dreams-through-kickstarter-state-of-the-art.html', 'snippet': 'Kickstarter is a site where creative people solicit start-up money for various projects. And people actually pledge money to help the projects in return for, maybe, a T-shirt or a CD.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ll admit it: I’m sometimes smugly entertained by the older generation’s reaction to the fads of the Web. “Facebook? Why on earth would I want other people to know about my private life?” “Twitter? Who’s got the time for