he stairs to his fourth-floor courtroom past his 100th birthday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Judge Wesley E. Brown, whose work ethic and a hale constitution kept him climbing the stairs to his fourth-floor courtroom past his 100th birthday, making him the oldest active federal judge in the nation’s history, died on Monday in Wichita, Kan. He was 104.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/us/26brown1/26brown1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/26/us/26brown1/26brown1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/us/26brown1/26brown1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/us/26brown1/26brown1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/26/us/26brown1/26brown1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wesley E. Brown, Oldest Judge in Nation’s History, Dies at 104', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wesley E. Brown, 104, Oldest Active Federal Judge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Judges', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wichita (Kan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Wesley E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T01:37:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. G. Sulzberger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Sulzberger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d4f2495-6d3f-5f33-8bc9-4fe5c9c5da65', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d4f2495-6d3f-5f33-8bc9-4fe5c9c5da65'}, {'abstract': 'An austere base, Camp Lemonnier, is part of an archipelago of outposts in high-risk environments that can preserve a military presence at relatively low cost.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/world/africa/camp-lemonier-in-djibouti-played-crucial-role-in-somalia-rescue.html', 'snippet': 'An austere base, Camp Lemonnier, is part of an archipelago of outposts in high-risk environments that can preserve a military presence at relatively low cost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — An austere Pentagon outpost in the hardscrabble desert on the Horn of Africa proved serendipitously ideal as a launching pad for Tuesday’s commando raid that freed two aid workers held in Somalia. The use of the base, Camp Lemonnier in neighboring Djibouti, is also a signpost to the future, as the military focuses on “economy of force” missions that can preserve an American military presence and protect national security interests at relatively low cost.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/world/djibouti/djibouti-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/26/world/djibouti/djibouti-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/world/djibouti/djibouti-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/world/djibouti/djibouti-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/26/world/djibouti/djibouti-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Djibouti Outpost Behind Somalia Rescue Is Part of New Defense Strategy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Djibouti Outpost Behind Somalia Rescue Is Part of New Defense Strategy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Somalia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Djibouti', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hostages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T01:39:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/863805d5-04ae-5429-b594-264a69cc5740', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/863805d5-04ae-5429-b594-264a69cc5740'}, {'abstract': 'Proven educational strategies to increase high school completion provide returns to the taxpayer that are two to four times their cost.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/opinion/the-true-cost-of-high-school-dropouts.html', 'snippet': 'Proven educational strategies to increase high school completion provide returns to the taxpayer that are two to four times their cost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONLY 21 states require students to attend high school until they graduate or turn 18. The proposal President Obama announced on Tuesday night in his State of the Union address — to make such attendance compulsory in every state — is a step in the right direction, but it would not go far enough to reduce a dropout rate that imposes a heavy cost on the entire economy, not just on those who fail to obtain a diploma.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/opinion/0126OPEDmunday/0126OPEDmunday-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/opinion/0126OPEDmunday/0126OPEDmunday-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/26/opinion/0126OPEDmunday/0126OPEDmunday-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The True Cost of High School Dropouts', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The True Cost Of High School Dropouts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Pre-School)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dropouts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T01:42:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry M. Levin and Cecilia E. Rouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f430dc5b-c944-5dad-8eec-f7640900da4a', 'word_count': 821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f430dc5b-c944-5dad-8eec-f7640900da4a'}, {'abstract': 'Eli and Peyton Manning, elite quarterbacks born less than five years apart, act close and loving and are barely competitive, bucking the conventional wisdom of birth order.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/sports/football/for-peyton-and-eli-manning-family-dynamics-are-a-boost.html', 'snippet': 'Eli and Peyton Manning, elite quarterbacks born less than five years apart, act close and loving and are barely competitive, bucking the conventional wisdom of birth order.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So you haven’t heard enough about Peyton and Eli Manning — their quarterbacking careers, their Super Bowl Most Valuable Player awards and their skills as Madison Avenue pitchmen?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/sports/birth/birth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 929, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/sports/birth/birth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/26/sports/birth/birth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Family Ties a Plus for Mannings', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Family Ties a Plus for Mannings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Eli', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Peyton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Cooper', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T01:44:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd105ae3-c7cc-51f1-886f-4975e4541e0c', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd105ae3-c7cc-51f1-886f-4975e4541e0c'}, {'abstract': 'Political vendettas have put a heroic Spanish jurist, Baltasar Garzón, on trial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/opinion/in-spain-baltasar-garzon-on-trial.html', 'snippet': 'Political vendettas have put a heroic Spanish jurist, Baltasar Garzón, on trial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN October 1998, British police officers arrested the Chilean general Augusto Pinochet while he was recuperating from back surgery at a London hospital. They were acting on an international warrant issued by the Spanish judge Baltasar Garzón seeking General Pinochet’s extradition to stand trial in Spain on charges of torture and murder. After a 17-month legal battle, General Pinochet was released on medical grounds, but Judge Garzón’s warrant paved the way for stripping the former dictator of immunity and prosecuting him in Chile. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Judge in the Dock', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Judge In the Dock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinochet Ugarte, Augusto', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garzon, Baltasar', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franco, Francisco', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T01:50:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Kaufman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaufman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6386b904-0bda-51b8-b2f0-3a0e261cfe89', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6386b904-0bda-51b8-b2f0-3a0e261cfe89'}, {'abstract': 'Evangelical concerns about Mitt Romney’s faith ultimately say more about the insecurities of the establishment denominations than about Mormonism itself.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/why-evangelicals-dont-like-mormons/', 'snippet': 'Evangelical concerns about Mitt Romney’s faith ultimately say more about the insecurities of the establishment denominations than about Mormonism itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'According to a CNN exit poll of South Carolina Republican primary voters, Newt Gingrich, a thrice-married Catholic, won twice as much support from evangelical Protestants as Mitt Romney, a Protestant. And among voters for whom religion meant “a great deal,” 46 percent voted for Mr. Gingrich and only 10 percent for Mr. Romney.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Evangelicals Don’t Like Mormons', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evangelical Movement', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mormons (Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T01:59:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David S. Reynolds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Reynolds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09f399fa-ce8f-5168-bf0f-144b437494df', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09f399fa-ce8f-5168-bf0f-144b437494df'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court’s decision last week giving an Alabama death-row inmate a second chance presents a puzzle for anyone who witnessed the sharp right turn that the Roberts court took during the 2006-2007 term.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/25/second-chances/', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court’s decision last week giving an Alabama death-row inmate a second chance presents a puzzle for anyone who witnessed the sharp right turn that the Roberts court took during the 2006-2007 term.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Supreme Court’s decision last week giving an Alabama death-row inmate who was abandoned by his lawyers a second chance at an appeal has been justly celebrated. The 7-to-2 decision in Maples v. Thomas was a welcome display of compassion by a substantial majority of the Roberts court – for which compassion (dare I say empathy?) is not a descriptor that often springs to mind.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Second Chances', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T02:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/207404ce-9270-50df-bdd9-71aa66d506d8', 'word_count': 1351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/207404ce-9270-50df-bdd9-71aa66d506d8'}, {'abstract': 'Toronto’s Phil Kessel was the final selection when the All-Stars picked sides last year. At least he got a free car out of the embarrassment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/sports/hockey/being-last-nhl-star-picked-is-a-dubious-honor.html', 'snippet': 'Toronto’s Phil Kessel was the final selection when the All-Stars picked sides last year. At least he got a free car out of the embarrassment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Being named an All-Star is an honor for professional athletes, but it could turn out to be an embarrassment for one N.H.L. player.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When N.H.L.’s All-Star Captains Choose Sides, Someone Has to Be Last', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'W