ll the breach — deliciously.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/davos-grand-cru-2012/', 'snippet': 'It no longer looks right to sip the finest wines as the world economy goes down the drain, so tastings have not been on the official schedule for years. Fortunately, the private sector stepped in to fill the breach — deliciously.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — If you gather enough millionaires and billionaires in a single place, the talk during the evening hours inevitably turns to wine. The World Economic Forum used to cater to them by conducting tastings, led by well-known critics, as part of its official schedule of events in Davos. Then the global financial crisis struck, and it no longer looked right to sip grands crus while the economy went down the drain. Wine tastings were taken off the schedule.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Davos Grand Cru 2012', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T11:16:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Pfanner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfanner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a1cf8d3-7071-5bba-b596-3ea66ec2ef45', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a1cf8d3-7071-5bba-b596-3ea66ec2ef45'}, {'abstract': 'Six recipes based on a given root, with room for improvisation. Bake beets instead of celeriac; make creamy potato soup, braise carrots, braise parsnips and so on.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/01/29/magazine/recipes-root-vegetables.html', 'snippet': 'Six recipes based on a given root, with room for improvisation. Bake beets instead of celeriac; make creamy potato soup, braise carrots, braise parsnips and so on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six recipes based on a given root, with room for improvisation. Bake beets instead of celeriac; make creamy potato soup, braise carrots, braise parsnips and so on.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Root Vegetable Recipes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegetables', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Potatoes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sweet Potatoes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T11:50:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/acded126-3bc7-52b5-b8ff-ed655a772d9d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/acded126-3bc7-52b5-b8ff-ed655a772d9d'}, {'abstract': 'Reasons to root for the underdogs of vegetables.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/magazine/root-vegetables.html', 'snippet': 'Reasons to root for the underdogs of vegetables.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a time when the only root vegetables anyone paid attention to were carrots and potatoes. (I know potatoes are tubers. Whatever.) Turnips were déclassé, celeriac unheard of, beets a pain to clean. The perception has changed, in part because it was all wrong; in part because if you’re going to eat seasonal and local, you are going to eat roots in winter, even if you live in California; and in part because roasted root vegetables, which most of us have discovered only recently, are, like, the greatest thing ever, and even the company cafeteria can’t ruin them.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29eat_span/mag-29Eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 462, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29eat_span/mag-29Eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 462}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29eat_span/mag-29Eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29eat_span/mag-29Eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29eat_span/mag-29Eat-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From Underground', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Notes From Underground', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegetables', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Potatoes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carrots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beets', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T11:50:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittm