1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74402b37-fe53-5e6e-ab19-45ce48cd507d', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74402b37-fe53-5e6e-ab19-45ce48cd507d'}, {'abstract': 'Zynga makes money by selling virtual goods to a handful of people playing its games. How exactly does the process work? The social gaming company, whether for competitive or other reasons, has tried mightily to keep things obscure.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/at-zynga-keeping-it-virtual-and-vague/', 'snippet': 'Zynga makes money by selling virtual goods to a handful of people playing its games. How exactly does the process work? The social gaming company, whether for competitive or other reasons, has tried mightily to keep things obscure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even for an Internet company, Zynga has an unusual way of making money. Tens of millions of people play its games, like FarmVille and Mafia Wars, but only a relative handful buy the virtual goods that make the company viable. In the months running up to Zynga’s public offering in December, the Securities and Exchange Commission tried to get some clarity on this new-fangled business model. Zynga, whether for competitive or other reasons, tried mightily to keep things obscure.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/business/26bits-zynga-pic/26bits-zynga-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Zynga, Keeping It Virtual and Vague', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zynga Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T13:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_d