ks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T13:51:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95387612-82bf-53e1-9197-19e840828062', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95387612-82bf-53e1-9197-19e840828062'}, {'abstract': 'Also, American attitudes concerning where Apple’s products are made, and does the Internet work better without Megaupload?', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/the-breakfast-meeting-netflix-bounces-back-and-giving-debates-a-tv-moment/', 'snippet': 'Also, American attitudes concerning where Apple’s products are made, and does the Internet work better without Megaupload?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Netflix reported fourth-quarter numbers that showed it had bounced back after bungling – and later abandoning – a plan to spin off its DVD-by-mail service, Brian Stelter reported. The company had lost about 800,000 subscribers in the United States in the third quarter, leveling off at 23.8 million; at the end of the fourth quarter, it had 24.4 million, somewhat more than it had expected to have.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Netflix Bounces Back, and Giving Debates a ‘TV Moment’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Debates (Political)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DVD (Digital Versatile Disc)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject',