cle/b82fc61e-b4d6-5fa1-acae-b5af853065eb', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b82fc61e-b4d6-5fa1-acae-b5af853065eb'}, {'abstract': 'This month the esteemed designer estate and the American brand Coach are launching a collaborative collection that has all the splash, for a lot less cash.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/the-get-tony-duquette-for-coach-rings/', 'snippet': 'This month the esteemed designer estate and the American brand Coach are launching a collaborative collection that has all the splash, for a lot less cash.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oftentimes the first thing that Tony Duquette jewelry brings to mind are enormous gems with — unsurprisingly — matching price tags. But this month the estate of the esteemed designer and the American brand Coach are introducing a collaborative collection that has all the splash, for a lot less cash. There are 25 pieces in all, starting at a modest $48 for a bejeweled ladybug stickpin, and the most identifiably Duquette-esque stunners are like this one, the Empire Stone ($650): a malachite cocktail ring set in 18-karat gold-plated silver. Knock, knock.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Get | Tony Duquette for Coach Rings', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85488d07-3135-5c83-be07-ee889f630c16', 'word_count': 104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85488d07-3135-5c83-be07-ee889f630c16'}, {'abstract': 'A start-up in Silicon Valley is hoping to streamline and simplify the process of seeing a doctor with a suite of applications aimed at both physicians and patients. It is gaining customers, and raising money.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/drchrono-raises-2-8-million-to-digitize-doctors-visits/', 'snippet': 'A start-up in Silicon Valley is hoping to streamline and simplify the process of seeing a doctor with a suite of applications aimed at both physicians and patients. It is gaining customers, and raising money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone who has ever spent any amount of time shuttling between doctors’ offices for medical treatment knows how difficult it is to stay on top of all the diagnoses, treatments, recommendations and prescriptions between visits.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/technology/25bits-drchrono-pic/25bits-drchrono-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/25/technology/25bits-drchrono-pic/25bits-drchrono-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/technology/25bits-drchrono-pic/25bits-drchrono-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'DrChrono Raises $2.8 Million to Digitize Doctor’s Visits', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Health Records', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/862ef85e-6544-5f57-9e6b-f7a164685181', 'word_count': 420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/862ef85e-6544-5f57-9e6b-f7a164685181'}, {'abstract': 'A new survey by Forrester Research provides an in-depth profile of the typical user of Apple products inside the workplace. The study, which Forrester plans to release Thursday, says that 43 percent of people making over $150,000 a year said they use an iPhone, iPad or Mac for work, making them far more likely than any other group to use an Apple product.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/who-has-apples-at-work-more-likely-its-the-bosses/', 'snippet': 'A new survey by Forrester Research provides an in-depth profile of the typical user of Apple products inside the workplace. The study, which Forrester plans to release Thursday, says that 43 percent of people making over $150,000 a year said they ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Try McKaskle, the chief technology officer of Symantec Services Group, with an iPad.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Has Apples at Work? In Many Cases, It’s the Bosses', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:01:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Wingfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wingfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d71cc366-b61f-5a99-a018-676f6dd54735', 'word_count': 535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d71cc366-b61f-5a99-a018-676f6dd54735'}, {'abstract': 'After crashing a stolen vehicle into a pole in Cypress Hills, Brooklyn, the suspect shot at an off-duty lieutenant, who shot back, the police said.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/carjacking-suspect-fatally-shot-by-police/', 'snippet': 'After crashing a stolen vehicle into a pole in Cypress Hills, Brooklyn, the suspect shot at an off-duty lieutenant, who shot back, the police said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 3:39 p.m. | A 22-year-old carjacking suspect was fatally shot by an off-duty police lieutenant after a predawn car crash in Cypress Hills, Brooklyn, on Thursday, the police said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Carjacking Suspect Fatally Shot by Police', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carjacking', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cypress Hills (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:03:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman and Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f87e5fa-de5b-51f0-a022-0d44646c7810', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f87e5fa-de5b-51f0-a022-0d44646c7810'}, {'abstract': 'With concern lingering about the future of the euro zone, Prime Minister David Cameron of Britain donned his salesman’s cap on Thursday and called on executives and bankers gathered in Davos, Switzerland, to invest in Britain instead.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/cameron-sells-britain-to-investors-sour-on-euro-zone/', 'snippet': 'With concern lingering about the future of the euro zone, Prime Minister David Cameron of Britain donned his salesman’s cap on Thursday and called on executives and bankers gathered in Davos, Switzerland, to invest in Britain instead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVOS, Switzerland — With concern lingering about the future of the euro zone, Prime Minister David Cameron of Britain donned his salesman’s cap on Thursday and delivered a full-throated pitch to the throngs of executives and bankers gathered here: invest in Britain instead.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cameron Pitches Britain to Investors Sour on Euro Zone', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:06:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Alderman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/023ed2bd-5926-599a-9bc9-c6911143d0e1', 'word_count': 795, 'uri': 'nyt://article/023ed2bd-5926-599a-9bc9-c6911143d0e1'}, {'abstract': 'Micromet designs cancer treatments that target blood and solid tumor cancers, including leukemia and non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/amgen-to-buy-micromet-for-1-16-billion/', 'snippet': 'Micromet designs cancer treatments that target blood and solid tumor cancers, including leukemia and non-Hodgkin’s lymphomas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amgen has agreed to buy Micromet, a biotechnology company that develops cancer drugs, for $1.16 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amgen to Buy Micromet for $1.16 Billion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biotechnology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMGEN INC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Moelis & Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:21:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30ad0edb-f9e8-5c42-9fb0-8c1de95e1f91', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30ad0edb-f9e8-5c42-9fb0-8c1de95e1f91'}, {'abstract': 'A wide range of commentators are taking a close look at Nigeria’s ethnic strife, and they are worried.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/ethnic-violence-in-nigeria/', 'snippet': 'A wide range of commentators are taking a close look at Nigeria’s ethnic strife, and they are worried.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since the radical Islamic sect Boko Haram carried out its deadliest attack yet last Friday in the northern Nigerian city of Kano, killing at least 185 people, opinion writers have been mulling over what the country should do about the group that has killed at least 935 people since 2009.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nigeria’s Dangerous Slide', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War and Guerrilla Warfare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jonathan, Goodluck', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boko Haram', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nigeria', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:24:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Politi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Politi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ea98b1f-62a1-5b04-b5a2-d01a3d7ad854', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ea98b1f-62a1-5b04-b5a2-d01a3d7ad854'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Brantley sees “The Mousetrap” in London for the first time to see how the longest-running play in modern history is holding up.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/london-theater-journal-comfortably-mousetrapped/', 'snippet': 'Ben Brantley sees “The Mousetrap” in London for the first time to see how the longest-running play in modern history is holding up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — It was a dark and stormy afternoon when I ventured into the old building with the twisting staircases, on one of those London side streets that always seems to be in different places when you look for them. Oh, I knew what I was in for: screams, gunshots, a whistling psychopath, fraught minutes in the dark and rigid postures of fear. But I hadn’t come to St. Martin’s Theater to be frightened or even stimulated. I was there for comfort.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Theater Journal: Comfortably Mousetrapped', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Mousetrap, The (Play)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Agatha', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:30:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0a9d139-a7cc-5426-8801-b0399aae7a0b', 'word_count': 1298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0a9d139-a7cc-5426-8801-b0399aae7a0b'}, {'abstract': 'After a wave of consolidations, airlines cut routes and seats but raised prices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/business/merger-costs-weigh-on-united-continental.html', 'snippet': 'After a wave of consolidations, airlines cut routes and seats but raised prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite everything that has been working against them, from a dismal global economy to rising fuel prices, the nation’s top airlines — United Continental Holdings, Delta Air Lines, US Airways, and Southwest Airlines — all turned a profit last year. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/business/Air/Air-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 236, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/27/business/Air/Air-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 236}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/business/Air/Air-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/business/Air/Air-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/27/business/Air/Air-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Leaner Operations Give U.S. Airlines a Profitable Year', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leaner Operations Give U.S. Airlines a Profitable Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southwest Airlines Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Continental Airlines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Airlines', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Continental Holdings Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'US Airways Group Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Delta Air Lines, Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:38:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jad Mouawad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mouawad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea1424a0-ff77-5ad0-a8a1-0d37ae5565be', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea1424a0-ff77-5ad0-a8a1-0d37ae5565be'}, {'abstract': 'The City Council and the mayor disagree over whether the Sanitation Department should stop issuing stickers to drivers who violate alternate-side parking rules.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/outrage-over-those-stickers-of-shame/', 'snippet': 'The City Council and the mayor disagree over whether the Sanitation Department should stop issuing stickers to drivers who violate alternate-side parking rules.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Does public shame not have a proper role in New York civic life? A dispute between the city’s lawmakers and the mayor boils down to that question.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Deter Illegal Parking, Why Not a Little Shame?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sanitation Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:49:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58d363c6-458a-5f2a-8311-228ae7ead8b4', 'word_count': 786, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58d363c6-458a-5f2a-8311-228ae7ead8b4'}, {'abstract': 'The latest installment of the iEconomy series takes an inside look at the working conditions inside Chinese factories that make electronics. The authors say that inside Apple, the leader in the industry, there is an unresolved tension: executives want to improve conditions within factories, but that dedication falters when it conflicts with crucial supplier relationships or the fast delivery of new products.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/daily-report-chinese-factories-and-the-ieconomy/', 'snippet': 'The latest installment of the iEconomy series takes an inside look at the working conditions inside Chinese factories that make electronics. The authors say that inside Apple, the leader in the industry, there is an unresolved tension: executives ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the latest installment of the iEconomy series in The New York Times, Charles Duhigg and David Barboza report on the manufacturing of consumer electronics in China. They report that the workers assembling iPhones, iPads and other devices often labor in harsh conditions, according to employees inside those plants, worker advocates and documents published by companies themselves. Problems are as varied as onerous work environments and serious — sometimes deadly — safety problems:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Chinese Factories and the iEconomy', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T14:54:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73ab1848-db7b-56f9-a049-f92331168a33', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73ab1848-db7b-56f9-a049-f92331168a33'}, {'abstract': 'Reporters Without Borders ranked the United States at 47 in its annual Press Freedom Index. It put Hungary at 40. Does that make sense?', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/usa-usa-were-no-47/', 'snippet': 'Reporters Without Borders ranked the United States at 47 in its annual Press Freedom Index. It put Hungary at 40. Does that make sense?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I like Reporters Without Borders; the Paris-based organization does important work. But its annual Press Freedom Index, which ranks countries from 1 to 179, gets a little ridiculous. Like US News & World Report with colleges, they freshen the list each year based on new developments, and—again like US News—they sometimes end up with a pecking-order that doesn’t quite mesh with reality.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'USA, USA, We’re No. 47!', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Reporters Without Borders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T15:01:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e04acfe9-7890-5071-a6f5-e87e67fa7076', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e04acfe9-7890-5071-a6f5-e87e67fa7076'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/what-were-reading-359/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'National Restaurant Association: There’s no escaping the pizza and chicken wings now. Some 48 million Americans are expected to order takeout or restaurant-delivery food on Super Bowl Sunday, Feb. 5. And 12 million will visit a restaurant or bar — primarily families with children, or adults between the ages of 18 and 34. — Glenn CollinsThe Washington Post: “Everything has to be perfect or it is not accepted.” A sushi master laments the decline of traditional Japanese sushi in the United States. Why? Cultural issues, for one thing, along with difficulties in training and immigration. — Eric Asimov ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T15:04:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd390190-d54f-5697-a799-0c64e73daf92', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd390190-d54f-5697-a799-0c64e73daf92'}, {'abstract': 'In a list of the biggest losers of this Super Bowl matchup, Rex Ryan may be No. 1.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/tough-super-bowl-for-ryan-and-jets/', 'snippet': 'In a list of the biggest losers of this Super Bowl matchup, Rex Ryan may be No. 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Silver of Yahoo Sports has listed the five biggest losers heading into the Super Bowl. Read his reasons for each pick.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tough Super Bowl for Ryan and Jets', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T15:05:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95ae86b1-f1c6-53bb-893f-26f7eae68d8d', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95ae86b1-f1c6-53bb-893f-26f7eae68d8d'}, {'abstract': 'The company, trying to move beyond television to attract high-speed Internet customers, also reported that revenue increased 4 percent in the fourth quarter of 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/time-warner-cables-net-profit-increases-by-44/', 'snippet': 'The company, trying to move beyond television to attract high-speed Internet customers, also reported that revenue increased 4 percent in the fourth quarter of 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Time Warner Cable, in the process of repositioning itself beyond cable television to attract high-speed Internet users, said Thursday that its net profit increased 44 percent in the fourth quarter that ended Dec. 31, to $564 million.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Time Warner Cable’s Net Profit Increases by 44%', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Warner Cable Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T15:06:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f923a24c-1830-5832-b289-58b37d7be6ca', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f923a24c-1830-5832-b289-58b37d7be6ca'}, {'abstract': 'This week, we pair Maxine Kumin’s “Which One” with a Science Times essay by Natalie Angier, “The Creature Connection.”', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/poetry-pairing-which-one/', 'snippet': 'This week, we pair Maxine Kumin’s “Which One” with a Science Times essay by Natalie Angier, “The Creature Connection.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s Poetry Pairing matches Maxine Kumin’s “Which One” with a Science Times essay by Natalie Angier, “The Creature Connection.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Pairing | ‘Which One’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T15:14:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97b98c2a-393d-5186-8ef5-7a8f20b5bd71', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97b98c2a-393d-5186-8ef5-7a8f20b5bd71'}, {'abstract': 'Eat Your World, a new Web site, helps travelers find the local foods that define a place.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/world-roaming-web-site-tells-how-to-eat-like-the-locals/', 'snippet': 'Eat Your World, a new Web site, helps travelers find the local foods that define a place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eating locally doesn’t necessarily mean eating close to home: That’s what one New York couple learned from years of roaming the globe. Now they’ve created Eat Your World, a Web site that steers fellow travelers to indigenous food in scores of destinations, from Detroit to Bali.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'World-Roaming Web Site Tells How to Eat Like the Locals', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T15:21:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Farrell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f131f97-4ebc-59ed-a781-bcc5353af6ae', 'word_count': 532, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f131f97-4ebc-59ed-a781-bcc5353af6ae'}, {'abstract': 'Melena Ryzik talks with Jeff Cronenweth, the Oscar-nominated cinematographer of “The Girl With the Dragon Tattoo.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/movies/awardsseason/100000001313070/through-a-glass-darkly.html', 'snippet': 'Melena Ryzik talks with Jeff Cronenweth, the Oscar-nominated cinematographer of “The Girl With th