, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T16:45:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison S. Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92d11401-446d-5e34-a4a3-9b16fc969c9d', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92d11401-446d-5e34-a4a3-9b16fc969c9d'}, {'abstract': 'Cameron Brown, Carnegie-Mellon ’95, and e-commerce director at The New York Times, shares insights into news and technology with his alma mater.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/4771/', 'snippet': 'Cameron Brown, Carnegie-Mellon ’95, and e-commerce director at The New York Times, shares insights into news and technology with his alma mater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cameron Brown, Carnegie-Mellon ’95, and e-commerce director at The New York Times, shares insights into news and technology with his alma mater: Extra, Extra dot com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T16:46:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By BRAD STENGER', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'STENGER', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10591164-5c98-506d-aeca-ba464cef4edd', 'word_count': 26, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10591164-5c98-506d-aeca-ba464cef4edd'}, {'abstract': 'In a video, the cinematographer of “The Girl With the Dragon Tattoo” talks about his style and growing up with his cinematographer father, whose credits include “Blade Runner.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/a-cinematographers-fincher-factor/', 'snippet': 'In a video, the cinematographer of “The Girl With the Dragon Tattoo” talks about his style and growing up with his cinematographer father, whose credits include “Blade Runner.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can cinematography be in your DNA? It is for Jeff Cronenweth, son of the cinematographer Jordan Cronenweth. Jeff, Oscar-nominated this week for his work on David Fincher’s “Girl With the Dragon Tattoo” (and last year for “The Social Network”), grew up on sets assisting his father, a veteran cinematographer who worked on movies ranging from “Peggy Sue Got Married” to “Blade Runner” and who died in 1996. It was while Jeff was helping his father that he was introduced to Mr. Fincher on the set of a music video. Mr. Fincher eventually hired him as the cinematographer of “Fight Club,” and from then they developed their signature moody, dimly lit style – not too different from Jordan Cronenweth’s – together. Here, Jeff Cronenweth talks about his father’s influence, the “Fincher factor” and whether he’ll ever shoot something light and fluffy again.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Cinematographer’s ‘Fincher Factor’', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T16:56:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07e10c66-4bf8-5323-b845-59576aeab72d', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07e10c66-4bf8-5323-b845-59576aeab72d'}, {'abstract': '2011 was a tough year for Prime Minister David Cameron of Britain — the London riots, allegations of cronyism around the phone hacking scandal, and widespread predictions of his early political demise — but 2012 has been better and at Davos he seems uncharacteristically happy, even lighthearted.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/david-camerons-excellent-adventure/', 'snippet': '2011 was a tough year for Prime Minister David Cameron of Britain — the London riots, allegations of cronyism around the phone hacking scandal, and widespread predictions of his early political demise — but 2012 has been better and at Davos he see...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite David Cameron’s downbeat talk about the euro zone, the British prime minister appears to be enjoying himself on the Continent. Shortly after delivering a speech at the World Economic Forum in Davos, Mr. Cameron dropped in on a private afternoon tea in a cafe on the Davos main street, the Promenade. There, the mayor of London, Boris Johnson, was detailing a new advertising campaign to promote investment in Britain, in conjunction with the 2012 London Olympics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/26/blogs/cameron-davos/cameron-davos-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Cameron’s Excellent Adventure', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jagger, Mick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Economic Forum', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Davos (Switzerland)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Pfanner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfanner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0cb968e-610e-57bc-b14c-b176e5b82889', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0cb968e-610e-57bc-b14c-b176e5b82889'}, {'abstract': 'For its 2012-13 season Carnegie Hall will return to its recent practice of holding monthlong festivals, in this case Voices of Latin America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/arts/music/gustavo-dudamel-part-of-carnegie-halls-2012-13-season.html', 'snippet': 'For its 2012-13 season Carnegie Hall will return to its recent practice of holding monthlong festivals, in this case Voices of Latin America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An Arab-Israeli orchestra, a Latin American festival and the crowning ensemble of Venezuela’s music program aimed at helping the poor will be major presences at Carnegie Hall next season, examples of the growing urge to make classical music socially relevant.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27SEASON/27SEASON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 824, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27SEASON/27SEASON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27SEASON/27SEASON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carnegie Hall’s 2012-13 Season Includes a Latin American Festival', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Carnegie Hall’s 2012-13 Season Includes a Latin American Festival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Latin America', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Hall', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dudamel, Gustavo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel J. Wakin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Wakin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6404c367-9cb3-5d58-8547-455abe5879d6', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6404c367-9cb3-5d58-8547-455abe5879d6'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Bev Perdue has decided not to seek a second term, while Representative Brad Miller will forgo a sixth term. Both are Democrats.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/in-north-carolina-the-governor-and-a-lawmaker-will-not-seek-re-election/', 'snippet': 'Gov. Bev Perdue has decided not to seek a second term, while Representative Brad Miller will forgo a sixth term. Both are Democrats.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:20 p.m. | Updated Bev Perdue, a Democrat who made history when she became North Carolina’s first female governor in 2009, will not seek re-election this year, a Democratic source with knowledge of her decision said on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In North Carolina, the Governor and a Lawmaker Will Not Seek Re-election', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Brad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perdue, Beverly E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:01:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73af4670-e5cb-5411-83b4-533462f682c4', 'word_count': 753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73af4670-e5cb-5411-83b4-533462f682c4'}, {'abstract': 'Mosireen a group of activist filmmakers in Cairo, produced a video report documenting Wednesday’s protest marches in the Egyptian capital, with English translations of the chants against the military.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/video-of-wednesdays-protest-marches-in-egypt/', 'snippet': 'Mosireen a group of activist filmmakers in Cairo, produced a video report documenting Wednesday’s protest marches in the Egyptian capital, with English translations of the chants against the military.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While Wednesday’s first anniversary of the Egyptian revolution was treated, officially, as a celebration by the military council that still rules the country, activists who insist that their revolution remains unfinished also took to the streets in protest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video of Wednesday’s Protest Marches in Egypt', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/264248cf-816b-5702-991b-7fed2a3c8816', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/264248cf-816b-5702-991b-7fed2a3c8816'}, {'abstract': 'A survey shows disturbing trends in financial aid and first choice attendance, as well as increasing support for liberal social views and a greater focus on academics.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/survey-first-choice/', 'snippet': 'A survey shows disturbing trends in financial aid and first choice attendance, as well as increasing support for liberal social views and a greater focus on academics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A survey of nearly 204,000 college freshmen who started school in 2011 indicated that only 58 percent of them enrolled at their first-choice college, the lowest percentage to do so since the question was first asked in 1974.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fewer Students Attending Their First-Choice Colleges, Survey Shows', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:09:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/710236af-8f81-5db4-bc21-d6018586de1f', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/710236af-8f81-5db4-bc21-d6018586de1f'}, {'abstract': 'If the proposed regulation is adopted this week, it would require zero-emission and partial zero-emission vehicles to account for 15 percent of the marketplace in 2025, up from a current 4 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/california-to-consider-major-revision-to-zero-emission-mandate/', 'snippet': 'If the proposed regulation is adopted this week, it would require zero-emission and partial zero-emission vehicles to account for 15 percent of the marketplace in 2025, up from a current 4 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, the California Air Resources Board, commonly known as CARB, will vote this week on a staff recommendation to require a significant increase of zero- and near-zero-emission cars to be on state roads in 2025. If the program is adopted, it would effectively require the addition of 1.4 million advanced-powertrain vehicles in 2025. They would include 500,000 battery-electric or fuel-cell cars and about 900,000 plug-in hybrids, called transitional zero-emission vehicles, or TZEVs, by CARB.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California to Consider Major Revision to Zero-Emission Mandate', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric and Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fuel Efficiency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALLIANCE OF AUTOMOBILE MANUFACTURERS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Air Resources Board', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:14:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Motavalli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Motavalli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/385941df-6558-5311-829d-256df2152c80', 'word_count': 695, 'uri': 'nyt://article/385941df-6558-5311-829d-256df2152c80'}, {'abstract': 'Checklists of symptoms have become a critical part of every major pharmaceutical marketing campaign, making it easy for patients to diagnose their own ailments — and making it easy for drug makers to sell drugs.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/using-symptom-checklists-to-sell-drugs/', 'snippet': 'Checklists of symptoms have become a critical part of every major pharmaceutical marketing campaign, making it easy for patients to diagnose their own ailments — and making it easy for drug makers to sell drugs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It began suddenly a little over 10 years ago. With impressive fluency, friends, family members and patients started asking me about random medications, the odd syncopations of those invented, polysyllabic pharmaceutical brand names – Viagra, Lipitor — rolling perfectly off their tongues.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Have These Symptoms? Buy This Drug', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:18:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauline W. Chen, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauline', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8b88feb-84d6-5456-8776-b1a67efb8cb1', 'word_count': 959, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8b88feb-84d6-5456-8776-b1a67efb8cb1'}, {'abstract': 'I found myself oddly compelled by a new Web series that goes behind the scenes of the most recent CFDA/Vogue Fashion Fund competition.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/fashion-fund-confidential/', 'snippet': 'I found myself oddly compelled by a new Web series that goes behind the scenes of the most recent CFDA/Vogue Fashion Fund competition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems hard to imagine that there is a great consumer desire for another documentary following the lives of impoverished fashion designers as they struggle to succeed. And yet I found myself oddly compelled by a new Web series that goes behind the scenes of the most recent CFDA/Vogue Fashion Fund competition, namely because the directors Ricki Stern and Annie Sundberg managed to pull back the curtain well beyond the typical gloss.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fashion Fund Confidential', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:21:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a18a0d0d-61cc-5b6b-9959-502788da386e', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a18a0d0d-61cc-5b6b-9959-502788da386e'}, {'abstract': 'Look for Mr. Gingrich to abandon his above-it-all “presidential” tone at Thursday’s debate and come out rip-roaring about Mitt Romney’s investments that, Mr. Gingrich contends, profited from home foreclosures in Florida.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/gingrich-opens-new-line-of-attack-on-romney/', 'snippet': 'Look for Mr. Gingrich to abandon his above-it-all “presidential” tone at Thursday’s debate and come out rip-roaring about Mitt Romney’s investments that, Mr. Gingrich contends, profited from home foreclosures in Florida.', 'lead_paragraph': '12:55 p.m. | Updated  MOUNT DORA, Fla. — Look for Newt Gingrich to abandon his above-it-all “presidential” tone at Thursday’s debate and come out rip-roaring about Mitt Romney’s investments that, Mr. Gingrich contends, profited from home foreclosures in Florida. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gingrich Opens Up New Line of Attack on Romney', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABRAMS, ELLIOTT', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corp (Freddie Mac)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:27:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e09af97-5f7d-559d-aa80-323ba1537be7', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e09af97-5f7d-559d-aa80-323ba1537be7'}, {'abstract': 'At Valentino, the designers sent out 43 bewitchingly fragile, feminine looks — dresses of smocking, lace, silver thread embroideries and floral taffetas.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/just-one-look-valentino-3/', 'snippet': 'At Valentino, the designers sent out 43 bewitchingly fragile, feminine looks — dresses of smocking, lace, silver thread embroideries and floral taffetas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At Valentino, Maria Grazia Chiuri and Pier Paolo Piccioli sent out 43 bewitchingly fragile, feminine looks — dresses of smocking, lace, silver thread embroideries and floral taffetas. As is their custom, the designers listed the number of hours certain embroideries required on the show notes. A stunning sheath with lace collar, sheer sleeves and a caged bodice (crocheted in gold thread and decorated with pearls) required 650 hours just for the crochet. This is, indeed, really slow fashion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just One Look | Valentino', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chiuri, Maria Grazia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Piccioli, Pier Paolo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T17:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sally Singer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sally', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'N