rd shrinking its budget after a decade of war as it announced Thursday that it wanted to limit pay raises for troops, increase health insurance fees for military retirees and close bases in the United States. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/us/PENTAGON/PENTAGON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Defense Budget Cuts Would Limit Raises and Close Bases', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pentagon Says Cuts Would Affect Raises, Health Plan and Base Closings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T19:08:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Bumiller and Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bumiller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/856cff4d-14b6-5cc6-9847-02e95f2c93a3', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/856cff4d-14b6-5cc6-9847-02e95f2c93a3'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers report that viruses deprived of their usual channel for infiltrating the E. coli bacterium evolved a different route into the cells in little more than two weeks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/science/in-real-time-a-virus-learns-a-new-way-to-infect.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers report that viruses deprived of their usual channel for infiltrating the E. coli bacterium evolved a different route into the cells in little more than two weeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Viruses regularly evolve new ways of making people sick, but scientists usually do not become aware of these new strategies until years or centuries after they have evolved. In a new study published on Thursday in the journal Science, however, a team of scientists at Michigan State University describes how viruses evolved a new way of infecting cells in little more than two weeks. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/us/VIRUS/VIRUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 831, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/us/VIRUS/VIRUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/27/us/VIRUS/VIRUS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Finds Virus to Be Fast Learner on Infecting', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Study Finds Virus to Be Fast Learner on Infecting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E Coli (Bacteria)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Avian Influenza', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Viruses', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T19:09:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d7e4e8b-f756-5685-80c3-73b7c51f8c51', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d7e4e8b-f756-5685-80c3-73b7c51f8c51'}, {'abstract': 'The former Republican presidential candidate said Gingrich “had a new idea every minute and most of them were off the wall.”', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/dole-releases-stinging-critique-of-gingrich/', 'snippet': 'The former Republican presidential candidate said Gingrich “had a new idea every minute and most of them were off the wall.”', 'lead_paragraph': '3:59 p.m. | Updated The Romney campaign on Thursday released a scathing critique of Newt Gingrich by Bob Dole, the former Republican presidential candidate and senator from Kansas. The statement comes as some establishment Republicans have been stepping up their attacks of Mr. Gingrich.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dole Releases Stinging Critique of Gingrich', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endorsements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dole, Bob', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T19:12:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gerry Mullany and Richard A. Oppel Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mullany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Oppel', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f371c6b-5528-5a89-8f09-c3783d4133dc', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f371c6b-5528-5a89-8f09-c3783d4133dc'}, {'abstract': 'This month, eight Rosewood properties began supplying guests with a fragrance butler who is trained on the different scents being offered.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/out-of-perfume-rosewood-hotels-enlist-fragrance-butlers/', 'snippet': 'This month, eight Rosewood properties began supplying guests with a fragrance butler who is trained on the different scents being offered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hotels today are in the thick of a trend that caters to their guests’ seemingly smallest needs, by doing things like providing custom playlists for their iPods and giving them eight choices of pillows for their beds. Now, the luxury brand Rosewood has a new amenity to add to the list: perfumes that are specific to each property. This month, eight Rosewood properties began supplying guests with a fragrance butler, who is trained in the merits of the different scents being offered.Within five minutes of receiving a call to the concierge to request the service, a fragrance butler arrives at a room, carrying a silver tray of perfumes and colognes. Guests spritz themselves with a scent from the fragrance menu, which is tailored to each hotel.\xa0 (The Carlyle, for example, has an elegant, old-world feel, and the choices include Chanel Chance and Hermès Eau des Merveilles for women and Ralph Lauren Polo Black for men.) There is no limit to the number of times that the fragrance butler can be summoned.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Out of Perfume? Rosewood Hotels Enlist Fragrance Butlers', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Perfumes and Fragrances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T19:13:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c861818-e732-54ab-83b6-894175a5e959', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c861818-e732-54ab-83b6-894175a5e959'}, {'abstract': 'Environmental groups say that while multinational corporations like Apple are trying to improve conditions, thousands — or perhaps tens of thousands — of smaller companies are cutting corners and dumping hazardous chemicals in rural areas and even near densely populated areas.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/apple-electronics-and-environmental-ills/', 'snippet': 'Environmental groups say that while multinational corporations like Apple are trying to improve conditions, thousands — or perhaps tens of thousands — of smaller companies are cutting corners and dumping hazardous chemicals in rural areas and even...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an article in Thursday’s Times, my colleague Charles Duhigg and I detail the human cost of producing some of the world’s most popular gadgets in an investigation into labor conditions at the plants of some of Apple’s suppliers in China.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apple, Electronics and Environmental Ills', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T19:19:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b8b4326-22fd-5843-8166-6445296112c5', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b8b4326-22fd-5843-8166-6445296112c5'}, {'abstract': 'Hints of past grandeur are revealed in photographs of a decaying theater on Avenue A.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/a-forgotten-theater-above-an-east-village-grocery/', 'snippet': 'Hints of past grandeur are revealed in photographs of a decaying theater on Avenue A.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like many in the neighborhood, Kevin Shea Adams, a photographer and musician living in the East Village, wondered what secrets lay behind a massive brick facade above the awnings of East Village Farms, a grocery store on Avenue A between Sixth and Seventh Streets. Eventually he got permission to photograph the space, a long abandoned theater. The grocery used the vaulted cavern as a storage room, but remnants of the theater’s past glory remain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Forgotten Theater Above an East Village Grocery', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theaters (Buildings)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T19:19:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/754bad7a-50d9-5ce1-a7d0-9be41900f2e0', 'word_count': 108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/754bad7a-50d9-5ce1-a7d0-9be41900f2e0'}, {'abstract': 'The story of the Australian prime minister’s rough departure through crowds amid protests by indigenous groups called to mind a kind of Cinderella story turned on its head.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/on-national-holiday-australian-leader-forced-to-flee-protesters/', 'snippet': 'The story of the Australian prime minister’s rough departure through crowds amid protests by indigenous groups called to mind a kind of Cinderella story turned on its head.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A fancy dinner. A hurried escape. A shoe left behind, found and offered back by an unlikely suitor. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On National Holiday, Australian Lead