ration-of-war-directed-by-valerie-donzelli.html', 'snippet': 'In “Declaration of War,” which carries echoes of the French New Wave, a child’s illness spurs a family to action.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Declaration of War” is a manic sprint whose style and momentum recall French New Wave films like “Jules and Jim” revved up to double-time. On paper, the story of Juliette (Valérie Donzelli) and Roméo (Jérémie Elkaïm), bohemian Parisians whose 18-month-old son develops a brain tumor, may sound depressing. But the emergency channels their energy into an exhilarating race against time. A crucial insight of the movie, directed by Ms. Donzelli, is that a crisis can infuse daily life with a surreal, zany urgency. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27DECLARATION1_SPAN/DECLARATION1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27DECLARATION1_SPAN/DECLARATION1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 255}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27DECLARATION1_SPAN/DECLARATION1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27DECLARATION1_SPAN/27DECLARATION1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27DECLARATION1_SPAN/27DECLARATION1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Parents Waging a Battle Against the Ultimate Foe', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Parents Waging a Battle Against the Ultimate Foe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T22:45:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee5fc1fa-2cbe-56a2-aebc-834a0815630b', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee5fc1fa-2cbe-56a2-aebc-834a0815630b'}, {'abstract': 'Rick Santorum, all but shut out of coverage since Newt Gingrich won the South Carolina primary last week, says he is staying in the race for the long haul.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/santorum-says-hes-not-going-anywhere/', 'snippet': 'Rick Santorum, all but shut out of coverage since Newt Gingrich won the South Carolina primary last week, says he is staying in the race for the long haul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIAMI — Rick Santorum, who has been all but shut out of news media coverage since Newt Gingrich won the South Carolina primary last week, said Thursday that he was staying in the race for the long haul.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Santorum Says He’s ‘Not Going Anywhere’', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T22:49:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a413f213-b9c7-5f91-9f84-dbb084fb697a', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a413f213-b9c7-5f91-9f84-dbb084fb697a'}, {'abstract': 'Expansive shows at Sperone Westwater feature portraits from as far back as the 16th century and marble sculptures from 350 B.C. to the present.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/arts/design/portraits-from-16th-century-and-sculpture-at-sperone-westwater.html', 'snippet': 'Expansive shows at Sperone Westwater feature portraits from as far back as the 16th century and marble sculptures from 350 B.C. to the present.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The tendency of commercial galleries to mount exhibitions of historical material is pushed to rewardingly crazed extremes by the latest offerings at Sperone Westwater. This blue-chip gallery, previously located in SoHo and then Chelsea, has crammed two lavish exhibitions into the four floors of available display space in the narrow, overly hygienic building on the Bowery — commissioned from the British starchitect Norman Foster — to which it moved in 2010. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27SPERONE1_SPAN/27SPERONE1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/27/arts/27SPERONE1_SPAN/27SPE