 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95d665d6-dc33-5383-b291-e63c575691ca'}, {'abstract': 'Twitter will remove certain posts in certain countries, if asked. In principle, that could apply to a message promoting Nazi ideology in Germany, a critical remark about the monarchy in Thailand or perhaps even lines from “The Satanic Verses” in India, where the book remains banned. Exactly why the company chose to make the change now remains unclear, except for its global ambitions.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/twitter-announces-micro-censorship-policy/', 'snippet': 'Twitter will remove certain posts in certain countries, if asked. In principle, that could apply to a message promoting Nazi ideology in Germany, a critical remark about the monarchy in Thailand or perhaps even lines from “The Satanic Verses” in I...', 'lead_paragraph': '7:52 p.m. | Updated Within hours of the announcement, people had figured out ways to subvert Twitter’s blocking. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/blogs/bitpix-twitter-logo/bitpix-twitter-logo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 839, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/11/blogs/bitpix-twitter-logo/bitpix-twitter-logo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/11/blogs/bitpix-twitter-logo/bitpix-twitter-logo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Twitter Announces Micro-Censorship Policy', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-26T23:55:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Somini Sengupta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Somini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sengupta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3117531e-d892-5d5f-ae47-80185a4c3bb7', 'word_count': 384, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3117531e-d892-5d5f-ae47-80185a4c3bb7'}, {'abstract': 'AT&T’s chief executive says that because of the limited quantity of the frequency spectrum available, the nation may soon face a data shortage. He thinks the government is the problem. And he also thinks that because AT&T needs more spectrum, it will have to increase prices for customers and impose restrictions on data usage to manage rising demands for data.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/att-fcc/', 'snippet': 'AT&T’s chief executive says that because of the limited quantity of the frequency spectrum available, the nation may soon face a data shortage. He thinks the government is the problem. And he also thinks that because AT&T needs more spectrum, it w...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Smartphones and tablets are squeezing data networks. And because of the limited amount of the frequency spectrum available to expand wireless services, the nation may face a data network shortage in the coming years. AT&T thinks the government is the problem.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/10/blogs/bitpix-att-stephenson1/bitpix-att-stephenson1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo