 Pa. — Penn State concluded its weeklong goodbye to the former football coach Joe Paterno with a memorial Thursday at a packed arena that featured remembrances from a player from each of the six decades in which he coached, videos of him with the team and an emotional closing eulogy from his son Jay.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/sports/y-paterno/y-paterno-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 616, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/sports/y-paterno/y-paterno-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/27/sports/y-paterno/y-paterno-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Strong Words Resound at Tribute to Paterno', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strong Words Resound At Tribute To Paterno', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paterno, Joe', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania State University', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T02:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Viera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdffbbda-31b3-5a48-9353-958975c4d341', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdffbbda-31b3-5a48-9353-958975c4d341'}, {'abstract': 'Why did the Union sink dozens of whaling ships in Charleston Harbor during the Civil War?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/26/the-navys-stone-fleet/', 'snippet': 'Why did the Union sink dozens of whaling ships in Charleston Harbor during the Civil War?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In November 1861, the whaling ship Leonidas sailed from New Bedford, Mass., for the last time. Five times before, the Leonidas had departed New Bedford Harbor bound for whaling voyages in the Pacific, Indian and Atlantic Oceans; each time, the ship returned home with a hold full of oil extracted from sperm whales. Whaling was one of America’s first global industries, and oil carried home in ships like the Leonidas lubricated machinery in booming factories and lit homes in the nation’s growing cities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Navy’s Stone Fleet', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whales and Whaling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Melville, Herman', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Charleston (SC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T02:30:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jamie L. Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jamie', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Jones', 'qualifier': None, '