'', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bff6880-8b4c-56b0-8ddc-64d2334be269', 'word_count': 569, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bff6880-8b4c-56b0-8ddc-64d2334be269'}, {'abstract': 'The siblings who form a string trio have played in concert halls, cathedrals, even the White House, but a neighborhood pool hall presents a different kind of challenge and reward.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/a-trio-of-siblings-with-a-classical-sound/', 'snippet': 'The siblings who form a string trio have played in concert halls, cathedrals, even the White House, but a neighborhood pool hall presents a different kind of challenge and reward.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A basement bar and pool hall where a bright neon sign touts “$1 Massage” is the last place you’d expect to find a home-schooled classical string ensemble. But the JoSunJari String Trio is used to popping up in unexpected places – like parks and beaches – where their music is like a random gift bestowed upon you by strangers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Trio of Siblings With a Classical Sound', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Musical Instruments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Violins', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T14:54:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48eaaacc-ceff-5c8d-92bd-3392f1804147', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48eaaacc-ceff-5c8d-92bd-3392f1804147'}, {'abstract': 'The actress provides her nominations for Best Supporting Beauty Products.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/face-time-gretchen-mol/', 'snippet': 'The actress provides her nominations for Best Supporting Beauty Products.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Gretchen Mol gets ready to attend the S.A.G. Awards in Los Angeles this weekend (she plays Gillian, a beautiful showgirl in “Boardwalk Empire,” which has been nominated for two awards), she reveals what she does when not shooting the show, as well as some of her nominations for Best Supporting Beauty Products.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Face Time | Gretchen Mol', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Turnbow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Turnbow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95c9bc8b-0b32-5765-9a7a-7a8b7d54702d', 'word_count': 1019, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95c9bc8b-0b32-5765-9a7a-7a8b7d54702d'}, {'abstract': 'Greenlight Capital’s former compliance officer and a JPMorgan Cazenove trader were fined in connection with Greenlight’s sale of shares in a British pub operator more than two years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/more-fines-in-greenlight-insider-case-in-britain/', 'snippet': 'Greenlight Capital’s former compliance officer and a JPMorgan Cazenove trader were fined in connection with Greenlight’s sale of shares in a British pub operator more than two years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – Britain’s financial regulator said Friday that it had fined a former Greenlight Capital compliance officer and a JPMorgan Cazenove trader in connection with Greenlight’s sale of shares in a British pub operator more than two years ago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Fines in Greenlight Insider Case in Britain', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Einhorn, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Services Authority', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:04:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Werdigier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werdigier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90a80b35-d327-5873-b2a4-c2ac0a246de2', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90a80b35-d327-5873-b2a4-c2ac0a246de2'}, {'abstract': 'U.S. Agriculture Secretary Tom Vilsack sets out the Obama Administration’s new blueprint for management of 193 million acres of national forests, emphasizing collaborative development of forest plans and protection of wildlife and watersheds.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/how-to-manage-u-s-forests-version-3-1/', 'snippet': 'U.S. Agriculture Secretary Tom Vilsack sets out the Obama Administration’s new blueprint for management of 193 million acres of national forests, emphasizing collaborative development of forest plans and protection of wildlife and watersheds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the high-tech world, new versions of programs are released to fix new bugs. In the federal regulatory world, new versions of management blueprints are released to address legal problems. Each is generally judged by a simple metric: did the fixes work, or will they have to be redone?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Manage U.S. Forests, Version 3.1', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:07:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbe683e3-81db-5294-a7ab-69ec2979839e', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbe683e3-81db-5294-a7ab-69ec2979839e'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/what-were-reading-360/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Specialty Food: Hot trends from last week’s Winter Fancy Food Show in San Francisco: heritage grain products (quinoa, amaranth, chia) proliferate in snacks and pastas; pickling of beets, carrots, peas, okra and fruits is increasing; oats and almonds are coming to juice drinks; coconut is in everything, and the gluten-free category is expanding rapidly (gluten-free granola, anyone?). — Glenn Collins ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:07:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4349d531-68d4-5fce-b453-2606d48989c3', 'word_count': 793, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4349d531-68d4-5fce-b453-2606d48989c3'}, {'abstract': 'English artist David Shrigley is a master of the incompetent line.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/01/29/magazine/look-david-shrigley.html', 'snippet': 'English artist David Shrigley is a master of the incompetent line.', 'lead_paragraph': 'English artist David Shrigley is a master of the incompetent line.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Art of the Doodle', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Glasgow (Scotland)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:15:09+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/f9a373c1-1bf2-5407-be5f-b8990c10479e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/f9a373c1-1bf2-5407-be5f-b8990c10479e'}, {'abstract': 'Three friends got unexpected news about H.I.V. Was I next?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/magazine/the-hiv-scare.html', 'snippet': 'Three friends got unexpected news about H.I.V. Was I next?', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day last summer I got an e-mail from one of my best friends, updating me on the last few months of his life: work developments; a vacation recap; a reply to a barbecue invitation; and “one other thing” that he would tell me about when we met. We had dinner a few weeks later, and at the end of our second beers, he casually said, “I’m H.I.V.-positive.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29lives/mag-29lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 933, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29lives/mag-29lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29lives/mag-29lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Scare', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Scare', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:15:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Hoerburger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoerburger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9418da9b-3c50-5834-a754-55a88312e808', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9418da9b-3c50-5834-a754-55a88312e808'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/magazine/reply-all-judith-clark.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If Judy Clark is not a fit candidate for clemency, it is hard to imagine what argument could be made for giving clemency to anyone. The pain and anger of the Rockland families is completely understandable, but should the desire for endless retribution prevail over every other consideration? I hope that Governor Cuomo will choose the just and courageous course and release her.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judith Clark’s Radical Transformation', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Judith Clark’s Radical Transformation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Judith', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:15:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f0b2505-e8be-527f-a1ac-dd660a4cbfb4', 'word_count': 834, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f0b2505-e8be-527f-a1ac-dd660a4cbfb4'}, {'abstract': 'Bad teacher; smooth rabbi.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/magazine/ethicist-preschool-of-hard-knocks.html', 'snippet': 'Bad teacher; smooth rabbi.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My daughter loves her preschool. So do my wife and I: It’s affordable, it’s on our way to work and it teaches Mandarin, her second language. Recently, the school’s co-owner and director was indicted for stealing a ton of money meant to feed low-income children at her other business. I wouldn’t be shocked if the charges stick. How do my ethical obligations not to support a (supposed) crook square with my daughter’s love of the school and its teachers, especially given the lack of equivalent options? NAME WITHHELD, BROOKLYN', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 610, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/02/28/magazine/ethicist-image/ethicist-image-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Preschool of Hard Knocks', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Preschool of Hard Knocks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rabbis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Pre-School)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:15:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc5b983c-4c71-5fbb-be38-ed63c28c9404', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc5b983c-4c71-5fbb-be38-ed63c28c9404'}, {'abstract': 'Many of the keepsakes we used to hold in our hands now exist as data on digital devices. What happens when the data disappears?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/magazine/what-happens-when-data-disappears.html', 'snippet': 'Many of the keepsakes we used to hold in our hands now exist as data on digital devices. What happens when the data disappears?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This kind of thing happens all the time now, but three or four years ago it still seemed momentous and thrilling: a sudden argument at a dinner party (something about Tom Selleck and Burt Reynolds and iconic mustaches of the ’70s) was quickly put to rest when half the people present picked up their phones and started to Google. Observing this, a friend of mine made what struck me as a very clever remark about our “stegosaurus brains.” It didn’t occur to me that only a dinosaur would delight in a dinosaur analogy.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29riff/mag-29Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 92, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29riff/mag-29Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 92}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29riff/mag-29Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 167, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29riff/mag-29Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/magazine/29riff/mag-29Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dilemma of Being a Cyborg', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘The Essence of Being Human Is Not Remembering but Forgetting’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Archives and Records', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foer, Joshua', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:15:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carina Chocano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chocano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/596e7fe4-7fad-5f0e-9a2e-456a5e1b93d7', 'word_count': 1861, 'uri': 'nyt://article/596e7fe4-7fad-5f0e-9a2e-456a5e1b93d7'}, {'abstract': 'The former governor of Massachusetts moved past his rival, Newt Gingrich, heading into the final weekend before the vote.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/us/politics/as-primary-nears-florida-poll-favors-romney.html', 'snippet': 'The former governor of Massachusetts moved past his rival, Newt Gingrich, heading into the final weekend before the vote.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0MIAMI —\xa0Mitt Romney might have felt on Friday that he had finally gotten his groove back.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/us/28campaign/28campaign-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/28/us/28campaign/28campaign-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/us/28campaign/28campaign-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Goes on Offensive in Florida, Bolstered by Debate Performance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Romney Goes on Offensive in Florida, Bolstered by Debate Performance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:16:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcdaaf3d-2f48-5750-b82d-0347330c3d04', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcdaaf3d-2f48-5750-b82d-0347330c3d04'}, {'abstract': 'Two reissues reveal the development and revisions of a novel and a long experimental poem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/reconsidering-the-genius-of-gertrude-stein.html', 'snippet': 'Two reissues reveal the development and revisions of a novel and a long experimental poem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Approaching Gertrude Stein’s writing critically is tricky. Because she strove to reshape literary conventions — syntax, language usage, narrative order and the sense of making sense — any comment on her choices may already be rebuffed in her poetics and practice. Stein is a trickster. This may be why, as I read “Ida” and “Stanzas in Meditation,” both reissued in corrected, authoritative editions from Yale University Press, I remembered a Jonathan Richman lyric I’ll paraphrase as “Pablo Picasso never got called a jackass.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/books/review/29stein/29stein-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 956, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/books/review/29stein/29stein-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/25/books/review/29stein/29stein-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reconsidering the Genius of Gertrude Stein', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Books', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stein, Gertrude', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:31:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynne Tillman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tillman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aef6f903-9889-548a-8564-e58cc65d2fdc', 'word_count': 1382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aef6f903-9889-548a-8564-e58cc65d2fdc'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of “The Anointed: Evangelical Truth in a Secular Age.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/the-anointed.html