particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INDIA CALLING: An Intimate Portrait of a Nation’s Remaking,  by Anand Giridharadas (St. Martin’s Griffin, $14.99.) Through the prism of his émigré family history, Giridharadas, a columnist for The International Herald Tribune, captures fundamental changes in the very idea of what it is to be Indian. Moving among entrepreneurs, radicals and religious seekers, he notes that the greatest upheaval is “in the mind, in how people conceived of their possibilities.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:33:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cf74657-ab05-5d2b-bd67-3c97a3f14d4a', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cf74657-ab05-5d2b-bd67-3c97a3f14d4a'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEN JONSON: A Life,  by Ian Donaldson (Oxford University, $39.95.) In his time, Jonson was as central to the development of British theater as Shakespeare was, if not more, Donaldson argues in this deeply researched but happily readable biography.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice: Recent Books Of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:34:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/161ed9f5-2213-5152-87ae-08aa4690b2e2', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/161ed9f5-2213-5152-87ae-08aa4690b2e2'}, {'abstract': 'Highlights from Sundance include sweets from the non-professional star of “Beasts of the Southern Wild” and an afterparty DJ’ed by LCD Soundsystem’s James Murphy, who happened to be the subject of a documentary.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/at-sundance-one-star-bakes-another-keeps-the-party-going/', 'snippet': 'Highlights from Sundance include sweets from the non-professional star of “Beasts of the Southern Wild” and an afterparty DJ’ed by LCD Soundsystem’s James Murphy, who happened to be the subject of a documentary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah – Sundance is drawing to a close just as the Bagger is emerging from our Academy foxhole, where (incoming!) we have been dodging snubs while hoping to see Michael Fassbender in a pirate hat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Sundance, One Star Bakes; Another Keeps the Party Going', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:34:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By MELENA RYZIK', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'RYZIK', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f84bd57-5c11-53e2-8fef-15acd9f712b2', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f84bd57-5c11-53e2-8fef-15acd9f712b2'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Jeanette Winterson discusses the legacy of Henry Miller’s “Tropic of Cancer”; Charles McGrath on the deeply personal poetry of publisher Jonathan Galassi; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2012/01/27/books/review/27books_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Jeanette Winterson discusses the legacy of Henry Miller’s “Tropic of Cancer”; Charles McGrath on the deeply personal poetry of publisher Jonathan Galassi; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Jeanette Winterson discusses the legacy of Henry Miller’s “Tropic of Cancer”; Charles McGrath on the deeply personal poetry of publisher Jonathan Galassi; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. Sam Tanenhaus is the host.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:38:25+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/a3912e0f-f875-590b-a56b-9d553457fda6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/a3912e0f-f875-590b-a56b-9d553457fda6'}, {'abstract': 'A moment of televised silence (if you turn the volume down) to mark the passing of Robert Hegyes, better known as Juan Epstein.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/fourth-largest-city-in-america-in-mourning/', 'snippet': 'A moment of televised silence (if you turn the volume down) to mark the passing of Robert Hegyes, better known as Juan Epstein.', 'lead_paragraph': 'And now, a moment of televised silence (if you turn the volume down) to mark the passing of Robert Hegyes, former portrayer of the small screen’s reigning 26-year-old Puerto Rican Jewish high school student and sideways role model.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fourth-Largest City in America in Mourning', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:43:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61717b98-694d-5121-a21a-d2b7bfb82730', 'word_count': 44, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61717b98-694d-5121-a21a-d2b7bfb82730'}, {'abstract': 'The author taps a host of public sources and tracks down friends and former courtiers of the queen who are willing to share intimate tidbits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/elizabeth-the-queen-the-life-of-a-modern-monarch-by-sally-bedell-smith-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The author taps a host of public sources and tracks down friends and former courtiers of the queen who are willing to share intimate tidbits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a British expat trying to keep in touch with the old sod, I’ve acquired the slightly eccentric habit of buying coffee mugs recording key moments in the public life of our royal family. Alas, those celebrating the weddings of Prince Charles and Prince Andrew broke soon after their marriages fell apart. Not so the mug I bought for Queen Elizabeth’s Golden Jubilee in 2002. In fine bone china, no less than in person, she just keeps on going.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-29-RIDING/SUB-29-RIDING-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-29-RIDING/SUB-29-RIDING-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-29-RIDING/SUB-29-RIDING-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 820, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-29-RIDING/SUB-29-RIDING-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-29-RIDING/SUB-29-RIDING-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Queen Elizabeth II in Public and Private', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wife, Mother, Monarch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Sally Bedell', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elizabeth II, Queen of Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royal Family', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:43:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Riding', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Riding', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b58e233-0f2a-5822-aa58-dff6114d1c5f', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b58e233-0f2a-5822-aa58-dff6114d1c5f'}, {'abstract': 'Marni Davis explores how Jews balanced assimilation with historical continuity during America’s failed “noble experiment.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/jews-and-booze-becoming-american-in-the-age-of-prohibition-by-marni-davis-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Marni Davis explores how Jews balanced assimilation with historical continuity during America’s failed “noble experiment.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even in New York Times headlines, they were known as “Izzy and Moe.” Their very presence could make bartenders faint. They possessed a keen sense of smell and a theatrical flair that included disguises as fruit peddlers and football players. Stumpy Izzy, who had rejected a career as a rabbi, and his partner were credited with 4,392 arrests in five years, including one in a Manhattan meatpacking district club where Izzy posed as a turkey salesman and found bottles of booze concealed in a huge stuffed bear.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-22-Roberts/SUB-22-Roberts-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-22-Roberts/SUB-22-Roberts-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-22-Roberts/SUB-22-Roberts-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 936, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-22-Roberts/SUB-22-Roberts-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/SUB-22-Roberts/SUB-22-Roberts-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Jews Handled Prohibition', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Good for the Jews?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prohibition Era (1920-1933)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:43:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cdc4e3f-ad0e-5a1d-bfc4-b820f009b1e8', 'word_count': 766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cdc4e3f-ad0e-5a1d-bfc4-b820f009b1e8'}, {'abstract': 'A double biography of high-profile Muslim women, one who denounces Islam, the other, the West.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/wanted-women-the-lives-of-ayaan-hirsi-ali-and-aafia-siddiqui-by-deborah-scroggins-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A double biography of high-profile Muslim women, one who denounces Islam, the other, the West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Very few of the heroes and villains made famous in the wars of the past decade are women. Of the scant exceptions, two of the most fascinating are the subjects of Deborah Scroggins’s thoughtful double biography, “Wanted Women.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Griswold/Griswold-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 296, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Griswold/Griswold-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 296}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Griswold/Griswold-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 505, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Griswold/Griswold-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Griswold/Griswold-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Islam and the West Through the Eyes of Two Women', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Firebrands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scroggins, Deborah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siddiqui, Aafia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirsi Ali, Ayaan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:44:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eliza Griswold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eliza', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Griswold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c16c88ff-a85f-5979-8e3e-fce2e53416bb', 'word_count': 1180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c16c88ff-a85f-5979-8e3e-fce2e53416bb'}, {'abstract': 'Cullen Murphy sees the Inquisition as a template for the war on terror.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/gods-jury-the-inquisition-and-the-making-of-the-modern-world-by-cullen-murphy-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Cullen Murphy sees the Inquisition as a template for the war on terror.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the moral atlas of Cullen Murphy, the road to Abu Ghraib, Guantánamo Bay and the secret “black sites” of the war on terror begins in Montségur, a fortress in the foothills of the Pyrenees. There, in 1244, a French army assembled at the behest of the Roman Catholic Church besieged several hundred Cathars. Their sect’s heresy was dualism, a belief in both a beneficent God and an equivalent evil deity. Enticed into surrendering by the promise that their followers would be spared, the Cathars were burned alive on a pyre.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/FREEDMAN/FREEDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/FREEDMAN/FREEDMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/FREEDMAN/FREEDMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/FREEDMAN/FREEDMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/FREEDMAN/FREEDMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Influence of the Inquisition', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Interrogation Age', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Cullen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:45:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel G. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a15a6e48-fc4b-5334-8211-4cb01c50b778', 'word_count': 963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a15a6e48-fc4b-5334-8211-4cb01c50b778'}, {'abstract': 'Lori Andrews looks at how personal data is exploited by Web sites, search engines and other Internet technologies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/i-know-who-you-are-and-i-saw-what-you-did-social-networks-and-the-death-of-privacy-by-lori-andrews-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Lori Andrews looks at how personal data is exploited by Web sites, search engines and other Internet technologies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It may surprise anyone under 16, but even before the advent of social networking we faced threats to our privacy. A hospital accidentally releasing patient records or a shady marketing firm engaging in Stasi-like data collection — such violations were substantial enough and disturbing enough to make the evening news. Today, however, the “death of privacy” is more like death by a thousand cuts: information leaks out slowly and invisibly, and so routinely that we’re hardly shocked when it does. Internet companies, which use the word “sharing” almost as a euphemism for “oops,” like to pretend these lapses are normal, even natural. If Mark Zuckerberg’s private photos are up for grabs (as when a recent glitch exposed his Facebook account), what can the rest of us expect?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Morozov-sub/Morozov-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 741, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Morozov-sub/Morozov-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Morozov-sub/Morozov-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dangers of Sharing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sharing It All', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:45:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evgeny Morozov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evgeny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morozov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cea223b6-1e8d-546b-8696-10f1629e7aa0', 'word_count': 1171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cea223b6-1e8d-546b-8696-10f1629e7aa0'}, {'abstract': 'Edmund White’s novel follows the friendship of two libertines, from 1960s New York to the advent of AIDS in the ’80s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/jack-holmes-and-his-friend-by-edmund-white-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Edmund White’s novel follows the friendship of two libertines, from 1960s New York to the advent of AIDS in the ’80s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some novels plumb psyches, others draw social outlines and still others leave character pretty much out of it in favor of cerebral concepts or intricate plotlines. Over the course of his long career, Edmund White has written all of the above. But with his latest, “Jack Holmes and His Friend,” White delivers something rare: a novel that above all else leaves its readers intimately familiar with its characters’ bodies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/books/review/Christensen/Christensen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 949, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/books/review/Christensen/Christensen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/18/books/review/Christensen/Christensen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Edmund White’s Body Language', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Body Language', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Edmund', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:46:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Christensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Christensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/42350cde-2d24-57a3-bf24-d86287871d5f', 'word_count': 1181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42350cde-2d24-57a3-bf24-d86287871d5f'}, {'abstract': 'Sara Wheeler’s experience with the rigors — and the literature — of polar exploration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Sara Wheeler’s experience with the rigors — and the literature — of polar exploration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sara Wheeler is no stranger to the rigors — and the literature — of polar exploration. The author of “The Magnetic North: Notes From the Arctic Circle” and “Terra Incognita: Travels in Antarctica,” she has also written a biography of Apsley Cherry-Garrard, whose classic account of the doomed Robert Falcon Scott expedition, “The Worst Journey in the World,” she first read “in a lonely tent on the West Antarctic ice sheet.” In a recent e-mail, Wheeler recalled that her travels have also taken her to Spitsbergen, Norway, the remote site from which S. A. Andrée began his aeronautical assault on the North Pole — yet another frigid failure and the subject of Alec Wilkinson’s “Ice Balloon,” which she reviews this week.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/18/books/review/Upfront/Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 619, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:46:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e36a4cd-04f0-5f0e-b95f-4a3b8e630314', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e36a4cd-04f0-5f0e-b95f-4a3b8e630314'}, {'abstract': 'A Swedish aeronaut made headlines with his 1897 attempt to reach the North Pole.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/the-ice-balloon-s-a-andree-and-the-heroic-age-of-arctic-exploration-by-alec-wilkinson-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A Swedish aeronaut made headlines with his 1897 attempt to reach the North Pole.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The history of 19th-century Arctic exploration is gruesome. Ice floes throttle creaky ships, men vanish into the white silence, and the North Pole remains elusive. At that time, nobody even knew if that sacred spot lay on land or sea. Then a Swedish aeronaut had a new idea. Approaches by sledge and ship had failed, so why not fly to the pole?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-Wheeler/29-Wheeler-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-Wheeler/29-Wheeler-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-Wheeler/29-Wheeler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-Wheeler/29-Wheeler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-Wheeler/29-Wheeler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Falling Short of the North Pole', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Back to Earth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilkinson, Alec', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Pole', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exploration and Explorers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:47:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Wheeler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheeler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0d1772d-f8ea-5950-8cf5-932aec57f117', 'word_count': 960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0d1772d-f8ea-5950-8cf5-932aec57f117'}, {'abstract': 'Stories and novels by Robert Walser, Alan Lightman, Karin Altenberg, Bret Lott and Daphne du Maurier.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/new-novels-by-alan-lightman-karin-altenberg-and-bret-lott-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Stories and novels by Robert Walser, Alan Lightman, Karin Altenberg, Bret Lott and Daphne du Maurier.', 'lead_paragraph': ' BERLIN STORIES  By Robert Walser.  New York Review Books; paper, $14.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/books/review/29porter/29porter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 808, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/25/books/review/29porter/29porter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/25/books/review/29porter/29porter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lightman, Alan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Du Maurier, Daphne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lott, Bret', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:48:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jan Stuart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stuart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9b6dc89-9f0f-5440-af78-2f7b094497c6', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9b6dc89-9f0f-5440-af78-2f7b094497c6'}, {'abstract': 'Roger Crowley traces the rise and fall of Venice’s naval empire.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/city-of-fortune-how-venice-ruled-the-seas-by-roger-crowley-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'Roger Crowley traces the rise and fall of Venice’s naval empire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Medieval travelers were overwhelmed by Venice’s affluence — the sacks of spices! the bales of brocades! — and driven to distraction by its seeming paradoxes. The city was surrounded by sand and mud, yet its markets dazzled with variety. Since there was no land to speak of, it had no feudal system, yet its councils handed down orders like holy writ. Its people reveled in their republican freedom, yet bowed undemurringly to the collective good. Even the physical city defied logic: a wooden settlement perched on piles in a swampy lagoon had turned into the densest urban area in Europe, its brick bell towers jostling for airspace, its stone palaces squatting on reclaimed land. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-CLIFF/29-CLIFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 228, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-CLIFF/29-CLIFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 228}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-CLIFF/29-CLIFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-CLIFF/29-CLIFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-CLIFF/29-CLIFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Reign of Venice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Water Everywhere', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venice (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:49:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nigel Cliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nigel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/57cb1acb-b26c-5188-bb59-901b93add21c', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57cb1acb-b26c-5188-bb59-901b93add21c'}, {'abstract': 'How one semiautomatic became our weapon of choice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/glock-the-rise-of-americas-gun-by-paul-m-barrett-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'How one semiautomatic became our weapon of choice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You rarely pull a gun to start a conversation, but when I recently told an in-law that I was writing about Glock pistols, he improvised a research project. Reaching beneath his jacket, he quickly unholstered, unloaded and handed me his Glock 9 millimeter — this was in Kentucky, land of permissive \xadconcealed-carry laws. “I always carry this, and I always will,” he said before giving me a primer that could have been used in a promotional video for both the pistol and “Glock: The Rise of America’s Gun,” Paul M. Barrett’s engaging if uneven history of the most famous handgun in contemporary America. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-WASHBURN/29-WASHBURN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 787, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-WASHBURN/29-WASHBURN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-WASHBURN/29-WASHBURN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Favorite Weapon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Business End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barrett, Paul M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:50:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Washburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Washburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/38430405-5cb5-579b-99e9-1a22b283296d', 'word_count': 661, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38430405-5cb5-579b-99e9-1a22b283296d'}, {'abstract': 'In defending his stance on marriage equality, the New Jersey governor made a very odd comment about the civil rights movement.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/chris-christie-revisionist-historian/', 'snippet': 'In defending his stance on marriage equality, the New Jersey governor made a very odd comment about the civil rights movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some readers thought I was a little hard on Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey this week. On Wednesday I wrote about his decision to veto any law guaranteeing marriage rights to all state residents. He said he thought the issue should be left up to voters. And I said that civil rights don’t belong on a ballot. Besides, referendums are a bad way to govern a state. Check out California sometime.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chris Christie, Revisionist Historian', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:50:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfaa49c1-6137-503c-9d1a-4c0400ab459a', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfaa49c1-6137-503c-9d1a-4c0400ab459a'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir by Zakes Mda, a writer who has flourished in democratizing South Africa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/sometimes-there-is-a-void-memoirs-of-an-outsider-by-zakes-mda-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir by Zakes Mda, a writer who has flourished in democratizing South Africa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Zakes MDA and J. M. Coetzee, the two most acclaimed South African novelists of the past 20 years, could be writing about different countries — and, in a way, they are. Coetzee excels at sparsely populated fictions of gnawing interiority. His socially uneasy and anguished solitaries, often academics like himself, wrestle with psychological dilemmas and metaphysical maladies. In contrast, Mda’s greatest gift is his Dickensian social range, his ability to generate characters from diverse backgrounds, colluding and colliding across the barriers erected to divide them. Where Coetzee is drawn to existential hinterlands, Mda takes us deep into his country’s literal interior, those rural regions that, in most South African writing, are pushed to the margins.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/NIXON/NIXON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1009, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/NIXON/NIXON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/NIXON/NIXON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Memories of South Africa During and After Apartheid', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Apartheid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mda, Zakes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:51:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Nixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9259674f-1b2d-549f-a7f4-d7366ea57b01', 'word_count': 1195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9259674f-1b2d-549f-a7f4-d7366ea57b01'}, {'abstract': 'The troubled women in Francesca Kay’s novel are caught up in strange happenings at a South London church.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/the-translation-of-the-bones-by-francesca-kay-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'The troubled women in Francesca Kay’s novel are caught up in strange happenings at a South London church.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stella Morrison is an elegant woman: an accomplished hostess for her financier-turned-member-of-Parliament husband, a devoted but not doting mother, tastefully devout in her religious beliefs. She’s also beguiling to the opposite sex, one of whom regards her charm as “will-o’-the-wisp and indefinable, all strands and tendrils and impossible to pin down.” Yet Stella is unhappy, trapped in a loveless marriage, pining for her children, all of whom have left home. Getting ready for yet another dinner party, she thinks of herself as “a priestess in an Attic temple preparing for sacrifice.” This unsettling combination of magnificence and pathos suffuses Fran\xadcesca Kay’s second novel, “The Translation of the Bones.” (Her first, “An Equal Stillness,” won Britain’s Orange Award for New Writers.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-THOMPSON/29-THOMPSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 910, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-THOMPSON/29-THOMPSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/29-THOMPSON/29-THOMPSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Will to Believe', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Waiting for a Sign', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:51:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrea Thompson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thompson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/44b66e5f-5276-570f-917f-98b76268b52c', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44b66e5f-5276-570f-917f-98b76268b52c'}, {'abstract': 'A sense of history links the characters in Elliot Perlman’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/books/review/the-street-sweeper-by-elliot-perlman-book-review.html', 'snippet': 'A sense of history links the characters in Elliot Perlman’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Any beginning writer could find both instruction and inspiration in Elliot Perlman’s new novel, “The Street Sweeper.” If it were tricked out with commentary in the margins — in fact, now that I’ve finished marking it up, anyone’s welcome to take my copy — it could serve as a textbook on how not to write fiction. At the same time, it gives the lie to those killjoy teachers who tell you that no reputable publisher will touch your work if you don’t learn your craft. This 600-plus-page epic turns out to have only a novella’s worth of story and substance: the rest is repetition, over and over, of basic information about who the characters are, where they are and what’s going on, long expository and hortatory speeches that no real person would ever deliver and the padded, ping-ponging dialogue beloved of amateur writers. “ ‘And your family?’ ‘My family?’ ” “ ‘I’ve always felt kind of . . . fraudulent.’ ‘Fraudulent?’ ”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Gates/Gates-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 859, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Gates/Gates-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/books/review/Gates/Gates-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Connecting the Lowly and Mighty in New York', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Collective Memory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perlman, Elliot', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:51:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e56af89a-f00e-57f4-b102-9df877501d7d', 'word_count': 1424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e56af89a-f00e-57f4-b102-9df877501d7d'}, {'abstract': 'The latest episode was the third of its kind this week, activist groups say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/world/asia/chinese-police-fire-on-tibetan-protesters-again.html', 'snippet': 'The latest episode was the third of its kind this week, activist groups say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — The police in the western Chinese province of Sichuan fired on Tibetan protesters for the third time this week, killing at least one and injuring several, overseas Tibetan activist groups said on Friday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese Police Fire on Tibetan Protesters Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Police Shoot Tibetan Protesters, Activists Say', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tibet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sichuan Province (China)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:56:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/291b9819-1a0a-5020-a317-47da6c0bcdbb', 'word_count': 453, 'uri': 'nyt://article/291b9819-1a0a-5020-a317-47da6c0bcdbb'}, {'abstract': 'The attention to Tim Tebow’s on-field displays of religiosity is the latest example of the way the sport reflects the culture in this country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/us/cultural-conflicts-playing-out-on-the-football-field.html', 'snippet': 'The attention to Tim Tebow’s on-field displays of religiosity is the latest example of the way the sport reflects the culture in this country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After his team was routed by the New England Patriots this month, driving the Denver Broncos out of the N.F.L. playoffs, Tim Tebow jogged off the field. Camera crews and photographers surrounded him, waiting for Mr. Tebow, the quarterback, to drop to one knee and bow his head in prayer, his famous and controversial signature gesture.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/us/RELIGION-1/RELIGION-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/us/RELIGION-1/RELIGION-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/28/us/RELIGION-1/RELIGION-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cultural Conflicts, Playing Out on the Football Field', 'kicker': 'On Religion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cultural Conflicts, Playing Out on the Football Field', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prayers and Prayer Books', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Denver Broncos', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T15:57:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel G. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/469f12fa-07bd-52f1-ac8e-ac5e064051a2', 'word_count': 1043, 'uri': 'nyt://article/469f12fa-07bd-52f1-ac8e-ac5e064051a2'}, {'abstract': 'Leonard Cohen reckons with himself, with God and maybe with Satan in his new album, “Old Ideas.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/arts/music/leonard-cohen-reckons-with-god-in-old-ideas.html', 'snippet': 'Leonard Cohen reckons with himself, with God and maybe with Satan in his new album, “Old Ideas.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LEONARD COHEN generally takes his time. He sings, speaks, writes and records at his own measured pace. Concerts on his recent world tour have stretched to three hours. His new album, “Old Ideas,” is his first studio album since “Dear Heather” in 2004. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29COHEN2_SPAN/29COHEN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29COHEN2_SPAN/29COHEN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29COHEN2_SPAN/29COHEN2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29COHEN2_SPAN/29COHEN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29COHEN2_SPAN/29COHEN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Final Reckonings, a Tuneful Fedora and Forgiveness', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Final Reckonings, A Tuneful Fedora And Forgiveness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Leonard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date'