ave found that those costs can be offset by energy efficiency measures paid for by the cap-and-trade program itself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reaping a Bonus From Cap-and-Trade', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Delaware', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T16:37:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8aba631e-d95e-528f-8197-9e41023aadae', 'word_count': 340, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8aba631e-d95e-528f-8197-9e41023aadae'}, {'abstract': 'An increase in the number of students who qualify for free tutoring this year has led New York City to narrow its eligibility requirements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/01/27/city-limits-free-tutoring-to-the-poorest-students/', 'snippet': 'An increase in the number of students who qualify for free tutoring this year has led New York City to narrow its eligibility requirements.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Limits Free Tutoring to the Poorest Students', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T16:40:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By ANNA M. PHILLIPS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/649cb735-c94b-5b43-9035-20768f1d7745', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/649cb735-c94b-5b43-9035-20768f1d7745'}, {'abstract': 'Under popular pressure, Chinese officials have begun to track the most pernicious measure of urban air pollution and promise to set new health standards and publish data on air pollution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/world/asia/internet-criticism-pushes-china-to-act-on-air-pollution.html', 'snippet': 'Under popular pressure, Chinese officials have begun to track the most pernicious measure of urban air pollution and promise to set new health standards and publish data on air pollution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Weary of waiting for the authorities to alert residents to the city’s most pernicious air pollutant, citizen activists last May took matters here into their own hands: they bought their own $4,000 air-quality monitor and started posting its daily readings on the Internet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/world/28china/28china-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/28/world/28china/28china-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/world/28china/28china-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo