 '2012-01-27T17:18:10+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/a958bcf8-dd86-5898-98bc-499f7bba1eca', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/a958bcf8-dd86-5898-98bc-499f7bba1eca'}, {'abstract': 'East London may be the shining new home of the Olympics, but it is also a collection of vivid neighborhoods where artists, immigrants and young professionals live and mingle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/travel/touring-londons-east-end.html', 'snippet': 'East London may be the shining new home of the Olympics, but it is also a collection of vivid neighborhoods where artists, immigrants and young professionals live and mingle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EVEN on uncharacteristically sunny days, it’s a lonely walk from the closest tube stop to London’s forthcoming 500-acre Olympic Park. But after reaching the 80,000-seat Olympic Stadium — skeleton in place and awaiting its exterior sheeting — I found hundreds of other construction-site rubberneckers clogging the sidewalk.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/travel/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/travel/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/travel/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN/29JOURNEYS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/travel/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/travel/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN/29JOURNEYS1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Olympic Preview in London’s East End', 'kicker': 'Journeys', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'London’s East End, an Olympics Canvas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T17:19:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02f6dd30-f706-5bac-a5e6-7b975812cf2d', 'word_count': 1296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02f6dd30-f706-5bac-a5e6-7b975812cf2d'}, {'abstract': 'Talking with two of the writers up for an adapted-screenplay Oscar for “The Descendants.”', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/making-the-descendants-funny-and-de-funnying-it-too/', 'snippet': 'Talking with two of the writers up for an adapted-screenplay Oscar for “The Descendants.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you were taken with the wry tone and occasionally absurd laugh lines of “The Descendants,” you can tip your hat to the co-writers, Nat Faxon and Jim Rash. The pair are comedians and performers: Mr. Rash plays Dean Pelton on the (we hope not canceled) NBC sitcom “Community,” and Mr. Faxon has appeared in “Bad Teacher” and “Zookeeper.” They met at the Groundlings, the storied Los Angeles comedy troupe, around 2000 and became writing partners, first producing a TV pilot based on Mr. Rash’s experiences as an adoptee (it was quickly canceled). “The Descendants,” based on Kaui Hart Hemmings’s novel of the same name, is their first produced screenplay, and it earned them an Oscar nomination for best adapted screenplay.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making ‘The Descendants’ Funny — and De-Funnying It, Too', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clooney, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forster, Robert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frears, Stephen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hemmings, Kaui Hart', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Payne, Alexander', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Jim', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woodley, Shailene', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T17:21:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc1e0bb5-3891-5458-b72b-1c435fada529', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc1e0bb5-3891-5458-b72b-1c435fada529'}, {'abstract': 'In a hidden corner of the city, a neighborhood bistro like the ones you probably assumed disappeared from the landscape long ago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/travel/restaurant-review-le-grand-pan-in-paris.html', 'snippet': 'In a hidden corner of the city, a neighborhood bistro like the ones you probably assumed disappeared from the landscape long ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It can take a bit of effort to get to Le Grand Pan, especially if you’re a Métro gal like me. You take the No. 12 line deep into the most boring part of the 15th Arrondissement, then walk for a good 10 to 15 minutes. The first time I took my husband, he asked, “Are you sure you know where we’re going?”', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/travel/29BITES/29BITES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 717, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/travel/29BITES/29BITES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/travel/29BITES/29BITES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Restaurant Review: Le Grand Pan, in Paris', 'kicker': 'Bites', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paris: Le Gran Pan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T17:22:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Sciolino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sciolino', 