cument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ca8bab5-5213-5074-a854-2692ddfb8eb1', 'word_count': 1272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ca8bab5-5213-5074-a854-2692ddfb8eb1'}, {'abstract': 'FirstEnergy says it would not be cost-effective to install scrubbing technology at aging plants to meet new federal emissions rules.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/on-our-radar-6-more-coal-plants-to-close/', 'snippet': 'FirstEnergy says it would not be cost-effective to install scrubbing technology at aging plants to meet new federal emissions rules.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FirstEnergy says it will retire six older coal-fired power plants in Ohio, Pennsylvania and Maryland by Sept. 1 because of tighter federal rules on emissions. [The Akron Beacon Journal]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: 6 More Coal Plants to Close', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T17:45:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9769986c-3dbe-58fe-aafd-bb9c2956ac9f', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9769986c-3dbe-58fe-aafd-bb9c2956ac9f'}, {'abstract': 'A survey shows there is widespread agreement around the world on one point: the current level of business regulation is not right.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/business/survey-takes-publics-pulse-on-business-regulations.html', 'snippet': 'A survey shows there is widespread agreement around the world on one point: the current level of business regulation is not right.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMERICANS are split almost down the middle on the issue of whether there is too much regulation of business or too little. Residents of most other countries are more likely to think more regulation is needed.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Public Sees Business Rules', 'kicker': 'Off the Charts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How the Public Sees Business Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Edelman', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T17:54:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/883c5dbf-d4b2-523f-bb20-46d83e7f5058', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/883c5dbf-d4b2-523f-bb20-46d83e7f5058'}, {'abstract': 'One teacher finds a path from the United States to Afghanistan and discovers classrooms vastly changed since the time of Taliban control.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/changes-in-kabul-classrooms/', 'snippet': 'One teacher finds a path from the United States to Afghanistan and discovers classrooms vastly changed since the time of Taliban control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eleven years ago, if you walked into a classroom in Kabul, this is what you might have seen: all boys; Korans resting on every desk; men leading prayers and study; only religious subjects being taught at every school and university in the country; students wearing uniforms of payraan tumbaan (long shirts over baggy pants) and turbans; empty chairs pushed to the back of the classrooms where girls once sat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Changes in Kabul Classrooms', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private and Sectarian Schools', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kabul (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T17:57:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jaala A. Thibault', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jaala', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Thibault', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f5e0440-1df5-5341-ab4a-7dca74b1f961', 'word_count': 1172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f5e0440-1df5-5341-ab4a-7dca74b1f961'}, {'abstract': '“The Broken Heart” and “ ’Tis Pity She’s a Whore,” blood-soaked dramas by the 17th-century playwright John Ford, are landing in New York in the coming weeks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/theater/john-fords-broken-heart-and-tis-pity-shes-a-whore.html', 'snippet': '“The Broken Heart” and “ ’Tis Pity She’s a Whore,” blood-soaked dramas by the 17th-century playwright John Ford, are landing in New York in the coming weeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE 17th-century playwright John Ford never met a character he didn’t want to kill: gruesomely, ingeniously, poignantly. He liked them stabbed, starved, poisoned, burnt, bled and assaulted by roving packs of bandits. His two best plays — both having forthcoming New York revivals — boast body counts nearly as long as the cast lists. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29SUBFORD2/29SUBFORD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29SUBFORD2/29SUBFORD2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29SUBFORD2/29SUBFORD2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 641, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29SUBFORD2/29SUBFORD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/arts/29SUBFORD2/29SUBFORD2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Extreme Theater: Wake-Up Calls From the 1600s', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Extreme Theater: Wake-Up Calls From the 1600s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Theater for a New Audience', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cheek by Jowl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T18:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f0c1ecf-5a3c-534a-a2a1-08b694fcef4d', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f0c1ecf-5a3c-534a-a2a1-08b694fcef4d'}, {'abstract': 'Once upon a time, there were physical places where you could buy physical objects with music physically engraved onto them. One was called Bleecker Bob’s. It’s closing now. Tell us what you found there, and what it felt like.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/what-did-you-buy-at-bleecker-bobs/', 'snippet': 'Once upon a time, there were physical places where you could buy physical objects with music physically engraved onto them. One was called Bleecker Bob’s. It’s closing now. Tell us what you found there, and what it felt like.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the more primitive features, historians and old people will recall, of the era before recorded music could be summoned on command from a tap like water or electricity, was this: not only was music physically engraved onto physical objects (made of plastic, though it might as well have been stone or walrus tusk), but these physical objects could be acquired only from specific physical locations — yard sales, mail-order houses or, more usually, those boxy fluorescent-lit places smelling of shrink-wrap and cardboard dust known as record stores. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Did You Buy at Bleecker Bob’s?', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenwich Village (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T18:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3663f2e3-0bd9-5cae-af6f-3e37caf7be47', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3663f2e3-0bd9-5cae-af6f-3e37caf7be47'}, {'abstract': 'Pacific Standard Time is now neck deep in its eleven day Performance and Public Art Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/out-there-encore-performances/', 'snippet': 'Pacific Standard Time is now neck deep in its eleven day Performance and Public Art Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just about halfway through its marathon, six-month broad-based excavation of Southern California’s formative postwar art scene, Pacific Standard Time is now neck deep in its 11-day Performance and Public Art Festival. Though the menu includes plenty of new commissions, many events are re-creations of ephemeral works from the 1960s and ’70s, and some of these have been among the most evocative moments of the whole shebang.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Out There | Encore Performances', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chicago, Judy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turrell, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pomona College', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pomona (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T18:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 