 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/a5a7835c-dbc8-5119-9f91-b55f297950ec', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a5a7835c-dbc8-5119-9f91-b55f297950ec'}, {'abstract': 'The art dealer, Robert Scott Cook, is accused of secretly selling a client’s works.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/madison-avenue-art-dealer-indicted-on-fraud-charges/', 'snippet': 'The art dealer, Robert Scott Cook, is accused of secretly selling a client’s works.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A Madison Avenue art dealer, Robert Scott Cook, has been charged with defrauding a client of 16 artworks by Picasso, Manet, Matisse, Renoir and others worth more than $4.2 million, the United States Attorney’s Office in Manhattan announced on Thursday. Over a period of six years Mr. Cook, the principal of Cook Fine Art, secretly sold watercolors, drawings and photographs to galleries and auction houses behind the owner’s back, according to the attorney’s office. The F.B.I.’s assistant director in charge in New York, Janice K. Fedarcyk, said the charge “is that Mr. Cook is a crook.” If convicted he faces a maximum of 20 years in jail and a $250,000 fine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Madison Avenue Art Dealer Faces Fraud Charges', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manet, Edouard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Matisse, Henri', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Picasso, Pablo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Robert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 6, 'major':