in tropical regions poses a threat to animals like the dugong, which is similar to a manatee, and the Atlantic humpback dolphin, researchers write.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dolphin for dinner? It may be more common than you think. The first global study on human consumption of marine mammals found that the practice is widespread and is becoming more prevalent in many tropical regions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Marine Mammals Become Food', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mammals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Read, Andrew', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wildlife Conservation Society', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indian Ocean', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madagascar', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sri Lanka', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T18:46:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas M. Main', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://