'word_count': 537, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c19e3f6-b45c-5c05-8a1b-961691cc29f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Scottish government recently passed a law making it illegal for fans to attack each other using religious or ethnic slurs in chants, Internet postings and even stray remarks at the pub.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/sports/soccer/taking-on-soccer-violence-one-derogatory-chant-at-a-time.html', 'snippet': 'The Scottish government recently passed a law making it illegal for fans to attack each other using religious or ethnic slurs in chants, Internet postings and even stray remarks at the pub.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GLASGOW — It was a normal Saturday game at Ibrox Stadium, home of the Rangers soccer team. A prematch fight broke out on the subway. The crowd lobbed trash onto the field. Fans of Rangers and their mutually despised opponents, Aberdeen, abused one another with traditional chants, songs and obscene gestures. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/sports/28scotland3/28scotland3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/28/sports/28scotland3/28scotland3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/sports/28scotland3/28scotland3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/sports/28scotland3/28scotland3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/28/sports/28scotland3/28scotland3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking On Soccer Violence, One Derogatory Chant at a Time', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Combating Soccer Violence, One Derogatory Chant at a Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rangers (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T18:56:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Lyall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d306014-d1e9-503d-9c9c-43edb9c2f4d9', 'word_count': 1398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d306014-d1e9-503d-9c9c-43edb9c2f4d9'}, {'abstract': 'Aesa’s new bracelet was inspired by the dreams of an ill-fated queen.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/the-get-aesa-cuff/', 'snippet': 'Aesa’s new bracelet was inspired by the dreams of an ill-fated queen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Aesa designer Randi Mates was fantasizing about fantasies — Marie Antoinette’s, to be exact — when she designed this cuff. As part of a collection called “Antoinette’s Dream,” the bracelet is what Mates imagines her muse thinking of during her daily public dressings. Call it an escapist’s accessory. Made of hand-wrought bronze, it features one real, semi-spherical mabe pearl and a second hand-hewn brass one that “balances the overall form and calls even more attention to the undulations of the real pearl,” Mates says. “Both jewels look ancient, futuristic and contemporary all at once.” Imagine that.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Get | Aesa Cuff', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T19:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mallory Schlau', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mallory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schlau', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d534847-ef0f-5537-8bd3-886a2819cfb7', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d534847-ef0f-5537-8bd3-886a2819cfb7'}, {'abstract': 'Costa Rica is the only team standing between the United States and a berth in the Olympic women’s soccer tournament.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/costa-rica-blocking-path-to-london-for-u-s-women/', 'snippet': 'Costa Rica is the only team standing between the United States and a berth in the Olympic women’s soccer tournament.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States women’s soccer team could provide a riveting story line at the 2012 London Olympics, given the household names of players like Hope Solo and Abby Wambach, and the galvanizing performance the Americans displayed last year at the World Cup. But first comes the urgent matter of qualifying for the Games. Jeré Longman previews Friday night’s win-or-else Concacaf qualifying tournament semifinal. Read the article, then come back to comment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Costa Rica Blocking Path to London for U.S. Women', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T19:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': '