en to the public on Saturday and Sunday from 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chelsea Piers, 12:04 P.M.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greenpeace', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T22:39:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9a4c1f5-85bb-5949-ba2a-4f7dd606c91e', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9a4c1f5-85bb-5949-ba2a-4f7dd606c91e'}, {'abstract': 'Yesterday, with little hoopla, Washington raised the debt limit again.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/when-is-a-debt-not-a-debt-when-republicans-need-a-propaganda-tool/', 'snippet': 'Yesterday, with little hoopla, Washington raised the debt limit again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his State of the Union address the other day, President Obama made a powerful point about the political nonsense that has been consuming Washington. “The greatest blow to confidence in our economy last year didn’t come from events beyond our control,” he said. “It came from a debate in Washington over whether the United States would pay its bills.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Is a Debt Not a Debt? When Republicans Need a Propaganda Tool', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T22:42:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37c4646e-ed12-597b-b0a5-886417d2f93c', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37c4646e-ed12-597b-b0a5-886417d2f93c'}, {'abstract': 'By 180 to 86, the cable television employees decided to join the Communication Workers of America, despite a vigorous management campaign to reject the affiliation.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/cablevision-workers-in-brooklyn-vote-to-join-union/', 'snippet': 'By 180 to 86, the cable television employees decided to join the Communication Workers of America, despite a vigorous management campaign to reject the affiliation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a 180-to-86 vote on Thursday, Brooklyn’s Cablevision workers unionized and became members of Communications Workers of America Local 1109.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cablevision Workers in Brooklyn Vote to Join Union', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cablevision Systems Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communications Workers of America', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T22:43:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Reeve', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reeve', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f71eaa1-