 '', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'Joanna Nikas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nikas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/901bebdc-a535-51ea-8fca-3c8c75c0ffb0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/901bebdc-a535-51ea-8fca-3c8c75c0ffb0'}, {'abstract': 'The banks that are engaged in a Manhattan land grab seem to be in the business of taking our money but not in the business of giving it back.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/opinion/banks-taketh-but-dont-giveth.html', 'snippet': 'The banks that are engaged in a Manhattan land grab seem to be in the business of taking our money but not in the business of giving it back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANKS are eating up all the real estate in my neighborhood. I live on a basically residential street, and within three and a half short blocks of my house are eight banks: two Chase, one Wells Fargo, one Citi, one HSBC, one Bank of America, one Sovereign and one Capital One. Go two more blocks and there are 10 banks (one more Chase and one more Citi).', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/opinion/0128OPEDmenchin/0128OPEDmenchin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/opinion/0128OPEDmenchin/0128OPEDmenchin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/28/opinion/0128OPEDmenchin/0128OPEDmenchin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Banks Taketh, but Don’t Giveth', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Banks Taketh, but Don’t Giveth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Savings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T23:54:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Delia Ephron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Delia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ephron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6633321-42d9-5f02-bfe3-1f6feb665030', 'word_count': 803, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6633321-42d9-5f02-bfe3-1f6feb665030'}, {'abstract': 'Halfway through his first season, Ludovic Morlot of the Seattle Symphony makes his presence known about town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/arts/music/ludovic-morlot-stands-out-at-the-seattle-symphony.html', 'snippet': 'Halfway through his first season, Ludovic Morlot of the Seattle Symphony makes his presence known about town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — At a cocktail party on Wednesday evening in an art-filled home in the fashionable Capitol Hill neighborhood here, Andrew Russell, the 28-year-old artistic director of the Intiman Theater, addressed a small crowd in front of a picture window and unexpectedly mentioned Ludovic Morlot. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Symphony’s Leader Takes Seattle by Storm', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Symphony’s Leader Takes Seattle by Storm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Seattle (Wash)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morlot, Ludovic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intiman Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Symphony', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-27T23:57:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd042cb5-eaa0-59cc-b5d8-b620cb8c861c', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd042cb5-eaa0-59cc-b5d8-b620cb8c861c'}, {'abstract': 'Gary L. Sharpe’s decision in federal court in Albany could mean New Yorkers have primaries in April for president, June for Congress and September for legislators.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/judge-moves-congressional-primary-date-to-june/', 'snippet': 'Gary L. Sharpe’s decision in federal court in Albany could mean New Yorkers have primaries in April for president, June for Congress and September for legislators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge ordered New York State on Friday to move its primary elections for Congressional races to June from September, creating a major complication for the state’s election calendar.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judge Moves Congressional Primary Date to June', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T00:01:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88da8c72-0655-5eaf-998d-11016cca168c', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88da8c72-0655-5eaf-998d-11016cca168c'}, {'abstract': 'Among the feature films presented at the somewhat lower-key 28th Sundance Film Festival, there was “Beasts of the Southern Wild,” and there was everything else.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/movies/at-sundance-beasts-of-the-southern-wild-is-standout.html', 'snippet': 'Among the feature films presented at the somewhat lower-key 28th Sundance Film Festival, there was “Beasts of the Southern Wild,” and there was everything else.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah — Now in its 28th edition, the Sundance Film Festival has eased into a mellower groove, hallelujah and pass the parka. Despite the predictable hazards and hassles, like slip-sliding on black ice and waiting for shuttles in the frigid cold, it no longer is the nightmare it had become back when Paris Hilton and frat types descended. The economic downturn plays a part — there are fewer self-important industry players crowding the scene — as does the affable presence of the festival’s director, John Cooper, who took over in 2009. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/arts/28FESTIVAL1/28FESTIVAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/28/arts/28FESTIVAL1/28FESTIVAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/arts/28FESTIVAL1/28FESTIVAL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/arts/28FESTIVAL1/28FESTIVAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/28/arts/28FESTIVAL1/28FESTIVAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amazing Child, Typical Grown-Ups', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At a Subtler Sundance, One Film Sparkles: Amazing Child, Typical Grown-Ups', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T00:05:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef405df5-0c5c-52df-bd30-de636d77e571', 'word_count': 1574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef405df5-0c5c-52df-bd30-de636d77e571'}, {'abstract': 'Nkeiru Okoye created “Songs of Harriet Tubman,” arias to be performed in “A Ride on the Underground Railroad” at the John Cranford Adams Playhouse at Hofstra University.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/nyregion/a-ride-on-the-underground-railroad-at-hofstra-recalls-harriet-tubman.html', 'snippet': 'Nkeiru Okoye created “Songs of Harriet Tubman,” arias to be performed in “A Ride on the Underground Railroad” at the John Cranford Adams Playhouse at Hofstra University.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN Nkeiru Okoye, a composer who lives in Massapequa, decided to move beyond the mostly orchestral works she had written, she set out to create some vocal music.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSLI_SPAN/29ARTSLI_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSLI_SPAN/29ARTSLI_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSLI_SPAN/29ARTSLI2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSLI_SPAN/29ARTSLI_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSLI_SPAN/29ARTSLI_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Songs, Some Coded, of Liberation', 'kicker': 'Arts | Long Island', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Songs, Some Coded, Of Liberation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tubman, Harriet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T00:05:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karin Lipson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/469e98fb-f7ae-5d95-abaa-25abb10e1b88', 'word_count': 863, 'uri': 'nyt://article/469e98fb-f7ae-5d95-abaa-25abb10e1b88'}, {'abstract': 'News Corporation is said to be in advanced discussions with Lex Fenwick to become chief executive of Dow Jones, publisher of The Wall Street Journal, Barron’s and the Dow Jones Newswires.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/bloomberg-executive-is-said-to-be-in-talks-to-lead-dow-jones/', 'snippet': 'News Corporation is said to be in advanced discussions with Lex Fenwick to become chief executive of Dow Jones, publisher of The Wall Street Journal, Barron’s and the Dow Jones Newswires.', 'lead_paragraph': 'News Corporation is said to be in advanced talks to hire Lex Fenwick as the chief executive of Dow Jones, filling a six-month vacancy created when the publisher’s top executive resigned amid the phone-hacking scandal in Britain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bloomberg Executive Is Said to Be in Talks to Lead Dow Jones', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hinton, Les', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dow Jones & Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News International Ltd', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T00:12:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fc68a22-4039-54ae-8eb0-4e61fdbd7aa2', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fc68a22-4039-54ae-8eb0-4e61fdbd7aa2'}, {'abstract': 'At the Australian Open, Novak Djokovic and Andy Murray played a brutal, five-set match, and Djokovic’s aggression was a major factor in his victory.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/in-a-grueling-match-djokovic-dominates-murrays-second-serve/', 'snippet': 'At the Australian Open, Novak Djokovic and Andy Murray played a brutal, five-set match, and Djokovic’s aggression was a major factor in his victory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A more aggressive Andy Murray battled world No. 1 Novak Djokovic for five sets but could not quite will himself over the finish line to score the upset. It was a physically brutal match, with both men going through patches in the match where fatigue and pain lowered their level of play. The match lasted nearly five hours, with each player committed to playing long, grinding baseline points that featured clean hitting, pinpoint accuracy, and breathtaking pace.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Grueling Match, Djokovic Dominates Murray’s Second Serve', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T00:29:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By GEOFF MACDONALD', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MACDONALD', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank':