Fertel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fertel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/35b949ee-6533-56b1-9a9c-338d388e04a8', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35b949ee-6533-56b1-9a9c-338d388e04a8'}, {'abstract': 'The Outsider Art Fair celebrates self-taught artists with a compulsion to create.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/arts/the-20th-anniversary-of-the-outsider-art-fair.html', 'snippet': 'The Outsider Art Fair celebrates self-taught artists with a compulsion to create.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s all agree that “outsider artist” is a term of convenience, encompassing the self-taught, the visionary, the geographically isolated, the mentally ill or developmentally disabled, and (in one memorable episode) Homer Simpson. And in that spirit, we must judge the Outsider Art Fair, now celebrating its 20th anniversary, not by its title but by the vitality and variety of works on offer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/arts/28FAIR/28FAIR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/arts/28FAIR/28FAIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/28/arts/28FAIR/28FAIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Creating Artworks Without a Net', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Creating Artworks Without a Net', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Darger, Henry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ramirez, Martin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Outsider Art Fair', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T00:34:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b42a7cc8-c8b3-5400-ba55-f90fb23d5813', 'word_count': 739, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b42a7cc8-c8b3-5400-ba55-f90fb23d5813'}, {'abstract': 'Subjects include the first snowfall in 2012, a great apartment deal in the West Village and a subway track that doubles as a wishing well.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/the-week-in-pictures-for-jan-27/', 'snippet': 'Subjects include the first snowfall in 2012, a great apartment deal in the West Village and a subway track that doubles as a wishing well.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here is a slide show of photographs from the past week in New York City and the region. Subjects include the first snowfall in 2012, a great apartment deal in the West Village and a subway track that doubles as a wishing well.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Week in Pictures for Jan. 27', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T00:40:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f364d43-bbf2-5dc8-bd91-f7788e94017b', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f364d43-bbf2-5dc8-bd91-f7788e94017b'}, {'abstract': '“Boeing-Boeing” is at the Paper Mill Playhouse. The appeal of the play today is the amusing look at the peculiar customs of a misguided era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/nyregion/in-boeing-boeing-the-60s-as-a-misguided-era-review.html', 'snippet': '“Boeing-Boeing” is at the Paper Mill Playhouse. The appeal of the play today is the amusing look at the peculiar customs of a misguided era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The word “stewardess” is never spoken in the Paper Mill Playhouse’s bubbly new production of “Boeing-Boeing.” But neither are the words “flight attendant,” because the play is set long, long ago in the 1960s. Instead the three young, sexy, female airline employees who all live in the same apartment in Paris (but don’t realize it) are referred to as “air hostesses.”', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSNJ4_SPAN/29ARTSNJ4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSNJ4_SPAN/29ARTSNJ4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSNJ4_SPAN/29ARTSNJ4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/nyregion/29ARTSNJ4_SPAN/29ARTSNJ4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'c