theft was not arrested.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The white van parked on East Houston Street as it does most mornings. The sign on the back read, “Follow This Truck for the Best Chopped Liver in New York.” The driver, Pablo Allende, a muscular 44-year-old, stepped out carrying a box of lunches from the Second Avenue Deli and approached an unmarked door. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/28/nyregion/CRIMESCENE/CRIMESCENE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Crime Apparently in Progress, and a Man Who Had Seen Enough', 'kicker': 'Crime Scene', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Crime Apparently in Progress, and a Man Who Had Seen Enough', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Second Avenue Deli', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Good Samaritans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T02:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3618cf73-87c3-506f-937d-9d1fa77cbba5', 'word_count': 814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3618cf73-87c3-506f-937d-9d1fa77cbba5'}, {'abstract': 'Storytelling was at the core of Lincoln’s character, and humor an essential outlet for the president.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/27/lincoln-tells-a-story/', 'snippet': 'Storytelling was at the core of Lincoln’s character, and humor an essential outlet for the president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Tuesday, Jan. 28, 1862, George Templeton Strong, New York lawyer and philanthropist, and Henry Ward Bellows, Unitarian minister and president of the United States Sanitary Commission, called on the president to discuss reform of the Army medical bureau. The next day, Strong, an inveterate diarist, wrote at length about the meeting, and included a Lincoln story in dialect so as to capture the president’s diction. The elitist Strong described the backwoods president as “a barbarian, Scythian, yahoo, or gorilla, in respect to outside polish (for example, he uses “humans” as English for homines), but a most sensible, straightforward, honest old codger.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lincoln Tells a Story', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emancipation Proclamation (1863)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T02:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louis P. Masur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louis', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Masur', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec8f88c1-62d6-56fc-8341-5b9c10fe479b', 'word_count': 1572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec8f88c1-62d6-56fc-8341-5b9c10fe479b'}, {'abstract': 'Twitter’s announcement that it would agree to block certain messages in countries where they were deemed illegal prompted outcry, argument and even calls for a boycott.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/technology/when-twitter-blocks-tweets-its-outrage.html', 'snippet': 'Twitter’s announcement that it would agree to block certain messages in countries where they were deemed illegal prompted outcry, argument and even calls for a boycott.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — It started five years ago after a young engineer in San Francisco sketched out a quirky little Web tool for telling your friends what you were up to. It became a bullhorn for millions of people worldwide, especially vital in nations that tend to muzzle their own people.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/us/TWITTER/TWITTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/us/TWITTER/TWITTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/28/us/TWITTER/TWITTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Censoring of Tweets Sets Off #Outrage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Twitter Blocks Tweets, It’s #Outrage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boycotts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T02:02:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Somini Sengupta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Somini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sengupta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd6d8772-69f5-54b4-809d-5cb523445df7', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd6d8772-69f5-54b4-809d-5cb523445df7'}, {'abstract': 'Written by an adviser to the Republican candidate Mitt Romney, the book, “The World America Made,” makes the case that the nation’s decline is a myth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/us/politics/obamas-theme-of-us-resilience-finds-support-in-new-book.html', 'snippet': 'Written by an adviser to the Republican candidate Mitt Romney, the book, “The World America Made,” makes the case that the nation’s decline is a myth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — When Senator Barack Obama was photographed clutching a copy of “The Post-American World” as he left his campaign plane during the Democratic primaries in May 2008, some critics viewed it as a telling sign that he embraced a view of the United States as a waning world power.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/us/politics/28obama-slide-T4YV/28obama-slide-T4YV-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Buttresses Case for U.S. Resilience With Book From Unlikely Source', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Buttresses Case for U.S. Resilience With Book From Unlikely Source', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kagan, Robert', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T02:05:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8457bd5e-74a0-525f-8835-a1f50b8e91e0', 'word_count': 1034, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8457bd5e-74a0-525f-8835-a1f50b8e91e0'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama finally pushed back against the pro-fossil fuel, anti-regulatory crowd. Now he has to keep it up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/opinion/in-defense-of-clean-energy.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama finally pushed back against the pro-fossil fuel, anti-regulatory crowd. Now he has to keep it up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After months of standing by as Republicans tried to gut environmental laws and discredit his clean energy initiatives, President Obama took the offensive in his State of the Union address. He blasted Republicans for their single-minded commitment to fossil fuels, argued the case for a diversified energy strategy and dared to talk about climate change, a topic he has been silent on for months. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Defense of Clean Energy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Defense of Clean Energy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Solyndra Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-28T02:06:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bb5c3f4-aba0-5e10-b16b-ee0187945b8c', 'word_count': 472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bb5c3f4-aba0-5e10-b16b-ee0187945b8c'}, {'abstract': 'The Boston Patriots bounced from stadium to stadium and played “home” games as far away as San Diego. Bizarre occurrences seemed to follow them wherever they went.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/28/sports/football/early-patriots-were-a-comical-traveling-sideshow.html', 'snippet': 'The Boston Patriots bounced from stadium to stadium and played “home” games as far away as San Diego. Bizarre occurrences seemed to follow them wherever they went.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New England Patriots play in a $325 million palatial stadium complex, their star quarterback appears on fashion billboards nationwide and the team has won three Super Bowls. They are one of the N.F.L.’s glamour franchises and a model for the prosperous professional sports operation.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/sports/jp-stadiums-1/jp-stadiums-1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 809, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/28/sports/jp-stadiums-1/jp-stadiums-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/28/sports/jp-stad