ion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d589a451-18ba-58a6-b7d6-c3b5b397bc2d', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d589a451-18ba-58a6-b7d6-c3b5b397bc2d'}, {'abstract': 'Three officers were injured and about 200 people arrested as protesters marched with the aim of taking over a vacant convention center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/us/occupy-protesters-and-police-clash-in-oakland.html', 'snippet': 'Three officers were injured and about 200 people arrested as protesters marched with the aim of taking over a vacant convention center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A march to take over a vacant building by members of the Occupy movement in Oakland, Calif., turned into a violent confrontation with the police on Saturday, leaving three officers injured and about 200 people arrested.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/us/29oakland/29oakland-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/29/us/29oakland/29oakland-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/us/29oakland/occupy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/29/us/29oakland/29oakland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/29/us/29oakland/29oakland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupy Protesters and Police Clash in Oakland', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Occupy Protesters and Police Clash in Oakland', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oakland (Calif)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-29T06:32:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Maslin Nir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': 'Maslin', 'lastname': 'Nir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0116784e-30c4-52f8-94e4-6ae269cac297', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0116784e-30c4-52f8-94e4-6ae269cac297'}, {'abstract': 'Top magazine and long-form articles this week.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/newswallah-long-reads-edition-17/', 'snippet': 'Top magazine and long-form articles this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Traversing the Bundelkhand region, traditionally considered the stronghold of Mayawati, chief minister of Uttar Pradesh, the journalist Revati Laul finds that the elephant — the symbol of the Bahujan Samaj Party (B.S.P.) — has lost its draw. In a piece titled “Dalit and the dominoes” in Tehelka, Ms. Laul analyzes the reasons behind this shift in perception and how it might affect the outcome of the state assembly elections scheduled for February. With devastating drought and farmer suicides growing at an alarming rate in her voter base, disgruntled farmers from the dalit, or untouchable, caste have begun to voice their discontent. Ms. Laul speculates that this might mean that the B.S.P. will not sweep Uttar Pradesh with the sizable majority it achieved in 2007, leading to instability in the state and a possible harbinger to an era of coalition politics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: Long Reads Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-29T10:04:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neha Thirani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thirani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/783f4bb5-57f7-5234-a53e-d7feff8d7edb', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/783f4bb5-57f7-5234-a53e-d7feff8d7edb'}, {'abstract': 'A spin around the primaries and caucuses for February.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/advantage-romney-in-february-but-risks-abound/', 'snippet': 'A spin around the primaries and caucuses for February.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Conventional wisdom holds that the handful of states that vote in February are favorable ones for Mitt Romney. Still, Mr. Romney could be vulnerable in several of them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Advantage Romney in February, but Risks Abound', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARIZONA', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maine', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nevada', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-29T12:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06dae88c-5c3d-594c-9833-c38665e8079b', 'word_count': 1438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06dae88c-5c3d-594c-9833-c38665e8079b'}, {'abstract': 'Martina Hingis, who won three Grand Slams before she turned 17, is now 31 and teaching younger players.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/sports/tennis/30iht-hingis30.html', 'snippet': 'Martina Hingis, who won three Grand Slams before she turned 17, is now 31 and teaching younger players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE — Show Court 3 was not far from empty on Friday as Yulia Putintseva and Eugenie Bouchard played in the full heat of a Melbourne summer in the semifinals of the Australian Open girls tournament.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Prodigy and Retiree (Twice), Hingis Adds Coach', 'kicker': 'Australian Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Prodigy and Retiree (Twice), Hingis Adds Coach', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hingis, Martina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-29T12:07:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ace4328-47db-5b4e-9097-30e20b90bf98', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ace4328-47db-5b4e-9097-30e20b90bf98'}, {'abstract': 'The Giants’ general manager, under a barrage of criticism in August, said simply: “We’ll make the plays this time and we’ll get into the playoffs and we’ll make a run.”', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/29/for-giants-fans-just-read-what-jerry-reese-said/', 'snippet': 'The Giants’ general manager, under a barrage of criticism in August, said simply: “We’ll make the plays this time and we’ll get into the playoffs and we’ll make a run.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Again, we won 10 games last year. It wasn’t like we were 6-10. We were 10-6. We expect to build off that. If we made a couple of plays here and there last year, we would’ve made the playoffs and who knows what would’ve happened if that were to happen, but it didn’t happen. We’ll make the plays this time and we’ll get into the playoffs and we’ll make a run.”  — Jerry Reese, Giants general manager, channeling Joe Namath and Mark Messier, on Aug. 11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Giants Fans, Just Read What Jerry Reese Said', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reese, Jerry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-29T12:57:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc43fae0-728b-5c09-a910-2cc93aab1ab9', 'word_count': 3110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc43fae0-728b-5c09-a910-2cc93aab1ab9'}, {'abstract': 'The abduction in oil-rich South Kordofan state underlined China’s risk in sending ever-greater numbers of its workers into turbulent regions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/world/asia/chinese-workers-are-reported-captured-by-sudan-rebels.html', 'snippet': 'The abduction in oil-rich South Kordofan state underlined China’s risk in sending ever-greater numbers of its workers into turbulent regions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — More than two dozen Chinese road workers have been seized by a Sudanese rebel group, Chinese and Sudanese officials said Sunday, underlining the risks for China in sending ever-greater numbers of its workers into some of the world’s most turbulent countries.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sudan Rebels Are Said to Hold Road Crew From China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sudan Rebels Said to Hold Road Crew From China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Sudan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidnapping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 3, 'major': '