acy (Myanmar)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yangon (Myanmar)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T05:51:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/038000a6-ea78-5a71-9fb6-cad7b988c890', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/038000a6-ea78-5a71-9fb6-cad7b988c890'}, {'abstract': 'On “The Good Wife,” confrontations are front and center (especially between Alicia and Peter).', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/good-wife-watch-moments-savored-loose-ends-tied-up/', 'snippet': 'On “The Good Wife,” confrontations are front and center (especially between Alicia and Peter).', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sunday night’s chapter of “The Good Wife,” titled “Another Ham Sandwich,” was like a highlight reel — an agglomeration of the sort of too-good-to-be-true moments that are usually rationed, two or three to an episode. Alicia saying to Will, as the grand jury considered indicting him, “So use me.” The ever smug David Lee telling Will: “That’s how a pro does it. You’re welcome.” The final, reassuring reveal that Kalinda was working a long con on the prosecutors rather than selling out Will, followed by Dana slapping an impassive Kalinda in the courthouse. A classic David Lee-Eli Gold confrontation. Peter finally snapping and admitting to Alicia that this entire plot line was driven by his anger over her affair.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Good Wife’ Watch: Moments Savored, Loose Ends Tied Up', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'su