'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Middle-Income Borrowers Seek Debt Help', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Cards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:06:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/081c05e4-99ba-5a48-ad1f-eb6ade775d9c', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/081c05e4-99ba-5a48-ad1f-eb6ade775d9c'}, {'abstract': 'Whether red, black or green, curly or otherwise, the many varieties of this nutritional powerhouse serve up plenty of flavor and texture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/health/nutrition/spicy-stir-fried-tofu-with-kale-and-red-pepper.html', 'snippet': 'Whether red, black or green, curly or otherwise, the many varieties of this nutritional powerhouse serve up plenty of flavor and texture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent Thursday at my neighborhood farmers’ market, one farmer had three different varieties of kale on offer – black kale (also known as cavolo nero, dinosaur kale, dragon tongue, lacinato or Tuscan kale), curly gray-green kale and red Russian kale, another ruffled variety that has red veins and a purplish color. I bought all three and had a lot of fun turning them into dinners over the next 6 days.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/science/30recipehealth/30recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/31/science/30recipehealth/30recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/science/30recipehealth/30recipehealth-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/science/30recipehealth/30recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/science/30recipehealth/30recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/science/30recipehealth/30recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/science/30recipehealth/30recipehealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'All Kinds of Kale', 'kicker': 'Recipes for Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kale (Vegetable)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:08:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'subsection_name': 'Fitness & Nutrition', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ef02b48-b509-59ef-afd1-b62f1beea409', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ef02b48-b509-59ef-afd1-b62f1beea409'}, {'abstract': 'A list of must-reads from Adam C. Smith, political editor of The Tampa Bay Times, for understanding Florida’s political environment a day before the state’s G.O.P. primary.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/the-pros-tip-sheet-adam-smith-on-floridas-political-environment/', 'snippet': 'A list of must-reads from Adam C. Smith, political editor of The Tampa Bay Times, for understanding Florida’s political environment a day before the state’s G.O.P. primary.', 'lead_paragraph': '“We’re not just The Villages,” opined reporters for The Tampa Bay Times in a list of unconventional campaign stops for Republican candidates. Florida is so much more than retirees and snowbirds (though they are a big part of the equation, to be sure). It’s the most populous primary state yet, mired in a housing bust after a population boom that drove the latest rotation of its exaggerated economic cycles. Its political campaigns are primarily waged over the air, while developers, farmers and corporations wage constant battles over the water, be they the Everglades or Florida’s 1,197 miles of coastline.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pro’s Tip Sheet: Adam Smith on Florida’s Political Environment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:23:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Wheaton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheaton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf4470ed-839b-51ae-87da-b81f2fe05026', 'word_count': 385, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf4470ed-839b-51ae-87da-b81f2fe05026'}, {'abstract': 'As much of a pop milestone as “Downtown” was, who in 1965 would have imagined that Clark would still be singing it, at a swank Park Avenue cabaret no less, almost 50 years later? Probably not even Clark herself.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/petula-clark-downtown-uptown/', 'snippet': 'As much of a pop milestone as “Downtown” was, who in 1965 would have imagined that Clark would still be singing it, at a swank Park Avenue cabaret no less, almost 50 years later? Probably not even Clark herself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Petula Clark’s cloud-breaking voice first entered my life when, as a nursery schooler, I begged my parents to buy me her big new hit single, “Downtown.” So it seemed appropriate that I spent Friday night, the 47th anniversary of the song’s run at No. 1, hearing her sing it at Feinstein’s at Loews Regency, during her first extended New York engagement in decades. Appropriate, but also odd. As much of a pop milestone as “Downtown” was, who in 1965 would have imagined that Clark would still be singing it, at a swank Park Avenue cabaret no less, almost 50 years later? Probably not even Clark herself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Petula Clark: ‘Downtown’ Uptown', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:27:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Hoerburger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoerburger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c72ede5-70d2-5af0-8bd4-c078acc5cee1', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c72ede5-70d2-5af0-8bd4-c078acc5cee1'}, {'abstract': 'A fast-growing start-up, ZocDoc, is both an example of a bottom-up success story in health information technology, and of the cost of scaling out a Web service.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/lessons-from-zocdoc-a-health-tech-start-up-that-works/', 'snippet': 'A fast-growing start-up, ZocDoc, is both an example of a bottom-up success story in health information technology, and of the cost of scaling out a Web service.', 'lead_paragraph': ' ZocDoc, an oddly named Web service for scheduling appointments with doctors, started in New York in 2007 and has been growing at a torrid pace. Last week the company added Seattle, its 14th metropolitan market in the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/27/technology/27bits-docs/27bits-docs-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lessons From ZocDoc, a Health Tech Start-Up That Works', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benioff, Marc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ZocDoc Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:28:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Lohr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lohr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60c758bc-eb50-5c96-81d7-8fcd7d869971', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60c758bc-eb50-5c96-81d7-8fcd7d869971'}, {'abstract': 'The manufacturer determined the cause of a safety defect in April 2011, but it announced a recall of its Chaperone infant car seat only on Jan. 26.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/britax-recalls-14220-child-restraints-for-harness-flaw/', 'snippet': 'The manufacturer determined the cause of a safety defect in April 2011, but it announced a recall of its Chaperone infant car seat only on Jan. 26.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Britax Child Safety is recalling 14,220 child restraints because the shoulder harness may not hold the child, according to a document posted Saturday on the Web site of the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration. A further 3,600 seats are being recalled in Canada.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britax Recalls 14,220 Child Restraints for Harness Flaw', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Car Seats', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mazda Motor Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:30:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62da774d-a25d-5bc0-997f-b1b03395177c', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62da774d-a25d-5bc0-997f-b1b03395177c'}, {'abstract': 'On Monday, demonstrators added to their camp at McPherson Square even though the National Park Service had instructed them to remove their gear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/us/tensions-rise-but-no-evictions-at-occupy-dc.html', 'snippet': 'On Monday, demonstrators added to their camp at McPherson Square even though the National Park Service had instructed them to remove their gear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Demonstrators affiliated with the Occupy movement maintained a wary vigil here on Monday as a deadline from the National Park Service for campers to remove their gear or depart from two downtown parks came and went.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/us/OCCUPY/OCCUPY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/us/OCCUPY/OCCUPY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/us/OCCUPY/OCCUPY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupy D.C. Protesters Stay Put as Evacuation Deadline Passes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Occupy D.C. Protesters Stay Put as Evacuation Deadline Passes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Park Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:34:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Theo Emery', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Theo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Emery', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4b015a4-43d2-5592-b17e-8fdffb07dde0', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4b015a4-43d2-5592-b17e-8fdffb07dde0'}, {'abstract': 'The case could come down to who the jury decides was the mastermind of what prosecutors called an international crime that defrauded nearly 30,000 investors over more than 20 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/business/in-stanford-fraud-case-longtime-friends-become-courtroom-enemies.html', 'snippet': 'The case could come down to who the jury decides was the mastermind of what prosecutors called an international crime that defrauded nearly 30,000 investors over more than 20 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOUSTON — R. Allen Stanford and James M. Davis were roommates at Baylor University and trusted friends over three decades who together built an international financial empire. But this week they are scheduled to confront each other in federal court as indicted swindlers ready to cross swords. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/business/31stanford-span/31stanford-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/business/31stanford-span/31stanford-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/business/31stanford-span/31stanford-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stanford Fraud Case Puts Old Friends at Odds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stanford Fraud Case Puts Old Friends at Odds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stanford, Robert Allen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ponzi and Pyramid Schemes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford Financial Group', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, James M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:35:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clifford Krauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clifford', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90f48ba9-dcfc-5bb5-aa1c-8eca1e42d343', 'word_count': 1375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90f48ba9-dcfc-5bb5-aa1c-8eca1e42d343'}, {'abstract': 'Mormons, Catholics and the politics of religious charity.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/religious-giving-and-its-critics/', 'snippet': 'Mormons, Catholics and the politics of religious charity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since I used my Sunday column to write about the ways that government can crowd out — sometimes gently and subtly, and sometimes much more directly —\xa0private efforts to serve the common good, I was struck by the way that The New Republic’s Alec MacGillis reacted to conservative praise for the millions of dollars that Mitt Romney has given away to private charity:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Religious Giving and Its Critics', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:36:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f508be0-1ffc-59ef-a895-7c10ea857f59', 'word_count': 797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f508be0-1ffc-59ef-a895-7c10ea857f59'}, {'abstract': 'A view of the day in sports, from the Giants’ trip to Indianapolis for the Super Bowl to action from the African Cup of Nations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/01/30/sports/20120130photoreplay.html', 'snippet': 'A view of the day in sports, from the Giants’ trip to Indianapolis for the Super Bowl to action from the African Cup of Nations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A view of the day in sports, from the Giants’ trip to Indianapolis for the Super Bowl to action from the African Cup of Nations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/30/sports/20120130photoreplay-slide-Q4HS/20120130photoreplay-slide-Q4HS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 623, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/30/sports/20120130photoreplay-slide-Q4HS/20120130photoreplay-slide-Q4HS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/30/sports/20120130photoreplay-slide-Q4HS/20120130photoreplay-slide-Q4HS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Photo Replay: Jan. 30', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skiing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:39:24+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/293a3aea-59d4-5e07-a73d-079862120796', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/293a3aea-59d4-5e07-a73d-079862120796'}, {'abstract': 'One of the most talked about Super Bowl commercials — at first, on the basis of a 10-second teaser — features Matthew Broderick in a tribute to his long-remembered role in “Ferris Bueller’s Day Off” in advertisement for the Honda CR-V.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/in-honda-ad-matthew-broderick-takes-a-day-off/', 'snippet': 'One of the most talked about Super Bowl commercials — at first, on the basis of a 10-second teaser — features Matthew Broderick in a tribute to his long-remembered role in “Ferris Bueller’s Day Off” in advertisement for the Honda CR-V.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most talked about commercials in the coming Super Bowl — on the basis of a 10-second teaser — features Matthew Broderick in a tribute to his long-remembered role in “Ferris Bueller’s Day Off” in an advertisement for the Honda CR-V. That clip has now been superseded by a nearly two-and-a-half-minute version, our friends at the Wheels blog reported. Unlike most viewers, perhaps, Jonathan Schultz was struck “by the level of suspension compression that Honda would allow the cameras to capture in the jump sequence.” Read more at the Wheels blog »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Honda Ad, Matthew Broderick Takes a Day Off', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Broderick, Matthew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Honda Motor Co Ltd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:39:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44498449-4a20-53e0-8289-50a6a2e4db51', 'word_count': 94, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44498449-4a20-53e0-8289-50a6a2e4db51'}, {'abstract': 'The New Jersey Symphony Orchestra’s opening night next season will feature Gershwin and Ellington.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/new-jersey-symphony-to-mark-jazz-age-and-wagner-next-season/', 'snippet': 'The New Jersey Symphony Orchestra’s opening night next season will feature Gershwin and Ellington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jazz Age American music and a celebration of the 200th anniversary of Wagner’s birth will be among the highlights of the New Jersey Symphony Orchestra’s 2012–13 season. Jacques Lacombe, in his third year as music director, will lead 16 weeks of traditional classical programs, four pops programs in Newark and New Brunswick and three family concerts. Opening night, on Sept. 28, will feature the pianist Jean-Yves Thibaudet and include Gershwin’s “Rhapsody in Blue.” The program also features “Remembering Gatsby: Foxtrot for Orchestra,” by John Harbison, a New Jersey native, that is part of the orchestra’s New Jersey Roots Project. During the season the orchestra will also perform music from Wagner’s “Tristan und Isolde,” “Die Meistersinger von Nürnberg” and “Parsifal.” More information about the season is available here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Jersey Symphony to Mark Jazz Age and Wagner Next Season', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harbison, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lacombe, Jacques', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagner, Richard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Brunswick (NJ)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Newark (NJ)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:39:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93c32278-da01-54f1-b2f9-8f1a272496d2', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93c32278-da01-54f1-b2f9-8f1a272496d2'}, {'abstract': 'There has been a diversity of polling results in Florida — more so than in the three earlier nominating contests — even among surveys conducted at the same time.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/polls-diverge-but-all-point-to-a-romney-win/', 'snippet': 'There has been a diversity of polling results in Florida — more so than in the three earlier nominating contests — even among surveys conducted at the same time.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Polls Diverge, but All Point to a Romney Win', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:39:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d23ce65-979c-5b55-bbfe-6d87ce90ce44', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d23ce65-979c-5b55-bbfe-6d87ce90ce44'}, {'abstract': 'Compare commercials from year to year and watch some of the most memorable spots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2008/01/31/business/media/20080131_SUPERBOWLADS_GRAPHIC.html', 'snippet': 'Compare commercials from year to year and watch some of the most memorable spots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Compare commercials from year to year and watch some of the most memorable spots.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Super Ad Bowl: Two Decades of Players', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:48:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/1d6c7f67-a29d-56ec-b9f5-7926cfa55e32', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/1d6c7f67-a29d-56ec-b9f5-7926cfa55e32'}, {'abstract': 'The sedentary comforts of suburban expansion have fostered obesity, poor health, social isolation, excessive stress and depression.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/communities-learn-the-good-life-can-be-a-killer/', 'snippet': 'The sedentary comforts of suburban expansion have fostered obesity, poor health, social isolation, excessive stress and depression.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Developers in the last half-century called it progress when they built homes and shopping malls far from city centers throughout the country, sounding the death knell for many downtowns. But now an alarmed cadre of public health experts say these expanded metropolitan areas have had a far more serious impact on the people who live there by creating vehicle-dependent environments that foster obesity, poor health, social isolation, excessive stress and depression.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Communities Learn the Good Life Can Be a Killer', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Personal Health: Communities Learn the Good Life Can Be a Killer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suburbs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:52:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane E. Brody', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Brody', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bb76fdb-9348-5cbd-8ceb-89863874968c', 'word_count': 1023, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bb76fdb-9348-5cbd-8ceb-89863874968c'}, {'abstract': 'Nail Rock, Sally Hansen Salon Effects and Impress Press-On Manicure are the products that were sampled.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/02/02/fashion/products-for-do-it-yourself-nail-artists.html', 'snippet': 'Nail Rock, Sally Hansen Salon Effects and Impress Press-On Manicure are the products that were sampled.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WITH the rising popularity of salon nail art, companies have started to introduce affordable do-it-yourself versions. Impress Press-On Manicure by Broadway Nails is like an updated version of Lee Press-On Nails. More substantial than polish strips, the hard, nail-like adhesives are long and come in 18 trendy colors ($5.99 at drugstores, including Walgreens), and 18 patterns ($7.99). Once applied, there seemed to be too much space between the real and fake nails toward the cuticle, which made an irritating gathering spot for stray fibers. The adhesives looked decent and survived a multitude of weekend chores. But after 24 hours they were officially annoying me, so I pulled them off, which felt odd but not painful.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/fashion/0130trialrun/0130trialrun-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/31/fashion/0130trialrun/0130trialrun-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/fashion/0130trialrun/0130trialrun-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/fashion/0130trialrun/0130trialrun-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/fashion/0130trialrun/0130trialrun-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Do-It-Yourself Nail Artists', 'kicker': 'Trial Run', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nails (Body Part)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T19:53:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilary Howard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Howard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/627c540d-f0d0-5539-a79e-604a6e6f2dff', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/627c540d-f0d0-5539-a79e-604a6e6f2dff'}, {'abstract': 'The New Museum for Contemporary Art – which raised its admission fee recently to $16 – has reset the fee to the previous level, $14 for adults.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/new-museum-lowers-entry-fee/', 'snippet': 'The New Museum for Contemporary Art – which raised its admission fee recently to $16 – has reset the fee to the previous level, $14 for adults.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Museums often make news by raising their admission prices. (The Metropolitan Museum of Art’s recommended admission went to $25 from $20 last year; the Museum of Modern Art, whose fee is not recommended but mandatory, also rose to $25 from $20.) It’s much rarer – in fact, almost unheard of – for a museum to get attention by reducing its prices. But the New Museum for Contemporary Art – which raised its admission fee recently to cover higher operating costs for the highly participatory show of the work of Carsten Höller – has decided to lower it. Before the show the fee for adults was $12, which was raised to $16 for the Höller show. It has now been set at $14. The museum continues to offer free admission every Thursday evening from 7 to 9 p.m. and to anyone under 18 at all times. The museum’s next exhibition, its second Triennial, titled “The Ungovernables,” opens Feb. 15.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Museum Lowers Entry Fee After Raising It', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holler, Carsten', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Museum of Contemporary Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T20:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', '