: {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon and Jeffrey E. Singer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Singer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21b35a9c-3749-50a4-bb7f-633f098d5d31', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21b35a9c-3749-50a4-bb7f-633f098d5d31'}, {'abstract': 'Smile, the already popular restaurant on Bond Street, opens a take-out joint.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/now-serving-smile-to-go/', 'snippet': 'Smile, the already popular restaurant on Bond Street, opens a take-out joint.', 'lead_paragraph': '“For a long time I have wanted to do takeaway food. Other cities have great takeaway, but I haven’t found it in Manhattan,” explains Matt Kliegman, who with co-owner Carlos Quirarte and head chef Melia Marden, open their long-awaited Smile to Go today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Serving | Smile to Go', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T20:32:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Moonves', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moonves', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da2ee7f4-35cc-50ee-96fb-f860b202eaba', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da2ee7f4-35cc-50ee-96fb-f860b202eaba'}, {'abstract': 'The N.F.L.’s version of an all-star game delivered the kind of adult viewership to NBC that advertisers desire.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/pro-bowl-helps-nbc-in-ratings/', 'snippet': 'The N.F.L.’s version of an all-star game delivered the kind of adult viewership to NBC that advertisers desire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC’s coverage of the 2012 N.F.L. Pro Bowl drew a decent number of fans on Sunday as the American Football Conference defeated the National Football Conference 59-41, though not nearly enough to dominate the overall broadcast network ratings. NBC was No. 1 in the 18-to-49-year-old demographic segment, according to preliminary overnight Nielsen estimates, and it finished second in total viewers with 10.7 million, just behind CBS (11.4 million). Those two networks were close enough in total viewers that NBC might yet squeak by when the final numbers are released. “Undercover Boss” had the largest audience for CBS, at 13.1 million total viewers. On ABC, “Once Upon a Time” continued to perform well – especially for a freshman drama – with its highest audience total since November (10.8 million).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pro Bowl Helps NBC in Ratings', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pro Bowl', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T20:33:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9caf5b2-260d-5bf0-8535-e6cd736bb2c0', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9caf5b2-260d-5bf0-8535-e6cd736bb2c0'}, {'abstract': 'A frenetic little industry has taken root in New York City based on finding and publicizing the once-and-future homes of just about anyone with name recognition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/nyregion/in-selling-home-attaching-fame-to-find-fortune.html', 'snippet': 'A frenetic little industry has taken root in New York City based on finding and publicizing the once-and-future homes of just about anyone with name recognition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than 15 years, Michael A. Gales has lived in a cozy but serviceable studio apartment on 66th Street just off First Avenue in Manhattan. There is not a lot of glamour about the place — just enough room for a queen-sized bed, a puffy green sofa and a small, separate kitchen to the right of the front door.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/nyregion/31appraisal-span/31appraisal-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/31/nyregion/31appraisal-span/31appraisal-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/nyregion/31appraisal-span/31appraisal-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/nyregion/31appraisal-span/31appraisal-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/nyregion/31appraisal-span/31appraisal-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To Sell Home, Attaching Fame to Find Fortune', 'kicker': 'The Appraisal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Sell Home, Attaching Fame To Find Fortune', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T20:34:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a8be75e-e506-5419-9e18-9de2f10f5ff2', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a8be75e-e506-5419-9e18-9de2f10f5ff2'}, {'abstract': 'The Great Performers series has announced its 2012-13 lineup.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/dudamel-gergiev-salonen-and-more-coming-to-great-performers/', 'snippet': 'The Great Performers series has announced its 2012-13 lineup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gustavo Dudamel and his orchestra, the Los Angeles Philharmonic, will give the first New York performance of the oratorio “The Gospel According to the Other Mary” composed by John Adams next season, Lincoln Center said on Monday. The concert was announced as part of the Great Performers 2012-13 lineup, which will include two concerts devoted to Brahms by Valery Gergiev and the London Symphony Orchestra.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dudamel, Gergiev, Salonen and More Coming to ‘Great Performers’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berg, Alban', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 