, 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'Claiborne', 'lastname': 'Ray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/58068b85-8e7c-5230-9ae9-0e5860ebd75c', 'word_count': 247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58068b85-8e7c-5230-9ae9-0e5860ebd75c'}, {'abstract': 'The London Handel Players make their New York debut with works by Johann Joachim Quantz, the great flute virtuoso, and Frederick the Great, the Prussian king and talented amateur flutist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/arts/music/handel-players-make-their-new-york-debut-at-the-frick.html', 'snippet': 'The London Handel Players make their New York debut with works by Johann Joachim Quantz, the great flute virtuoso, and Frederick the Great, the Prussian king and talented amateur flutist.', 'lead_paragraph': '“On Playing the Flute,” the manual written in 1752 by Johann Joachim Quantz, has long been a benchmark for flute players. But much of Quantz’s vast collection of concertos and sonatas languished in the Berlin State Library until recently, when Rachel Brown, the Baroque flutist, sifted through the manuscripts, then edited and published a few, including the delightful Flute Sonata in B flat (No. 275).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Ensemble Raises a Flute to Frederick the Great', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'London Ensemble Raises a Flute to Frederick the Great', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frederick II, King of Prussia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Frick Collection', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quantz, Johann Joachim', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T22:04:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b95b463-06c3-5202-ae12-5cbc789ad1b3', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b95b463-06c3-5202-ae12-5cbc789ad1b3'}, {'abstract': 'Does Vassar College owe the 76 accepted-then-rejected students anything, or are mistakes just part of a digital application process?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/what-should-vassar-college-do-about-its-admissions-mistake/', 'snippet': 'Does Vassar College owe the 76 accepted-then-rejected students anything, or are mistakes just part of a digital application process?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Electronic college admissions decisions make me nostalgic for the old slim letter/fat letter days. Waiting for a piece of paper in a stamped envelope was a rite of passage. Plus, sending word the old-fashioned way provided more opportunities to catch an error like the one that happened at Vassar College over the weekend.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Should Vassar College Do About Its Admissions Mistake?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vassar College', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T22:08:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judy Bolton-Fasman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bolton-Fasman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f323d355-e916-5eec-805e-75280e2f8c2e', 'word_count': 864, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f323d355-e916-5eec-805e-75280e2f8c2e'}, {'abstract': 'At the National Hot Rod Association Museum in Pomona, Calif., a new exhibition documents the engineering prowess and branding acumen of Dean Moon, a pivotal figure in early drag-racing culture.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/a-tribute-to-moon-the-man-the-machines-and-those-eyes/', 'snippet': 'At the National Hot Rod Association Museum in Pomona, Calif., a new exhibition documents the engineering prowess and branding acumen of Dean Moon, a pivotal figure in early drag-racing culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'POMONA, Calif. — Dean Moon, who died in 1987 at age 60, established a small production shop behind his father’s restaurant in the Los Angeles suburb of Norwalk in 1952. Moon Equipment would become one of the most recognized and respected names in the hot-rod and drag-racing cultures, in part for its unmistakable logo: two goggling eyes casting a sidelong glance, a design Mr. Moon first toyed with in his high school typing class.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tribute to Moon: The Man, the Machines and Those Eyes', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drag Racing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hot Rod Assn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Specialty Equipment Market Association', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pomona (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-30T22:09:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronald Ahrens', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronald', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahrens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec4c65be-ce6c-5078-a314-bb4f22a7adbc', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec4c65be-ce6c-5078-a314-bb4f22a7adbc'}, {'abstract': 'Miami City Ballet is performing “Viscera,” a new ballet by the rising British choreographer Liam Scarlett.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/arts/dance/miami-city-ballet-performs-liam-scarletts-viscera.html', 'snippet': 'Miami City Ballet is performing “Viscera,” a new ballet by the rising British choreographer Liam Scarlett.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WEST PALM BEACH, Fla. — In the past two years the young British choreographer Liam Scarlett has achieved the status of an important classical-ballet choreographer. The images, constructions and textures of “Viscera,” his new creation for Miami City Ballet, show why. I watched it with three different casts over the weekend at the Kravis Center for the Performing Arts here. Each time the audience proclaimed its excitement the moment it ended. That’s a natural response to the terrific control of physical energy in its elaborate finale; but in terms of architectural organization too, this is an impressive piece.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/arts/SUB-SCARLETT/SUB-SCARLETT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 910, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ran