rod has discovered a new skill — the snarky, sharp-edged retort, delivered in fewer than 140 characters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Axelrod has found his id. On Twitter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If Twitter Is Any Clue, Axelrod Thinks a Lot About Romney', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T00:11:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df7c4d7d-e3bd-5b0d-be2d-c7148b2d89c9', 'word_count': 600, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df7c4d7d-e3bd-5b0d-be2d-c7148b2d89c9'}, {'abstract': 'Remains from a Siberian cave and faster, cheaper technology are helping scientists draw a complex new picture of human origins.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/science/gains-in-dna-are-speeding-research-into-human-origins.html', 'snippet': 'Remains from a Siberian cave and faster, cheaper technology are helping scientists draw a complex new picture of human origins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The tip of a girl’s 40,000-year-old pinky finger found in a cold Siberian cave, paired with faster and cheaper genetic sequencing technology, is helping scientists draw a surprisingly complex new picture of human origins.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/science/31ORIG/31ORIG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 