ed in from the runway. When the door opened, numerous coaches, assorted staff members and players stepped out, and the Giants had officially arrived at Super Bowl XLVI.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/sports/GIANTS1/GIANTS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 312, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/31/sports/GIANTS1/GIANTS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 312}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/sports/GIANTS1/GIANTS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 532, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/sports/GIANTS1/GIANTS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/sports/GIANTS1/GIANTS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Giants Leave the Injured Back at Home, for Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Touchdown, in Style: Giants Leave the Injured Back at Home, for Now', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sports Injuries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Super Bowl', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T02:23:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d237d40-1d2b-56c1-aa4c-ee9301359ab2', 'word_count': 1004, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d237d40-1d2b-56c1-aa4c-ee9301359ab2'}, {'abstract': 'So far in 2012, the bounces that candidates have received from primary and caucus wins have been minimal or have faded much too quickly to help the candidate in the next state.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/the-incredible-shrinking-primary-bounce/', 'snippet': 'So far in 2012, the bounces that candidates have received from primary and caucus wins have been minimal or have faded much too quickly to help the candidate in the next state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the maxims of past nomination campaigns is that a candidate should expect to receive a bounce in the polls after winning a major primary or caucus — particularly if he or she beat the expectations established by the polls and the news media while doing so. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Incredible Shrinking Primary Bounce', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T02:24:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1885556f-2f91-53ad-8156-00f2aa519e69', 'word_count': 1106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1885556f-2f91-53ad-8156-00f2aa519e69'}, {'abstract': 'Patricia Schultz, author of “1,000 Places to See Before You Die,” is an anxious flier, but radiant scenery from Africa to Alaska has helped her appreciate flying.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/business/above-africa-and-alaska-awe-replaces-anxiety-frequent-flier.html', 'snippet': 'Patricia Schultz, author of “1,000 Places to See Before You Die,” is an anxious flier, but radiant scenery from Africa to Alaska has helped her appreciate flying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CONSIDERING the fact that I’m an author and I’m telling people about places they need to see, it’s crazy that I’m actually a nervous flier. Maybe people who don’t like to fly can take some solace in that. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/business/subFlier/subFlier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/business/subFlier/subFlier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/business/subFlier/subFlier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Above Africa and Alaska, Awe Replaces Anxiety', 'kicker': 'Frequent Flier', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Above Africa and Alaska, Awe Replaces Anxiety', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schultz, Patricia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Travel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T02:25:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/208bacd5-1aab-5f35-9460-82b3e3085387', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/208bacd5-1aab-5f35-9460-82b3e3085387'}, {'abstract': 'How a controversial inventor built the Navy’s first ironclad.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/a-cheesebox-on-a-raft/', 'snippet': 'How a controversial inventor built the Navy’s first ironclad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A month before the first shots were fired at Fort Sumter, the Philadelphia Examiner editorialized, “If we intend to have a national naval force worthy of our power and pretensions, we shall have to … build iron-cased vessels. … Before the end of the year, France will have eight and England six. … How many are we to have?” At that moment, most of the country’s ranking naval officers would have answered “none,” for they were uniformly skeptical of both the need for and the practicality of “ironclads.” To John Lenthall, the Navy’s chief of construction, equipment and repair, the very idea was a “humbug.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Cheesebox on a Raft', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Engineering and Engineers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ericsson, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norfolk (Va)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T02:30:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Beard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fd4bcef-699a-5fd2-ae11-120014ece69c', 'word_count': 1668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fd4bcef-699a-5fd2-ae11-120014ece69c'}, {'abstract': 'The strange history of cities split in two by international borders.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/can-a-town-divided-against-itself-stand/', 'snippet': 'The strange history of cities split in two by international borders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Abraham Lincoln, what does he know? A house divided against itself can stand, and will do so for decades, no sweat. Berlin, Nicosia, Jerusalem — heck, even Kansas City: all are cities that have been split in two by borders. All are still around. But if being split down the middle doesn’t mean the end of the world, it ain’t fun either. A house divided is a house of pain, with degrees of inconvenience varying from administrative annoyance to frequent lethality. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can a Town Divided Against Itself Stand?', 'kicker': 'Borderlines', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cyprus', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jerusalem (Israel)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kosovo', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Latvia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T02:30:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/201b6012-b0f3-5f98-b1a5-61d022a17654', 'word_count': 2033, 'uri': 'nyt://article/201b6012-b0f3-5f98-b1a5-61d022a17654'}, {'abstract': 'Two days after 76 applicants were mistakenly told they had been accepted to Vassar College, the school’s president apologized for the confusion and said the college would reimburse the students’ $65 application fees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/nyregion/after-mistake-a-mea-culpa-from-vassar.html', 'snippet': 'Two days after 76 applicants were mistakenly told they had been accepted to Vassar College, the school’s president apologized for the confusion and said the college would reimburse the students’ $65 application fees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After 76 applicants were mistakenly told they had been accepted to Vassar College, its president has apologized for the “considerable confusion and hurt” caused by the “terribly upsetting event,” and said the college would reimburse the students’ $65 application fees.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Mistake, a Mea Culpa From Vassar', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Mistake, A Mea Culpa From Vassar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vassar College', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T02:32:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Flegenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Flegenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9efbe33-a758-57b2-9768-3daa51fb915e', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9efbe33-a758-57b2-9768-3daa51fb915e'}, {'abstract': 'The Patriots know that to beat the Giants in the Super Bowl on Sunday, they must protect quarterback Tom Brady.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/sports/football/giants-defensive-line-has-tom-brady-in-its-crosshairs.html', 'snippet': 'The Patriots know that to beat the Giants in the Super Bowl on Sunday, they must protect quarterback Tom Brady.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INDIANAPOLIS — From studying videotape of the Giants’ defense this season, the New England Patriots know they must protect Tom Brady on Sunday. This revelation was disclosed Monday by their center, Dan Connolly, who concluded that it will take “work and effort” to shield Brady from a pass rush from the team that pummeled him four years ago in Super Bowl XLII but, in what must be a relief to New England fans everywhere, “yes, I think we can do it.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/sports/YPATRIOTS/YPATRIOTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/31/sports/YPATRIOTS/YPATRIOTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/sports/YPATRIOTS/YPATRIOTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'su