/video/93b8a4ac-85ec-5b0f-806e-4f43859ab129'}, {'abstract': 'A noxious bill passed by the House seeks to deny a life-saving cash refund under the child tax credit to those who file tax returns using individual taxpayer identification numbers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/opinion/a-harder-squeeze-on-the-poor.html', 'snippet': 'A noxious bill passed by the House seeks to deny a life-saving cash refund under the child tax credit to those who file tax returns using individual taxpayer identification numbers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'House Republicans have hit upon a noxious scheme to help pay for an extension of the payroll tax cut: a tax increase on millions of poor working families. A bill passed by the House and now in conference seeks to deny cash refunds under the child tax credit to those who file tax returns using “individual taxpayer identification numbers” issued by the Internal Revenue Service. Only those using Social Security numbers would be eligible. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Harder Squeeze on the Poor', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Harder Squeeze on the Poor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payroll Tax', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T03:51:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/60871df6-05ad-53ed-a1a7-e9c23700ac96', 'word_count': 327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60871df6-05ad-53ed-a1a7-e9c23700ac96'}, {'abstract': 'A series of articles and videos chronicling the experiences of military veterans who have returned from Iraq and Afghanistan but continue to confront the medical and psychological scars of battle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/us/the-hard-road-back.html', 'snippet': 'A series of articles and videos chronicling the experiences of military veterans who have returned from Iraq and Afghanistan but continue to confront the medical and psychological scars of battle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A series of articles and videos chronicling the experiences of military veterans who have returned from Iraq and Afghanistan but continue to confront the medical and psychological scars of battle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hard Road Back', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T03:54:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a854e97d-6ce2-57d3-a68a-fd35bc260a1c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a854e97d-6ce2-57d3-a68a-fd35bc260a1c'}, {'abstract': 'With help from a program that aids badly burned veterans, Joey Paulk received surgery that revived a measure of his self-confidence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/us/for-soldier-disfigured-in-afghanistan-a-way-to-return-to-the-world.html', 'snippet': 'With help from a program that aids badly burned veterans, Joey Paulk received surgery that revived a measure of his self-confidence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Specialist Joey Paulk awoke from a coma in a Texas hospital three weeks after he was burned nearly to death in Afghanistan. Wrapped in bandages from head almost to toe, he immediately saw his girlfriend and mother, and felt comforted. Then he glanced at his hands, two balls of white gauze, and realized that he had no fingers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/us/RECOVERY/RECOVERY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/01/31/us/RECOVERY/RECOVERY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/us/RECOVERY/RECOVERY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 855, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/us/RECOVERY/RECOVERY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/us/RECOVERY/RECOVERY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Soldier Disfigured in War, a Way to Return to the World', 'kicker': 'The Hard Road Back | Scars of Battle', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Soldier Disfigured in War, A Way to Return to the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Series', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plastic Surgery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of California, Los Angeles', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Burns', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Face', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooke Army Medical Center', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T03:54:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Dao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce9cee44-97bf-5269-9123-1a993b17b605', 'word_count': 1510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce9cee44-97bf-5269-9123-1a993b17b605'}, {'abstract': 'The New York City Fire Department’s commissioner should craft a policy with firefighters that respects free expression without violating employment laws.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/opinion/free-opinions-at-firehouses.html', 'snippet': 'The New York City Fire Department’s commissioner should craft a policy with firefighters that respects free expression without violating employment laws.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some firefighters in the New York City Fire Department are actively opposing a federal judge’s order to reform the department’s long history of bad employment practices. They have started a misguided campaign called Merit Matters to stop the judge and the city from creating new exams for potential applicants and have distributed materials to various stationhouses. Last month, the Fire Department, in what seems like a misguided response, tried to ban “material presenting opinions or viewpoints” anywhere in the city’s firehouses. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Free Opinions at Firehouses', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Free Opinions at Firehouses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fire Department (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T03:55:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f05a1dde-e447-561f-ade7-d8a77e0b2aaf', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f05a1dde-e447-561f-ade7-d8a77e0b2aaf'}, {'abstract': 'If Mormons are such responsible people, why are voters turned off?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2012/01/30/what-is-it-about-mormons/', 'snippet': 'If Mormons are such responsible people, why are voters turned off?', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is It About Mormons?', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T04:03:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eec73e5e-cd3c-5211-b818-4728fbbd2607', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eec73e5e-cd3c-5211-b818-4728fbbd2607'}, {'abstract': 'Transmediale, a five-day festival in Berlin, has a rich international program of artwork, installations, video screenings, workshops, performances and an inter-disciplinary symposiums, all organized by various guest curators.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/in-berlin-a-festival-focuses-on-the-contemporary-arts/', 'snippet': 'Transmediale, a five-day festival in Berlin, has a rich international program of artwork, installations, video screenings, workshops, performances and an inter-disciplinary symposiums, all organized by various guest curators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year is the 25th anniversary of Berlin’s alternative media arts festival, Transmediale. This five-day event, which starts on Tuesday at the Haus der Kulturen der Welt contemporary arts center, has a rich international program of artworks, installations, video screenings, workshops, performances and an interdisciplinary symposiums, all organized by various guest curators.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Berlin, a Festival Focuses on the Contemporary Arts', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Joshua', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T04:10:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By JJ Hurvich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hurvich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5347adb1-691c-57b4-9d03-117f15ea2eaf', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5347adb1-691c-57b4-9d03-117f15ea2eaf'}, {'abstract': 'France’s political system requires 500 mayors and local officials to sign their names in support of a presidential candidate before he or she can be put on the ballot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/world/europe/in-political-rite-french-hopefuls-seek-500-backers.html', 'snippet': 'France’s political system requires 500 mayors and local officials to sign their names in support of a presidential candidate before he or she can be put on the ballot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — In 1965, Marcel Barbu, a jeweler and philanthropist, ran for the French presidency as “the candidate of battered dogs.” In 2002, Pierre Rabhi, a farmer and philosopher of Algerian descent, built himself as the candidate of “peaceful insurrection.” Coluche, a well-known comedian, called for “the lazy, the filthy, the drug addicts, the alcoholics, the pedophiles” and other nonmainstream voters to elect him in 1981.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/world/FRANCE-4/FRANCE-4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/world/FRANCE-4/FRANCE-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/01/31/world/FRANCE-4/FRANCE-4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Political Ritual, Candidates Tour France in a Race for 500 Signatures', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Political Ritual, Candidates Tour France in a Race for 500 Signatures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endorsements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Le Pen, Marine', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T04:10:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By MAIA de la BAUME', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de la BAUME', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bf8efe2-2469-5ca6-b18f-2a0bf7d496f5', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bf8efe2-2469-5ca6-b18f-2a0bf7d496f5'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney may be winning votes again, but is he winning hearts?', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/money-cant-buy-him-love/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney may be winning votes again, but is he winning hearts?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most polls predict a sizable victory for Mitt Romney in Florida. How did this happen? How did Newt Gingrich lose his grip on the state? Money, that’s how.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Money Can’t Buy Him Love', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Restore Our Future', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Winning Our Future', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T04:17:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles M. Blow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72e2c9db-5b7e-5d8e-8905-aa4f793d6479', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72e2c9db-5b7e-5d8e-8905-aa4f793d6479'}, {'abstract': 'A selective liberal arts college admits to falsifying its’ students median SAT scores, in an apparent bid to boost its standing on college rankings.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/30/claremont-exaggerating-sat/', 'snippet': 'A selective liberal arts college admits to falsifying its’ students median SAT scores, in an apparent bid to boost its standing on college rankings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pamela B. Gann, the president of Claremont McKenna College, a small, selective liberal arts college in California, admitted on Monday that a “senior administrator” had been submitting inflated SAT data to ratings agencies since 2005, in an apparent effort to increase the college’s standing. My colleague Richard Pérez-Peña and I report in Tuesday’s Times that Richard C. Vos, vice president and dean of admissions, is believed to be the source of the false information, and the only individual involved.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Claremont McKenna in California Admits Exaggerating Students’ SAT Scores', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T04:28:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab6ea7f2-e0b7-5e1c-bc89-309f5aa59f83', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab6ea7f2-e0b7-5e1c-bc89-309f5aa59f83'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama defended the use of drones to strike suspected terrorists, saying the clandestine program was “kept on a very tight leash.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/world/middleeast/civilian-deaths-due-to-drones-are-few-obama-says.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama defended the use of drones to strike suspected terrorists, saying the clandestine program was “kept on a very tight leash.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama on Monday defended the use of drones to strike suspected terrorists in Pakistan and elsewhere, saying the clandestine program was “kept on a very tight leash” and enabled the United States to use “pinpoint” targeting to avoid more intrusive military action.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multime