sentatives passed a constitutional amendment to abolish slavery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Jan. 31, 1865, the House of Representatives passed a constitutional amendment to abolish slavery. The amendment, which would become the 13th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution, read, “Neither slavery nor involuntary servitude, except as a punishment for crime whereof the party shall have been duly convicted, shall exist within the United States, or any place subject to their jurisdiction.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jan. 31, 1865 | House Passes 13th Amendment, Abolishing Slavery', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T09:02:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f805e163-9ece-566d-aad5-c30026946c64', 'word_count': 510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f805e163-9ece-566d-aad5-c30026946c64'}, {'abstract': 'Why are book publishers worried?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/banking-on-the-future-of-print/', 'snippet': 'Why are book publishers worried?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO is William R. Lynch? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Banking on the Future of Print', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T09:21:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Altavena', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altavena', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_i