ytimes.com/2012/02/01/theater/reviews/matilda-by-the-royal-shakespeare-company-in-london.html', 'snippet': '“Matilda,” the hit musical in London, follows Roald Dahl’s young character as she fights a sadistic headmistress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Smells like pre-teen spirit at the Cambridge Theater, where a throng of irresistibly fed-up boys and girls are storming the barricades of adult oppression. “Revolting children” they call themselves in the rousing final number of the musical “Matilda,” the fattest, sassiest hit of the season here. And the words have special savor for these kids because they’ve been used before, in another way.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/01/arts/01matilda-span/01matilda-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/02/01/arts/01matilda-span/01matilda-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/01/arts/01matilda-span/01matilda-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/01/arts/01matilda-span/01matilda-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/02/01/arts/01matilda-span/01matilda-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sugar and Spice, and Something Sinister', 'kicker': "Theater Review | 'Matilda'", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sugar and Spice, and Something Sinister', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Shakespeare Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dahl, Roald', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Minchin, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dad44554-6ffb-5aac-9661-ada4be48a7d6', 'word_count': 1088, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dad44554-6ffb-5aac-9661-ada4be48a7d6'}, {'abstract': 'For Kyu Nakama, a senior at Brooklyn Technical High School in New York City, the college acceptance Facebook page conveyed good news for others, at least early on.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/an-emphatic-congratulations/', 'snippet': 'For Kyu Nakama, a senior at Brooklyn Technical High School in New York City, the college acceptance Facebook page conveyed good news for others, at least early on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Never have I ever been so happy to see green triangles. When one appears next to each completed application on the common application Web site those shapes ensure that colleges see that I am here, asking to join them. However, that is just the beginning of the whole process. Before me still lies the long wait, shuffling through files of tax forms and applying to scholarships to help me survive through college.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Smiles and Exclamation Points as College Decisions Arrive, But Not for Me', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T11:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kyu Nakama', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kyu', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nakama', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8cd663a9-5312-5ae8-ae27-6ab632830b78', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8cd663a9-5312-5ae8-ae27-6ab632830b78'}, {'abstract': 'The author, balking at high replacement costs for the headlight lenses on his 2001 BMW 3 Series, tests a new product said to restore like-new clarity to the lenses.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/genie-in-a-bottle-testing-a-headlight-lens-defogging-solution/', 'snippet': 'The author, balking at high replacement costs for the headlight lenses on his 2001 BMW 3 Series, tests a new product said to restore like-new clarity to the lenses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a car ages, its plastic headlight lens covers tend to fog and yellow, which can reduce the amount of light that hits the road. Though it’s not as routine as an oil change or a tire rotation, replacing fogged lenses is no less critical maintenance for car owners.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Genie in a Bottle? Testing a Headlight-Lens Defogging Solution', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '3M Company', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bayerische Motorenwerke AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T11:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Schembari', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schembari', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f63b28c1-95d3-5862-831f-53dc756d8002', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f63b28c1-95d3-5862-831f-53dc756d8002'}, {'abstract': 'It’s hard to overhaul the tax code without an outline upfront of what is intended, which can be used to weigh the impacts of proposed changes against one another, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/how-to-avoid-reinventing-the-wheel-on-tax-reform-part-2/', 'snippet': 'It’s hard to overhaul the tax code without an outline upfront of what is intended, which can be used to weigh the impacts of proposed changes against one another, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bartlett held senior policy roles in the Reagan and George H.W. Bush administrations and served on the staffs of Representatives Jack Kemp and Ron Paul. He is the author of “The Benefit and the Burden: Tax Reform — Why We Need It and What It Will Take.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Avoid Reinventing the Wheel on Tax Reform, Part 2', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House Committee on Ways and Means', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T11:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bartlett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f475020-91dc-5361-b397-818e2c694e68', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f475020-91dc-5361-b397-818e2c694e68'}, {'abstract': 'Russia seems prepared to oppose both the West and its own traditional Arab friends by blocking a United Nations resolution condemning Syria’s bloody crackdown. Moscow’s realpolitik means that only a watered down declaration has any chance of passage, but from Vladimir V. Putin’s point of view, Russia has its reasons.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/russias-reasons-for-saying-no-on-syria/', 'snippet': 'Russia seems prepared to oppose both the West and its own traditional Arab friends by blocking a United Nations resolution condemning Syria’s bloody crackdown. Moscow’s realpolitik means that only a watered down declaration has any chance of passa...', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – Two decades after the collapse of the Soviet Union, Russia is regaining a reputation in international diplomacy as the country that likes to say nyet. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/01/31/blogs/syriaputin/syriaputin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia’s Reasons for Saying ‘No’ on Syria', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARAB LEAGUE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Syrian National Council', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T11:25:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78dc0105-16b0-56a6-b5c6-ac7870a7057d', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78dc0105-16b0-56a6-b5c6-ac7870a7057d'}, {'abstract': 'As the diplomacy gathered pace, Russia again renewed its opposition to an Arab League plan that calls for President Bashar al-Assad of Syria to cede power.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/02/01/world/middleeast/diplomats-discuss-bashar-al-assads-future-as-syria-fights-rebels.html', 'snippet': 'As the diplomacy gathered pace, Russia again renewed its opposition to an Arab League plan that calls for President Bashar al-Assad of Syria to cede power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNITED NATIONS — Syrian forces pushed rebels back from strongholds near Damascus on Tuesday in new spasms of fighting as some of the world’s top diplomats prepared at the United Nations to try to press President Bashar al-Assad to leave office through a Security Council resolution. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Diplomats Jockey Over Assad’s Future as Syrian Troops Press Attacks Near Capital', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Diplomats Jockey Over Assad’s Future as Syria Fighting Escalates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARAB LEAGUE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T11:55:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar and Nada Bakri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d59d1a3a-b2d5-5eb1-bd2c-6c8f90b7a9b5', 'word_count': 1298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d59d1a3a-b2d5-5eb1-bd2c-6c8f90b7a9b5'}, {'abstract': 'Should I pay people what they deserve — or just enough so that they don’t leave? It’s easy to let payroll get out of control and suck up all of the profit in the company.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/running-the-numbers-controlling-labor-costs/', 'snippet': 'Should I pay people what they deserve — or just enough so that they don’t leave? It’s easy to let payroll get out of control and suck up all of the profit in the company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I don’t keep close track of the hours I spend at work, never have, and probably never will. I have, in the past, worked fairly long hours, but I rarely exceed 60 hours a week. Most days, I work about 10 hours, but I’m not what you would call a steady worker — too many things to distract me, and no one looking over my shoulder. My people, though, work very hard and very steadily.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Running the Numbers: Controlling Labor Costs', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bonuses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Overtime', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Productivity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T12:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Downs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5e1336e-6eec-530b-a418-3f200c128ab6', 'word_count': 942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5e1336e-6eec-530b-a418-3f200c128ab6'}, {'abstract': 'Banco Santander announced that quarterly earnings fell nearly 98 percent, to 47 million euros, as it dealt with the downturn in the Spanish real estate market and the European debt crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/santanders-earnings-slump-on-european-debt-crisis/', 'snippet': 'Banco Santander announced that quarterly earnings fell nearly 98 percent, to 47 million euros, as it dealt with the downturn in the Spanish real estate market and the European debt crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Banco Santander of Spain announced on Tuesday that its net profit fell nearly 98 percent, to 47 million euros, during the final three months of last year, as the bank dealt with the downturn in the Spanish real estate market and the European debt crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Santander’s Profit Plunges on European Debt Crisis', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Botin, Emilio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Banco Santander S A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T12:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9a87b80-257b-5f11-935e-7223ec7c5e89', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9a87b80-257b-5f11-935e-7223ec7c5e89'}, {'abstract': 'New York’s newest K9 unit members will wear the kind of cameras used by Navy Seals in the raid on Osama bin Laden’s compound.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/k9-by-abigail-meisel/', 'snippet': 'New York’s newest K9 unit members will wear the kind of cameras used by Navy Seals in the raid on Osama bin Laden’s compound.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apache, a two-year-old German shepherd, tracked the suspect down a pitch-black passageway in the Brooklyn Bridge/City Hall subway station the other day, helped by the oldest of canine technology, his nose, and bearing the newest, an infrared camera harnessed to his back. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A City So Tough the New Police Dogs Shoot', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T12:04:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abigail Meisel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abigail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meisel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7158c082-0796-52a5-a16d-c241b9b45788', 'word_count': 469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7158c082-0796-52a5-a16d-c241b9b45788'}, {'abstract': 'Edgewood Park in California, which had three full-time rangers in the mid-1990s, now has just one who is shared with two other parks. Volunteers help the park scrape by.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/a-stalwart-park-makes-do-with-less/', 'snippet': 'Edgewood Park in California, which had three full-time rangers in the mid-1990s, now has just one who is shared with two other parks. Volunteers help the park scrape by.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a typical day at Edgewood Park, a 500-acre nature preserve in the hills above Silicon Valley 25 miles south of San Francisco. Even in January, thanks to the mild winter climate, scores of joggers, hikers, picnickers and the occasional horseback rider enjoy the five miles of trails and scenic spots.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Stalwart Park Makes Do With Less', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volunteers and Community Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joint Select Committee on Deficit Reduction', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Park Service', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parks Foundation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T12:10:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Witkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33723357-e493-5b5c-9962-4c0fb2bdfa32', 'word_count': 774, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33723357-e493-5b5c-9962-4c0fb2bdfa32'}, {'abstract': 'Caracas may be one of the least bike-friendly cities anywhere, but that hasn’t stopped cyclists from trying to shake things up.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/critical-mass-in-caracas/', 'snippet': 'Caracas may be one of the least bike-friendly cities anywhere, but that hasn’t stopped cyclists from trying to shake things up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CARACAS, Venezuela — Start with one of the world’s highest murder rates, add in a mountain setting and some of the cheapest gasoline anywhere, then sprinkle liberally with a hostile and chaotic car culture and you get Caracas, perhaps one