'snippet': 'Box office figures show this year’s batch of best-picture nominees in significantly worse shape than last year’s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No matter how you slice the numbers for this year’s best picture nominees, they still look the same: Dismal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oscar Hopefuls at the Box Office: It’s Not a Pretty Picture', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T15:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply and Paula Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad925099-a933-5660-8071-63fa84ce3c46', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad925099-a933-5660-8071-63fa84ce3c46'}, {'abstract': 'How far should The Times go when seeking out interviews with those confined to hospital beds? The public editor takes a look at some examples.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/intruding-on-others-misfortune-when-is-it-warranted/', 'snippet': 'How far should The Times go when seeking out interviews with those confined to hospital beds? The public editor takes a look at some examples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When is a reporter doing the essential work of journalism by interviewing a victim, and when is it an invasion of privacy that yields no benefit to the public?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Intruding on Others’ Misfortune: When is it Warranted?', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T15:01:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arthur S. Brisbane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arthur', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Brisbane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84b542ef-29f8-5df3-af73-a29f0c18e7c5', 'word_count': 1355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84b542ef-29f8-5df3-af73-a29f0c18e7c5'}, {'abstract': 'To help offset the payroll tax cut, Republicans want to freeze the pay of federal workers.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/maybe-they-should-just-start-super-pacs/', 'snippet': 'To help offset the payroll tax cut, Republicans want to freeze the pay of federal workers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not always easy to figure out if Congress is in session. When they’re not, they hold pro-forma meetings designed to block presidential appointees. When they are, they hold meetings easily mistaken for the pro-forma versions in which little gets done beyond the routine blocking of presidential appointees. That, or they muck about with Band-Aid solutions instead of actually trying to help the economy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Maybe They Should Just Start Super PACs', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payroll Tax', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T15:02:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifie