stems Biology lab was far from play. Her experiments with worms shed light on how genes control behavior. And her work earned her a place as a finalist in the Intel Science Talent Search, along with HuiHui Fan of Stuyvesant and Danielle Goldman of Bronx Science. Here are their stories.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/01/31/worms-roots-and-anxiety-lead-three-young-scientists-to-the-intel-finals/', 'snippet': "UPDATED | Mimi Yen's father thought she was just playing with worms. But Ms. Yen’s countless hours spent in New York University’s Center for Genomics and Systems Biology lab was far from play. Her experiments with worms shed light on how genes con...", 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Worms, Roots and Anxiety Lead Three Young Scientists to the Intel Finals', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T20:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By ELBERT CHU', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elbert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'CHU', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c00f2cb-b392-5915-84f0-57759df87a10', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c00f2cb-b392-5915-84f0-57759df87a10'}, {'abstract': 'The actress is quietly elegant in a crimson keyhole dress by Burberry.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/look-of-the-moment-keira-knightley/', 'snippet': 'The actress is quietly elegant in a crimson keyhole dress by Burberry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Look: Natural Glow. A fresh face and simple upsweep echo the quiet elegance of this crimson keyhole dress.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Look of The Moment | Keira Knightley', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knightley, Keira', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Burberry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mayfair Hotel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T20:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea7c905e-d2fa-54f6-9aad-70046d5b1de9', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea7c905e-d2fa-54f6-9aad-70046d5b1de9'}, {'abstract': 'A gap in the law means employers aren’t required to accommodate pregnant women. A proposed bill in New York State would change that.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/accommodating-pregnant-women-at-work/', 'snippet': 'A gap in the law means employers aren’t required to accommodate pregnant women. A proposed bill in New York State would change that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Depending on your circumstances, being pregnant and needing just that little bit of extra accommodation at work — more bathroom breaks, a stool at the counter, a break from the requirement that all retail employees share in the lifting of boxes and climbing of ladders — could mean you’re out of a job. Laws that ban discrimination against pregnant women in the workplace don’t require that any special needs be accommodated, and the Americans With Disabilities Act, which would, doesn’t apply to most pregnant women.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Accommodating Pregnant Women at Work', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMERICANS WITH DISABILITIES ACT', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Obstetrics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-01-31T20:03:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2770787-f20c-5a55-8cbc-3500841a89e1', 'word_count': 179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2770787-f20c-5a55-8cbc-3500841a89e1'}, {'abstract': 'The Romney campaign protests a report card put out by an evangelical supporter of Newt Gingrich.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/01/31/a-conservative-report-card-with-facts-disputed/', 'snippet': 'The Romney campaign protests a report card put out by an evangelical supporter of Newt Gingrich.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a last-minute effort to derail Mitt Romney, conservative evangelical groups say they e-mailed a “report card” on the candidates’ “real positions” on marriage and moral standards on Monday to 1.6 million Florida residents, mainly self-identified Christians and pastors. The message is signed by the American Family Association, which strongly opposes same-sex marriage and equal rights for homosexuals, and claims to list the candidates’ positions on 10 issues. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Conservative Report Card, With Facts Disputed', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kick